var e={format:`tetra-vision-reviewed-chapters`,version:2,assignmentSha256:`1e0efdc1765601bce38a312937121db7c1f42e6d9e0ea704f3359298b5e62a6a`,releases:[{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.019`,candidateId:`EU-019`,definitionId:0,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`de9abb5843d7dc3011e03cd54fb3dd1c73596fa16f0793df105013619c110085`,variantSha256:`06c4c06d28310fd4d1029d594fcd6ca53444fd15a272adf22224198524e295c5`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.019.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Bronze Guardian`,text:`Crete rose ahead of the Argo after another weary stretch of sea. The voyagers needed more than a glimpse of land: they were thirsty, and their bodies had endured enough rowing. At Dicte's haven, however, someone already stood between them and the shore.

Talos lifted stones from the cliff and hurled them toward the approaching ship. His body was bronze, his feet bronze, his strength enough to make the coastline itself a weapon. Zeus had given him to Europa as the guardian of Crete, and three times each day he traveled around the island. In Apollonius's account he was the last survivor of an ancient bronze race, rather than a machine assembled in a workshop.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.019.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Medea’s Challenge`,text:`The crew pulled away. Across the widening water, the guardian seemed beyond anything ordinary weapons could accomplish. Yet his body was not equally protected everywhere. A vein near his ankle lay beneath only a thin covering; that small place bound his great frame to the possibility of death.

Medea asked the men to stop their retreat. Keep the ship beyond the stones, she told them, and let her attempt what their strength could not. Jason helped her across the benches to a place on deck. With her robe drawn around her face, she called upon powers of death and fixed her hostile attention on Talos.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.019.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Open Harbor`,text:`He continued lifting the rocks that guarded his harbor. Under Medea's influence, he scraped the vulnerable ankle against a sharp crag. His strength began to fail. For a moment he still towered over the coast, swaying; then the bronze guardian fell.

The Argo could finally reach shelter. The crew rested in Crete for the night, drew the water they needed, and departed with the growing light. Their escape owed its possibility to Medea, whose knowledge had crossed the sea where their weapons could not.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.048`,candidateId:`EU-048`,definitionId:2,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`047a83a89709a52791b5299f9ab4c6cd44cc25bad37a7ef99daed286d052c5cf`,variantSha256:`c84ace4df239425f10625ade15296eea8f488da058dafb6345d5f8c84d15d5d3`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r05-norse.json`,reportSha256:`9399338c3425cc079bcfa934e259fe499f8786dc49ea4024b5c1f92f4fdf57b4`,packetPath:`traditions/old-norse-and-icelandic.json`,packetSha256:`a02f4faf3561b8b80955b4a3658737373f0f4ef80afc37cffde3580f4c24e019`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:36:07.885Z`,independence:`Original packet authored by /root/norse_research. Coordinator independently reread the selected Anderson source passages and all card text, claims, adaptation limits and exact count briefs; not self-review.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.048.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Ivaldi’s Craft`,text:`Loki needed a gift that could repair the damage he had done. He had cut away Sif's hair, and Thor had demanded that he replace it. So Loki went to the sons of Ivaldi, whose craft could make gold grow like living hair. Their work did not end with that promise. They also fashioned a spear for Odin and a ship for Freyr.

The ship was called Skidbladnir. Its makers had prepared room for every god, along with weapons and gear. A voyage would never have to wait for the wind to turn. Once its sail was raised, the breeze would favor whatever course its owner chose.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.048.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Treasures Before the Gods`,text:`Loki took these treasures toward the gods, but on the way his confidence became a wager. He staked his head that another pair of smiths, Brok and Sindri, could not match the three marvelous gifts. The brothers answered with a golden-bristled boar, a ring that produced other rings, and a hammer whose handle had come out short.

At the judgment, Loki laid out the gifts from Ivaldi's sons. Freyr received the ship and learned its strangest quality: when the journey was done, he could fold the whole vessel like a napkin and carry it in his pocket. The sail, the space aboard, the promise of a ready wind—all belonged to something his hand could gather together.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.048.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Folded Voyage`,text:`The judges chose the hammer as the greatest treasure. Skidbladnir did not need to win that contest to remain extraordinary. Other ships demanded a harbor and a crew to guard them. Freyr's ship could rest beside him, folded away until the next departure. Within that little bundle waited room enough for the gods.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.003`,candidateId:`EU-003`,definitionId:3,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`775c5e4d1946cf28ff0d5b465e19d72409599b81bdf839735ae39ad5d6f36915`,variantSha256:`7fcf536698ed0cad14c6350331cd6028604d672b448027f58eaa427ca130a6c0`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.003.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Orchard’s Bright Thief`,text:`Each night the royal orchard lost something that could not be replaced by another season's harvest. Its finest tree bore golden apples, and a bright visitor arrived in darkness to carry them away. Two princes slept through their watches. Ivan, the youngest, kept looking until the branches lit around a bird whose feathers were gold and whose eyes had the clarity of crystal. He reached for it and came away with a single tail feather. The creature escaped, but its feather could light a dark room.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.003.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Cage and Further Demands`,text:`Possessing that small light only sharpened the king's desire for the living bird. Ivan rode out after it. His journey soon left him without his horse, and the grey wolf responsible became an unexpected guide. Beyond a stone wall, the wolf knew, the bird waited in a golden cage. Ivan could take the bird, but he must leave its cage untouched.

There lay the difficulty: the bird was marvelous, and the container seemed worthy of it. Ivan took both. The cage raised an alarm; guards caught him, and a foreign king made his freedom depend on another impossible acquisition. A golden-maned horse led to another forbidden object, another alarm, and finally a demand for Princess Elena.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.003.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Return, Betrayal and Recognition`,text:`The wolf's speed and transformations found a way through those bargains. Ivan returned with Elena, the horse, and the bird. Yet the road home held one last danger: his brothers attacked him while he slept and claimed the treasures as their own. The wolf came back and restored Ivan through the waters of death and life. Elena identified the rightful traveler at court, and the deception ended.

The bird had begun the tale as a thief of apples. By the end, its light had exposed more than an orchard's missing fruit: desire, bad judgment, loyalty, and the cost of treating living wonders as prizes.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.072`,candidateId:`EU-072`,definitionId:4,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6e827ed471ab6b7217d463281854aea940b9d3b86b35659b7e74af50076a7aa3`,variantSha256:`417b9cb9e2419e3e2a725411c95ba3559f72f4986bebf0f80ac668502851295a`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.072.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Quest and First Encounters`,text:`Culhwch's hope of marrying Olwen had become a list of impossible tasks. Among them lay a very particular difficulty: the grooming of Olwen's formidable father required a comb, a razor and shears carried between the ears of Twrch Trwyth. Arthur gathered helpers, horses and hounds, but the desired objects were easier to name than to take.

In Ireland, a scout approached the boar in bird form. He came away with a bristle instead of a treasure, and the encounter left him ill. Later, Arthur's fighters could make no easy progress against Twrch Trwyth and his young. Arthur said that the boar had once been a king, changed into this shape for his sins. That account did not make the creature any less dangerous.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.072.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Crossing into Wales`,text:`A second bird-shaped messenger tried speaking with the group. One of the young boars answered. They had already suffered transformation, he said; they would not agree to Arthur's demand. Soon they crossed the sea into Wales, taking the struggle with them.

The hunt moved through a long succession of named places. At each halt, what looked like an opportunity could become another disaster. Hunters, hounds and people of the countryside died. Arthur's own son was among those lost. The small objects stayed on the boar's head while the cost of reaching them grew.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.072.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Severn and the Sea`,text:`At the Severn, the company finally forced Twrch Trwyth into the water. Mabon took the razor, and Kyledyr seized the shears. The boar recovered his footing before they could obtain the comb, and raced into Cornwall.

There the pursuit became harder still. At last the remaining tool was secured. Twrch Trwyth was driven out into the open sea, and nobody knew where he went afterward. The task could be crossed from Culhwch's list; the losses could not.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.101`,candidateId:`AS-101`,definitionId:5,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`8bc10d48da77fc39ea8ba10d92bfb33ec58e0dd1e145a78aa30f7ceea3782688`,variantSha256:`fd3c1c502643e042299ce0eef4ce1f572becc7f5e64c112589fc2a249da480ca`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.101.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Sleeper in the Reeds`,text:`Rostam had taken the shorter road toward the captive king, a road his father warned would ask much of him. Rakhsh carried him swiftly enough to shorten the days of travel. But even a mighty rider had to eat and sleep, and evening found them beside a patch of reeds. Rostam prepared his meal, removed the saddle and lay down. The horse remained awake.

The reeds were already home to a lion. Returning to its lair, it discovered the traveler and the horse. To the lion, the standing animal was the first obstacle; once that obstacle was gone, the sleeper would be easy prey. It approached Rakhsh rather than Rostam.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.101.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Horse Acts Alone`,text:`Rakhsh did not wait for the man to rise. The horse met the attack with the strength that had made him Rostam’s chosen companion. What followed was brief and fierce. When the danger ended, the lion was dead and the sleeping traveler was still alive. The noise woke Rostam, who saw enough to understand what his horse had done.

His first words were a reprimand. Why had Rakhsh fought alone instead of waking him? If the horse had fallen, who could have carried such a heavy warrior onward to the king? Rostam spoke as a commander anxious about his journey, while the animal who had protected that journey stood listening. The hero returned to sleep; the horse was left downcast.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.101.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Rescue Before the Rescue`,text:`At dawn they continued together. The rescue was not finished, and more dangers waited beyond the reeds. Yet its first success belonged to Rakhsh. Before Rostam could become the king’s deliverer, his own companion had delivered him. The road required a horse whose courage was more than obedience, even when the rider failed to greet that courage kindly.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.001`,candidateId:`EU-001`,definitionId:6,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ae8e2d365c42dbe1efbdb22a3bdc150b7587bf3fb3522cc3186815ce80ac238b`,variantSha256:`d62e5f2528c133ff85299d508551340f09eb94f631268724790879fce2abfb87`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.001.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Fire on the Hostess’s Terms`,text:`The visitor at Baba Yaga's gate had come asking for fire. The forest host answered with a bargain: work first, light afterward. Failure carried a terrible threat. Vasilisa followed her inside, where an enormous supper had to be set before the old woman and only a few scraps remained for the guest.

Baba Yaga's house ran according to its own timetable. A white rider brought day, a red rider the sun, and a black rider night. The hostess traveled in a mortar, driving it with a pestle and sweeping away her traces with a broom. Nothing about her movement suggested an ordinary walk home.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.001.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Tasks and Carefully Chosen Questions`,text:`Before sleeping, she listed the next day's work: a clean courtyard and room, washing, dinner, and carefully sorted oats. Vasilisa's hidden doll finished the impossible share while its owner rested and then prepared the meal. Baba Yaga returned expecting faults. Finding none, she called her servants. Three pairs of disembodied hands carried away the grain.

The next day brought another sorting demand. Again the girl and her helper completed it, and again the strange hands appeared. At supper Vasilisa asked about the riders. Baba Yaga identified them, then pressed her to ask more. The girl left the hands unmentioned. She had heard the warning that too much curiosity could make a guest unwelcome.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.001.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Blessed Visitor Leaves`,text:`Finally Baba Yaga asked how all the labor had been accomplished. Vasilisa credited her mother's blessing. The answer ended the visit immediately: the hostess would not keep a blessed daughter in her house. She pushed Vasilisa outside and supplied the requested fire in a burning skull.

She had tested and threatened the traveler, yet she kept her part of the bargain. The light she gave was dangerous rather than comforting. Her tale leaves that contradiction intact: a formidable elder whose hospitality has rules, whose gifts carry consequences, and whose house cannot be understood by ordinary household manners.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.078`,candidateId:`AS-078`,definitionId:7,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d1ea59cea3b96d5285e27f8733ac333b4d3751714ad30e331668a8e8c6ed450c`,variantSha256:`be437f0316eb1227411eb0f68696ad15577b5d684980d86dd3f90ce4736177b5`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/sumerian.json`,packetSha256:`3b5c8afbe0a208195e2916a95bd5972f38f3c66f2ce26bdd1866d27064c30ccf`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.078.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Care at the Nest`,text:`Far from his companions, Lugalbanda wandered through the mountain country. Above him stood a great tree with an extraordinary nest. Its young bird was alone while its parents searched for food. Lugalbanda approached with care, thinking how he might honour the family rather than frighten it.

He brought the nestling sweet cakes and prepared meat. He tended its eyes, arranged fragrant cedar, and made the nest a welcoming place. Then he withdrew. He would not claim the nest or wait there as though its owners owed him a greeting.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.078.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Parents Return`,text:`When Anzud returned with its mate, its call rolled through the mountains. The chick did not answer as expected. Fear overtook the parents: a silence where they had anticipated a familiar cry could mean that someone had harmed their young.

But the nest held a different discovery. Their chick was safe, well fed and beautifully tended. Anzud called out to whoever had done this kindness, promising that such care would be remembered. Lugalbanda emerged and greeted the great bird respectfully.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.078.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Gift of Movement`,text:`Anzud offered him a powerful destiny. He might gain prosperity, unfailing weapons, or strength for battle. Lugalbanda declined each offer. He asked instead for arms and legs that would not tire, so he could travel where he needed to go and return to the people he had left behind.

Anzud granted the request and accompanied him toward his companions. The bird travelled through the sky while Lugalbanda moved swiftly below. Before parting, Anzud warned him to keep the gift private.

The encounter began with a dependent creature, far from the battles and possessions that ambitious travellers might seek. By attending to the chick, Lugalbanda found a way across the mountains—and a relationship he had never set out to demand.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.004`,candidateId:`EU-004`,definitionId:8,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`9435e29094efb63b59515581dd6c58fc8934311aa6cada9d06bfef47980186ce`,variantSha256:`7fcf536698ed0cad14c6350331cd6028604d672b448027f58eaa427ca130a6c0`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.004.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Road’s Price and a Guide`,text:`The road sign did not conceal its price. One direction promised survival for the rider and death for his horse. Ivan chose that road, and a grey wolf carried out its warning. Left on foot, the prince had every reason to fear the animal. Instead, the wolf offered its own back and a speed no horse could match.

Its help was practical. It knew where the Bird of Light was kept and how to reach it. Take the bird, leave the golden cage: a clear instruction, undone by Ivan's wish to bring home something finer. When the guards seized him, the wolf did not disappear. It heard the new demand, reproached him, and carried him toward the golden-maned horse.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.004.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Broken Advice and Borrowed Shapes`,text:`Again it warned him to leave a tempting object alone, this time a golden bridle. Again Ivan disobeyed. The resulting bargain required Princess Elena, whom the wolf seized from her garden and carried away. When Ivan loved her and grieved at surrendering her, the helper supplied a more difficult kind of rescue: it took her appearance and let itself be exchanged. Later it became the horse as well, so Ivan could keep the real horse and obtain the bird.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.004.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Loyalty Beyond the Farewell`,text:`At the place where the first horse had died, the wolf finally parted from him. Their account seemed settled. But Ivan's brothers attacked the sleeping traveler, and loyalty drew the wolf back into a danger beyond any royal bargain. It compelled a crow to fetch the waters of death and life, then restored Ivan and bore him swiftly home.

The wolf had never become a gentle pet. It harms, threatens, speaks sharply, changes shape, and knows hidden roads. Its loyalty matters because those powers remain frightening. Ivan reaches his destination through a companion he cannot mistake for a tame possession.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.007`,candidateId:`EU-007`,definitionId:9,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`8f47356baffde4899c9cf16b502698309cea76f61b825a66088cf0fcdeae2e40`,variantSha256:`4bbc15d876d90fb8d6eccba2b2ecfdfaad92d7347ac5258e65dcfe243692578f`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.007.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Hunger at Easter`,text:`By Easter morning, the peasant's hunger had become the measure of everything around him. Other households were preparing to end the fast with food. He had no bread to put on his table. He had worked without finding prosperity, and his attempts to pray his way out of poverty had left him hungry still. At last he took a small can to the well. Water, at least, could fill the vessel and give him something to swallow.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.007.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Fish Offers Another Future`,text:`Instead, a large pike dropped into the can. For a moment the man's problem seemed solved in the most ordinary way: he could cook a fish. He imagined the meal before him, something warm where there had been nothing.

Then the pike spoke in a human voice. It asked to be returned to the water and promised a different answer to his need. If he released it, a spoken request invoking the pike and God would bring what he desired. The creature's bargain placed two futures in the small vessel: one supper from a dead fish, or an uncertain power from a living one.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.007.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Release and a First Meal`,text:`The peasant let it go. The water that he had gone to fetch now held the fish again, and he returned to his hut without the supper he had expected.

Back home, he sat before the empty table and tried the promise. He asked for dinner. Food and drink appeared, abundant enough for a royal household, though the room belonged to a poor man. He gave thanks and ate. Hunger no longer filled every thought. There was food where he had expected only water, and the astonishing meal left him able to rise from the table and go to church.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.008`,candidateId:`EU-008`,definitionId:10,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`62d624f76b873710d79fc15b5c64e677480a101e2325404b937457d6f5ba5202`,variantSha256:`7e7f568fcdd0341d6408d8f69e6586e565eb978163dc4f745bef0d24bcd7bbac`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.008.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`An Unusual Fair Gift`,text:`The youngest daughter knew what she wanted from the fair: a silver saucer and a crystal apple. Her sisters asked for cloth and mocked the strange request. They treated her as foolish, though she had once shared bread with an old woman and received knowledge they did not possess.

When her father brought the gifts home, she placed the apple on the saucer and set it rolling. The silver grew transparent. Cities, fields, ships, mountains and the changing sky appeared within the little dish. A household corner opened onto distances no one there could travel in a day.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.008.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Jealousy and the Reed’s Testimony`,text:`Her sisters wanted the marvel for themselves. They led her into the woods under the pretense of gathering berries, killed her, and concealed what they had done. The object remained locked away by a grieving father. Its beautiful visions could not tell him where his daughter had gone.

A shepherd looking for a lamb found flowers near a birch and cut a reed for a pipe. The pipe spoke with the lost girl's story. The villagers followed its testimony to the place where she lay, and her father learned that water from the tsar's well could restore her. He went to ask for it, returned, and brought his daughter back to life.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.008.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Vision and a Request for Mercy`,text:`At court she rolled the apple again. The ruler saw his cities and soldiers, the sea and ships, then the movements of sun and stars. He also saw the sisters brought before him for judgment. The daughter asked him to spare them, offering the very object that had provoked their crime.

The tale closes with marriage and a restored household. Its wonder is therefore more than a viewing device. The saucer enlarges what people can see, while the girl's choices determine what they do with that vision. Her request for mercy remained her own choice after others had tried to take everything from her.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.099`,candidateId:`AS-099`,definitionId:11,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`a15db316d2cb5edb5ce69afc7afae14c5f681a7b3c433b257dd7755140daa34b`,variantSha256:`325bd31d26de074ba414a6530af78ff3a6e33f6101b5ca45b6f7ea9906ea3899`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.099.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Home He Knows`,text:`From the mountain nest, Zal could see the people gathering below, but they were not the family he knew. He had grown beneath Simurgh’s wings, among her young, cared for by the extraordinary bird who had taken him in when his father abandoned him. The nest was his home. The figures at the foot of the mountain belonged to a world he had never entered.

Simurgh understood why they had come. Sam, the father who had rejected Zal for his white hair, now regretted his deed and sought his son. The bird told Zal that a life among his own people awaited him. He heard the offer as a loss. Had she tired of him? Was he no longer welcome beside her young?`},{id:`vision.definition.as.099.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`One Feather and a Promise`,text:`She reassured him that affection had not ended. She was letting him go because another future was possible for him. Then she gave him a feather from her breast. Its importance was not its size or splendor but the promise attached to it: in a moment of need, he could summon her, and she would come to help. The story makes fire the means of that summons, a marvel belonging to this tale.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.099.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Bond Across the Distance`,text:`With the feather, departure became something other than separation. Zal would travel beyond the mountain, learn the ways of courts and face dangers Simurgh could not stand beside him to meet every day. Yet he would carry a way to call his first protector.

She bore him down to Sam, who asked for forgiveness and welcomed him before the assembled people. Robes, music and honor marked the return. The feather was smaller than any of those public signs. It held the private assurance that the nest had not cast him out. As Simurgh rose again toward the heights, her gift stayed with the son she had raised.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.009`,candidateId:`EU-009`,definitionId:12,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`1bbd8704bbf26aa78db7c5c920f834fdc00dc1e2438fec26fe1f47a732ed7895`,variantSha256:`d62e5f2528c133ff85299d508551340f09eb94f631268724790879fce2abfb87`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.009.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Small Inheritance Through Hard Years`,text:`The doll was small enough to stay hidden, but its first importance came from the person who gave it. Vasilisa's mother, dying when her daughter was eight, left it with her blessing. She told the child to keep it close, give it food, and ask its advice when trouble came.

Trouble arrived in the familiar rooms of home. A new stepmother and two stepsisters burdened Vasilisa with work and treated her badly. At night she shared the best morsels she could save with the doll, told it what had happened, and received comfort. By morning, tasks that would have exhausted her were complete. Years passed; the protected child grew into a young woman.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.009.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Helper in the Forest House`,text:`Then the household sent her into the forest for fire. Before leaving, she fed the doll and explained her fear of Baba Yaga. Its eyes brightened. It told her to take it along, and she put it in her pocket.

Inside the forest house, the same quiet exchange continued. The hostess demanded cleaning, washing, cooking and grain sorting under threat. Vasilisa fed the doll from her scant supper. It urged her to rest. When she woke, the impossible sorting was finished, leaving her to prepare dinner and face the hostess herself. The helper did not replace her judgment when she chose which questions to ask.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.009.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Cloth and Lasting Care`,text:`After the dangerous visit, Vasilisa found shelter with an older woman in town. She spun exceptionally fine flax, but nobody would undertake the weaving. The doll asked for an old comb, spindle, shuttle and horse mane, and supplied the means to complete the cloth. Vasilisa then sewed the shirts that carried her work to court.

Even at the tale's happy ending, the doll remained in her pocket. Food, counsel, craft and remembered care had carried the small companion from a grieving child's room into the grown woman's new household.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.010`,candidateId:`EU-010`,definitionId:13,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`dc754656cc202a5f9a736313c2525039591e1947760e1ec180668c252833fa21`,variantSha256:`7b2b320e2d4739ccfc2e81558f4615c88cfd9908d90a8a95df4b3686e727007a`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.010.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`An Unknown Debt Comes Due`,text:`The king's bargain sounded manageable because he did not know its price. Stranded with a marvelous coffer open and a whole herd scattered around him, he accepted help from a man who emerged from the water. In exchange he promised whatever awaited at home without his knowledge. That unknown thing was a newly born son.

Years later the debt came due. Ivan was left at the seashore and made his way toward the underwater kingdom. He received guidance from Baba Yaga and met the Sea Tsar's daughter, Vasilisa the Wise. With her help he found the path below the sea, where fields and sunlight existed much as they did above.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.010.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Tasks and Three Recognitions`,text:`The Sea Tsar greeted him with anger and work that could not be accomplished by ordinary hands. A rough tract had to be leveled and covered with tall rye overnight. Vasilisa summoned servants to do it. Next, thirty hayricks had to be threshed without damaging their sheaves; ants removed the grain. Finally a church of white wax had to rise before morning, and bees supplied it.

Satisfied, the ruler offered a bride from twelve daughters who looked alike. Vasilisa gave Ivan signs by which to identify her three times. The pair were betrothed, but when Ivan longed to see his parents, she warned that her father would pursue them.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.010.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Landscapes That Shelter Escape`,text:`Their escape turned the landscape into shelter. They became shepherd and lamb among meadow grass, then an old priest and church. When the Sea Tsar himself followed, horses became a lake and the fugitives a duck and drake. He recognized them and changed into an eagle, striking repeatedly. Each bird dived beneath the water before his attack could land.

At last he returned to his own kingdom. Ivan and Vasilisa reached the surface home. The Sea Tsar's authority had set the entire journey in motion, but his daughter's knowledge made it possible to leave his power.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.018`,candidateId:`EU-018`,definitionId:14,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d72bc944a37deff081d4a5772cf542aa71bc796883f345e74fb3d707423c59ca`,variantSha256:`59907f26b679753cf628a2971697265cb6f6d95a3a11a2ecb5b5a5169d1e9c76`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.018.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Place of Concealment`,text:`Minos wanted the Minotaur hidden from sight. The creature carried two kinds of life in one form: the Greek mythographic description gives him the face of a bull and the remainder of a human being. In Ovid's brief telling, the king treats that double nature as something shameful to conceal. The king's judgment belongs to the story's court, not to the creature's own voice.

Daedalus was asked to devise the place of confinement. He made a labyrinth of paths that folded back upon themselves, sending the eye and the feet in uncertain directions. A passage that appeared to lead outward could carry someone deeper in. The workmanship was so intricate that even Daedalus found his own route difficult to recover.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.018.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Inside the Labyrinth`,text:`Inside those confusing walls, the Minotaur remained. Athens sent victims to Crete, and the enclosure became part of a repeated human catastrophe. Ovid does not give the creature speeches, a private history of those years, or a reasoned account of his thoughts. The tale remembers the danger he presented and the power that had locked him away.

Theseus, son of Aegeus, eventually entered that place. His encounter ended in the Minotaur's death. But surviving the fight did not by itself provide a way back to daylight. The walls were still there, and their deceptive turns had not changed.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.018.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Beyond the Maze`,text:`Ariadne's thread supplied the route. Theseus could follow the line back through the paths that might otherwise have trapped him. He escaped from Crete with Ariadne, whose assistance had made his return possible.

Ovid carries the narrative onward to Naxos, where Theseus leaves her behind. Dionysus later finds the abandoned Ariadne. The maze has been escaped, but the story does not make every consequence disappear at its doorway.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.021`,candidateId:`EU-021`,definitionId:16,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6ea4a859f6dc736f30a725f23f1bf40faf18459a313f48a295114eac66a3a8ba`,variantSha256:`354c4717d757b33e7b7da6c9f4f7da49ba59c800b206b4e85b68c84ce715d6a6`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.021.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Promise in Crete`,text:`The labyrinth was made to defeat a visitor's memory. Once a person had passed enough turns, the way back could no longer be trusted to recognition alone. Theseus had come to Crete among those destined for the Minotaur. Whether he was chosen or volunteered was already a point on which storytellers differed.

Ariadne, daughter of Minos, offered him help. She asked in return that he take her to Athens and marry her. Theseus swore that he would. His promise mattered because her assistance would bind her future to the visitor's escape.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.021.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Ariadne’s Plan`,text:`She went to Daedalus, the maker of the labyrinth, and asked him to reveal how someone could find the outward path. His knowledge became her plan. Ariadne gave Theseus a ball of thread: something small enough to carry, but long enough to preserve a connection with the entrance.

Theseus fastened the end at the door. As he went inward, the thread followed him through the labyrinth. Every turn that confused the eye now had a line running through it. The thread did not fight for him or transform the walls; it kept the route from disappearing behind him.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.021.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Way Out`,text:`In the farthest part of the enclosure, Theseus found the Minotaur. In this telling he killed the creature with his fists. Then he followed the thread back, recovering the path that Daedalus's design had hidden. The escape depended on Ariadne's inquiry and the maker's advice as well as the fighter's strength.

That night Theseus, Ariadne and the young Athenians left Crete and reached Naxos. The Epitome says Dionysus carried Ariadne away from there. Theseus departed in grief and forgot the white sail his father had expected.

The thread had answered one difficult question: how to return through the maze. It could not settle everything that followed beyond the entrance.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.022`,candidateId:`EU-022`,definitionId:17,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`66052b2e2a2bcc6df5b0f59d6c85b870469cfb6c1fdf5341891697cb6f22d422`,variantSha256:`35ca8ef28ee334e37d94f405f3f0eab3b14063b1e62d256c8915da28ebf68222`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.022.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`An Escape Through Air`,text:`Daedalus longed to leave Crete. Minos could prevent an escape across the land or over the sea, but the maker imagined a passage beyond those controlled roads. The air remained open.

He arranged feathers in a sequence from small to large, building curved surfaces like the wings of birds. Thread held their middle portions together; wax secured the lower feathers. Beside him, Icarus played with what the work produced. He chased feathers disturbed by the breeze and pressed his thumb into the soft wax, unaware of how much his future depended on those materials.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.022.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Father’s Warning`,text:`When the wings were finished, Daedalus tried them himself. Then he prepared a second pair for his son. The new route came with limits. Too close to the water, the feathers would become heavy; too high, the sun's heat would undo the wax. Icarus must keep between them and follow his father's course.

The maker's hands trembled as he fitted the wings. He kissed the boy and went ahead, looking back to see whether his son could follow. Below, people working at fishing, herding and ploughing looked up in astonishment at the figures moving through the sky.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.022.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Cost of Flight`,text:`For a time, father and son traveled together. Then Icarus grew confident in his flight. He left the guiding path and climbed higher. Heat softened the wax. The feathers separated, and the wings that had carried him no longer held their shape.

He fell into the sea, calling for his father. Daedalus searched and called in turn, until the floating feathers told him what had happened. He recovered his son's body and buried him.

The wings had opened the boundary that Minos could guard. They had also carried a child into danger his father understood but could not finally prevent. Ovid leaves the maker grieving beside the result of his own extraordinary craft.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.024`,candidateId:`EU-024`,definitionId:18,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7e831c5abc1e0bc90a6978f60e2dc328499bb54ab8cc9b058a2ace10fb9b7d29`,variantSha256:`35b7c26e9a965a52c3557a41b2173ef0483a18123f814bee008b22cb16305ec6`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.024.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The King’s Trial`,text:`Jason's journey began with a command from Pelias: bring back the Golden Fleece. Far away in Colchis, it hung in an oak within a grove of Ares. A sleepless dragon guarded it. Reaching the distant land was only the beginning of the difficulty.

When Jason came before Aeetes, the king offered a bargain. Jason could have the fleece if he yoked two enormous bulls with bronze feet and fiery breath, then sowed dragon's teeth. The challenge concealed another danger: armed men would rise from the planted teeth.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.024.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Medea’s Knowledge`,text:`Medea understood what the visitor faced. She promised to help him, asking that he marry her and take her with him to Greece. Jason swore to do so. She gave him protection against the bulls' fire and against iron for a single day. She also told him how to turn the newly risen men against one another.

With that knowledge, Jason accomplished the tasks. His victory was not an unaided triumph of courage. Medea had supplied the means of surviving a trial designed to overwhelm him.

Aeetes nevertheless refused to hand over the fleece. Instead, he planned to destroy the Argo and its crew. The promised exchange would not carry Jason safely home.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.024.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Night Departure`,text:`Before the king could act, Medea brought Jason to the fleece at night. She put the dragon to sleep with her preparations, and in the Bibliotheca's account she took possession of the golden fleece herself. The treasure passed from the oak to the escape party through her action.

Medea and Jason returned to the ship. The Argonauts departed in darkness, carrying the prize that had drawn them across the sea. This episode ends at their departure, with Aeetes's broken bargain behind them and an uncertain voyage ahead. The fleece shines at its center, but Medea's knowledge moves the story.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.034`,candidateId:`EU-034`,definitionId:19,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7bc8ea3fbba1709c931bdf9b3e753ca73bbd2f051c0ec5e53a6c01d8d1570f20`,variantSha256:`45c30f55d5456d3d86d22a0e85879f2942b6e02c4a7b2d3b1fd4f51ba4f17d0f`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.034.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Group Across the Tiber`,text:`Peace with Porsinna had a price. Among the people Rome delivered as hostages were girls whose safety now depended on an agreement made by others. Cloelia was one of them. The enemy camp stood close to the Tiber, and the river offered a dangerous road home.

She slipped past the guards and led her companions into the water. Weapons threatened them from the bank. Cloelia kept the group moving across the current until they reached the Roman side, then brought the girls back to their families. She crossed as the leader of a group; her courage mattered because the others came through with her.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.034.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Hostage Returns Under a Promise`,text:`Porsinna demanded her return. At first he treated the escape as a breach of the settlement. Yet as he heard what she had done, anger turned into admiration. He compared her achievement with the celebrated courage of Roman men and promised that, if she was sent back, she would remain unharmed. His demand concerned Cloelia; he did not insist on recovering every girl she had led away.

Rome faced an uncomfortable choice. A brave escape had restored children to their households, but the treaty still bound the city. The Romans returned Cloelia under the king's promise. Her story therefore turns twice: first toward the far bank, then back toward the place she had escaped.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.034.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Reward Used for Others`,text:`Porsinna honored his word. He praised her and allowed her to choose some of the remaining hostages for release. She selected the youngest, whom the account describes as especially exposed to harm. Her reward became another act of protection.

After peace was restored, Rome commemorated Cloelia with an equestrian statue on the Sacred Way. Livy's ending preserves both her daring and the obligations surrounding it. The figure remembered above the street represents a girl who crossed a river for others, then used the honor she earned to bring more vulnerable people home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.035`,candidateId:`EU-035`,definitionId:20,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d2bd61c3c16fde378c3f5138a51cd1c453996a287799eec7562318c201fe34cf`,variantSha256:`31a28f9d1f257a266265f000ffb5b675394ff17a4cb238e70bb9f65e875a7258`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.035.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Command on the Flooded Bank`,text:`The river was swollen when the servants arrived with the twins. Their order was to drown the children, but the flooded banks made the usual channel difficult to reach. Water spread across the low ground where dry paths should have been.

Ovid names their mother Silvia and tells of a royal command against her sons. The servants were troubled by what they carried. One wondered aloud whether such children might belong to a god, and what grief awaited their mother. Those thoughts did not turn the men into rescuers. They left the infants in their little container upon the water.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.035.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Container and the Nurse`,text:`The current carried the burden away. Then the flood began to fall. Instead of disappearing into the river's depths, the container settled on muddy ground beneath the shade of a tree. The twins remained there, exposed and unable to save themselves. Their survival depended on an encounter that no command had arranged.

A she-wolf came toward them. Ovid describes an animal that had recently borne young. She did not seize the abandoned boys as prey. Her tail moved gently; her tongue passed over their small bodies. The children received milk from her, and the poet gives them a fearlessness worthy of their divine father.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.035.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Care Before the City`,text:`The wonder lies in that reversal. People entrusted with the babies had obeyed a lethal order. An animal outside the city's agreements and households supplied the care they needed. The poem lingers on nourishment rather than showing the boys already grown into founders or conquerors.

Ovid connects this place and encounter with the Lupercal and the name of its attendants. That explanation belongs to his account of Rome's festival calendar. Here the scene closes earlier than the familiar city's history: beneath a riverside tree, two infants live because a wolf bends close enough to tend them. There is no bronze monument in the mud, only vulnerable children, receding water, and an unexpected nurse.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.004`,candidateId:`AS-004`,definitionId:21,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`437194147b40e194f55956e1fbaa2283081e8b7105afd6774df1a1a8cf4221cf`,variantSha256:`40fc935ef472857b54e783946baa655fad2a96afa79484561cfa842bf6e24d55`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.004.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Through the Fish Gate`,text:`From his fishing boat, Urashima had looked upon an ordinary summer sea. Riding the creature he had saved, he travelled beneath it without wetting his clothes. A gate appeared far ahead, and behind it rose the long sloping roofs of a palace. The journey ended at the entrance, where a fish gatekeeper received him.

Inside, red bream, flounder, sole and cuttlefish offered a welcome as formal as any court. Urashima knew the movement of waves better than the manners of a palace. Yet the kindness of the guides let him walk without embarrassment through a world that ought to have been impossible.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.004.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Four Seasons in One Palace`,text:`The Sea King's daughter met him at the inner entrance. Her red and green garments caught glimmers of gold. She told him that she had been the turtle whose life he had rescued. His small act on the shore had reached further than he could have imagined. In her father's kingdom she offered him a home and became his bride.

Fish in trailing ceremonial garments carried dishes on coral trays. Music and dancing filled the hall. Beyond the celebration stood the palace itself, built of coral and decorated with pearls. Its richest wonder, however, lay around it.

To the east, plum and cherry trees blossomed among butterflies. To the south, full green foliage held the sound of summer insects. To the west, bright maples and chrysanthemums displayed autumn. To the north, snow covered trees and bamboo, and ice lay across a pond. Urashima could turn his gaze from one season to another without waiting for a year to move.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.004.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Farewell Threshold`,text:`For three days he delighted in the place. Then the thought of his elderly parents broke through his happiness. The palace could offer endless youth, but it could not quiet his concern for the household beyond the sea. He asked to leave, believing he could return after a brief visit.

The gate that had opened so easily became the threshold of a separation he did not yet understand. The palace remained a place of welcome, while time on his native shore had followed another course.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.044`,candidateId:`EU-044`,definitionId:22,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`1bae899558a8cb378748f238e1c3c5dabb08207bd29059150faad2b53346d6fc`,variantSha256:`3bc75ec41127234095bf1cbb0cb95690c21508978cfb70576d008affebeb4b5c`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r05-norse.json`,reportSha256:`9399338c3425cc079bcfa934e259fe499f8786dc49ea4024b5c1f92f4fdf57b4`,packetPath:`traditions/old-norse-and-icelandic.json`,packetSha256:`a02f4faf3561b8b80955b4a3658737373f0f4ef80afc37cffde3580f4c24e019`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:36:07.885Z`,independence:`Original packet authored by /root/norse_research. Coordinator independently reread the selected Anderson source passages and all card text, claims, adaptation limits and exact count briefs; not self-review.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.044.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Broken Fetters`,text:`Fenrir grew among the gods, and every day there was more wolf to fear. Tyr was the one who dared approach him with food. The others watched his strength increase and remembered the prophecies that linked him to their destruction.

They made an iron fetter and invited him to test it. Fenrir let them fasten it around him. Then he pushed against the iron, and it broke. The gods made another, stronger restraint and praised the fame he would gain by defeating it. He took the risk again. A great effort sent fragments flying, and once more he stood free.

For their third attempt the gods sought the work of dwarfs. The band they received was called Gleipnir. It was smooth and soft, deceptively slight beside the chains that had already failed.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.044.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Conditional Promise`,text:`They brought Fenrir to a rocky island and showed him the band. Their hands could not break it, they said, but surely his strength would be enough. Fenrir looked at the little restraint and saw no honor in snapping something so thin. If cunning had made it, however, accepting it might cost him everything.

The gods promised to free him if the band held. He answered with a condition: one of them must put a hand in his mouth as a pledge that the promise was honest.

For a moment no one moved. Then Tyr offered his right hand.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.044.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Unkept Pledge`,text:`Fenrir strained against Gleipnir. The band stiffened. He strained harder, and it tightened further. The gods had found what iron could not provide, and they did not keep their promise to release him. Tyr paid for that broken pledge with his hand.

The wolf remained bound to the island's stones, his rage answering a deception he had feared from the start. The gods had secured themselves against his strength. They had also shown him how little their assurances were worth.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.045`,candidateId:`EU-045`,definitionId:23,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c40b39dc18119c864f89eda92b171f739005923116d66f36762a5aedba70e110`,variantSha256:`14d23deb8276d1596cb101f3986dea7999751436c801c28f394105ae697b262d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r05-norse.json`,reportSha256:`9399338c3425cc079bcfa934e259fe499f8786dc49ea4024b5c1f92f4fdf57b4`,packetPath:`traditions/old-norse-and-icelandic.json`,packetSha256:`a02f4faf3561b8b80955b4a3658737373f0f4ef80afc37cffde3580f4c24e019`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:36:07.885Z`,independence:`Original packet authored by /root/norse_research. Coordinator independently reread the selected Anderson source passages and all card text, claims, adaptation limits and exact count briefs; not self-review.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.045.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Beyond the Fishing Ground`,text:`Far out in the sea, Jormungandr had grown until his body encircled the lands and his mouth reached his own tail. Odin had cast him into that ocean while he was young. Now the water concealed a creature whose measure belonged to the world.

Thor came to the giant Hymir's home in the guise of a young man and asked to join his fishing trip. Hymir doubted that such a small companion could endure the distance or the cold. Thor insisted, found powerful bait, and took his place in the boat.

Their oars drove them quickly beyond the usual fishing ground. Hymir wanted to stop, but Thor pressed farther outward. Even the warning that they were approaching the Midgard-serpent's waters could not turn him back.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.045.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Eyes Above the Waves`,text:`Thor lowered a strong line into the depths. Jormungandr seized the bait, and the hook caught. The serpent pulled with such force that Thor's hands struck the gunwale. Thor answered with his divine strength. His feet broke through the boat, finding a terrible footing beneath it as he hauled upward.

Then the serpent rose beside the hull.

Thor stared fiercely at Jormungandr. Jormungandr stared back and breathed venom. Between those two powers, Hymir saw the water entering his boat and feared what the next moment would bring.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.045.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Cut Line`,text:`Thor reached for his hammer. Before he could strike, Hymir cut the line.

The released serpent sank into the ocean. Thor hurled the hammer after him, but the deep closed over whatever happened below. Some said the blow killed Jormungandr; the teller of this account did not believe them. The serpent still lay in the sea.

Thor turned his anger on Hymir, sending him overboard, then waded ashore. The encounter had brought the world's encircling creature briefly into view. It had not emptied the ocean of him.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.046`,candidateId:`EU-046`,definitionId:24,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ca28acfd361b28e263a776db9047935d7235a103c28d08fa17c426d663e9364b`,variantSha256:`1424d6ac8b18701dc0b5683e19e64200956fb7c34cdbc7a849ce0294a190b2d6`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r05-norse.json`,reportSha256:`9399338c3425cc079bcfa934e259fe499f8786dc49ea4024b5c1f92f4fdf57b4`,packetPath:`traditions/old-norse-and-icelandic.json`,packetSha256:`a02f4faf3561b8b80955b4a3658737373f0f4ef80afc37cffde3580f4c24e019`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:36:07.885Z`,independence:`Original packet authored by /root/norse_research. Coordinator independently reread the selected Anderson source passages and all card text, claims, adaptation limits and exact count briefs; not self-review.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.046.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The First Nourishment`,text:`Before there were fields to graze or barns to shelter in, warmth met the ancient rime. Drops quickened into life, and the first giant, Ymir, came into being. More melting rime gave rise to a cow: Audhumla.

Ymir needed nourishment. Audhumla supplied it in four streams of milk. In that earliest place, the great living creature depended on another creature's patient abundance. There was no pasture for the cow herself. She fed by licking the salty stones beneath their covering of frost.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.046.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Three Days in the Rime`,text:`On the first day, her tongue wore away the rime until hair appeared in the stone. The frozen surface was hiding someone.

Audhumla continued to feed. On the second day, a head emerged. What had seemed only salt and ice now held the face of a person. The cow's task remained the same: she took what sustained her, and the covering diminished.

On the third day the whole figure stood free.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.046.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`An Ancestor Revealed`,text:`His name was Buri. He was powerful and fair of face, but he was not Ymir, the giant whom Audhumla's milk had nourished. Two different beginnings had shared the cow's presence: one life sustained by her, another uncovered as she fed.

Buri would have a son, Bor. Bor and Bestla would become the parents of Odin and his brothers. The line of the gods reached back through those generations to the person revealed within the rime-covered stones.

Audhumla had no need of a weapon or a command to make that opening. The earliest nourishment flowed from her, while three days of steady feeding brought a hidden ancestor into the world. Before the coming of the gods' great works, the cow had already made life possible.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.047`,candidateId:`EU-047`,definitionId:25,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`395b5115164272cbdc13959f9569cba1f4bf506481f77bbf34513d0075fd8fd3`,variantSha256:`ecea465713faff589f37968afd98e60fdb9fdb3f2d57b80b179dc08a230cdc80`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r05-norse.json`,reportSha256:`9399338c3425cc079bcfa934e259fe499f8786dc49ea4024b5c1f92f4fdf57b4`,packetPath:`traditions/old-norse-and-icelandic.json`,packetSha256:`a02f4faf3561b8b80955b4a3658737373f0f4ef80afc37cffde3580f4c24e019`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:36:07.885Z`,independence:`Original packet authored by /root/norse_research. Coordinator independently reread the selected Anderson source passages and all card text, claims, adaptation limits and exact count briefs; not self-review.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.047.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Messenger’s Road`,text:`When Balder died, grief left the gods almost unable to speak. Frigg asked whether anyone would ride to Hel, seek him there, and offer a ransom for his return. Hermod accepted the journey.

Odin's horse, Sleipnir, was brought forward. He was grey, with eight feet: the extraordinary horse born after Loki's part in the gods' bargain with a builder. Now his strength was needed for a journey no ordinary road could contain.

Hermod mounted, and Sleipnir carried him into the dark.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.047.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Beyond Hel’s Gate`,text:`For nine nights they traveled through deep valleys without seeing light. At last they reached the Gjallar bridge, shining with its covering of gold. Its guardian, Modgud, questioned the rider. Bands of the dead had crossed before him, she said, yet the bridge shook beneath him alone. He did not have the appearance of a dead man. Why had he come?

Hermod told her he sought Balder. She had seen him pass. The way onward lay downward and northward.

Sleipnir carried Hermod to Hel's gate. There the rider dismounted, tightened the girths, and climbed back into place. The grey horse gathered himself and leaped. Eight hooves cleared the barrier without touching it.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.047.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Terms of Return`,text:`Inside the hall, Hermod found Balder and stayed the night. In the morning he asked Hel to let his brother return. She set a condition: everything, living and lifeless, must weep for him. If even one thing refused, Balder would remain.

Hermod departed with gifts and the terms of that hope. Sleipnir could carry him back to the gods, as he had carried him beyond the gate. The horse had overcome the road and the barrier. Neither his speed nor his great leap could decide whether the whole world would answer Hel's demand.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.058`,candidateId:`EU-058`,definitionId:26,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`361c0b2013397b5b14653eef4ac7be716d3ef8850f8fceb21bd538fb86eb1ad6`,variantSha256:`46848811b35cfcfda94e9d048c956bb7e9145b8b1c41fa649041ecb12a7bd067`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.058.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Silent Harp on the Wall`,text:`The great battle had broken the Fomorian force, but not everything taken from the Tuatha Dé had been recovered. The Dagda went after the retreating company with Lug and Ogma. Their pursuit brought them to a banqueting hall where Bres and Elatha were gathered. On the wall hung the harp.

It was visible to anyone in the room, yet visibility did not give those around it command of its music. The Dagda had bound its melodies so that they would remain silent until his summons released them. The instrument could be carried away and hung among another company’s possessions; the bond between its music and its owner had not been transferred.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.058.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Call and Two Strains`,text:`The Dagda called to it by its names. The harp left the wall and came to him in a sudden passage across the hall. He now held what the enemy had taken, but he and his companions were still inside a place where people wanted them dead. Possession alone would not bring them safely out.

He played sorrowful music first. Those who heard it wept. Then he changed the strain, and the room that had filled with grief turned toward laughter and delight. Women and children laughed under its power. The harp could move a gathering between feelings as decisively as a warrior could move through a doorway.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.058.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Sleep Opens the Way`,text:`Last came the music of sleep. Its sound quieted the company until the hosts themselves slept. In that stillness, the three companions escaped without injury.

The recovered instrument had done more than return to its owner. It had changed the danger surrounding them. The Dagda did not need to persuade the gathering to release him; the final strain left a path through their hostility. Lug, Ogma and the Dagda could depart, carrying the music away from the wall where it had been made to wait.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.063`,candidateId:`EU-063`,definitionId:28,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`29946df7bbeeb5e536a0832e398698b263441fe705620cfdca688bb5939fb9cd`,variantSha256:`ff53850d645fbce3ecafa11448b57e0d98a7ec6871da940fda44f22cc6b78618`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.063.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Bride and Punishment`,text:`Branwen crossed the sea as a royal bride. Her marriage to Matholwch, king of Ireland, was meant to strengthen both his kingdom and the island ruled by her brother Bendigeidfran. The wedding offered the promise of friendship. Then her half-brother Evnissyen, angry that nobody had asked his consent, mutilated Matholwch's horses.

Compensation restored the appearance of peace. Branwen went to Ireland, where she welcomed visitors generously and became the mother of a son, Gwern. For a time she enjoyed honour. But resentment over the horses returned, and the king's household answered an old insult by punishing her. She was sent to work in the court kitchen and struck each day. Travel to her homeland was stopped so that the news would not escape.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.063.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Starling Message`,text:`Branwen found another messenger. Beneath the cover of a kneading trough she reared a starling. She taught it to speak and to recognise the brother she hoped would help her. When she had written her suffering in a letter, she fastened it at the root of the bird's wing and sent it over the sea.

The starling reached Bendigeidfran and settled on his shoulder. Its ruffled feathers revealed the letter. He read it, grieved, and called an expedition together.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.063.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Peace That Failed`,text:`Branwen later understood the strange shapes approaching Ireland: what looked like a floating forest was the fleet; the moving mountain beside it was her giant brother. She urged acceptance of a peace settlement, hoping to spare the country. But treachery and Evnissyen's killing of Gwern turned the meeting into catastrophe.

Only a handful returned with her to the shore of the Alaw. Looking toward both islands, she blamed herself for their destruction, although the tale had shown others choosing insult, abuse and violence. Her heart broke from grief. The bird had carried her voice beyond the kitchen; it could not carry her family safely home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.064`,candidateId:`EU-064`,definitionId:29,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`8020dfb23a982df77cf1219ea362d0fb3fbe0e1faff0ae30a0e4f693789f3067`,variantSha256:`43c85f5d59525d5dc42b1958f09baeaf63675c610c58c0ed75ec03ab44095e25`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.064.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Sister's Call`,text:`At Harlech, Bendigeidfran had watched ships arrive with a proposal of marriage and alliance. Later, at Caer Seiont, a much smaller arrival brought news that the alliance had failed: Branwen's trained starling perched on his shoulder, carrying her letter. His sister was suffering in the country where she had gone as a bride.

He gathered the people of his island and sailed with them toward Ireland. Yet no vessel could carry the king himself. He moved beside the fleet through the sea, so immense that those watching from the shore mistook the ships for a forest and him for a mountain. Branwen explained what they were seeing. Her brother had come.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.064.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Body Becomes a Bridge`,text:`The Irish force withdrew across the Linon and broke the bridge behind it. The river had another obstacle: the tale places a lodestone at its bottom that prevented ships from passing. Bendigeidfran's followers reached the bank with no crossing before them. Their king offered his own body.

He stretched across the river. Hurdles were laid upon him, making a surface over which the company could pass. His action gave the story its memorable principle of leadership: a chief could become the means by which others reached the far side.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.064.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Beyond the Crossing`,text:`Once he rose, envoys approached with terms. A new hall large enough to contain him and his host was built, and Branwen counselled peace. It might have been the crossing that led to safety. Instead, hidden treachery and Evnissyen's killing of the child Gwern broke the gathering apart.

In the fighting, Bendigeidfran protected Branwen beside his shoulder and shield. He was eventually wounded, and only seven of his men survived. His last instructions sent them home with his head, which would remain their companion on a long, marvellous journey.

The king had made a bridge where none remained. The tale does not turn that act into a guarantee that everything beyond the river would be just.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.065`,candidateId:`EU-065`,definitionId:30,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c6172b39da834269401d68c96434f5a750756d4d4090012f2c3eb33d2955ab6a`,variantSha256:`cdf6fbe50c1129e48f4032de00d311cb48dbe3584325b28682ca2a7fa19937d9`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.065.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`An Empty Country and Honest Work`,text:`Manawydan returned from Ireland with grief and no home of his own. Pryderi invited him to Dyfed, where Rhiannon received him. Their conversation became affection, and they married. With Pryderi and his wife Cigfa, they shared a country rich in hunting, honey and fish.

Then thunder sounded over the mound at Narberth. Mist shut the four companions away from one another. When it cleared, the land was empty of the people and herds they had known. The court stood deserted.

For a while they lived from hunting. When that life grew weary, Manawydan proposed earning their keep through craft. In England he made excellent saddles, shields and shoes. Success brought hostility from local craftsmen. Pryderi wanted to answer with violence; Manawydan repeatedly chose to move away instead.

Back in Dyfed, Pryderi followed missing hounds into an unfamiliar castle. Rhiannon went after him. Both vanished in its enchantment. Manawydan reassured Cigfa of his loyalty and again worked to support them. Eventually they returned to Narberth carrying wheat to sow.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.065.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Mouse and the Bargain`,text:`Three fields ripened. The first was stripped overnight, then the second. Manawydan watched the last and discovered mice cutting down the ears of grain. He caught one slower animal and declared that it would be punished as a thief.

Offers of money could not persuade him to release it. At last a visitor dressed as a bishop promised whatever terms he required. Manawydan demanded Pryderi and Rhiannon, the undoing of the spell, and protection from renewed enchantment and revenge.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.065.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Restoration Without Revenge`,text:`The visitor was Llwyd, who had enchanted Dyfed to avenge an old humiliation of his friend Gwawl. The mouse was his pregnant wife. Manawydan waited until his companions stood beside him, then released her.

The country returned, tilled and peopled. The mouse became a woman again. Manawydan had demanded more than a moment's relief: his companions were free, the fields restored, and their enemy had promised that the spell and the revenge would end.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.071`,candidateId:`EU-071`,definitionId:31,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`3c769d4574e476641af6f611d8754bc13eeb5d2d87fbc698c7d94012ccc15749`,variantSha256:`43ddd769b9334f433126d5142894052193f35b32b73df2bd7f947cf3bdd1de66`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.071.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Strange Gift of Peace`,text:`The cauldron entered the story as compensation. Matholwch had come to marry Branwen, but Evnissyen's attack on his horses had threatened the alliance. Bendigeidfran offered replacements and treasure. Seeing that the guest still lacked his former cheer, he added a stranger gift.

A man killed today, he explained, could be placed in this cauldron and emerge the next day as well as before. Only speech would be missing. Matholwch accepted it with delight.

As the two kings talked, the object's history came into view. Matholwch had once seen a huge man rise from an Irish lake carrying the vessel, followed by an even larger woman. Their household grew troublesome, and his people tried to destroy them in an iron chamber heated from outside. The man and woman broke through the burning walls and escaped. They reached Bendigeidfran, who received the cauldron from them.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.071.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A War That Would Not End`,text:`Now it travelled back to Ireland with a marriage that seemed repaired. Yet the insult was remembered, Branwen was mistreated, and her message brought her brother's expedition across the sea.

A meeting intended to settle the quarrel became a battle after Evnissyen killed Branwen's son. In the fighting, the cauldron was put over a fire. Slain Irish warriors were returned through it, ready to fight again but unable to speak. The vessel that had helped purchase peace now helped sustain war.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.071.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Broken Vessel`,text:`Evnissyen saw the unequal losses and recognised his part in causing the disaster. He concealed himself among the dead and was placed in the cauldron. From inside, he stretched until it broke into four pieces. The effort killed him.

Destroying it gave the other side such relief as was possible; the tale still says they were not victorious. Seven men escaped. The broken vessel left no simple miracle behind, only the knowledge that restored fighting strength had never meant restored peace.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.081`,candidateId:`EU-081`,definitionId:32,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`b885736e3c2cace89f93a41002caad53c48b9bca1015cf9f546a82f0cd305617`,variantSha256:`e3ac8ec0746c1c5c1a1a25007646fc4ff3243cdae417d50fd8f5aa15354a6d6d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.081.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Forge in Pohjola`,text:`Ilmarinen came to Pohjola because Väinämöinen had sent him there by a trick. Louhi welcomed the smith with food and comfort, then asked whether he could forge the Sampo. She named extraordinary ingredients and promised her daughter in return for the work.

The smith found no ready workshop. He searched until he discovered a suitable rock, then made a place for his furnace and bellows. His assistants tended the heat while he watched what the metals would become.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.081.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Four Unsuitable Creations`,text:`The first creation was a magnificent bow. It was strong and gleaming, but demanded victims. Ilmarinen broke it and returned its material to the fire. Next came a beautiful boat that sought quarrels. It too went back into the furnace.

A splendid cow followed, but it wasted its milk as it rushed through the countryside. A plough came next, working where it should not and spoiling fields. Beauty and movement were not enough; each creation failed the purpose the smith sought.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.081.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Sampo Stays Behind`,text:`At last the winds worked the bellows, and the Sampo rose. Ilmarinen hammered it into completion. Beneath its many-coloured lid, one side made flour, another salt and another money. Its production could feed people, supply the market and fill a storehouse.

Louhi carried the wonder into a copper-bearing mountain. Nine locks secured it, while three great roots extended beneath mountain, sea-bed and dwelling.

The smith had completed the task, but the promised daughter would not leave her home and freedom. Louhi gave him provisions and a vessel for his return. He travelled back with knowledge of what he had made, while the Sampo remained in Pohjola. The completed marvel and the unfulfilled marriage promise were different outcomes of the same journey.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.084`,candidateId:`EU-084`,definitionId:33,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4de1033c1d8f4dea55451c44aafbf2507b4d216009839394203a4fe08305112a`,variantSha256:`5bd9d3f6a45f795d02f558f70fd2ab9f003aa014b2d044b2ef973d8eef6963c2`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.084.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Silent Smithy`,text:`After his wife’s death, Ilmarinen’s smithy fell silent. For three months he did not work at the anvil. Morning and evening offered little relief, and the empty place beside him kept returning his thoughts to the companion he had lost.

At last he gathered gold, silver and fuel. If his craft could make extraordinary things, perhaps it could also answer the absence in his home. He set his assistants to the bellows and began seeking a woman-shaped image from the furnace.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.084.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Likeness Without Life`,text:`The first result was a beautiful lamb made of shining metals. Others admired it, but Ilmarinen wanted something different and returned it to the fire. The next was a splendid colt. Again he rejected the form and continued.

Then a figure appeared with golden hair and a silver head. He worked on it night and day. He made feet, hands and arms, then ears, mouth and bright eyes. Its outward form became more complete with every addition.

But the feet would not walk. The hands and arms could not embrace. The ears heard nothing; the eyes did not see; the mouth remained silent. No amount of careful shaping supplied the life he had hoped to recover.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.084.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Furnace’s Limit`,text:`Even surrounded by warm coverings, the metal image stayed cold. Ilmarinen took it to Väinämöinen, proposing that it might comfort the older singer. Väinämöinen refused. He would not choose an image of precious metal as a living companion.

Ilmarinen had demonstrated the reach of his craft and found its boundary at the same time. The figure could resemble someone in every visible part, yet resemblance could not answer him. His loss had not become a problem the furnace could solve.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.096`,candidateId:`EU-096`,definitionId:34,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f2c04fb98f9c548b7058a7f2e15e4b4cfca7609ae7f56caa9acef0094e1714bf`,variantSha256:`a23d8c8ce210926d80b3a41cb71441d4f7fca6a56f5fe86fd83f5a45bb1da015`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/lithuanian-and-latvian-baltic.json`,packetSha256:`78481592db3b2917bd7f0c752eaaf9ca0b09332f74ee570ee9d5d7fad057e283`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.096.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Hidden Inheritance`,text:`Two brothers had divided an inheritance, but the division was unfair. The elder took wealth for himself and left his younger brother poor. Both had homes and wives. When the wealthy man became gravely ill, he worried less about the brother he had wronged than about where his money would go.

He carried a cauldron to the barn and buried it in a hole. Then he began bringing coins to fill it. His younger brother noticed him moving about despite his weakness, followed him, and hid behind the straw. From that quiet place he watched the money disappear into the pot.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.096.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Two Claims on One Pot`,text:`The younger man dropped his rosary into the cauldron. When the elder returned and tried to assign both treasure and vessel to the velnias, a reply came from behind the straw: the vessel could belong to that power, but the money belonged to the speaker. The elder looked around without finding anyone. The exchange happened again, and he finally left.

Later, the younger brother lay at home beside his wife. Something like wind circled the window, insisting that he take the coins and surrender the cauldron. Fear kept him indoors. The demand returned, louder and more urgent.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.096.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Coins and a Death`,text:`Then the window rattled and broke. Coins fell into the room; the pot was carried away. A cry announced that the elder brother was dying. The younger hurried to him and arrived too late.

The teller understands the departure of the cauldron as the carrying away of the dead brother’s soul. For the survivor, possession of the coins could not undo the night’s loss. An inheritance dispute had crossed the wall of the barn and entered both homes.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.100`,candidateId:`EU-100`,definitionId:35,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0077200ffbfe532887d17c275dcf69050d5b352779ab05f06f698a9d2411eaad`,variantSha256:`101d5f040884c096010e7013e40a82eaa5c303c90099dc580fa2c1acd4280309`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.100.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A House of Dangerous Hospitality`,text:`The house beside the stream did not look like the end of a road. Yet travellers who followed the wrong trail could find themselves there, facing a host whose hundred heads made ordinary courage seem very small.

Three brothers had already come looking for their missing sister. The devi's mother hid them, then persuaded her son to receive them as his wife's relatives. He agreed, fed them, and offered them choices: meat or bones, a large wine vessel or a drinking horn, a bed or the stable. The brothers answered timidly. By morning his promise of hospitality had become a trap, and they disappeared into his keeping.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.100.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Unexpected Guest`,text:`Later another brother arrived. This was Asphurtzela, born after the family's loss and grown with astonishing speed. He crossed the stream in a leap and stood at the door. When the devi returned with game and uprooted wood, he found someone who would neither hide nor lower his voice.

The newcomer claimed kinship and asked to be received as a guest. At supper he worked faster than the host. He chose meat, the larger vessel, and a bed. Every choice contradicted the devi's expectation that fear would do his work for him.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.100.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Log and the Hidden Brothers`,text:`During the night the giant sharpened a sword. But the sound warned Asphurtzela, who slipped away and left a log beneath the bedcover. The blow that followed split wood rather than a sleeping guest.

Morning brought a second astonishment: the boy was alive. In their wrestling contest the devi found that strength could fail him too. Forced to reveal where the brothers were hidden, he disclosed a coffer and a handkerchief that could restore them. Asphurtzela brought his brothers back to life. The tale ends this encounter with the devi's death, but the decisive change had come earlier, at a threshold where an expected victim insisted on being heard.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.101`,candidateId:`EU-101`,definitionId:36,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4999ce21594d45d79f582c1fc146391aa25cb0acb1b15660174a8ce1ab30dcf6`,variantSha256:`f388bdca3641089da24c09a67d1678a76451ea248baa4192f126f9c8e13805af`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.101.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Nestlings Speak`,text:`Ghvthisavari travelled toward a tree so tall that its crown seemed to belong to the sky. He had been sent to fetch the flesh of a phascundzi's young. He believed the errand would help his mother, whose illness had filled the house with urgency. He did not yet understand that someone had chosen an errand meant to kill him.

The nest held small birds looking out from the branch. Before their mother returned, a huge strange bird swept toward them. Ghvthisavari raised his bow and stopped the attacker. When the phascundzi arrived, she saw an armed stranger below her young and moved against him.

The fledglings spoke first. This was the person who had protected them. Their testimony changed the meeting: suspicion gave way to gratitude, and the mother asked what had brought him so far.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.101.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Feather and a New Danger`,text:`He explained the demand for a young bird. She told him that his mother was deceiving him. Nevertheless, she offered to let him take a fledgling alive, and drew out a feather as a pledge. If he needed help, he was to remember her.

The feather mattered because the danger did not end with his return. Ghvthisavari was shut inside a tub and cast into the sea. His dogs gathered the friends he had met on those dangerous errands: a stag, a boar, and the phascundzi.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.101.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Three Friends Divide the Sea`,text:`They made a plan together. The bird rose above the sea and beat her wings until the water divided. The stag lifted the exposed tub to shore with its horns. The boar broke it open while warning the trapped youth to keep low. Ghvthisavari stepped out alive.

The nest's small voices had preserved their protector once; their mother's great wings preserved him again. Neither rescue belonged to a solitary hero. It passed between animals and a human who, at a crucial moment, had chosen to defend the lives he had been sent to take.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.103`,candidateId:`EU-103`,definitionId:37,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`96766dd5cdecfbbe244bc7e80c84757dc07efb057da7156bfb8ddcffeae7b9bc`,variantSha256:`251a60c0ae98beac98a6fad1d2b5c6f19112526ccaa3f01562c6f15216c12b4b`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.103.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Kindness in the Fields`,text:`Conkiajgharuna carried her stepmother's demands into the fields each morning. She was to eat from a poor loaf, share it with strangers, and still return with it whole. Later she was given wool to finish spinning before evening. The work was designed to wear her down.

The cow she watched became her helper. Its horns supplied honey and butter, giving the girl nourishment where the household withheld it. On another day, following the cow onto a roof, she dropped her spindle and found an old woman below. The woman asked for help, and Conkiajgharuna answered her gently. In return she learned which of three springs to use. At the yellow spring her hair and hands began to shine like gold.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.103.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Bones Remembered, Chores Answered`,text:`Her good fortune angered her stepmother. Eventually the cow was killed. Before its death, it had told the girl to bury its bones and remember its grave when trouble came. Conkiajgharuna kept that promise.

On a holiday the others went to church, leaving millet scattered through the courtyard and a trough to be filled with tears. A neighbour found the girl crying. She brought hens and chicks to gather the grain, then made salt water stand for the impossible tears. Someone had finally treated the demand as a problem to solve, rather than a punishment to endure.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.103.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Slipper and the Basket`,text:`Conkiajgharuna went to the cow's grave. A horse and splendid garments appeared when she called for them. She rode to church, where people marvelled without recognising her. Hurrying home across a stream, she lost a golden slipper in the water.

The king's search eventually reached her house, where her stepmother hid her beneath a basket. From underneath, Conkiajgharuna used a needle to make her presence impossible to ignore. The basket was lifted. She named the slipper as hers, and it fitted.

Her tale ends in marriage, but its path is marked by other forms of help: an animal's care, a neighbour's practical kindness, and the girl's own refusal to remain hidden.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.106`,candidateId:`EU-106`,definitionId:38,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`8a3178b34f27110be5b1f5049fa2a8393e7ff5e0127eab8469c1740c13a33b4b`,variantSha256:`a270d652a985102536ff3e46aafcb102dc5eba4f3cdef1d4f3e9726495861e5f`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.106.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Failed Escape and a New Horse`,text:`The prince had found his wife in Kazha-Ndii's house once already. He had lifted her onto a horse and ridden for the gate, hoping speed would carry them beyond the captor's reach. Instead, the gate cried an alarm and the pursuing horse gave a neigh that stopped their escape.

The attempt ended in disaster. A little dog brought the prince back to the three demis who were married to his sisters. The youngest demi restored him to life. When the prince prepared to try again, the same helper brought out a different horse: a horse with three legs.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.106.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Gate and the Missing Fourth Leg`,text:`No one in the tale pretended it had four. Its body was particular, and so was the help it could offer. The prince mounted and returned to the imprisoned princess. Together they passed toward the gate, which sounded an even louder warning than before.

Kazha-Ndii came after them. His horse neighed, and the three-legged horse began to slow. The prince asked why. The horse answered him, speaking directly about the missing fourth leg and the difficulty of winning this contest.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.106.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`An Answering Neigh`,text:`Then the pursuit came close enough for the helper's own power to matter. The three-legged horse gave a great neigh. This time it was Kazha-Ndii's horse that stopped.

The reversal opened a moment the prince had never had on his first journey. He faced the captor and killed him with his sword. The prince placed the princess on the horse again, and they left together.

They returned first to the demis and then home. The escape had required many kinds of assistance: sisters who welcomed a brother, a dog that would not abandon him, a demi who restored him, and a three-legged horse whose voice changed the direction of a chase. The marvel was not a concealed extra limb. It was the answer the horse gave with the body it actually had.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.114`,candidateId:`AS-114`,definitionId:40,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`26506b9c44aa0830df4ba97be15e09a25ab8094c102e42c1cd1c8b05e28a250b`,variantSha256:`1a2465f77e0fb04ec55c83b3f14d8f0474835cb720ea31b72c8f3c8c84d837ff`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r21-anatolian.json`,reportSha256:`a052453e50deb8318cd4a5daf1148acbadc9993054c39fcea368100fad7beba3`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-anatolian.json`,packetSha256:`173670a1fbc6eb274364fda5ac2f078ae4c6d1fc393e266c64e75f65c3dcdb56`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:23:40.400Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read assigned actual prose/prompts and primary translations; added two actually read direct witnesses, not a fabricated third.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.114.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Search That Has Failed`,text:`The gods had searched, and Telipinu was still missing. His absence reached into fields and households: growth failed, hunger persisted, and even a divine feast could not satisfy those who ate there. The search had become more urgent than the feast.

The Sun God sent an eagle across high mountains, deep valleys, and quiet waters. It returned without finding the missing god. Great gods and lesser gods had also looked. Their numbers had not supplied the answer.

The Storm God turned to Hannahanna. He feared that everyone would starve. She urged him to act and search for his son himself. He went, but the passage brings him to a city gate he could not open. His hammer and peg broke in the effort. After a damaged stretch of text, he wrapped himself in his garment and sat down.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.114.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Smaller Messenger Chosen`,text:`Hannahanna chose another searcher: a bee. She sent it to look for Telipinu. The Storm God objected. If the greater and lesser gods had failed, why expect this little creature to succeed? Its wings were small; its whole body was small. He measured the proposed messenger against the impressive search that had already accomplished nothing.

The surviving words do not give Hannahanna a long speech in reply. Her decision is present in the dispatch itself. A different searcher would have a chance. The bee did not need to become an enormous animal or take a human body for the contrast to matter.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.114.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`At the Surviving Tablet Break`,text:`At this point the selected first version is damaged. It does not preserve, in the passage read here, an account of the bee locating or waking Telipinu. The story therefore pauses at its genuine surviving turn: a small messenger is entrusted with work that the powerful have not completed.

Its importance comes from that commission, rather than a victory added from another version. The bee carries the search forward into the break in the tablet, while the Storm God's doubts remain behind it.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.116`,candidateId:`EU-116`,definitionId:41,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`3b97595b0bbebfab93e1c61ff8194c008615f416f1247fa570353f8a03933204`,variantSha256:`777f86cc5e8e22ac7eb0968b9000c71995b3d85c3929af291bf85fb44fdea366`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r20-armenian.json`,reportSha256:`1f94ec3b88574c170face0ecbc8905626cd25c44c6a1552a2cab49856605436c`,packetPath:`traditions/armenian.json`,packetSha256:`b546842bb40d23bb25872cb0c571f75de78174cf72a0d70dc1d1dce375fd4f07`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:17:08.056Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently compared actual stories/prompts to selected relevant witnesses.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.116.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Father’s Silent Cloak`,text:`The sister called for her father, and the mountains sent her own voice back. He had left his cloak standing over a stick and told his children to wait while he looked for water. After a long wait, she and her brother searched among the trees and rocks. No one answered them. They returned to the cloak, but their father was gone.

Between them they gathered the cloak and the stick. Her brother followed her out of the wood, growing thirstier with every step. Water shone in a horse’s hoofprint, but she warned him that drinking there would turn him into a colt. At another track she stopped him again: that water would make him a calf. They passed the tracks of a bear, a pig and a wolf, each small pool offering relief with a different danger hidden in it.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.116.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Brother Changed`,text:`At last they came to a lamb’s track. The boy could bear his thirst no longer. Before his sister could prevent it, he bent down and drank.

Where her brother had stood, a lamb looked up at her.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.116.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Refuge Above the House`,text:`She did not abandon him. She led him back toward the house they knew, hoping that home might still offer shelter. But their stepmother saw meat where the sister saw her brother. The danger that had driven them into the forest had followed them home.

The sister took the lamb away again, this time toward the mountains. They climbed until the house and its threats lay below them. On a high rock she settled with her spinning, while the lamb found grass nearby.

Nothing had restored his human voice or changed him back into a boy. Yet when he grazed beside the rock, he was still the brother she had led through the forest. She kept him close, turning wool into thread as the mountain gave them room to stay together.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.117`,candidateId:`EU-117`,definitionId:42,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`42f7ec5c4f811479bd93cae3ff1ce21a51a78b738802b6c4b0f4778a8cbcff0d`,variantSha256:`2bb0a272acbc261e101f51ce59e211f331cfd055305c7f68064a413580f57ff5`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r20-armenian.json`,reportSha256:`1f94ec3b88574c170face0ecbc8905626cd25c44c6a1552a2cab49856605436c`,packetPath:`traditions/armenian.json`,packetSha256:`b546842bb40d23bb25872cb0c571f75de78174cf72a0d70dc1d1dce375fd4f07`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:17:08.056Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently compared actual stories/prompts to selected relevant witnesses.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.117.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Stolen Singer and the Betrayal`,text:`The nightingale belonged to the Fairy Queen. Its songs could carry her through the whole passage from waking into sleep. A young prince came into her chamber during that sleep, took the cage and left with the bird. He also kissed her forehead without asking her leave.

His escape passed through a country that remembered his earlier kindness. He had put food where a hungry wolf and lamb could reach it. He had treated a thorny grove and a bitter river with respect. When the queen called on the gates, the animals, the trees and the water to stop him, they allowed him through.

But the road home held a danger those helpers could not prevent. After the prince rescued his elder brothers, they lowered him into a well and cut the rope. They returned to their father with the cage, claiming the success for themselves.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.117.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Silence Exposes False Credit`,text:`The nightingale was hung in the new church. It did not sing.

The Fairy Queen followed the stolen bird. When the brothers could not explain the journey, their story failed. She demanded the true traveller. Meanwhile, women gathering barley found the prince in the well and brought him out; one gave him a place in her household.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.117.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Prince Returns and the Bird Sings`,text:`He finally returned to the city, where he freed his brothers and told the queen what had happened. She recognized details they had never known. The tale makes the unasked kiss part of his claim to marriage, and has the queen accept him; it does not give the bird a choice about its captivity.

At their wedding in the church, the long silence ended. The nightingale opened its beak. Song followed song until the tale counted a thousand and one.

The brothers had been able to carry its cage home. They had never been able to make it sing.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.120`,candidateId:`EU-120`,definitionId:43,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`db8ce62c21d56bb2b2ec4b89286b97061e1ec08f945a0fb13505472a2589321d`,variantSha256:`821fcc44bfb26f8e9421552d03455e4fd7b4c6482dfa68828d2aaa3517000d0c`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r20-armenian.json`,reportSha256:`1f94ec3b88574c170face0ecbc8905626cd25c44c6a1552a2cab49856605436c`,packetPath:`traditions/armenian.json`,packetSha256:`b546842bb40d23bb25872cb0c571f75de78174cf72a0d70dc1d1dce375fd4f07`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:17:08.056Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently compared actual stories/prompts to selected relevant witnesses.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.120.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Long Search`,text:`The king could no longer see. The healers of his own country had failed to help him, and a stranger arrived with a desperate promise: the blood of a golden-headed fish, he claimed, would restore the king’s sight.

The king sent his only son to find it. A hundred men went with the prince, carrying a hundred nets. Day after day they worked at the sea. The nets came up with their ordinary catches, but never with the fish the king expected. Ninety-nine days passed, and the men prepared to return without it.

On the last day, the last net brought up a fish with a golden head.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.120.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Fish Asks to Be Freed`,text:`They kept it alive in a vessel of water and carried it aboard. To the men it was the answer to their long search. To the prince, looking into the vessel in his cabin, it was a living creature whose capture had already decided its fate.

Then the fish spoke.

It told him that it was a prince, and begged to be released. If he would return it to the sea, it promised to remember his mercy. Nothing in the cabin could prove that promise. The king’s demand was much more immediate, and the young man knew what it meant to disobey his father.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.120.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`What the Prince Chooses`,text:`He had travelled so far to bring this creature back. He had watched so many empty nets. Now the prize of the whole journey was asking him for its life.

The prince carried the vessel to the sea and let the fish go.

Water closed around the golden head. The ship remained, the waiting men remained, and home still lay ahead. He would return without the supposed remedy. But he would not return carrying a creature that had spoken to him, asked for mercy, and been denied it.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.132`,candidateId:`EU-132`,definitionId:44,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6c6227b4cdbeea8d58c8b5b8417040228812eb8042845b3c40bb531b5d8b556f`,variantSha256:`803ed3cae2720cb85d03b16aff754899e4003a6be7b15908bb0aa0560fc95c0c`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r22-basque.json`,reportSha256:`59c49d8e023c50ba78dc1b1789a62fc255e8ae981f14c3576591a489dc1b5661`,packetPath:`traditions/basque.json`,packetSha256:`f18c2e140da525792c23ba62405368e3402e3b663dbe872528cceafa2076324b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:31:12.705Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read actual current card prose/prompts and specifically scoped fresh source passages.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.132.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Cave Guarded by Touch`,text:`The shepherd had gone into the mountains looking for his scattered sheep when he met someone far larger than any man he knew. One eye watched from the giant’s forehead. Tartalo offered shelter, but inside his cave the shepherd understood that the invitation was a trap.

He needed more than speed to escape. The shepherd acted while Tartalo slept and left him unable to see. The giant could no longer search by looking, so he took command of the doorway. He stood with his legs apart and let the sheep pass underneath, feeling their wool as each animal went by. Nothing would leave without passing his hands.

The shepherd found a sheepskin and bent beneath it. He followed the flock toward the opening, matching their slow movement instead of bolting. Tartalo’s hand touched the fleece on his back. The shepherd held himself still, then moved on. For a moment, the mountain air seemed to promise freedom.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.132.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Speaking Pursuit`,text:`Outside, he cast the hide aside and ran. Yet Tartalo possessed something that could follow an escape without seeing it. His ring spoke, warning him that the shepherd was getting away. The giant hurled it after the fugitive, and it fastened upon the shepherd’s little finger.

Now the ring carried the shepherd’s whereabouts back to his pursuer. Every stride that should have brought safety instead gave the giant another direction. He struggled to be free of it. The old tale gives that separation a grievous cost; its violent moment passes outside this telling.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.132.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Voice from the Water`,text:`At last the ring lay apart from him in the water. Its voice continued, careless of whose danger it announced. Tartalo followed that voice to the water and plunged after it. The mountain did not return him.

The shepherd remained above the water, free at last. The ring had obeyed its own strange nature to the end: it could announce a place, but it could not tell its owner whether that place was safe.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.133`,candidateId:`EU-133`,definitionId:45,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`1706577c50bb30bc18eda07b3419eec07b9a4e496b65cd25da846643d28b512f`,variantSha256:`cb2b6b0c34f0982c683a8ba399f645260d9d0a0a7b57f5a8b136d9badfa3a5bf`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.133.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Meadow Beyond the Well`,text:`A girl spent her days spinning beside a well. Her stepmother demanded the work and showed little care for the worker. When the shuttle slipped into the water, the girl was ordered to retrieve it. She vanished into the well and woke in a bright meadow, far from everything she knew.

Walking through the flowers, she heard bread calling from an oven. The loaves had finished baking and feared they would burn. She drew them out with the bread shovel. Farther along, an apple tree asked to be shaken: its fruit was ripe. She gathered the fallen apples before continuing on her way.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.133.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Work in Holle’s House`,text:`At a small house, an old woman looked out. Her large teeth startled the visitor, but her voice offered a kinder welcome. She was Frau Holle. The girl could stay and help with the household, and would receive good care in return. One task mattered especially: the bedding must be shaken until its feathers flew. Those flying feathers became snow on earth.

The girl agreed. She worked, ate satisfying meals, and heard no angry words. Each thorough shaking of the bed sent another flutter of white into the world below. Yet after a time, even this comfortable place could not ease her longing for home.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.133.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Door Home`,text:`She told Holle she wished to leave. Holle accepted the wish and took her to a great door. As the girl crossed the threshold, gold rained down and clung to her. Holle also returned the shuttle that had begun the journey.

Then the door closed. The girl stood near her old home, carrying what she had lost and the reward for what she had done. A rooster announced her arrival. The extraordinary household had let her go.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.134`,candidateId:`EU-134`,definitionId:46,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d4554d6aaadb7eef65def509b7e54498592079d4fdc601f81c022e71bc5d9289`,variantSha256:`ba3950179ec76a9a7d83d19a3da8b8ac9bb8c2858ba3501a63ed9f253e1cceb5`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.134.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Impossible Work and Two Payments`,text:`The miller boasted that his daughter could spin straw into gold. She could not. Nevertheless, the king shut her into a room of straw and threatened her life if the task remained unfinished at dawn. No skill she possessed could meet his demand.

A small man entered and offered to do the work. She gave him her necklace. He sat at the wheel, and the reels filled with gold. Morning brought no freedom: the delighted king demanded a larger roomful. The spinner returned, and this time she paid with her ring.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.134.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Promise Comes Due`,text:`On the third night, the king required still more and promised marriage if she succeeded. She had nothing left to offer the little man. He demanded her first child if she became queen. Trapped between an immediate threat and an uncertain future, she made the promise. Gold again filled the room, and she became the king’s wife.

A year later, the spinner came for the child. The queen offered him all the kingdom’s riches instead. He refused, but her distress moved him to allow another condition: if she could discover his name within three days, she could keep her child.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.134.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Name in the Forest`,text:`She searched every name she knew and sent a messenger across the country. Two days passed without a correct answer. Then the messenger returned from a distant woodland mountain. Outside a little house, beside a fire, he had seen a small man dancing and heard him proclaim the secret he believed nobody knew.

When the spinner returned, the queen tried two ordinary names first. Then she spoke the one the messenger had heard: Rumpelstiltskin. His triumphant confidence turned to furious disbelief. The bargain was broken. The child remained with the queen; the reels of gold had never offered her so much relief.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.136`,candidateId:`EU-136`,definitionId:47,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`5ba1fda5d938beddac84c4ccb377cab92ab765dea6cb3808c1b2624226c65544`,variantSha256:`a9bf18a3db42287b25450a1ef7ce734589e331d4cd7a7225cc34b7629409adc5`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.136.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Four Fugitives on the Road`,text:`The donkey had carried sacks to the mill for years, but his strength was fading and his master no longer wished to keep him. He left for Bremen, hoping to become a musician. On the road he found an old hound whose hunting days had ended. The hound was also fleeing a master’s threat. They decided to travel together.

Next came an aging cat, driven away from safety because she no longer caught enough mice. The donkey invited her into their company. At a farm gate they met a rooster crowing at full force: he feared he would become the next day’s meal. His voice would fit their new profession. Now there were four.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.136.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Chorus at the Window`,text:`Evening found them still far from Bremen. They settled in a forest, where the rooster climbed high into a tree and spotted a distant light. Following it, they reached a house full of robbers enjoying supper. Shelter and food stood just beyond the window.

The four devised a plan. The donkey put his forefeet on the sill. The dog climbed onto his back, the cat onto the dog, and the rooster onto the cat. Together they brayed, barked, mewed and crowed, then burst into the room. The robbers believed a terrible apparition had arrived and fled.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.136.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Home Before Bremen`,text:`After supper, each animal chose a sleeping place: donkey in the yard, dog behind the door, cat at the hearth, rooster on a roof beam. When a robber returned after midnight, their startled reactions terrified him again. He mistook ordinary animals for a witch, an armed man, a monster and a judge.

The robbers stayed away. Bremen remained somewhere beyond the forest, but the four companions had found what they most needed already. They kept the house, and stayed together.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.137`,candidateId:`EU-137`,definitionId:48,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`e04bdcad5388cc56904d1c4070ac0413ff88a3d75023d2bae3cfde9bfba4baee`,variantSha256:`e592f061faa1151e62c112d67f16ff6f5f7d6b55a645bee2b7ace11c43fe6200`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.137.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Ball in the Well`,text:`Near a king’s castle, beneath an old lime tree, lay a deep well. On a hot day the youngest princess sat beside it, tossing her golden ball and catching it again. One throw escaped her hand. The ball rolled over the grass and disappeared into water too deep for her to see its resting place.

As she wept, a frog raised his head above the surface and asked what had happened. He could retrieve the ball, he said, but jewels and fine clothes were not the payment he wanted. He asked to become her companion and share her table and daily life.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.137.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Promise at the Palace`,text:`The princess promised quickly, thinking a creature of the water could never really accompany her. The frog dived, returned with the ball in his mouth, and set it on the grass. Delighted, she picked it up and ran home. His calls followed her, but she did not turn back.

The next day, the frog arrived at the palace door. The king heard the story and insisted that her promise be honoured. At dinner the frog demanded a place beside her and food from her plate. She obeyed under her father’s pressure, though she remained frightened and unwilling.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.137.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Prince and Henry’s Heart`,text:`Their conflict did not end at the table. In anger, the princess rejected the frog, and his enchantment broke: before her stood a king’s son. He explained that a witch had trapped him in that form. They were to leave for his kingdom the following morning.

A carriage drawn by eight white horses arrived. Behind it stood faithful Henry, whose grief for his enchanted master had been bound by three iron bands around his heart. As the carriage travelled, each band cracked free. The prince feared the carriage was breaking. Henry explained that the sound was his own sorrow finally giving way.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.138`,candidateId:`EU-138`,definitionId:49,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`930785f00f815855ec0943d62351193bcda1bfc77063fc4d98d39a0237c33bfa`,variantSha256:`a248c38944a15d338979e211c5dddbc4c688f34462fb2159dd5810e5dbb862f7`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.138.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Child Taken and Confined`,text:`Behind a couple’s house stood an enchantress’s walled garden. The woman longed for its fresh rampion, and her husband stole some for her. When he returned for more, the enchantress caught him. She offered the plants on one terrible condition: the couple must give her their expected child. In fear, he agreed.

The enchantress named the child Rapunzel and took her away. When Rapunzel was twelve, she confined her in a forest tower that had neither door nor stairs. Its only opening was a small high window. At the enchantress’s call, Rapunzel unwound her long golden braids, looped them around a hook, and lowered them as the route into her room.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.138.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`An Escape Plan Discovered`,text:`A year or two passed. A prince heard Rapunzel singing through the trees and searched for a doorway. Finding none, he returned repeatedly until he saw how the enchantress entered. Later he used the same call and climbed to the window. Rapunzel was frightened by the unfamiliar visitor, but their conversations led her to seek a life outside the tower.

She asked him to bring silk on each visit. From it she would weave a ladder and descend. Before the plan was complete, she accidentally revealed his visits to the enchantress. The woman cut Rapunzel’s braids and banished her to a lonely wilderness, then used the severed hair to deceive the prince. He escaped the tower injured and without his sight.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.138.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Voice in the Wilderness`,text:`Years of wandering followed. At last he reached the place where Rapunzel lived with their twins, a boy and a girl. Her familiar voice drew him closer. They recognized one another, and her tears restored his sight. Together they travelled to his kingdom. The reunion came through the voice that had carried beyond the tower from the beginning.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.139`,candidateId:`EU-139`,definitionId:50,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`9332774972e63cbf36850d81939f699e21bb6c781e68aba9b63d9b03ff89adc3`,variantSha256:`7368a9736204c19a859b896992db21b24b469a9c5041f5833ad986f8f509ffd4`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.139.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Journey and the Stolen Place`,text:`An old queen prepared her grown daughter for a journey to a distant prince. She sent rich gifts, an attendant, and a speaking horse named Falada. Mother and daughter parted sorrowfully. The princess carried a small cloth her mother had given her for protection.

On the road, the attendant refused to fetch drinking water. While the princess bent to a stream, the cloth slipped away unnoticed. The attendant saw it go. She forced the princess to exchange royal clothes for worn ones, took Falada, and threatened her life unless she swore never to reveal the deception at court.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.139.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Goose Meadow’s Witnesses`,text:`The attendant arrived as the bride. The true princess remained below in the courtyard and was sent to help a boy called Conrad tend the geese. Falada had witnessed everything. Fearing his testimony, the false bride ordered him killed. Yet at the town gateway, his voice continued to recognize the displaced princess.

In the meadow Conrad tried to take some of her golden hair. She called a wind that carried his hat away, giving her time to braid it undisturbed. Angered, Conrad complained to the old king about her strange behaviour and the voice at the gate. The king watched and listened for himself.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.139.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Words at the Iron Stove`,text:`When he questioned her, the princess said she was bound by an oath made under threat. He suggested she tell her sorrows to an iron stove instead. There, away from a person’s face, she spoke the whole account: the stolen clothes, stolen place, fear and lonely work among the geese.

The king listened through the stove’s pipe. He brought her out, told his son what had happened, and restored her identity. The false bride’s deception ended. The princess’s words, finally heard, carried the truth that royal appearance alone had concealed.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.141`,candidateId:`EU-141`,definitionId:51,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`2beaa6b70087113372957c0a2a30d3f33adfb02cbb62f9cfb1f5b47687e672fa`,variantSha256:`9fa1351bed1b994a44f4f29b1aeb03e6d20395c4b0c37bbe03c71736aad7beb0`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.141.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Plain Table with a Gift`,text:`A tailor believed his goat’s false complaints and drove his three sons from home. The eldest found a joiner who taught him a trade. When his apprenticeship ended, his master gave him an unremarkable little table made from common wood. Its gift could not be seen in the grain or workmanship.

Set it down and ask it to prepare a meal, the master explained, and it would cover itself with a clean cloth, plates, cutlery, food and wine. The young joiner carried it on his travels. Wherever he chose to rest, he could put down the table and eat without searching for an inn.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.141.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Feast and the Swap`,text:`Eventually he wished to return to his father. On the last evening of the journey, he stopped at a crowded inn. The other guests offered him a place at their supper. Rather than take their food, he offered them his own. The little table filled with dishes, and each emptied plate was replaced with another. Everyone ate well.

The innkeeper watched and wanted the gift for himself. After the joiner fell asleep, he fetched a similar table from a storeroom and quietly exchanged them. The traveller went home carrying ordinary wood, unaware of the theft.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.141.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Wishing-Table Returns`,text:`His father welcomed him and invited their relatives to see the marvel. But the command produced nothing. The young man stood embarrassed beside a bare tabletop, accused by appearances of promising what he could not supply.

His second brother was tricked by the same host. Their letters warned the youngest, who used his own enchanted gift to demand the stolen property back. The wishing-table returned home with him. This time the eldest spoke, the cloth and dishes appeared, and the family’s disappointed gathering became a genuine feast.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.144`,candidateId:`EU-144`,definitionId:52,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`21acb8ad7808333e59c1066af6bd19b63e272a00f1bd999c0d3f625b514cd8fa`,variantSha256:`d40d1a94179cb11f7484677929e36be3eb78479da31eaaaada49a2489279257b`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.144.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Courteous Seeker’s Instructions`,text:`A king lay gravely ill, and his three sons feared they would lose him. In the garden, an old man told them of a possible help: water from an enchanted place, difficult to reach. The eldest brothers rode out in turn. Each insulted a small guide who questioned him along the road, and each became trapped in a narrowing mountain passage.

The youngest met the same guide but stopped and explained his errand. He did not know where to find the water. The guide rewarded his courtesy with directions, an iron wand, and two small loaves. He must strike the castle’s iron door three times, give a loaf to each of the two lions inside, and leave before noon or be shut in.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.144.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Castle and the Sleeping Traveller`,text:`At the castle, every instruction mattered. The third strike opened the door. The bread quieted the waiting lions, and the prince entered a great hall of enchanted princes. He took a sword and another loaf from the hall, then met a maiden who rejoiced that he had freed her. She directed him toward the spring and repeated the warning about the closing hour.

Beyond her chamber, a freshly made bed tempted the exhausted traveller. He lay down and fell asleep. When he woke, only a quarter of an hour remained before noon.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.144.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Cup Before Noon`,text:`He ran to the courtyard spring and filled a cup standing nearby. The water he had come so far to seek was at last in his hands. He hurried back through the castle, past the places where its marvels had delayed him. As he crossed the iron doorway, the noon bell struck and the gate slammed shut behind him.

Outside, he carried the cup homeward. Finding the spring had required guidance, courtesy and care; keeping its gift would bring another trial.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.001`,candidateId:`AS-001`,definitionId:53,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`49fa92c73788e1c0cca6f09e5c0fe5116f717e1b25f9a3198f6e66373eced815`,variantSha256:`524177c0f2f3892f514419811ba2bdb806d68a3ed2167b859b101edd14210578`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.001.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Peach and the Farewell`,text:`The old woman went to the river with washing and returned with a peach too large to fit comfortably among the ordinary things in her house. Before she and her husband could eat it, the fruit opened to reveal a child. Their quiet home, shaped by work and loneliness, filled with the care of raising him. They named him Momotaro.

At fifteen, he asked to leave for the island where ogres were said to hold stolen treasure and captives. His foster-parents prepared rice cakes for the road. Their hands knew the labour of pounding rice; now that familiar labour became a way of accompanying him where they could not go.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.001.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Three Travelling Companions`,text:`A great dog challenged the traveller and demanded his food. The encounter began badly, but the dog recognised Momotaro and asked to follow him. Momotaro shared part of a cake. Further on, a monkey came down from the trees seeking the same journey. The dog objected at once. Momotaro intervened and gave the newcomer a portion too.

A pheasant joined after another quarrel. Its bright feathers and courage impressed Momotaro, but courage alone did not make the three animals companions. Each wanted its place recognised. The boy had to insist that they stop fighting one another before they could undertake anything together.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.001.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Island and the Return`,text:`The sea brought a different silence. None of the animals had crossed it before. They looked from the water to their leader, and eventually boarded a small ship with him. As the voyage continued, unfamiliar motion became ordinary. They paced the deck, traded stories and played games.

At the island, the pheasant flew ahead. Two captive women showed Momotaro a small entrance in the fortress wall. With his companions he overcame the ogres, released their prisoners and gathered the stolen wealth. 

Momotaro returned to the old couple with treasure and a story larger than his own strength. The rice they had packed had travelled through three strange friendships and across a sea. Home was still waiting when the journey ended.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.002`,candidateId:`AS-002`,definitionId:54,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0eb8a458898c90d55017a4d6d169ddc43affa30420855571f7e74a59cc653b91`,variantSha256:`4a193aea70195e75eee1eebc4a9d6719a6a015416ec45a2098092622200981a0`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.002.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Games in the Woods`,text:`Kintaro grew up in the Ashigara mountains with his mother. The forest gave him companions before any town did. He understood the animals' speech, and bear, deer, monkey and hare became his closest company. His strength surprised the woodcutters, but to his friends he was the boy who could join their games and bring lunch.

One day the five reached a grassy clearing full of flowers. It seemed a fine place for wrestling. The bear shaped a platform from earth, and the others helped. Kintaro settled down to watch and promised a prize for each winner.

The deer held a leaf as a signal. When it dropped, monkey and hare began. The hare won the first round, and Kintaro opened his lunch box to reward him with a rice dumpling. The monkey protested that he had slipped and asked to try again. This time he won, although the tale makes clear that he pulled the hare's ear to gain an advantage. Other matches followed, with the bear taking a turn as referee.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.002.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Unbridged River`,text:`By evening, even such energetic company had had enough. They set off through the woods, expecting to be home before long. Then they reached a rushing river with no bridge. The animals stood at the bank, all their sporting confidence suddenly of little use.

Kintaro examined the trees beside the water. He chose a large one and grasped its trunk. At the third pull the roots gave way. The fallen tree stretched across the stream, turning an obstacle into a crossing.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.002.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Way Home Together`,text:`He went first; bear, deer, monkey and hare followed. A woodcutter watching from a rock could scarcely believe what he had seen. He followed them over the new bridge, curious about the child who had made it.

On the far bank the animals went back to their woodland homes, and Kintaro returned to his mother. She asked which of his companions had proved strongest. He answered with the cheerful certainty of a child who had spent the day at play. The visitor had seen something extraordinary; Kintaro had simply brought his friends home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.003`,candidateId:`AS-003`,definitionId:55,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`1a9383aade3cc2954cbb17679668e9880d5aff2dbb1e3c85bb2a4d77bc8ebb16`,variantSha256:`88a47fa1b66dab77fc28b112225ed6067d83e376f85383291e52bd7dfd6274f3`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.003.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Saved Turtle`,text:`Urashima Taro knew the waters beside Mizu-no-ye and the people who worked along its shore. Returning from fishing one evening, he found children tormenting a turtle. He bought it from them and carried it back to the sea, expecting nothing beyond the relief of seeing it safe.

Next morning the creature came alongside his boat and spoke his name. It offered to guide him to the Sea King's palace. Urashima doubted that a human could make such a journey, but the turtle grew large enough to carry him, and the descent left his clothes dry.

Fish received him at a great gate. The princess who welcomed him revealed that she was the turtle he had saved. She offered him a place in her father's kingdom and became his wife. Surrounded by feasting, coral halls and gardens containing all four seasons, he forgot the ordinary world for three days.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.003.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Three Days and Three Centuries`,text:`Then he remembered his elderly parents. He could not enjoy another day without knowing whether they were well. Otohime reluctantly let him go, giving him a lacquer box tied with red silk and requiring a promise that he would never open it.

The turtle brought him to a shore whose hills looked familiar. The people did not. Urashima hurried to his home and found a stranger there. When he gave his name, the man answered that Urashima Taro belonged to a village story three hundred years old.

A few days had become centuries. His parents and every familiar neighbour were gone. He returned to the beach carrying the box, unable to find the route back to his wife.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.003.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Three Purple Wisps`,text:`Perhaps, he thought, her gift contained a way to reach her. In that desperate hope he untied the cord. Three soft wisps of purple cloud rose from the opened box and drifted over the sea. The years that had passed elsewhere reached him all at once, and his life ended on the beach.

The tale leaves the shore unchanged enough to recognise, yet emptied of everything that made it home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.005`,candidateId:`AS-005`,definitionId:56,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c5b51f5bc64e28680b64f66dcd41e1963541a53e8792b4d01aaaeef092b8b84d`,variantSha256:`1f6522cfa502783f6344f7c8362b3b17875a5ba388a6ebf030215bab7d71d2b8`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.005.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Gift and the Promise`,text:`When Urashima asked to leave the palace, Otohime could not persuade him to remain. His thoughts had returned to his elderly parents, and he wanted to see them before another day passed. She brought a beautiful lacquer box bound with a silken cord and red tassels.

He hesitated to accept another present. The princess had already given him more than he could have imagined. Yet she wanted him to carry this token of their love, so he took it and asked what it was.

She called it tamate-bako and said that something precious lay within. Whatever happened, he must not open it. He promised. At that moment the closed box belonged to a farewell that seemed temporary; he expected to visit his parents and return to his wife.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.005.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`An Unfamiliar Shore`,text:`The journey ended at his native shore. The hills were familiar, but the village no longer held the people he knew. A stranger told him that Urashima Taro had lived three hundred years before. The little box, carried through the sea without being opened, was now the one tangible thing linking him to the life he had just left.

He walked back to the beach. There was no turtle waiting and no visible road to the palace. His promise remained clear, but so did the emptiness around him. He began to hope that the precious contents might tell him how to return.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.005.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Opened Box`,text:`Slowly he loosened the red silk. He lifted the lid. No map, jewel or message appeared. Instead, three soft purple wisps rose, paused around him and floated out over the water.

He became old in a moment, and his life ended there. The box does not become a catalogue of magical powers: it belongs to this single irreversible moment.

The sea still extended toward the place he had loved. What had seemed a small gift now marked the distance between that place and the shore.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.007`,candidateId:`AS-007`,definitionId:57,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`2a5f2a6ca5947d2d348001291f8904d6ecae0d8662f96f5fc625d96573d34ca1`,variantSha256:`5eb7525de93a346b8ff0431e7f2bea47f927455c3d65aa9defb09d0e6b9148ab`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.007.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Lost Companion`,text:`The old man's sparrow had been the delight of his evenings. After work he let her fly about the room and saved little portions of supper for her. One day, mistaking laundry starch for food, she ate it and admitted the error. His wife punished her cruelly and drove her away. 

The old man came home to silence. When he learned what had happened, he could not stop worrying about the bird. At dawn he went into the hills, searching through the woods and calling at every stand of bamboo.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.007.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Welcome Among the Bamboo`,text:`By afternoon he found her at the edge of a great grove. She greeted him with familiar little bows and tricks. To his astonishment she was well and could speak again. The creature he had cared for was no ordinary sparrow.

She led him to a fine house among the bamboo. Her family brought food on beautiful trays and entertained him with dancing. He sat on cushions finer than anything in his cottage, yet the pleasure was less in the luxury than in discovering that his friend was safe.

When darkness came, he thanked Lady Sparrow and prepared to return. Her servants brought two boxes, one large and one small. She invited him to choose a farewell gift. He picked the smaller because he was old and could carry it more easily.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.007.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Two Gifts and Their Consequences`,text:`At home, the little box proved full of gold, silver and precious things. His wife heard about the larger box and imagined twice the wealth. Next morning she demanded the way to the grove, hurried there and asked for the gift left behind.

The large box was heavy. Unable to wait until home, she opened it beside the road. Frightening creatures sprang out, and she fled. Nothing about the gift resembled the riches she expected.

Ozaki lets the wife live and change. Gradually she abandons her unkind ways. The small box supports the old couple's remaining years, while the sparrow's welcome remains the story's generous centre.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.008`,candidateId:`AS-008`,definitionId:58,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7e074bc632f584537729ea537310588af58b38fddbabc9729b0e9afc4618f641`,variantSha256:`28502cc462472f5e69108c05372d8adad24c6bab4046ca227380ad1c189c3394`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.008.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Storm and the Secret`,text:`The river had swept away every bridge built across it. Mosaku and his apprentice Minokichi relied on a ferry when returning from the forest. One evening a snowstorm caught them before the crossing, and the boatman had already left. They took shelter in his tiny hut, which had one door, no window and no place for a fire.

They lay down under their straw raincoats. The old woodcutter slept first. Eighteen-year-old Minokichi listened to wind and rushing water until weariness overcame him too.

Snow striking his face woke him. The door stood open. In the pale reflected light he saw a woman dressed in white. She had taken Mosaku's life, and now bent toward the apprentice. Her gaze frightened him, but she chose to spare him because he was young. He must never tell anyone what he had seen. If he did, she would know.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.008.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Woman Called O-Yuki`,text:`She left through the doorway. Minokichi secured it again, but could not make sense of the night. The ferryman found him in the morning, and the village cared for him through a long illness. He kept the encounter secret.

The following winter he met a young traveller called O-Yuki. She intended to find work in Yedo. They walked together, liked each other, and reached his mother's house. A meal became a stay, and the stay became a marriage.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.008.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Broken Silence`,text:`They had ten children. O-Yuki remained youthful while the years moved around her. One night she was sewing by a paper lamp. Minokichi watched her face and remembered the woman from the ferry hut. Believing the old fear distant, he told his wife the story.

She set down her sewing. She was the woman he had promised never to name. Anger rose, but she would spare him for their sleeping children. He must care for them well.

Her voice thinned into the sound of wind. She became white mist, rose to the roof beams and passed through the smoke-hole. Minokichi had kept a secret for years without recognising that it shared his home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.009`,candidateId:`AS-009`,definitionId:59,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7e0842205c5d5c16bd293de2c58959edb38119454c2a79ab6919d18f40b0db18`,variantSha256:`c1b92c9441ddeb92ffef60cb1862501e488dccd53d01fe4d39ec73ff392dc895`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.009.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Woman Beside the Moat`,text:`Before street lamps made the road familiar after dark, the slope called Kii-no-kuni-zaka could feel very lonely. An old moat lay on one side and high palace walls on the other. Travellers sometimes took a much longer route to avoid walking there alone.

One merchant hurried along it late at night. By the moat he saw a woman crouching and weeping. Her fine clothes and carefully arranged hair suggested that she belonged somewhere safer than an empty road. He worried that she was in danger and stopped.

He offered to help. She hid her face behind a long sleeve and gave no explanation. He spoke more gently, asking her to tell him what had happened. She rose and turned away, still crying. He reached toward her shoulder, hoping she would listen.

Then she faced him and lowered the sleeve. She passed a hand across her face. There were no eyes, no nose and no mouth.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.009.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Lantern Down the Road`,text:`He ran. The familiar slope became a stretch of blackness in which he dared not look behind him. Far ahead he saw a light as small as a firefly. He hurried toward it, thankful that somewhere a person was still awake.

The lantern belonged to a travelling soba seller who had set his stand beside the road. The merchant collapsed nearby and struggled to speak. The seller asked whether he had been hurt, then whether robbers had frightened him. Neither explanation fitted. The merchant tried to describe the woman and could not finish.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.009.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Face Returned`,text:`The seller asked whether she had shown him something like this. He stroked his own face. It became smooth and featureless, like an egg.

At the same instant the lantern went out.

The story supplies no chase after that darkness, no wound and no explanation of what body the strange being might possess. Its terror is the repetition: a refuge turns into the encounter the traveller believed he had escaped.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.010`,candidateId:`AS-010`,definitionId:60,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`bb572ba648456f9148fa470d48bf8f7d53a4a163eac7fb6406bfaf13b81dc909`,variantSha256:`d22e2e9010bd1d43bfff0260a8554b55e0bcbb1718e5b236537cb8d526d32e65`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.010.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Shelter Among the Willows`,text:`A snowstorm slowed Tomotada's journey through the mountains of Echizen. He was a young samurai carrying a message toward Kyoto, but neither his rank nor his horse could make the road shorter. When he saw a thatched cottage among willows, he asked for shelter.

An elderly couple welcomed him inside and offered food, warmth and a place for the horse. Their daughter Aoyagi came to serve him in clean homespun clothing. Her quickness with poetry impressed him as much as her beauty. Their exchanged verses let each know that the other's affection was returned.

By morning, the storm had cleared. Tomotada could resume his mission, but did not want to leave Aoyagi behind. She wished to accompany him. Her parents accepted their parting without taking the money he offered, and the two set out together.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.010.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Life Together`,text:`In Kyoto, their uncertain position brought trouble. Aoyagi was taken into Lord Hosokawa's household. Tomotada sent a poem to her despite the risk. When summoned before the lord, he expected punishment. Instead, Hosokawa recognised their affection and arranged their marriage.

The wedding closes that repaired episode, and five happy years follow.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.010.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Three Stumps and a Memorial`,text:`Then, during an ordinary conversation at home, Aoyagi suddenly understood that her life was ending. She told her husband the truth she had kept: she was not human. The soul and life of a willow were her own, and someone was cutting the tree. She asked him to repeat a Buddhist prayer for her.

He tried to support her, but her body vanished, leaving robes and hair ornaments on the mat. 

Tomotada became a wandering Buddhist priest. When he reached the place of the cottage, the house too had disappeared. Three old cuts marked where two elder willows and one younger willow had stood. He built a memorial beside their stumps and prayed for Aoyagi and her parents.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.046`,candidateId:`AS-046`,definitionId:63,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7a145b2c186b34156aab7a6105c1031d505e5f50bbdbc1faf706db2c7820edf8`,variantSha256:`e3189c20f2ba13819667abdba59bc386219314abd375bcbe07d2deba4af9a497`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.046.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The king of Âu Lạc believed his magic crossbow could keep an enemy at a distance. Its protection seemed strong enough to make reconciliation possible. When his daughter Mỵ Châu married Trọng Thủy, the son of Triệu Đà, the marriage appeared to offer another way for the two sides to live without war.

But Trọng Thủy had a purpose his wife did not know. He served his father’s plan to take the country. The household’s new intimacy brought him close to the very secret that had protected it.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.046.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`He asked Mỵ Châu to let him see the crossbow. She trusted the promises of her husband and showed it to him. In the heritage telling selected here, he damaged and exchanged the weapon. What remained with the king looked like the defense upon which he had relied, while the knowledge of its secret traveled elsewhere.

Trọng Thủy requested leave to visit his parents. The king agreed. Once away, he reported what he had learned to Triệu Đà, and preparation for an attack replaced the hope of reconciliation.

When the troops came, the king still expected the crossbow to be effective. He discovered its failure only in battle. The protection that had shaped his judgment was gone, and his country was defeated.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.046.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`He fled south with Mỵ Châu. The princess scattered marks along the route, believing they would let her husband find her after their separation. The same trust that had opened the crossbow’s secret now left a path for the pursuing force.

The crossbow’s place in the story ends as a broken certainty. Its loss was not simply a stronger weapon overcoming a weaker one. Someone had entered a family, learned what its protection depended on and carried that knowledge away. The king’s familiar object remained too convincing until he asked it to do what it could no longer do.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.054`,candidateId:`AS-054`,definitionId:65,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`466dd499e173679f80d256a9465bcc7f4c29af54b407b9829d48a014938aa75d`,variantSha256:`9575b26e9eba0c5a7785d14fcd316c497c8083b0f79a885f676cf0b331368400`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/mongolian.json`,packetSha256:`193a5d0dd39263731c7675c52f0b43267c4fefedfa3c7d29574767fec3de0051`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.054.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Seven Suns and a Proud Promise`,text:`Seven suns stood above a land that could no longer bear their heat. Rivers dried, vegetation failed, and people and animals struggled to survive.

The people went to Erkei-Mergen. He was young, brave and proud of his skill with a bow. They asked him to bring down the suns so that life could continue.

He promised seven shots for seven suns. But he added a harsh vow against himself if his aim failed. His confidence made no room for a chance he could not control.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.054.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Last Shot and the Chase`,text:`He began in the east and worked toward the west. Six arrows brought down six suns. Then, as he aimed at the seventh, a martin crossed his line of sight. The last arrow struck the bird’s tail instead. The remaining sun escaped behind West Mountain.

Erkei-Mergen turned his anger upon the martin. He set off after it on Qarcagai-Alag, his fast horse. The horse too made a reckless promise: it would catch the bird before dusk or give up its life as a horse.

Dusk came, and the martin was still beyond their reach.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.054.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`One Sun and Two Changed Lives`,text:`The archer answered failure with cruelty toward his horse. The animal was left changed upon the steppe and became a jerboa, with small forelegs and powerful hindlegs. Erkei-Mergen’s own vow followed him into a life as a marmot, living in a dark burrow.

The heat of seven suns was gone. One sun remained, and the story follows it into the rhythm of day and night. At evening, when the marmot emerged, the sun withdrew behind West Mountain as if it still feared the archer.

The people had asked for relief. They received a sky with one sun, but the rescuer’s pride had left him and his horse unable to return to their former lives.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.059`,candidateId:`AS-059`,definitionId:66,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`a27b74ebd06db819bb3f181c1b1628f7f470169cffe50c92ef548a2373c36e56`,variantSha256:`fd9bfedb599691529f9a2b78319000451d3fdc96d261c38050b94b0492a9091e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/mongolian.json`,packetSha256:`193a5d0dd39263731c7675c52f0b43267c4fefedfa3c7d29574767fec3de0051`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.059.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Spoon Takes the Sea`,text:`The old man was only a span high, but his beard was twice as long. He carried a skin sack and a horn spoon, and set out against the Black Dragon King, whose attacks made life on the land dangerous.

At the sea, he was asked where he was going. When he named his enemy, the sea mocked the thought that someone so small could defeat him.

The old man dipped his spoon. The entire sea came up in it, leaving the seabed dry. He poured the water into his sack and went on.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.059.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Companions in the Sack`,text:`A fox questioned him with the same contempt. He put the fox in the sack. Then a wolf mocked him, and the wolf joined the strange company he carried.

Behind the Dragon King’s palace, the old man climbed a hill and shouted his challenge. His voice shook the hill and frightened the ruler within.

The king first sent out ten thousand sheep to raise deadly dust. The old man released the wolf, and the sheep scattered. Next came the king’s two dogs. He released the fox; it ran, and the dogs chased it far away.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.059.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Water Answers the Final Threat`,text:`Now the Dragon King feared the small challenger. He ordered ten thousand soldiers forward.

The old man waited until they were near. Then he opened the sack.

The sea returned in powerful waves, sweeping upon the king, his soldiers and the palace. The ruler who had threatened the land with attacks no longer remained there. From then on, the Dragon King lived only in the sea.

A spoon, a sack, a fox and a wolf had answered the king’s great numbers. The small traveller’s burden had contained what his enemy never expected to meet.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.012`,candidateId:`LW-012`,definitionId:67,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0ea5afefa9e2a8b4f7e983444f41d1426ec170b8d319efdaadf8490d1038ddb5`,variantSha256:`d2842bd48d214c360e21589367cd616de9ccc9a4942b5bede2c00c1343434010`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.012.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Empty meals made Tortoise watch his neighbour’s doorway. Every morning Mr. Fly departed with a large sack; every evening he returned with it full, and food soon appeared in his house. Tortoise wanted to know what changed between those two moments. He wanted the answer badly enough to climb into the empty sack before Fly came out.

The journey lifted him away from familiar ground. At last the sack settled beside music and dancing in a town’s market. From his hiding place Tortoise watched people throw coins toward a drummer. Fly quietly gathered those coins for himself, dropping them into the sack instead of leaving them with the musician. The prosperity at home had an owner who was missing his money.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.012.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Tortoise did not challenge Fly. He waited among the growing heap until they reached home, then slipped away with most of it. Fly opened a nearly empty sack and could not understand the loss. Tortoise repeated his secret journey, turning his neighbour’s theft into an opportunity for another theft.

One day a coin dropped beside him and Fly looked closely enough to see a shell in the shadows. The hiding place had become evidence. Fly called the drummer and asked whether any money had disappeared. The answer was yes. Looking into the sack, the drummer discovered the passenger sitting among the coins.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.012.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Anger followed, and the punishment became cruel. Tortoise was beaten inside the tied sack; Fly then carried it away and dropped it near Tortoise’s home. His wife Nyanribo opened what neighbours thought was a present and found her injured husband. Fly spread an account of the deception through a song, and the drummers repeated it. The couple left for another country.

The empty meals had begun the watching. By the end, there was no clever hiding place left, and no honest victory for either thief.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.079`,candidateId:`AS-079`,definitionId:68,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`5669e18e1c346415ce3237b6821e3c68891e52dd4db281d7a80cf50bf6082723`,variantSha256:`79da0b47a3aba1ea4711e9a9f8255b07adb0251c6ade6078305dce3709744f30`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/sumerian.json`,packetSha256:`3b5c8afbe0a208195e2916a95bd5972f38f3c66f2ce26bdd1866d27064c30ccf`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.079.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Toward the Cedars`,text:`Gilgamesh wanted his name to outlast his life. Death carried people away, and he believed a great deed in the cedar mountains could leave a lasting memory. With Enkidu and a company of young men, he crossed the ranges toward Huwawa’s dwelling.

The cedar country was not an empty prize. Its guardian possessed seven terrors, and the travellers felt their force. Enkidu urged caution. He described Huwawa in frightening images, hoping to turn Gilgamesh back before the expedition became a disaster.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.079.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Bargain and Betrayal`,text:`Gilgamesh continued. Face to face with the guardian, he offered friendship and lavish gifts. Huwawa began surrendering his terrors, one after another, while the company felled the cedars. What looked like negotiation steadily stripped the guardian of the protection that had made him formidable.

At last Gilgamesh drew close as though to greet him. Instead, he struck. Huwawa was captured. The guardian pleaded with the sun god Utu and asked why the traveller had dealt with him so harshly. He spoke of the loneliness of his origins and appealed for his life.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.079.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Mercy Denied`,text:`Gilgamesh considered sparing him. Enkidu resisted. He feared the danger of leaving such an enemy alive and warned that mercy might undo the expedition. Despite the plea, he killed Huwawa. The cedar guardian could no longer answer either traveller.

They carried the result to Enlil, expecting their deed to stand as a triumph. His response overturned that expectation. Why had they treated Huwawa this way? They should have honoured and cared for him, he said. The terrifying powers were then assigned elsewhere across the world.

Gilgamesh had sought a deed worth remembering. He received one, but memory did not settle its meaning. The forest encounter left a record of courage, manipulation, broken trust and a divine judgement the victors had not anticipated.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.080`,candidateId:`AS-080`,definitionId:69,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`1c4fbcb67c6565c83bc58d5c685bfe98d949ae7f8f9ecd54751491aa2daef1b4`,variantSha256:`d7316784f9c8cfb323b1ef600cfd7738a7e6a150bf16b05c28acfcb6d7ff4e7c`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/sumerian.json`,packetSha256:`3b5c8afbe0a208195e2916a95bd5972f38f3c66f2ce26bdd1866d27064c30ccf`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.080.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Offered Destinies`,text:`When Anzud offered Lugalbanda a destiny, the traveller did not seize the first magnificent promise. He had cared for the great bird’s chick while its parents were away, and his kindness had brought him face to face with a giver of extraordinary gifts.

Anzud promised prosperity in the image of a richly laden boat, then offered unfailing weapons and strength for battle. Lugalbanda listened, but each answer left his own wish untouched. What he needed was not a greater store of possessions. His companions lay somewhere beyond the difficult mountain country, and he wanted the strength to reach them.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.080.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Speed and Reunion`,text:`He asked that his arms and legs should never tire. Let distance become passable; let his feet carry him where he wished to go. Anzud granted the request. The bird moved through the air above him while Lugalbanda travelled below with a speed he had never possessed before.

Before they parted, Anzud warned him not to disclose the gift to his companions. Lugalbanda accepted that condition. When the company saw him approaching, they had believed him lost. They welcomed him, brought him food, and rejoiced at the return they had not expected.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.080.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Lonely Messenger`,text:`Their journey did not end there. The army reached Aratta, but the siege dragged on and its king, Enmerkar, grew troubled. He needed someone to carry a message back to Inana in Kulaba. The way crossed difficult mountain ranges; no companion volunteered for the lonely errand.

Lugalbanda stepped forward. He would go alone. His brothers questioned the danger, but he set out without explaining the secret that made the journey possible. By midnight he had reached Kulaba and delivered the king’s appeal.

The boon Anzud gave him was not merely a way to escape isolation. It became a way to reconnect others across a distance they could not overcome.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.094`,candidateId:`AS-094`,definitionId:73,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`469bbdcbfcdc225fce1f674a4361de114a375a0a2a138cac439397f0f96513b4`,variantSha256:`c5f4242685cd9787d87adaaa9e6ab4e9c6fd1dcd433a9dc8a6a5fdfc37d4ad85`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/akkadian-mesopotamian.json`,packetSha256:`f2d1144a6f99531dfdb30bd58579eb7c438c5e18167100275519bc3fa8115d46`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.094.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Before Any City`,text:`Before a brick had been laid, there was no city to enter. No house stood beside a road, no tree offered shade, and no reed rose from the water. The places that would become familiar through their sanctuaries had not yet been built.

Water occupied the lands. Then movement began within it. Eridu came into being, and a divine house rose in the deep. Babylon was built and Esagil was completed. The gods were brought into the new order, and the sacred dwelling became a place their hearts desired.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.094.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Reed, Earth and Humanity`,text:`Marduk laid a reed upon the surface of the waters. Beside it he poured the earth he had formed. The immense beginning was expressed through something small enough to imagine: one reed and the gathering of earth next to it, where water alone had been before.

Humanity also entered the making of the world. Aruru worked together with Marduk in bringing its seed into existence. The new landscape would hold more than buildings and divine dwellings; it would become a place for living beings.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.094.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Rivers and Growing Land`,text:`Animals appeared in the fields. The Tigris and Euphrates were set in their places and given their names. Grasses spread, marsh reeds grew, and forests took form. Green plants belonged to the fields, while marshes and swamps gave the waters places within the land.

Cows and calves, ewes and lambs, plantations and woodland filled the account of what had been absent. Marduk placed a dam beside the sea. Earth, growth and water now met within an ordered landscape.

The city’s beginning belongs among these acts of making. Its walls do not rise after an ordinary builder chooses a site: its sacred house stands within a world taking shape, alongside rivers, reeds and the lives that will inhabit them.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.098`,candidateId:`AS-098`,definitionId:74,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f18de18bbdc6aabb431e91707587de084bec1d33b305e5feee94aba29b96aa6e`,variantSha256:`1ebca7ad5b1c6526f171ca7d058cfa29822ef97f191937ea31a61d9a6d125193`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.098.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Rejection and a Mountain Home`,text:`Sam’s newborn son had white hair, and the warrior allowed fear of other people’s judgments to overcome his duty as a father. He ordered the child taken away. The failure belonged to Sam, but its danger fell upon the boy, who was left near the high mountain where Simurgh nested.

The bird found him and brought him into her home. Her first intention changed into care, and her young learned to share the nest with him. Seasons passed. The abandoned child became strong, and reports of the remarkable youth spread beyond the mountain. Even Sam heard them. A troubling dream and the rebuke of wise counselors finally made him confront what he had done.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.098.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Reluctant Farewell`,text:`He traveled to the mountain with his people. From below he could see the nest and the son he had lost, but he could not reach them. Above, Simurgh explained to Zal why his father had come. Zal did not rush toward the strangers. He asked whether the bird was sending him away because she no longer wanted him. The home that had nourished him seemed more secure than any promised throne.

Simurgh answered with affection and gave him a feather, assuring him that her help would remain available. Then she carried him down. Sam greeted his son in remorse and asked him to open his heart. The white hair Sam had feared had not vanished; it shone above a youth whose strength and dignity the gathered people now celebrated.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.098.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Learning to Lead`,text:`Zal returned to the human world in splendid robes. At court, he began learning customs, judgment and the responsibilities of leadership. The bird’s teaching had given him life; further teaching gave him the means to govern. In time, Sam trusted him with the land during his own absence. Zal’s beginning had been marked by another person’s fear. His future was shaped by care, learning and the ability to act.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.100`,candidateId:`AS-100`,definitionId:75,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ab896520b3500c4514a6490685ba09c6ce01f53fb0b4ba1b35a02b2b51d5c6bc`,variantSha256:`f47d5b3d67b6ab45d0ecdc901dfb02569fb49e444e5bef25a89b385482c413ad`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.100.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Perilous Short Road`,text:`A message reached Zal from the captive king. Kai Kavus had entered a dangerous land against good counsel, and now his soldiers could not free themselves. Zal called Rostam to the task. There were two roads, he explained: one long, the other shorter and filled with perils. The rescue needed speed. Rostam took the harder road with Rakhsh.

The first night already showed that he could not meet every danger through his own vigilance. While he slept, his horse defended him from a lion. The next day brought a different enemy. The desert offered nothing to strike with a mace and no adversary to challenge. Heat pressed upon horse and rider; wherever Rostam searched, water was absent.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.100.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Strength Gives Way to a Clue`,text:`His strength ebbed. He thought of the king awaiting help and prayed that the rescue would not end with his own death in the sand. At last he lay down, unable to continue. Then a well-fed ram passed nearby.

Rostam saw more than another animal. A creature flourishing in this dry country must have access to water. The thought gave him something his strength could not: a direction. He rose, led Rakhsh and followed the ram’s track. It brought them to a clear spring.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.100.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Water and Gratitude`,text:`Rostam drank and gave water to his horse. He washed away the heat and dust, and the two recovered enough to continue. When he looked for their guide, the ram was gone. Rostam understood its arrival as divine help and bowed in gratitude.

There were further ordeals ahead, and he would still need all the courage for which he was renowned. But the desert had changed the shape of that courage. He had survived because he noticed another creature, trusted its path and cared for his companion beside himself. The man sent to save a king had first needed someone else to lead him to water.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.103`,candidateId:`AS-103`,definitionId:76,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`70045c5096d3ea718e6f5a07dfcf150e3e86f5551323bbb32a18a239a56ce475`,variantSha256:`96e142072488687654cf7d4cc551a4590984a70592319304def1752add340f99`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.103.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Beyond the Captive King`,text:`The captive king recognized Rakhsh’s neigh before his companions believed that rescue had arrived. Rostam reached them, but their welcome could not last. The White Div remained beyond the mountain passes, and the king feared what would happen when news of Rostam’s arrival reached that formidable guardian.

Rostam set out again with Ulad as guide. Ulad knew when the divs rested: at midday, when the sun stood high. Rostam followed the advice, waited for the proper hour and passed the outer opposition. The final lair lay within rock.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.103.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Mountain in the Cave`,text:`Inside, daylight failed. The air was heavy, the path difficult to see. At the end of the cavern stood what seemed a mass of mountain. It was the White Div asleep. The hero had crossed an immense distance to reach this body, and now all the distance between them could disappear in a moment.

Rostam woke him. The Div rose in astonishment and hurled a great stone. For an instant, fear reached the man who had undertaken the rescue. He understood that surviving this encounter would be an achievement beyond anything his confidence had promised. The two closed in a struggle of strength, and neither yielded easily.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.103.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Victory and an Obligation`,text:`Rostam called upon divine help and at last overcame his opponent. The White Div died, ending the threat that had held the rescue in suspense. The cave no longer barred Rostam’s return to the king.

Outside, Ulad reminded him of his own promise of reward and release. Victory had not erased that obligation or finished all the work ahead. Rostam turned back toward the prisoners. They greeted him with joy, knowing that the great adversary had fallen. What had seemed an immovable mountain in darkness had proved defeatable, but only after a struggle in which the rescuer himself had been afraid.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.105`,candidateId:`AS-105`,definitionId:77,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`5dd7dc10bc74f70ea2413231b674e3c8190afd5780a986e7156620b8d8743cfb`,variantSha256:`32ee6f0fcd01951eaeb99c6953e29f5e25841774c58d4a248be6b7b8312f0e49`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.105.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Petition Before the Throne`,text:`Zahhak wanted more than obedience. He wanted a declaration that his rule was just, and the people who feared him were expected to supply it. In the royal assembly, the document passed among those willing to put their names beside the king’s claim.

Then a voice outside demanded justice. The king allowed the petitioner to enter and asked who had wronged him. Kaveh stood before the nobles and answered that the wrongdoer was Zahhak himself. The blacksmith had already lost sons to the tyranny. Now the last son remaining to him had been taken as well. He asked for the young man’s life.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.105.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Declaration Torn`,text:`Zahhak was unsettled by his anger. He granted the request and tried to turn the favor into an endorsement. Kaveh should sign the declaration, he suggested, and testify to the justice of the man who had spared his son.

Kaveh refused. A release could not turn the earlier wrongs into good government. He took the document, tore it and scattered its pieces before the assembly. Then he left. The court that had seemed capable of compelling every person’s agreement did not stop him.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.105.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`An Apron Above the Crowd`,text:`In the marketplace, he told the people what had happened. He spoke of the suffering they knew and called them to seek another ruler. For a sign, he took the leather apron that protected him while he worked at his forge. He raised it on a lance, making the familiar cloth visible above their heads.

People gathered beneath it. Kaveh led them from the city toward Feredun, whose claim offered a way to challenge the tyrant. The blacksmith’s story in this episode ends with that alliance, rather than with him taking the crown. His work had begun inside the court by refusing a falsehood. Outside, he made that refusal something others could see and follow.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.107`,candidateId:`AS-107`,definitionId:78,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`df81ccf1882bce922c54085c54fbcb6cd0cfe66149e403dd53dc2ad4a86683ac`,variantSha256:`ee6b923fbfb4796219e4ec5a20f4098f273325ba6546ba66ced80998038b9709`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.107.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Care and a Gathering`,text:`Feredun had grown beyond Zahhak’s reach, protected by his mother’s care and by those who sheltered him. When he learned what the tyrant had done, he wanted to rush against him. His mother reminded him that courage needed preparation; the enemy commanded a kingdom, and a young man could not answer that power with anger alone.

Help came in the shape of Kaveh’s procession. The blacksmith had refused to praise Zahhak and led others beneath a leather apron raised as a banner. Feredun accepted the sign, dressed it in fine cloth and jewels, and made it the standard of their undertaking.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.107.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Mace and the River`,text:`He also designed a mace with the head of a cow. Its shape recalled the cow that had nourished him when he was small. A memory of protection thus traveled with the force he would use against the ruler who had threatened his life. Smiths made the weapon according to his design.

Feredun led his followers toward Zahhak’s seat of power. At a great river, the king’s authority barred their passage. They crossed with their horses despite the current and continued. In the city, people welcomed the challenger; he overcame the palace’s defenders and claimed the throne.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.107.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Hand That Is Checked`,text:`Zahhak returned and tried to recover it. The conflict ended with Feredun victorious. He raised his mace for the final blow, but a divine messenger intervened: the tyrant’s appointed end had not arrived. Feredun was to bind him instead.

He obeyed and took the defeated ruler to the mountain of Damavand, away from the people over whom he had ruled. The tyranny was broken. Feredun’s victory depended on many kinds of help: a mother who preserved him, a creature that fed him, a blacksmith who gathered followers and a warning that checked his hand at the moment of triumph.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.109`,candidateId:`AS-109`,definitionId:79,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c68ab668f83af6a67b48cf446cd78624ae53f03644d9707b010f462d65a17109`,variantSha256:`efb9af5787fa68c0888373a85d7700e1e7e828ff5c005dc1b5c43e6a7c4f3b49`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r21-anatolian.json`,reportSha256:`a052453e50deb8318cd4a5daf1148acbadc9993054c39fcea368100fad7beba3`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-anatolian.json`,packetSha256:`173670a1fbc6eb274364fda5ac2f078ae4c6d1fc393e266c64e75f65c3dcdb56`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:23:40.400Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read assigned actual prose/prompts and primary translations; added two actually read direct witnesses, not a fabricated third.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.109.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Defeat and a Different Plan`,text:`The serpent had already defeated the Storm God. At Kishkilussa, divine strength had met an opponent it could not overcome. The defeated god appealed to the other gods, and Inara prepared a different kind of confrontation.

She arranged a feast with abundant drink. Then she went to meet Hupasiya, a human whose help she needed. Their agreement included a sexual bargain in the surviving account; this retelling acknowledges it without turning the exchange into a romantic scene. The plan required a concealed helper as well as an inviting host.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.109.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Feast Outside the Pits`,text:`Inara brought Hupasiya to the place and hid him. She prepared herself and called the serpent up from its pit. Food and drink were offered as the reason to emerge. The invitation did not announce the danger waiting outside.

The serpent came up with its children, a phrase partly restored in this translation. They ate and drank, emptying the vessels prepared for them. The feast shifted the encounter onto Inara's chosen ground. The same opponent that had prevailed in combat now occupied a gathering arranged by someone else.

When the guests had drunk their fill, they did not go back into the pits. The German edition renders the line as unwillingness to return; the familiar explanation that their bodies physically could not fit is not asserted here. Either way, the surviving action leaves them outside their retreat.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.109.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Rope and the Reversal`,text:`Hupasiya emerged. He bound the serpent with a rope. The Storm God then arrived with the gods beside him and killed the bound opponent. The reversal was accomplished through preparation and human assistance, rather than a newly invented power in the serpent or a second identical duel.

This is the banquet-and-rope account. Another plot on the same tablet concerns stolen heart and eyes, marriage, and a fatal family conflict. None of those events completes this feast. The chosen version ends its serpent confrontation where its own surviving lines place the outcome, leaving the uncomfortable human aftermath to a separate card.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.111`,candidateId:`AS-111`,definitionId:80,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`5855b5c1ca3edca786796ce5d035b1fbe309ec4c42be3db5935204e577c42133`,variantSha256:`d2c17b65a4d5bd7469e6b24041de55044cfe4d8c96eadd92cf325f67f59076c1`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r21-anatolian.json`,reportSha256:`a052453e50deb8318cd4a5daf1148acbadc9993054c39fcea368100fad7beba3`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-anatolian.json`,packetSha256:`173670a1fbc6eb274364fda5ac2f078ae4c6d1fc393e266c64e75f65c3dcdb56`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:23:40.400Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read assigned actual prose/prompts and primary translations; added two actually read direct witnesses, not a fabricated third.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.111.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Mortal Binds the Serpent`,text:`Hupasiya entered the serpent story as a human helper. Inara had prepared a feast after the Storm God was defeated, but the reversal needed someone who could remain hidden until the guests had eaten and drunk. She approached him for assistance.

Their bargain included sex in the surviving text. The scene is reported briefly here because it belongs to the agreement, without being illustrated or made into a courtship. Hupasiya then accompanied Inara and waited out of sight as she called the serpent from its pit.

The serpent and its children came to the feast. After they emptied the drink vessels and remained outside their retreat, Hupasiya stepped forward. His rope bound the opponent whom the Storm God had failed to defeat. The god returned and killed the serpent, with other gods present. A mortal's part in the victory was therefore direct and practical.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.111.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Forbidden Window`,text:`That achievement did not send Hupasiya home. Inara built a house on a rock in the region of Tarukki and settled him there. When she went into the fields, she forbade him to look out of its window. Looking, she warned, would show him his wife and children.

For twenty days he remained before breaking the prohibition. He looked out and saw the family from whom he was separated. The account does not replace them with an invented enemy or make his desire to return a failure of courage. The man who had helped bind the serpent now wanted his own household.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.111.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Request Whose Answer Is Lost`,text:`When Inara came back, he appealed to her to let him go home. The surviving words preserve that request clearly enough to interrupt any easy celebration of his new dwelling. A house can be a place of confinement when its occupant cannot choose to leave.

The following passage is damaged. It does not establish a certain death, escape, reconciliation, or permanent arrangement. Hupasiya's story stops with the longing the tablet still allows us to hear, rather than a fate supplied to make the ending neat.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.002`,candidateId:`LW-002`,definitionId:87,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4238ff5c337fc31a0418a0d2863e626946273b42d1b56bf9d97a3090f70fd1a9`,variantSha256:`9ef77349fa3cddb423b0c20ae869ba9952f4b62991f0c7ecae642091c58a5f4c`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.002.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Akiti’s victory song had travelled farther than any challenger wished to follow it. He had defeated powerful opponents and declared himself ruler of the forest. Yet one great animal had never accepted that declaration. The elephant knew its own strength, and it knew charms. It challenged the hunter’s right to claim a title that the elephant considered its own.

The confrontation began with weapons. Akiti’s spear struck and glanced away. Arrows failed too, and then his knife. The elephant remained standing. Nothing in the hunter’s familiar equipment could overcome the protection surrounding his rival, and the forest title was still disputed.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.002.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Akiti possessed a charm of his own. His human shape became a lion, and he rushed at the elephant with a lion’s force. The elephant threw him off. The hunter changed again, becoming a serpent, but even that crushing strength could not bring his opponent down. The elephant had met metal, claws and coils without surrendering.

Then the contest changed scale. Akiti became a fly. Where the larger forms had arrived with an unmistakable threat, this form approached as a minute interruption in the air. The elephant’s great ear, which belonged to the same body that had withstood so much, became the hunter’s entrance.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.002.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The tale ends harshly. Akiti passed inside, resumed human form and killed the elephant from within. The great rival fell, and the hunter emerged claiming the forest’s kingship without another challenge.

The elephant’s protection had answered every attack it could meet on the outside. Its wisdom had not made it contemptible, nor had its strength made it wicked. It had contested a boast and defended its own claim. The ending grants Akiti the title, but leaves the listener facing the cost of the way he gained it.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.003`,candidateId:`LW-003`,definitionId:88,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f6c905b5d1b0a6c6431a160b240ef8bdde97457206bc816d56084674ab5c160b`,variantSha256:`e11ef19e41e102cf51831ce18420cb0f19d50e9a06eeed76c6689feb15e05271`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.003.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The hut had been closed around the Olofin so completely that he could find neither a door nor an opening in the roof. His enemies had left him there to starve. Sitting on the ground, he could see no route by which food, help or even news might arrive.

A mouse crossed the floor. Hunger made the king reach for his knife: perhaps this small creature could become a meal. Then he stopped. Such a meal would only postpone the same ending. He put the knife away and allowed the mouse to continue.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.003.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`To his astonishment, the mouse spoke. It thanked him for sparing its life and promised to preserve his in return. Then it vanished into a hole in the ground. The opening was far too small for the captive king, but it was large enough for the mouse and for a kind of help he had never considered.

When it returned, other mice followed it. Their little bodies carried grains of corn, gari and small fruits. One arrival could not make a great heap, but many arrivals could become a meal. The floor that had looked empty became a path of movement, and the king ate.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.003.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`For five days the mice brought food. The captors saw none of their work. On the sixth day they opened the hut, expecting the confinement to have ended the king’s life. Instead, they found him alive and well. Unable to account for this, they decided that his protection must be powerful enough to sustain him without food or drink.

They released him, provided a war canoe and allowed him to return to his own country. The Olofin departed because his captors feared what they could not explain. Yet the explanation had been travelling beneath their notice all along: a life spared, a promise answered, and many small carriers doing what one could not.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.006`,candidateId:`LW-006`,definitionId:89,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`26d49f7baca2d305231c7dc48783c29baa00df1a82cb4fdca2745066ab5d64c2`,variantSha256:`dd2cbf1c39ca5a83bb532c52baa7c7dd22d3689ea786719ded98c78592558371`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.006.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The water-bird had eaten the king of the crabs. For the bird, searching for food had ended in a meal; for the crabs, it had ended in a loss. They gathered in anger and decided to find the creature responsible. They knew what to look for: bright pink legs and feathers of pink and white. Once they found those legs, they intended to attack them.

The water-rat heard their plan and hurried to the bird. What had happened at the water’s edge was no longer finished. A search was coming, and the bird’s conspicuous body seemed to give it away before it could say anything.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.006.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Fear overtook its confidence. What would happen if the crabs reached its legs? The bird had used its height and beak easily enough while feeding, but neither answered the danger the rat described. Its pink legs were the very features by which the crabs expected to recognize it.

The rat proposed a small change: stand on one leg. The crabs were searching for two. If they saw only one, they might decide they had found another creature.

The bird thanked its adviser and tucked one leg up beneath its feathers. It remained tall and pink, with the same large beak. Nothing about its earlier act had changed. Only the shape visible from below had become different.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.006.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`When the crabs arrived, they looked up. The creature before them seemed to have a single leg. Their enemy had two, they reasoned, so this could not be the bird they wanted. They continued their search.

The water-bird was left standing, one foot hidden and the other holding its weight. The tale gives that familiar-looking posture a trickster explanation: the missing leg was there all along. Its successful concealment did not restore the crabs’ king; it merely carried the bird through their anger without recognition.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.007`,candidateId:`LW-007`,definitionId:90,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f9dcd89fbc6a8197cbfea53ef8607401641ca21beef0fa142b8e873b291ae4b6`,variantSha256:`dd2cbf1c39ca5a83bb532c52baa7c7dd22d3689ea786719ded98c78592558371`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.007.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The crabs had a reason for their anger. A water-bird had swallowed their king, and they wanted to find it. They spoke of the marks that would identify the enemy: two bright pink legs beneath pink-and-white feathers. Their planned retaliation would strike those legs.

The water-rat overheard them. It did not remain where the search was being prepared. Instead, it hurried to the bird and passed on what it had learned. The bird’s meal had become the beginning of a pursuit, and the rat was now carrying the news from one side of that conflict to the other.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.007.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`The bird was frightened. Its legs had seemed an ordinary part of its own beauty and height; now they were the things the crabs intended to recognize and harm. It asked what could possibly save them. A warning alone had made the danger clear, but had not removed it.

The water-rat’s answer was simple. The crabs were looking for a creature with two legs. The bird should stand on one and hide the other. If the searchers trusted that visible number, they would think it was someone else.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.007.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The bird thanked the rat and folded one leg into its plumage. The second remained planted beneath the tall body. Then the crabs came along. They saw the large beak, the pink bird and the single visible leg, but their expectation decided what those features meant. This creature could not be their enemy, they concluded, because their enemy had two legs. They passed it by.

The advice had worked. The water-rat had helped the bird escape recognition without changing its body or undoing what it had eaten. Its part in the story was neither a great spell nor a battle. It was an overheard conversation, a hurried warning and an observation about how a searcher might mistake what was hidden for what did not exist.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.008`,candidateId:`LW-008`,definitionId:91,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7e8003b00042300dcc55821cfb06e5ab0d1d5c1bc0c608a9e12989f69993f5d8`,variantSha256:`dd2cbf1c39ca5a83bb532c52baa7c7dd22d3689ea786719ded98c78592558371`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.008.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The king of the crabs was gone, swallowed by a water-bird looking for food. The other crabs gathered around the loss and turned their anger into a plan. They would search for the bird and attack its legs.

They had a description to guide them. The enemy’s legs were bright pink; its feathers were pink and white. It had two legs, and those were the things they intended to seize. The description seemed to make the search straightforward. A creature so tall and conspicuous should be difficult to mistake.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.008.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`But their conversation did not remain among them. A water-rat overheard the plan and hurried to warn the bird. Alarmed by what the crabs meant to do, the bird asked for advice. The rat suggested that it hide one leg. The searchers knew the enemy had two; let them see only one.

So the bird tucked a leg beneath its feathers and waited on the other. Its body still belonged to the same creature. Its large beak still rose above the bank, and its pink-and-white plumage had not changed. What changed was the number the approaching crabs could count.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.008.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`When they reached it, they saw a tall pink bird apparently standing on a single leg. They compared that sight with the enemy they had described to one another. Their enemy had two legs. This one appeared to have only one. They decided it could not be the creature they sought and continued onward.

The bird escaped their recognition, and the search failed at the very place where it might have ended. The crabs’ anger had not been invented: they had lost their king. Yet grief and determination did not make their description complete. A folded leg was enough to open a gap between what was there and what they believed they were seeing.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.009`,candidateId:`LW-009`,definitionId:92,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`305d137b937bc6466372351cb08c348cefe3f2fc0ee3b758f8fed6c313376e83`,variantSha256:`02a1d773386f31f410d2b483e6a36e6370b4b54daee881289305eb01c60f603e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.009.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`A man with little to spare still scattered a few grains for the ants each morning. He hoped that feeding them would keep his possessions safe, and the ants were grateful. Elsewhere in the village, a miser guarded gold gathered dishonestly. He drove birds away and crushed ants whenever he found them near him.

The ants wanted to punish his cruelty and help their friend. Why should one man have so much while the kinder one had so little? They dug a tunnel between the miser’s store and the poor man’s home, then began carrying the treasure through it.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.009.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`The first morning brought an astonishing heap of gold. The man believed the gods had rewarded his work. He covered the treasure with cloths. The miser, who had watched outside his store, could not understand where it had gone. For three nights the ants continued until they had moved it all.

Then the tunnel was discovered. Neighbours followed its route to the kind man’s home and found the missing gold beneath the cloths. He protested that he had not taken it and could never have entered such a narrow passage. They answered that he must have used a charm to become small. His truthful astonishment did not persuade them.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.009.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`They confined him in a hut and planned to kill him the next day. The ants saw that their reward had placed their friend in terrible danger. Gold was no longer the thing that needed moving. The walls were.

Over several hours they ate away the hut. The prisoner found himself standing in open space. He fled into the forest and did not return.

By morning, only a few stumps remained. The neighbours wrongly decided that the ants had consumed the prisoner as well as his confinement. They imagined the punishment had been completed for them. Only the ants knew the truth: their friend was alive, and the second undertaking had undone the danger created by the first.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.010`,candidateId:`LW-010`,definitionId:93,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0f982a54a5dbcc464a1e97ee4baa91fd425ebb8849ab0d6f679dd1d7f88c4e16`,variantSha256:`5125e6a31605e77fd3fd4df7def97d1a3f4d91a567fe59efec91f39f2174f5dd`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.010.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Brown birds filled a forest grove with songs. A passing magician sat down to listen. Their melodies delighted him at first, but delight turned into jealousy. He could not sing like them, and soon he wanted their voices more than he wanted to hear them.

He gathered the birds and directed their attention away from sound. Their feathers, he said, were plain and ugly. Imagine instead the brilliance of red, blue, orange and green. The birds began to regard their familiar brown coats through his words. What had not troubled them before now seemed a lack that he could remedy.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.010.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`The magician offered an exchange. His charms would give them beautiful feathers if they gave him their voices. He made the voices seem unimportant: after all, who came into the grove to listen? The listener who most desired their music was telling them it had little value.

The birds considered the promised colors and agreed. Their voices went together into a large calabash. The magician changed the feathers, and the birds looked at the brightness newly spread across their bodies. They were pleased with what they saw.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.010.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Carrying the calabash, he hurried away. When he reached a deserted place, he opened it and swallowed what it contained. The songs that had filled the grove now belonged to his voice. People travelled from far away to hear him sing.

The birds remained satisfied with their brilliant feathers. The tale does not have them chase the magician or recover the songs; it leaves them enjoying the visible part of the bargain. Its ending imagines an explanation for beautifully colored birds that cannot sing.

In the grove, beauty had become something to look at. Elsewhere, the voice of the solitary singer drew a crowd. One exchange had separated the two, and the person who arranged it had known from the beginning which gift he wanted.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.011`,candidateId:`LW-011`,definitionId:94,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`885164b688b645efdbcf9284d75585724376f0ec683e282c42c664f498e226a0`,variantSha256:`787defdd45c2c1380fe38043b0c8d17570c4c07038f32306ce1793d8d7e3d48c`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.011.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Leopard’s coat was once as plain as a lion’s. There were no dark markings to break its shape as it moved through the forest. Akiti, the renowned hunter, pursued it, and Leopard feared that the pursuit would end in its death.

The animal sought protection. It ate the roots of a magic plant, unnamed in the tale. What it gained was not a new weapon or a way to challenge the hunter in a contest. The roots made it invulnerable to the weapons Akiti would use against it.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.011.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Soon the hunter saw Leopard slipping through dense undergrowth. Leaves and stems interrupted the view, but the animal’s body was still there among them. Akiti shot his arrows. The chase had reached the instant Leopard feared, yet the protection held: the arrows did not kill it.

They left something else. At every place where an arrow struck, a dark mark appeared. Leopard escaped alive, carrying traces of attacks that had failed to end its life. The plain coat was no longer plain.

Those marks covered its body as spots. As Leopard moved between the trees, its new pattern mingled with the patches of sunlight and shade. The broken shapes around it seemed to continue across its flank. An animal that had once shown one unmarked color could now merge with the forest’s changing surface.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.011.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The tale keeps hunters pursuing Leopard, but says it is rarely caught. Its escape is not a claim that danger has disappeared; the pursuit continues. What remains is a body that bears the dark reminders of the arrows and a pattern that helps conceal it.

Thus the story joins protection and appearance in a single transformation. The plant prevents the feared death, while the unsuccessful arrows make the spots. Leopard carries neither a trophy nor a victory song out of the undergrowth—only its life, and a changed coat that looks like the light through the trees.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.006`,candidateId:`AS-006`,definitionId:100,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4b264dac36752e89a6227a638c717942033e61670779e1afc4eac81cb52ba9e8`,variantSha256:`2b57b701364b531595382ba3636e710898cffdae34aac6d08f4f6161a264349d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.006.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Child in the Bamboo`,text:`The bamboo-cutter earned a small living from the stems he gathered and made into household things. One morning light shone from a hollow bamboo. Within it stood a tiny girl, only three inches tall. He took her home, and his wife placed her carefully in a basket.

Their house had long been quiet. Now they devoted their care to the unexpected child. The cutter began finding gold and precious stones inside bamboo, and the household grew prosperous. In three months the girl became full-grown, luminous enough to brighten the rooms at night. She was named Princess Moonlight.

Her fame brought persistent suitors. She had no desire to marry and required impossible treasures from five men who pressed their claims. None succeeded. Even the Emperor could not compel her to enter his household: she began to fade when he tried, and resumed her form only after he agreed to leave her free.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.006.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Coming of the Moon Envoys`,text:`Later her foster-parents noticed her weeping while she watched the moon. She told them that she belonged there and would soon have to return. The life she had known on earth had made her love this home; she did not wish to leave them in their old age.

Guards surrounded the house on the appointed night. Near dawn a cloud descended, carrying a chariot and luminous envoys. Their leader said that her earthly stay had been a punishment whose term was over. The foster-father argued that the daughter he had raised for twenty years had never done wrong.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.006.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Garment to Remember`,text:`The screens opened of themselves. No guard could stop the summons. Princess Moonlight looked back at the old man and gave him her embroidered outer garment as a keepsake.

Before accepting the robe of wings, she insisted on writing a farewell letter to the Emperor. She sent it with the remaining elixir the envoys had given her. Then the chariot rose toward the moon and disappeared in dawn clouds.

The cutter had first carried her home in his hand. At the last threshold, the garment was what he could hold.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.011`,candidateId:`AS-011`,definitionId:101,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`90df4e6eca8b910050d16fe4930fc4f4bd207bb19e9d4afa4b6bdaf1c161e86e`,variantSha256:`c779573ad7c76750ed83a28bb334b2ee3f1094e9958c417193a8d72ee4e8ffb5`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/japanese.json`,packetSha256:`edfb38a564887d5a6dea4d070d955338e78e3d098a8d371782e91a045c0d360c`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.011.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Tree Across Generations`,text:`For generations the cherry tree had stood in the samurai's garden in Iyo. Its ordinary season was spring, when branches opened above the place where he had played as a child. His parents, grandparents and earlier ancestors had hung coloured paper among its blossoms, carrying poems of praise into the same familiar space.

He grew old. His children died before him, and the tree remained as the living connection to those he had lost. Its yearly return could not restore them, but it belonged to a world in which their presence was still remembered.

One summer the old tree withered and died.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.011.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Replacement and the Loss`,text:`The neighbours saw how deeply the loss troubled him. They found a young cherry tree and planted it in his garden, hoping its fresh growth would give him something to welcome. He thanked them. He tried to appear pleased, but the new tree could not carry the history of the old one. It stood nearby with a different future, while he mourned what had ended.

Hearn then relates a tragic claim of exchanging one life for another. On the sixteenth day of the first month in the old lunar calendar, the samurai died by his own hand. The details of that act are omitted here; it is a loss in the story, not a model of care or a means of saving a real tree.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.011.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Blossoms in the Great Cold`,text:`The legend says that his spirit entered the withered cherry and that it blossomed in the same hour. Thereafter it flowered only on that sixteenth lunar day, during the period of severe cold rather than in its old spring season.

The garden held both the neighbours' attempt to comfort him and the absence their kindness could not repair. Winter blossom made that absence visible in a strange form: an old tree returning when other cherries would wait.

The tale offers no return of the man or his children. Its wonder remains beside its grief.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.016`,candidateId:`AS-016`,definitionId:105,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6fc871bc998055e6472b126f735e53666bc800d2d32c79350e21f7d6f9fbcee9`,variantSha256:`5fe7477372b9cc83f3fc235078e1ec7e84850375c29d0825eda61e101ebae281`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/chinese.json`,packetSha256:`d8c49cc8b142e24561cdaf636ae6c24b38affe49e4e0521258b22626adbabd47`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.016.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Maker and His Long Work`,text:`At first there was no shaped world for Pangu to stand upon. There was Chaos, and within it the being who would give it form.

He set to work with a hammer and a chisel. What had no edge began to have an edge; what had no place began to take its place. The heavens and the earth, the sun and moon and scattered stars became the work of his hands.

His labour did not end in a day. For eighteen thousand years he worked, growing as the work around him grew. Every new day made him taller. The maker and the world increased together, until the task that had begun in shapelessness had become a universe.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.016.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Life Becomes the World`,text:`When the work was finished, Pangu’s life ended. But the world had not yet received everything he could give it.

His head became mountains. His breath moved as wind and gathered into clouds; his voice remained in thunder. His limbs became the four quarters of the earth. Rivers carried what had been his blood, and the ground took form from his flesh.

What had clothed his body now clothed the world. Hair became trees and growing plants. The stars kept the pattern of his beard. Stone, metal and precious mineral took the place of tooth and bone, and rain preserved the memory of his sweat. Even the small creatures that had lived upon him entered the story of human life.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.016.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Landscape Left Behind`,text:`There was no longer a solitary maker striking at Chaos with his tools. There were mountains beneath the sky, rivers moving through earth, weather passing above forests, and living beings within them.

His last gift was not another object to set beside the sun or moon. It was himself, changed into the world his labour had made.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.017`,candidateId:`AS-017`,definitionId:106,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ae59e292be4c5e3196310f46b732e8b1f82ac6ccaa86bb8d9cb6593a04c9e832`,variantSha256:`51ff6cdaf3ab911a5b4ded9ec06c056112a796c93a4ba5943063620940ceff17`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/chinese.json`,packetSha256:`d8c49cc8b142e24561cdaf636ae6c24b38affe49e4e0521258b22626adbabd47`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.017.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Mountain Breaks the World`,text:`The defeat of Gonggong should have ended the struggle. Instead, his rage struck the world itself.

He drove his head against the great mountain that supported the heavens. The mountain fell. The pillars above broke, and the corners of the earth gave way. A contest between powerful beings had become a danger to everything living beneath the sky.

Nüwa faced the broken heavens and the waters spreading across the land. She could not repair one while leaving the other to ruin. The damaged sky needed to be made whole, the earth needed support, and the flood needed to be stopped.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.017.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Three Parts of a Repair`,text:`She brought together stones of five colours and melted them for the work above. Their separate hardness gave way to material that could close the breach. Where the heavens had been broken, she made a repair.

Then she secured the four extremities of the earth. What had lost its support could stand again. The world needed more than a shining patch overhead: it needed its foundations restored.

The water still had to be held back. Nüwa gathered the ashes of reeds and used them to stop the flood. The land of Ji was rescued from the waters that had threatened it.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.017.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Life Beneath the Restored Sky`,text:`The mountain’s fall had spread one failure into many. Sky, earth and water had ceased to hold their proper relations. Her answer was a succession of repairs, each meeting a different part of the disaster.

She did not begin a second universe, or abandon the damaged one to make something easier. She worked upon what was already there. The heavens held, the earth stood, and the waters no longer swept through the land.

A world wounded by anger remained a world in which life could continue.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.018`,candidateId:`AS-018`,definitionId:107,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f76faa34cd9da4a6eb19b23fa709f411749eddc27eefa72f5eedc6c0185a3a21`,variantSha256:`5724e5a4f39c5cd55647d956aed8f0e3f5e3e6e41c8c4c58d5be13052305cfd9`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/chinese.json`,packetSha256:`d8c49cc8b142e24561cdaf636ae6c24b38affe49e4e0521258b22626adbabd47`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.018.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Stone for a Broken Sky`,text:`Stone usually belongs beneath a traveller’s feet. In the disaster Nüwa faced, it would have to serve the sky.

Gonggong had fallen in battle and struck the supporting mountain in rage. Its collapse broke the pillars of the heavens and unsettled the corners of the earth. The flood spread through a world whose boundaries could no longer be trusted.

Nüwa gathered stones of five colours.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.018.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Material and Joined Repairs`,text:`Each was a hard, separate piece of the earth. None, left as it was, could close the broken heavens. She melted them, bringing the different stones into material she could use for repair. What had been different colours in separate stones now belonged to one work of repair.

She used that material to mend the sky.

The task was immense, but it was not finished when the breach was closed. A repaired covering above could not by itself restore the earth’s damaged supports. Nüwa secured the four extremities, then gathered reed ashes to check the waters. The land of Ji survived through these joined acts of repair.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.018.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Strength at the Breach`,text:`The stones had done one part of that work. They had not become a weapon against the being whose anger caused the damage. They had not raised a new palace or rewarded a victor. They had gone into the place where the world needed to hold together.

When the sky could once more remain above the land, the stones were no longer merely stones lying apart on the ground. Their change had made possible a different kind of strength: a repaired expanse where there had been a break.

Below it, the restored supports and stopped waters completed what the five colours had helped begin.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.019`,candidateId:`AS-019`,definitionId:108,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`e25344433eac99000cde95dc25f304a47132524e3da659d823c16b0d2d21b584`,variantSha256:`ae7dfc4bbb85d366a95175d3f1138bdbd9797397ee45ddaeacdfe8374d96a466`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/chinese.json`,packetSha256:`d8c49cc8b142e24561cdaf636ae6c24b38affe49e4e0521258b22626adbabd47`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.019.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Light in the Rafters`,text:`A white light shone from a beam in the roof. Hêng Ô noticed it while her husband was away, and a sweet fragrance had filled the rooms beneath it.

She brought a ladder and climbed toward the light. Hidden above her was the pill her husband had received and put away. She found it, ate it, and felt her body become strangely light. The floor no longer held her in the familiar way. She tried to rise into the air.

Then Shên I returned.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.019.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Pursuit into the Cold Sphere`,text:`He looked for the missing pill and asked what had happened. Afraid, Hêng Ô opened the window and flew out. He took his bow and followed her into the clear night.

The moon was full. Against its light she seemed small as she moved farther from the house. Her husband pressed on behind her, until a sudden wind struck him down and broke the pursuit.

She continued alone.

The world she reached was immense, cold and bright, like a sphere of glass. Cinnamon trees were its only vegetation. At first she saw no other living creature. The strange covering of the pill left her body and became a rabbit, white as jade.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.019.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Home Beyond Familiar Ground`,text:`Hêng Ô tasted bitterness in her mouth. She drank dew and ate cinnamon when she grew hungry. This cold place would become her home.

The flight had carried her beyond the window, beyond the man pursuing her, and beyond the ground she had always known. Yet arrival did not bring her back to familiar rooms. Beneath the clear brilliance of the moon, she had to learn how to live in a place that had seemed empty.

She had followed a light hidden in the rafters. Now she remained within a far greater light, with cold trees, dew and a white rabbit where the earthly world had been.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.020`,candidateId:`AS-020`,definitionId:109,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4dc06d788c9d34b96534dbbfb75773121f6f7ba47526ddbfbf709f84d27af43f`,variantSha256:`49a85489599c8bcfcfe5a73fef2077b31ca5935a7da396cb720cf7cd90df210b`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/chinese.json`,packetSha256:`d8c49cc8b142e24561cdaf636ae6c24b38affe49e4e0521258b22626adbabd47`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.020.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`An Archer Proves His Skill`,text:`The man’s bow was bound with red cloth. When Emperor Yao met him, he claimed that he could shoot with great skill and travel upon the wind.

Yao gave him a test: strike a pine tree on a nearby mountain.

The arrow reached the tree. Then the archer rose upon a current of air, went to the mountain and brought it back. Yao called him Shên I, the Divine Archer, and kept him in his service.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.020.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Source of the False Suns`,text:`Before long, the country needed more than a demonstration. Ten suns burned above the fields. Crops withered, storms uprooted trees, and waters spread across the land. Yao gave Shên I three hundred men and sent him against the causes of the disaster.

The archer first dealt with the spirit releasing the storms. After that danger ceased, he led his men toward the West River at Lin Shan.

There, on three neighbouring peaks, he found the source of the extra suns. Nine extraordinary birds were blowing out fire. Their fire made nine new suns appear in the sky.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.020.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Nine Arrows in Red Stones`,text:`Shên I shot one arrow, then another. He kept shooting until nine arrows had left his bow.

The false suns changed as the birds were struck. What had looked like unbearable light became red cloud. The clouds dispersed, and the nine burning presences vanished from the sky.

The archer and his men climbed to the top of the mountain. They did not find nine ordinary fallen birds waiting there. They found nine red stones, with the nine arrows embedded in them.

His first arrow had shown an emperor what he could do. These nine left a different proof: the terrible lights had gone, and on the mountain the stones held the marks of the shots that ended them.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.023`,candidateId:`AS-023`,definitionId:111,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d605ccf1b7073334596e8da60d4ca3c8a5a2a911af40facd75a0b8750b055e0d`,variantSha256:`d3f61fb40c41ec70216403307f03d1ccde38da3074bdfcf2c2bf52380ee5ca78`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/chinese.json`,packetSha256:`d8c49cc8b142e24561cdaf636ae6c24b38affe49e4e0521258b22626adbabd47`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.023.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Stone Birth and the Search`,text:`The mountain had given birth to a stone monkey. Wind had stirred an egg at its rocky summit, and from it came a creature whose eyes sent golden light toward the heavens.

The light faded when he began to eat, but the life before him remained extraordinary. He moved among the mountain’s peaks and waters, feeding on fruit. His exploits made him king of the monkeys.

Kingship did not answer his fear of death. He left the mountain and travelled over land and sea in search of a way to live beyond it. Eighteen years passed before he found a teacher.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.023.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Name and a Sea-Bed Rod`,text:`The teacher gave him the name Sun Wukong and taught him to change his form and travel through the air. When Sun returned to his mountain, he defended the monkeys against the being that had troubled them in his absence. Then he organized his subjects and began looking for a weapon he could use.

Nothing he had found suited him.

He went to the Dragon King of the Eastern Sea. There he obtained an iron rod that had once been planted in the ocean bed to regulate the waters. Sun pulled it out and changed it to suit himself.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.023.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Weapon That Changes Its Reach`,text:`Gold bands marked its two ends. In his hands, the rod could grow to an astonishing length or shrink until it was no larger than a fine needle. Small enough, it could rest in his ear; enlarged, it could serve him as a staff.

The stone-born traveller had returned with a name and skills his younger self had never possessed. Now he held something that changed as readily as he could.

He had sought a weapon to fit his strength. The rod would not force him to accept one fixed size. It answered the reach he needed, from a small hidden needle to a length that could rise beyond sight.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.029`,candidateId:`AS-029`,definitionId:117,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0a47afb4109caecdeb7ab4097a0af55675872b9fe60239b706bd7db09371d911`,variantSha256:`fed7f3b9beba418069e238e984078dbb9dbc0e21b1e15e286a6029459addfb86`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r06-japan-korea.json`,reportSha256:`01126ed124799bcf4aea76f20908e233265c0730903e08329a1bd4506ca9466b`,packetPath:`traditions/korean.json`,packetSha256:`930e9d62907ee25a360a2dc8e8969b5af345b546a2232a9d35e0972f74db7a8f`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:48:08.296Z`,independence:`Packets originally authored by /root/japanese_research, not the coordinator. Coordinator reopened selected narrative witnesses and relevant native reference bodies, read all existing story paragraphs and manually selected chapter cuts.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.029.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`An Errand Beyond Ordinary Roads`,text:`Gangnim had been sent on an errand no ordinary road could complete: he had to bring the ruler of the afterworld before a human magistrate. The answer to a troubled case lay beyond the reach of the court. Gangnim's first wife helped prepare him for the journey, and household guardians became guides along the way. Help came in unexpected company, including an old woman whose knowledge carried him farther than his own confidence could.

At last he reached Yeomra. The ruler tested the visitor by hiding in changed forms, but Gangnim recognized him and secured a promise: Yeomra would come to the human court. The errand had an answer now. Getting home was another matter.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.029.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The White Puppy's Path`,text:`Yeomra gave Gangnim a white puppy and directed him to follow it. After so much difficulty, the guide was a small animal rather than another formidable official. Gangnim went after it, keeping to the way it took. When he tried to catch the puppy, he fell into water.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.029.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Return Recognised`,text:`His next destination was his wife's entrance. The long crossing had returned him to a familiar threshold, yet familiar ground did not mean familiar time. More than three years had passed in the living world. Those at home had counted him among the dead.

Gangnim announced his return. His wife did not simply accept the impossible news: she checked the needle left in his clothing when he departed. It was still there, and she allowed him inside.

The puppy's part in this episode is brief and decisive. A traveler who could confront an afterworld king still needed a guide for the return. In this Jeju telling, following a white puppy leads him through water and back to the person who had first helped him leave.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.037`,candidateId:`AS-037`,definitionId:124,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d536daf87f779b70c3196a27f797ff71163366382e26b9156c8bb2ed85090c7c`,variantSha256:`49b6d3a0fc6d8cb8bfdb545006576c5b8f671a30b792c77f29ff4116ee461160`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.037.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Lạc Long Quân belonged to a water lineage, yet people on land knew him as a guide. He taught ways of growing food and organizing their lives, then sometimes returned to his water dwelling. When trouble came, the people called for him as children might call for a father. The tale says he answered.

His own household began with something no ordinary family expected. He and Âu Cơ had a sac that opened to reveal a hundred eggs. Each egg produced a son. The children grew with extraordinary strength and understanding, and the family’s wonder became something the surrounding people also recognized.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.037.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`But being able to answer a call did not mean always being present. Lạc Long Quân stayed for a long time in the water realm. Âu Cơ and the sons lived without him. When they attempted to travel north, a guarded frontier prevented their return. They turned back and called for the absent father.

This time the question awaiting him was personal. Âu Cơ asked why she and the children had been left alone. Her words made the household’s difficulty visible: a hundred remarkable sons still needed a family that could care for them.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.037.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Lạc Long Quân answered by explaining the different realms to which he and Âu Cơ belonged. He would take fifty sons to the water dwelling; fifty would stay on land with their mother. Their destinations would differ, but they were to tell each other when difficulties arose and continue helping one another.

The sons accepted the separation. Âu Cơ’s group settled in Phong Hiệp and raised the eldest as Hùng king. The other fifty followed their father toward the water.

The ending does not put every child beneath one roof. It leaves a promise stretching between land and water, a connection that must be maintained across the very distance that first troubled the family.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.038`,candidateId:`AS-038`,definitionId:125,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`9b48a9e855c0cf88631084c80929fdce4e0ad7f29dbf016c7115caff6f777bc4`,variantSha256:`fcf9aade03daa6b32565b0273f1cf03bb5b832d8a1c075fe744e9b3bd2c46c4a`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.038.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Âu Cơ and Lạc Long Quân began a household that soon exceeded every familiar measure. A sac opened into a hundred eggs, and a son emerged from each. The sons grew without ordinary nursing, strong and capable in a way that astonished those who saw them.

Yet the extraordinary children did not remove an ordinary difficulty. Their father spent a long time away in his water dwelling. Âu Cơ remained with the sons, responsible for a household whose other parent was elsewhere.

She tried to travel north with them. At the frontier, guards prevented the family from crossing. There would be no simple return to the place she wished to reach. Mother and sons went back, and their call for Lạc Long Quân rose out of that thwarted journey.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.038.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`When he returned, Âu Cơ did not merely accept another disappearance. She spoke of being left without a husband and of the children being left without their father. The family’s scale made the absence no less painful. Her demand concerned shared care, not the marvelous origin of the sons.

Lạc Long Quân answered that they belonged to different kinds of realm: his was the water lineage, hers an immortal lineage living on land. They would divide the sons equally. Fifty would accompany him; fifty would remain with her. They were to keep helping one another whenever trouble came.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.038.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Âu Cơ led her fifty to Phong Hiệp. There, the group chose the eldest son as Hùng king and called their country Văn Lang. The account follows their settlement rather than leaving her suspended at the moment of farewell.

Land and water separated the parents, but the story gave the mother’s group a continuing place and purpose. Her part begins with a question about why she was alone. It ends with a household becoming a community, while the promise of mutual help remains open between the two destinations.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.039`,candidateId:`AS-039`,definitionId:126,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`a92da154ad14e6340e0ad4c318d896936ce20edfde0ac1ffa9dbbd2a18fc8da6`,variantSha256:`f04728c410c2c4316bcf5530719aa8a9b3445db7a2d9201c8f86ce8560e5e947`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.039.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The household of Âu Cơ and Lạc Long Quân received something it did not know how to understand: a sac rather than an ordinary infant. In the selected old account, they regarded it as an unsettling sign and left it outside in the fields.

Seven days passed. The sac opened, and a hundred eggs appeared. Each egg yielded a boy. What the household had feared as an anomaly became a hundred sons, and the parents brought them home.

The children did not grow in the customary way. They needed no nursing and developed remarkable strength and judgment. Those who saw them recognized something extraordinary, but the tale did not leave them as eggs forever. They became people capable of listening, deciding and taking part in a shared future.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.039.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`That future was complicated by their father’s long stays in his water dwelling. Âu Cơ and the sons lived apart from him. They attempted to go north, found the frontier guarded and returned. The family called Lạc Long Quân back, and their mother spoke about the loneliness his absence had caused.

The answer was a division. Lạc Long Quân would take fifty sons to the water realm. Fifty would follow Âu Cơ on land. The parents’ different destinations would not dissolve the bond between the groups: when difficulties came, each was to tell and help the other.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.039.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Âu Cơ’s fifty settled at Phong Hiệp, and the eldest was chosen as Hùng king. The account moved from a strange object lying beyond the household to a community making a place for itself.

The hundred eggs matter because none stands alone. Their number becomes the measure of a family that can divide equally without becoming unrelated. The sac’s beginning is brief and unsettling; the promise that follows its hundred sons stretches much farther, between land and water and toward the communities they will establish.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.040`,candidateId:`AS-040`,definitionId:127,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`763308ca5ee1a896d8903119fd73834384d1e8e019210a27df906efe906e5fe2`,variantSha256:`8410736c05e7fb51b31593ced21f05f787d40d53e6b454e8f72d34092eada728`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.040.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The mountain god came from the water realm to find a place on land. In this account of Tản Viên, he had been one of the fifty sons who followed Lạc Long Quân. He traveled along rivers, passed places that did not satisfy him and finally looked toward the high mountain.

Tản Viên offered the quiet ground he wanted, with smaller mountains gathered around it and people living below. He opened a route toward its southern side and made his dwelling high above the surrounding land. Later generations associated places along his journeys with his presence.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.040.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Another contest eventually brought danger to that home. Sơn Tinh and Thủy Tinh both sought marriage with Mỵ Nương, the Hùng king’s daughter. Sơn Tinh’s gifts arrived first. The king gave his daughter to him, and he took her toward Tản Viên.

Thủy Tinh came too late. Angry at the outcome, he brought the water creatures against his rival and tried to recover the bride. The contest left the court and became a threat across the rivers.

Sơn Tinh stretched an iron net across a river at Từ Liêm. His opponent opened channels and drove water toward the mountain, turning places along the route into broad pools. Rain and wind darkened the approach.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.040.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The people at the foot of Tản Viên did not simply wait to see which power prevailed. They made open bamboo barriers, beat drums and mortars and raised their voices in support. The danger reached their homes and fields, so their work entered the defense.

The selected tale says Thủy Tinh’s anger did not end with that attack. Floods returned in the eighth and ninth months, and crops continued to suffer. Tản Viên remained a place of refuge and struggle rather than a height outside human concerns. The god’s chosen home and the villagers’ defenses became parts of the same landscape.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.041`,candidateId:`AS-041`,definitionId:128,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0d6543a39c515ee24880cd413f25f7c0278233d735292d9b53d81540b342a2cb`,variantSha256:`89f264c802d24cfa79a5874a74bb0fece047c27cc3a45ac239f366cba5f5c9fc`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.041.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Thủy Tinh arrived at the king’s court with his wedding gifts and discovered that the choice had already been made. Sơn Tinh had brought his gifts first, received Mỵ Nương in marriage and taken her toward Tản Viên. The rival’s lateness had become an outcome he refused to accept.

He gathered the creatures of the waters and pursued the mountain god. What had been a competition at court spread outward through the landscape. The route to Tản Viên became a problem of rivers and channels rather than a road used by an ordinary traveler.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.041.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Sơn Tinh placed an iron net across a river at Từ Liêm. Thủy Tinh answered by opening another way. He sent a branch from the Lị Nhân River toward the Hát and then the Đà, seeking a passage for the water host. He opened another approach from Tiểu Tích Giang toward the mountain.

Places along that movement collapsed into broad pools. The water did not respect the shapes that had made the land familiar to its inhabitants. Rain and wind obscured the mountain while the flood advanced.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.041.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Below Tản Viên, villagers built open bamboo barriers. They sounded drums and mortars and called out to aid the mountain god. Their effort made the confrontation more than a rivalry between extraordinary beings: crops and homes lay in the direction of the attack.

The water creatures were defeated, but Thủy Tinh’s anger survived the attack. The danger had not vanished.

The account connects that unresolved pursuit with floods in the eighth and ninth months and with the losses suffered by people farming the region. Each returning flood gives the old contest another landscape to disturb. Water moves toward the mountain again, while those below must continue living where the two powers meet.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.042`,candidateId:`AS-042`,definitionId:129,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4cb929ec0ed9bae7340b5c677f8e9b69b6c4ed273cf5e114fbf2801a0e893970`,variantSha256:`b4cbdf8e9bae3a686424361902e0584ba081e871b25e1149eb74bc23eef96cd5`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.042.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`When danger threatened the kingdom, the king had already received a warning to prepare and seek someone capable of defending it. Messengers traveled among the villages, carrying a question no ordinary boast could answer.

At Phù Đổng, a three-year-old boy had not spoken or risen to sit. His mother heard that the messenger was near and spoke jokingly about the child’s inability to help. The boy suddenly answered. He asked her to invite the messenger into their home.

His request was astonishing: an iron horse, an iron sword and an iron helmet. The messenger returned to court with news that the silent child had offered to face the danger. Some doubted that one person could do what an army needed, but the king remembered the earlier warning and agreed.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.042.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`The child began to grow. Food disappeared faster than his mother could provide it. Neighbors brought cattle, cakes and fruit, and still his hunger exceeded their preparations. Cloth likewise failed to cover his expanding form; reeds were added to keep him clothed.

The transformation made a defender from a household that had seemed unable to offer one. It also made the neighbors part of his readiness. Their food and fabric accompanied the extraordinary growth that the royal equipment alone could not sustain.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.042.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`When the army approached Trâu Mountain, he rose to his full immense height, took the sword and helmet and mounted the horse. The threatening force was defeated, and its survivors submitted.

He traveled on to Sóc Sơn. There he left his garment and rode upward into the sky. The king honored him as Phù Đổng Thiên Vương, and the story continued through shrines and remembrance.

The household’s silent child had become a figure too large for its cloth, its food and eventually the ground itself. Yet the path from silence to departure had passed through many ordinary hands bringing what they could.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.045`,candidateId:`AS-045`,definitionId:132,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6bda8afd3a75d19bffc145e8936a74414a1e911b9986e30c9822e31042c7af7a`,variantSha256:`8710916aa493f190f461c01dd62eb31087bf6d220c78a2a2671a2d1da585f054`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.045.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The crossbow had protected the king of Âu Lạc until someone reached its secret through his daughter’s marriage. Trọng Thủy persuaded Mỵ Châu to show it to him, then substituted the part that carried its power. He returned to his father with the knowledge needed to attack.

The king did not discover the betrayal in time. He trusted the crossbow as the opposing force came closer, and only when he tried to use it did he realize that its protection had failed.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.045.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`He placed Mỵ Châu behind him on his horse and fled south. She had promised Trọng Thủy a way to find her if they were separated: feathers from her garment scattered along the route. She understood those marks as a bond between husband and wife. He followed them with the pursuit.

At the sea, the king found no way forward. The land route had ended, and the water offered no ordinary crossing. He called for the extraordinary adviser who had helped him before.

The Golden Turtle appeared on the surface. Kim Quy’s warning directed the king’s attention away from the distant army and toward the person seated behind him. The trusted family connection had become the means through which danger followed them.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.045.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The king answered with a fatal act against his daughter. The princess had been deceived, and her death restored neither her safety nor the lost country. No victory emerged from the warning.

In the selected literary account, the turtle then parted the water and guided the king away. The pursuing force arrived to find him gone.

Kim Quy’s appearance is brief, but it divides the ending. Before it, the king is still running from an enemy he expects to leave behind. After it, he sees how the pursuit entered his own household, at a cost no warning can undo.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.047`,candidateId:`AS-047`,definitionId:133,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`04f4161ca7be76d8812ebcc8dffbf9c8d1c135224b85fd37701b2f02674771e8`,variantSha256:`0b6c5042f42ade526e70afd81b0978d28ee9b0d8f6633f921d324e200b8c4f81`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.047.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Mỵ Châu believed her marriage to Trọng Thủy could connect two sides that had been enemies. Her father also hoped for reconciliation. Inside that hope, Trọng Thủy served a different plan: he would discover the secret of the king’s magic crossbow and carry it to his own father.

He asked to see the weapon. Mỵ Châu trusted him, and the secret became accessible. In the heritage telling, he damaged and exchanged the crossbow before receiving permission to visit his parents. Once away, he reported what he had learned.

When the attack came, the king expected the crossbow to protect Âu Lạc. Its failure overturned that confidence. He fled south with his daughter, leaving the defeated country behind them.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.047.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Mỵ Châu had arranged a sign by which her husband could find her after a separation. As she traveled, she scattered marks along the route. What she meant as a means of reunion became a path for the pursuit. The promise that had seemed personal remained attached to the military deception she had not understood.

At the shore, Kim Quy warned the king that the enemy was behind him. The king turned that warning against his daughter and killed her. There was no triumph in her death. Her trust had been exploited; the final act did not make the loss of country or family whole again.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.047.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The heritage legend connects her death at the coast with pearls forming in oysters. Its image carries the story from a hurried land route into the water, where something luminous remains after the princess herself is gone.

The pearl does not restore the marriage or excuse the killing. It lets the account remember Mỵ Châu through another kind of presence: small, enduring and associated with the sea at which her flight ended. A deception had made her trust dangerous. The ending gives that trust a memory that cannot be reduced to the deceiver’s success.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.048`,candidateId:`AS-048`,definitionId:134,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0e1f8b5570b7eb7ac929b9c4c95d094b44e959ab37dcc644033818040e53d01d`,variantSha256:`2c70c52536dd3658f51b25aef65f25d009fa5900be8c7e07696c72f68afd7ddd`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/vietnamese-kinh.json`,packetSha256:`423d1de49535974d02094d4c09ad9fa49b4a7053f38e8b06b0c6a45fea697bf4`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.048.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`At Đầm Cả, fishers found their work repeatedly interrupted by a stone. Their bamboo fishing containers yielded nothing else. They threw the stone back into the pond, but each return to the water failed to end the encounter. At last they left it on the shore.

The local account places this strange arrival after Mỵ Châu’s death. People began to treat the stone as extraordinary, and villagers tried to carry it away. The effort did not succeed. Groups from the surrounding villages could not move what the fishers had found.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.048.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Then people from Cổ Loa brought a hammock. Two elders approached the stone, and the impossible difficulty changed into an equally impossible movement: the stone rolled into the hammock by itself.

Its carriers set out. The task that many had failed to begin now seemed possible, and the woven support bore the weight across the route. Yet the journey still did not belong entirely to those carrying it.

Near the place associated with Mỵ Châu’s small temple, the hammock broke. The stone could be moved no farther. Whatever effort followed, the departure that had seemed settled could not resume. The villagers established a place of worship where its journey had stopped.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.048.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The tale then turns from movement to size. The stone had first been small, but it became larger and larger. Carrying it was no longer the question; the people needed its growth to cease. They asked, and the account says the stone stopped growing.

This selected episode leaves the object where the community’s route ended. A pond catch had resisted being discarded, then resisted being carried, then entered a hammock without being lifted. Its final position emerged from those repeated changes rather than an ordinary choice of destination. The villagers’ request gives the episode its closing limit, while the local story keeps the stone within Mỵ Châu’s remembrance.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.050`,candidateId:`AS-050`,definitionId:136,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`06bba054ae1b98218d5b80a9847c68abc311c7b350babdfae1261e6bbb5773ac`,variantSha256:`ce845ff7c2cc83bece5f4f4ab0a039b0624fa8b5a8384c5af13b46f4768a5035`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/mongolian.json`,packetSha256:`193a5d0dd39263731c7675c52f0b43267c4fefedfa3c7d29574767fec3de0051`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.050.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Bearer Beneath the Ocean`,text:`Before the earth existed, water lay everywhere. There was no shore beyond the ocean and no dry ground within it.

Sakyamuni passed over those waters, looking for a way for the earth to come into being. Below him, a frog swam from north toward south. Its body was golden.

He recognized that this creature’s back could bear the earth.

The frog turned until its head lay toward the north. Fire came from its mouth, and water streamed toward the south. Golden sand was cast upon its back. What had been an animal moving through an unbounded ocean now carried the ground where people would live.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.050.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Directions Acquire Their Places`,text:`The directions acquired different presences. The east became the place of wood and forest; the west, the place of metal. Fire marked the north, water the south, and sand formed the earth above the frog.

These were not separate worlds placed beyond one another’s reach. They belonged to the same world, held upon one creature. The forest and the metal region, the fire and the water, all had their place around the ground on its back.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.050.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A World Remembered as Carried`,text:`Yet the bearer remained alive within the tale. The earth rested on more than an unmoving surface. When the frog shifted its legs or shook its head, the movement reached the world above, and the land trembled.

At the beginning, the ocean had offered no place to stand. At the end, land existed, but it depended upon a body beneath it. The golden creature had become a bearer of land where no land had been.

The world could seem solid to those who lived upon it. The tale remembered that solidity as something carried.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.052`,candidateId:`AS-052`,definitionId:137,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`2ddbb9ee2f5dbf3e575d6cd1e3a64869f4f3253f42df594cbbae4a155c579658`,variantSha256:`7e06ef752c96ea1b42b148b249d79ef1dc47e390afbb1d076f7b9436d1633434`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/mongolian.json`,packetSha256:`193a5d0dd39263731c7675c52f0b43267c4fefedfa3c7d29574767fec3de0051`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.052.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Two Hunters Meet the Archer`,text:`Two brothers went out hunting in the morning and found a man standing still, looking into the sky.

He had shot a bird, he told them. Now he was waiting for it to come down.

The morning passed. At noon, the bird finally fell with his arrow in it. The brothers had seen a skill far beyond an ordinary shot. The three men joined themselves as sworn brothers and continued their journey together.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.052.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Company Grows to Seven`,text:`They met a man who could hear any sound on earth or in heaven. They accepted him into their company. Where the others could only look at what lay before them, his hearing reached beyond their sight.

Another man could pile mountains upon mountains. He too became their brother. Then came a runner who could catch an antelope, bringing speed into the company as surely as the strong man brought strength.

The last was a magician who could drink up the sea.

Seven men now travelled together: the two hunters, the archer, the listener, the strong man, the runner and the sea-drinking magician. The first two had begun with one another. The others had brought different powers, and all had entered the same bond.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.052.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Victory and the Great Bear`,text:`Together they defeated Magpie Khan. Their skills belonged to a company that had grown by encounters, rather than to one hero gathering every power within himself.

At the end, the seven became Ursa Major.

The hunters’ morning had led through waiting, discovery and new brotherhood. What began with two men on the earth ended with seven remembered in the sky. The archer’s impossible shot was only the first marvel they encountered; it was not the last thing their shared journey would become.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.053`,candidateId:`AS-053`,definitionId:138,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0c0a4518c362f6ba2e5e26bf906d5b10b4c8242e84fce35867d669ab9a4d81bf`,variantSha256:`e1f4f1ef7d4a7194d98eb966e19db14229a786e0598d4ff5ad469fe7e3dd1e8b`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/mongolian.json`,packetSha256:`193a5d0dd39263731c7675c52f0b43267c4fefedfa3c7d29574767fec3de0051`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.053.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Helper and the Khan’s Request`,text:`An old man’s cow gave birth to a calf unlike any other. Its front resembled a human being, while its rear resembled a cow. Its name was Ama-caγan.

The calf grew up and did good for people. Yet the help he gave did not bring him good treatment in return. His journey eventually took him into heaven, where he met White Khan.

White Khan had heard what he had done. He also knew how people had treated their helper. Now the khan needed help himself. Every day he struggled against the Khan of the devils, and he wanted Ama-caγan to aid him in overcoming that enemy.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.053.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Deception and Retaliation`,text:`Ama-caγan agreed to help, but his approach was not an open contest. He disguised himself as a doctor and entered Devil Khan’s palace under that false identity. There he killed the khan.

He had done what White Khan wanted. Escape was another matter.

Devil Khan’s wife, a monster, discovered what had happened. As Ama-caγan rose toward heaven, she hurled an iron scraper after him. Her attack ended his life before he could return.

The story does not leave him beneath the palace or upon the earth. From that final violence, seven stars take their place in the sky: Ama-caγan becomes the Great Bear.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.053.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Helper Remembered in the Sky`,text:`He had begun as a strange calf, had grown into a helper, and had gone to heaven after receiving poor treatment from the people he served. The task offered there carried him back into danger. His deception won one struggle but could not protect him from its aftermath.

Above the world, the seven stars preserve a different ending from the victory he had set out to achieve. They remember the helper who did not come safely home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.055`,candidateId:`AS-055`,definitionId:139,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`bbf2deac5d3826f5a24c7e868ef8b54d1a04653571aa0866ffe92cc0f5f26a20`,variantSha256:`18b4edc3020bc17abf8081b1e4fd59f1a4abe8b040c62a7d65ab89855ee5b8d2`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/mongolian.json`,packetSha256:`193a5d0dd39263731c7675c52f0b43267c4fefedfa3c7d29574767fec3de0051`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.055.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Speed Bound to a Promise`,text:`The martin was still flying ahead when Qarcagai-Alag began the chase.

His rider, Erkei-Mergen, had brought down six of the seven suns that scorched the land. At the seventh shot, the bird had crossed the arrow’s path. The remaining sun escaped, and the archer’s anger followed the martin.

Qarcagai-Alag was a fast horse. His strength now served a pursuit that began with resentment rather than the people’s need for relief. He promised to catch the bird before dusk, staking his continued life as a horse on the chase.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.055.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Dusk and the Changed Form`,text:`Speed did not make the promise safe.

The day moved toward evening. The horse kept pursuing the martin, but the bird remained beyond him. When dusk arrived, he had not caught it. The archer treated that failure with cruelty and left him altered upon the open steppe.

There his life changed into a jerboa’s.

The animal that had carried the archer now moved close to the ground. Its forelegs were small beside its much longer hindlegs. The story connects this new shape with the horse’s fate; it does not make the punishment fair.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.055.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Challenge Still Heard on the Steppe`,text:`The martin continued to circle horse riders, its chirping remembered as a challenge to catch it. The bird’s flight could still seem like an invitation to another chase, though the earlier one had cost Qarcagai-Alag his old form.

The last sun remained in the sky, bringing day before retreating behind West Mountain. The quest that had begun against unbearable heat had spared it.

For the horse, the change was more personal. He had been praised for speed, then bound by a promise speed could not guarantee. After dusk, no rider sat upon him. The tale leaves a small leaping animal upon the steppe where a fast horse had been.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.060`,candidateId:`AS-060`,definitionId:143,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f5fbfbc8f794b142ebe97e57e9b36102b2e6c977e7184775ce6ebf3696548b7d`,variantSha256:`e063996bcf9caf5a383b697a3c1034087648cbaf339e08edd507b1dd86522002`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r11-chinese-mongolian.json`,reportSha256:`7d6b37370c69b57005e9c7a27ca310a4c1abdc9c3081733c56abf8fc26354ebe`,packetPath:`traditions/mongolian.json`,packetSha256:`193a5d0dd39263731c7675c52f0b43267c4fefedfa3c7d29574767fec3de0051`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:35:46.073Z`,independence:`Both packets authored by another worker; this reviewer did not author either packet. Exact24 scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.060.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Hill’s Small Challenger`,text:`The Black Dragon King lived on land. His repeated attacks made him an enemy feared by people, and behind his palace he believed his strength was secure.

Then a voice rose from the hill.

It belonged to an old man no taller than a span, with a beard twice that length. The challenger had crossed the country carrying a skin sack. He had come to end the king’s attacks, and his shout shook the hill as well as the ruler’s confidence.

The king answered with numbers. Ten thousand sheep would run out, raising enough dust to overwhelm the old man.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.060.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Great Numbers Fail Twice`,text:`But the sack opened, and a wolf came out. The sheep fled in every direction. The great crowd had become a scattered flock before it could carry out the threat.

The king tried his two dogs next, Qasar and Qusar. The old man let a fox out of the sack. It ran away from them, and both dogs followed, drawn far from the place where the king needed them.

Neither threat had reached its target.

The Dragon King became afraid. He sent ten thousand soldiers, trusting once more in a multitude against one small opponent. The old man did not run. He waited for the troops to approach.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.060.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Sea Changes the King’s Place`,text:`This time, when he opened the sack, he released the sea.

Earlier in the journey, he had lifted it with a single dip of his horn spoon and carried it away. Now waves rushed upon the soldiers, the palace and the Dragon King himself. The ruler’s landbound power gave way before the water he had never imagined inside that small burden.

Afterward, the king no longer lived on land. His place was in the sea.

The challenger had not matched each threat with a larger army. He had brought the sea to the ruler who thought the land belonged to him.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.062`,candidateId:`AS-062`,definitionId:145,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`bef048909e3ab6815011271aadf0bbfd90b4f7dbdabcd19f0f1b0feebb9a59e8`,variantSha256:`c2b9cf80cb61140a4703ada84892c097d48945a6bb2ac0b8a61ff02179de67b4`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-egyptian.json`,packetSha256:`4a25bc19f39856bb8fd58fe27a1097c726ddc93e6b66ed9d82bc049235f32acc`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.062.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Departure and Fables`,text:`The goddess had gone away. In the version associated with these Egyptian objects, Bastet left Re in anger and travelled south to Nubia. Away from her father she took a feline form, and Re wanted her to return.

The task of bringing her back went to Thoth. He did not appear in the imposing form of a ruler or conqueror. He approached as a monkey and offered fables, drawing the goddess’s attention toward stories. The telling made room for listening where an angry departure had broken a relationship.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.062.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Returning Company`,text:`At last the journey changed direction. The goddess came back toward Egypt, and her return was accompanied by a procession. Bes was among its companions, together with musicians and dancers.

For Bes, this was a different expression of protection from the small household images associated with mothers and children. Here he belonged to a company whose movement welcomed an important presence home. Music and dance gave the return a form people could hear and see, rather than making it another contest of force.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.062.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Good Fortune Returns`,text:`Good fortune returned with the goddess. Thoth had helped turn the journey homeward, and Bes joined the company that welcomed her. Persuasion and welcome now belonged to the same journey: stories had opened a way back, and music and movement accompanied the presence that had once departed in anger.

The returning figure remained capable of fierceness. The company did not need to erase that power to accompany her. Bes moved with the others at the border between danger and welcome, a protective companion in a journey whose end opened again what anger had carried far away.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.063`,candidateId:`AS-063`,definitionId:146,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0e96631f85650aa31c3b30ff328f3f0532777c9fdcea13507f79788ec81f607c`,variantSha256:`d560b680fa9e0d338ceca80705a586c312b6275bcef73e8aaf0d39c1578b1e24`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-egyptian.json`,packetSha256:`4a25bc19f39856bb8fd58fe27a1097c726ddc93e6b66ed9d82bc049235f32acc`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.063.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Before the Balance`,text:`Nany stands in the hall where her life is to be judged. She has already faced questions about the harm she might have done, the truth she might have abandoned, and the suffering she might have caused. The next part of the scene rests on a balance.

Anubis attends to it. His jackal-headed figure is close to the apparatus, adjusting the scales rather than threatening the woman beside them. On one side is Nany’s heart; on the other is a small image of Maat, marked by a single feather.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.063.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Truthful Heart`,text:`Osiris presides on the other side of the scene. He carries signs of royal authority, while the balance draws attention to something no crown can replace: the witness of the heart. Behind Nany stands Isis. A baboon representing Thoth waits on the beam, ready to set down the result.

The weighing succeeds. Nany’s heart is not heavier than the figure of truth. Anubis reports that it is a reliable witness, and Osiris orders the restoration of her eyes and mouth. The woman who arrived holding those parts in the image can now enter the renewed existence the papyrus presents for her.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.063.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Sight and Voice Restored`,text:`Anubis has not won a fight or claimed a victim. His work belongs to the care and order of the passage. The balance is attended, the evidence is heard, and the successful decision is recorded.

Around this judgment the papyrus preserves other episodes of Nany’s journey. In this one, a very personal hope becomes visible: that her life can speak truly for her, and that she can continue beyond death with sight and voice restored.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.064`,candidateId:`AS-064`,definitionId:147,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c644ec45dd2077e44bd06a7ffc9ade10c876119c8863e1c4e4f07b145b4eb8d2`,variantSha256:`f332876b4e34bab1403f711b96f2c58a1bf4430d5a8de9397b480e5a12c139ad`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-egyptian.json`,packetSha256:`4a25bc19f39856bb8fd58fe27a1097c726ddc93e6b66ed9d82bc049235f32acc`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.064.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Music and Vigilance`,text:`Osiris had travelled away from Egypt. In Plutarch’s telling, his work brought cultivation, laws and reverence for the gods to the people he met. He relied on persuasive speech, song and music rather than the force of weapons.

While he was away, Isis remained alert at home. Typhon did not overturn her control. But when Osiris returned, a different danger was waiting: a plan concealed within a celebration.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.064.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Prize at the Feast`,text:`Typhon had measured the king in secret and ordered an ornamented chest made to fit him exactly. He gathered companions who knew what the object was for, then brought it into the room where the feast was taking place. The chest invited admiration; its careful workmanship made it appear valuable before anyone considered its purpose.

Typhon offered it as a prize to whoever could lie inside and match its length. People tried in turn. The game seemed harmless because every attempt so far had failed, and the chest remained merely an attractive possession awaiting an owner.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.064.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Chest Goes Downriver`,text:`Then Osiris entered. The dimensions were right because they had never been left to chance. The conspirators closed the lid and secured it. They carried the sealed chest to the river and sent it toward the sea.

The celebration ended with absence. A traveller who had persuaded others through words and music was removed by people who had disguised a settled plan as a game.

This episode does not end with his restoration. It leaves the chest moving away from home, carrying the loss that will set Isis searching. The admired object has changed from a promised gift into the centre of a betrayal.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.065`,candidateId:`AS-065`,definitionId:148,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ed60258917b2571701b8e497fc4519ac73f218bae168d5626e78fabf5ce56f12`,variantSha256:`aa88e47b7230c5345f5e28694875dff21dbb8429ad155472d736408d8c964776`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-egyptian.json`,packetSha256:`4a25bc19f39856bb8fd58fe27a1097c726ddc93e6b66ed9d82bc049235f32acc`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.065.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Name She Sought`,text:`Isis knew many things, but one remained beyond her reach: the hidden name of Ra. The sun god had many public names and forms. His deepest name stayed concealed within him, protected even from the gods who came after him.

As Ra grew old, Isis found a way to create something he would not recognise as his own work. She formed a snake from earth touched by his spittle and placed it on the route he followed. When he passed, it bit him.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.065.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Pain and Public Titles`,text:`Ra’s pain brought the other gods around him. The creator could describe what he felt, but he could not identify the unfamiliar source of his suffering. The powers he had brought into being could not readily help him. Isis approached with the knowledge that made this encounter possible.

She asked him for his name. Ra answered by describing his great works: heaven and earth, water, light and darkness, the hours and seasons. He named the forms by which he was known at different times of day. Yet the pain remained.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.065.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Secret Entrusted`,text:`Isis refused to mistake these titles for the secret she sought. At last Ra agreed to entrust the hidden name to her. The sharing carried a condition: it was to pass to her son Horus only with a divine oath.

Isis then brought the suffering to an end. The story never writes out a secret syllable for a listener to possess. What changes is the relation between its two figures. The name Ra guarded has crossed to Isis, and the power that threatened him has yielded to the knowledge she demanded.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.067`,candidateId:`AS-067`,definitionId:150,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`2596a890c56675e2be535359095356b495815e3bf9deb0d75a68001890698922`,variantSha256:`81341df492884fcc50114c651485b9db5c8ca1ca8efe6a35e6de5c93cb009fa9`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-egyptian.json`,packetSha256:`4a25bc19f39856bb8fd58fe27a1097c726ddc93e6b66ed9d82bc049235f32acc`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.067.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Marsh Refuge`,text:`Isis had hidden in the marshes with her son Horus. After the loss of Osiris, the child needed shelter from the dangers surrounding the family. The reeds provided concealment, but concealment could not remove every danger.

One day she found Horus sick. The child’s future, and all the care she had given to preserving it, stood at risk. Her distress rose beyond the place where they were hiding. She called to the boat that carried the sun through the sky.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.067.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Sun Boat Pauses`,text:`The sun’s movement stopped opposite her. An ordinary passage across the heavens had become a pause over one mother and her child. The call from the marsh had reached a company whose journey seemed far removed from that small refuge.

Thoth was sent from the boat to help. His power in this account belongs to wisdom and speech. He came to the place of need and restored Horus through words of protection. The appeal that began among concealed reeds had drawn a reply from the sun’s own company, joining the vulnerable refuge to the journey above it.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.067.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Recovery and Its Image`,text:`Help had reached the refuge, and the danger had passed. The child who had been sick was restored, and Isis’s desperate appeal had received an answer.

On the stone’s accompanying image, Thoth appears with an ibis head, holding the side of the reed shelter beside Isis. The gesture makes his role visible in another way. The returning life of Horus is not shown as a victory over his mother’s fear; it is the result of a call heard and help directed toward the vulnerable place where she stood.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.068`,candidateId:`AS-068`,definitionId:151,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`191e3574ce0e112af0f71211c21109e30718ab27f63a946d7d926a20e7375825`,variantSha256:`699a6c4ef6bd5ffeb45f7c6ce723961cc8fafaa009698e053681f3008bcd9616`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-egyptian.json`,packetSha256:`4a25bc19f39856bb8fd58fe27a1097c726ddc93e6b66ed9d82bc049235f32acc`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.068.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Training and Victory`,text:`Horus prepared for a struggle with Typhon. In Plutarch’s account, Osiris came from the other world to train his son and question what he thought worthy of a fighter. Horus answered with the wrongs done to his parents foremost in his mind.

As allegiance gathered around him, the fighting continued for many days. Horus prevailed. The captive Typhon was delivered to Isis in chains, and the victor might have expected that the conflict had reached its final decision.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.068.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Isis Releases the Captive`,text:`Isis chose to release him. Her choice did not satisfy Horus. He responded in anger, taking the royal diadem from his mother’s head. Hermes gave her a different head covering, described by the Greek author as resembling a cow’s head.

The victory had left a family divided over what should happen to its defeated enemy. Horus had shown strength in battle, but strength did not make his mother’s judgment identical to his own. The crown he seized could express his anger without resolving the question behind it.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.068.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Heir’s Hearing`,text:`Then Typhon brought an accusation about Horus’s legitimacy. The struggle moved into a divine hearing. Hermes spoke for Horus, and the gods decided that his claim was legitimate. Plutarch follows that judgment with two further victories over Typhon.

The selected episode therefore has more than one kind of outcome. A battlefield supplies a captive; Isis’s mercy releases him; a tribunal hears another claim and upholds the heir. Horus emerges supported, yet the telling preserves his harsh response to his mother. His power does not erase the decisions and arguments through which other figures have shaped the succession.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.073`,candidateId:`AS-073`,definitionId:156,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`48b6a93c06702c99e56d41986c60d75066fa581f7d3b52511895926c1e25184b`,variantSha256:`dc4f221aa897401fad1274516c7308608774d44491b065e840c9ffb3fa8d9cbb`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/sumerian.json`,packetSha256:`3b5c8afbe0a208195e2916a95bd5972f38f3c66f2ce26bdd1866d27064c30ccf`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.073.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Instructions Before the Gates`,text:`Before Inana left the world above, she prepared both her appearance and a safeguard. She took her regalia, dressed for the journey, and instructed her attendant Ninshubur to seek help if she did not return. Ninshubur was to appeal in turn to Enlil, Nanna and Enki.

At the entrance of the nether world, the gatekeeper Neti asked why she had come. Inana named the funeral of Gudgalana, husband of her elder sister Ereshkigal. Neti carried her answer to the ruler within. Ereshkigal ordered the gates opened one at a time, each governed by the laws of her realm.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.073.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Death and Rescue`,text:`At the first gate, Inana lost part of her regalia. The process continued through seven entrances: ornaments, signs of authority and finally her garment were removed. Each protest received the same unyielding answer. A power that commanded above could not carry its familiar protections unchanged into this place.

Inside, Inana confronted the nether world’s judges and was put to death. Three days and nights passed without her return.

Ninshubur kept the instructions. Enlil and Nanna refused the appeal, but Enki made two small beings and sent them with gifts that could restore life. They entered quietly and answered Ereshkigal’s cries of pain with sympathetic echoes. When offered a reward, they asked for Inana. Their work brought her back to life.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.073.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Price of Return`,text:`Returning was harder than simply walking away. The nether world demanded someone in her place, and its pursuers accompanied her upward. When they approached Ninshubur and other companions, Inana refused to surrender those who had mourned her absence. Their loyalty mattered in the decision she now faced.

Beyond them waited another encounter: Dumuzi upon his throne. The descent had ended, but the consequences of entering that realm had begun to move through the world above.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.074`,candidateId:`AS-074`,definitionId:157,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c3a0da35bace774538c381626f4835d0256aaf80a49a06b50bb4a3f9383dfe85`,variantSha256:`c8345501cd88b68ef0b31edaa878713dd20cf379f3b1a572767d0b19ba35c4df`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/sumerian.json`,packetSha256:`3b5c8afbe0a208195e2916a95bd5972f38f3c66f2ce26bdd1866d27064c30ccf`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.074.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Hospitality and Gifts`,text:`Inana travelled to Eridug, knowing that Enki would recognize her arrival. He instructed his minister Isimud to welcome her generously. There would be food, cool water and drink, with a place at the table fit for a distinguished visitor.

Host and guest drank together. During the banquet Enki gave Inana the me: powers of office, craft and human life. Some concerned kingship and priestly roles; others belonged to making, speaking, judgement and conduct. The gifts included troubling qualities as well as admirable ones. They were not simply a collection of pleasant talents.

Inana accepted them and left aboard the Boat of Heaven. By the time Enki’s clear judgement returned, the gifts were moving away from Eridug.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.074.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Pursuit of the Boat`,text:`He asked Isimud where each power had gone. Again and again, the minister answered that Enki himself had given it to Inana. Remembering the banquet did not make him content with its result. He sent Isimud after the boat with forces intended to bring it back.

Inana challenged the demand. How could Enki change his word after making the gifts? She called on Ninshubur, her faithful attendant, to keep the boat and its cargo from being taken. The pursuit continued in repeated attempts as new groups were sent to recover what had departed.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.074.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Arrival in Unug`,text:`The boat nevertheless reached Unug. Inana celebrated its arrival and addressed the city’s people; quays and festivities marked the gifts’ entrance into a new place. The encounter had shifted from a private banquet to a public event.

Enki’s hospitality set the journey in motion, but his later reversal made the transfer a contest. Once he had spoken the gifts into another’s keeping, neither his change of mind nor his minister’s reminders could simply turn the boat around.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.075`,candidateId:`AS-075`,definitionId:158,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c94d5840684e597cfea911c7a4ca543ba781d84d12aa270c6469f52d7ed006c2`,variantSha256:`09437df0810ff388767da1c36a1560ec307b3287c86d8024055b5b422f28ca40`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/sumerian.json`,packetSha256:`3b5c8afbe0a208195e2916a95bd5972f38f3c66f2ce26bdd1866d27064c30ccf`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.075.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Seven Gates`,text:`Neti came to Ereshkigal with news from the outer gate. Inana had arrived, dressed in the regalia of her authority above. She said she had come for the funeral of Ereshkigal’s husband, Gudgalana.

The ruler listened and gave a precise command. Admit the visitor through the seven gates, but open each separately. At every entrance, remove an item of Inana’s regalia. The rules of the nether world would govern her passage, whatever power she carried from elsewhere.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.075.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Listening Visitors`,text:`Neti obeyed. Inana’s questions could not change the process. By the time she reached the interior, the signs of her outward authority had been taken away. She confronted the realm’s judges and was put to death.

Days later, two small beings entered Ereshkigal’s domain. Enki had made and sent them after Ninshubur sought help for her absent mistress. The newcomers did not arrive with an army or a demand to overrule the sovereign.

They found Ereshkigal in pain. When she cried out, they answered with matching words of concern. They listened closely enough to echo what troubled her. Their response moved her to offer them a reward.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.075.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Revival and Its Condition`,text:`They would not accept the water or field offered to them. Instead, they asked for Inana. When their request was granted, they used the life-restoring gifts Enki had provided, and the dead visitor returned to life.

Ereshkigal’s authority had not vanished. The realm still required a substitute before Inana could depart, and its pursuers followed her toward the world above. Yet the approach that reached the ruler was different from the challenge that had brought Inana down. The small visitors answered a sufferer, and through that answer obtained what force had not secured.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.077`,candidateId:`AS-077`,definitionId:160,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6f848e4342e7d0294fe4c7ec8fd989a997d0975e40f0fcc3bf1157a2478910a8`,variantSha256:`58718c89b4fa703a3d59cd0aad63506a6235ca6cfe7cc751b5cd5eb8a95f2831`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/sumerian.json`,packetSha256:`3b5c8afbe0a208195e2916a95bd5972f38f3c66f2ce26bdd1866d27064c30ccf`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.077.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Remembered Plan`,text:`Before Inana set out for the nether world, she trusted Ninshubur with a plan. If her mistress failed to return, the attendant was to mourn her publicly and seek help from three gods. Each appeal had its place in the instructions; the journey below would leave someone above responsible for remembering them.

Three days and three nights passed. Inana did not return. Ninshubur began the mourning and went to Enlil’s house. She pleaded that her mistress should not be left among the dead.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.077.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Three Appeals`,text:`Enlil refused. Inana had chosen to seek authority in a realm beyond her own, he answered; entering it had consequences. Ninshubur left without the rescue she had sought.

She next approached Nanna. Again the appeal failed. The second refusal could have ended her efforts, but the instructions had named one more place. She continued to Enki.

Enki responded differently. He was troubled by what had happened to Inana. From a tiny beginning he made two rescuers and sent them into the nether world with the means to restore life. Their task depended on care and sympathetic answers, rather than a battle at the gates.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.077.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Loyalty Recognized`,text:`They succeeded. Inana returned to life, though leaving the realm required a substitute, and pursuers accompanied her upward.

When they found Ninshubur, they would have taken the attendant in her mistress’s place. Inana stopped them. Ninshubur had mourned her and faithfully followed the plan; she would not surrender the one who had worked to bring her back.

Ninshubur had neither broken the gates nor commanded the nether world. Her part lay in remembering, moving between houses and continuing after refusal. The return that seemed to belong to gods and rescuers began with the attendant who did not abandon her instructions.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.081`,candidateId:`AS-081`,definitionId:161,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d54ca04464a6e927af434fce11f73cd6d34b439b0105d0fc1ffc0602d38ddf3f`,variantSha256:`b5e1363af1c07b0382b7121cf2e879bb8ea1c9d06d98ecf22d861d8bc0df99ad`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/sumerian.json`,packetSha256:`3b5c8afbe0a208195e2916a95bd5972f38f3c66f2ce26bdd1866d27064c30ccf`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.081.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Messages Across Mountains`,text:`Messages had travelled back and forth between Unug and distant Aratta. Enmerkar demanded precious materials and service for his city’s sacred building projects. The lord of Aratta resisted, claimed divine favour for his own city, and answered with challenges. The messenger crossed the mountain ranges, carrying each ruler’s words to the other.

One exchange concerned grain; another demanded an extraordinary sceptre. The contest moved through gifts, threats and tests of skill. Neither city’s claim could simply erase the other’s reply. With every journey, the messenger had to preserve another speech and deliver it accurately after the long crossing.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.081.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Words Set in Clay`,text:`At last Enmerkar’s instructions grew too extensive for the messenger to repeat. The human voice and memory that had carried the rivalry so far could no longer hold the whole demand.

Enmerkar took clay and worked it into a surface. He set his message upon it in writing. The words could now travel with the messenger instead of depending entirely on what he could remember and say.

The messenger took the tablet through the mountains and placed it before the lord of Aratta. The marks confronting the ruler were unfamiliar and disturbing. Enmerkar had found a new form for his demand, but its arrival did not instantly settle the contest.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.081.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aratta Answers`,text:`A storm then brought growth to Aratta’s land. Its ruler pointed to the renewed abundance as evidence that his city had not been abandoned by Inana. The written message and the local harvest gave the rivals different ways to state their claims.

The messenger had carried something new, yet he still arrived between powers that could answer one another. Giving words a lasting surface changed the journey of the message; it did not turn a difficult rivalry into an unquestioned victory.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.085`,candidateId:`AS-085`,definitionId:163,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0c8ddcf138bdc88d6cf0bc476eb67b2b5a3e88a8ed1442fabd4f384175623a56`,variantSha256:`032fc289c11b13c5fb2ab5130ef9f03a38cf617ad91b55471777d71f668b2b0f`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/akkadian-mesopotamian.json`,packetSha256:`f2d1144a6f99531dfdb30bd58579eb7c438c5e18167100275519bc3fa8115d46`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.085.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Authority Promised`,text:`The gods prepared a place for Marduk in their assembly. He took his position before those who had called him to act, while the danger awaiting them remained beyond the gathering.

They promised him authority. His command would have consequence, and his decisions would establish boundaries that other gods could not cross. The appointment was immense: they were giving him rule over the world, expecting him to protect those who placed their trust in him and to confront the force opposing them.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.085.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Garment Test`,text:`Yet the promise still needed a visible test. A garment was set in the middle of the assembly. The gods asked Marduk to speak so that it would disappear, then to speak again so that it would return.

Nothing in this moment required the movement of an army. The test rested on one object and one command. The garment gave the whole gathering something to observe, a small thing through which the power they had proclaimed could become clear.

Marduk spoke, and the garment vanished. He commanded again, and it reappeared. The assembly had watched the same place through both changes. What they had entrusted to him was no longer only a declaration.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.085.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Kingship Acknowledged`,text:`The gods rejoiced and acknowledged him as king. They gave him a sceptre, a throne and a ring, together with a weapon for the struggle to come. The recognition linked his tested command to the task they expected him to undertake.

Then the gathering sent him on his way. The danger had not disappeared with the garment, and victory had not yet been won. Marduk left the assembly carrying authority that had been promised, demonstrated and publicly recognized before the confrontation began.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.086`,candidateId:`AS-086`,definitionId:164,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`e6be4bbccd88d49a812103108300862e130326d9b4ad83c35602068a5f6e0553`,variantSha256:`ed9dacef947310b534f4eba7b01439ce7003cf58534359ad3d55995547eeb5a4`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r03-sumerian-akkadian-egyptian.json`,reportSha256:`7af0f5def2e1b1faa8fbfad0906e3efa35b57098dd8f82084a17a0df22229c30`,packetPath:`traditions/akkadian-mesopotamian.json`,packetSha256:`f2d1144a6f99531dfdb30bd58579eb7c438c5e18167100275519bc3fa8115d46`,reviewer:`Independent bounded reviewer /root/persian_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"Coordinator authored all three packets; reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain untouched."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.086.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Waters and Gathering Forces`,text:`Before heaven and earth had names, Tiamat’s waters mingled with those of Apsu. No field had taken shape and no marsh could be seen. The generations of gods arose within this beginning, before the places of the later world were established.

When conflict divided those divine generations, Tiamat gathered a force at her side. The host prepared for battle. Formidable creatures stood among it: serpents, raging hounds, scorpion figures and other beings whose appearance inspired fear. The poem gives their gathering the scale of a crisis within the divine order.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.086.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Kingu and the Challenger`,text:`Tiamat placed Kingu above the forces. He would lead their advance, give the signal for battle and direct the confrontation. She made him her chosen spouse and entrusted him with authority, laying the Tablets of Destiny upon his breast.

Marduk came to face this host. Kingu faltered at his approach, while Tiamat continued to challenge the authority he claimed. Marduk answered by calling her to meet him in battle. Both sides now carried declarations of command; neither declaration could settle the struggle on its own.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.086.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Defeat and Seized Authority`,text:`Tiamat cried out, and the opposing forces prepared their weapons. She and Marduk advanced. He caught her within his net and drove his powerful wind against her. Their confrontation ended with her defeat and death.

Her followers scattered in fear, but Marduk captured them and broke their weapons. He overcame Kingu and took the Tablets of Destiny that Tiamat had entrusted to him.

The authority she had bestowed could no longer hold the host together. The mother present at the world’s unnamed beginning had become the defeated leader of its divine conflict, while the rival who confronted her now carried the tablets away.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.097`,candidateId:`AS-097`,definitionId:173,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ed771d07ce39bc4593ff3eac657e46491c2619e40f1d5769968f0bb36fbba1cf`,variantSha256:`2c9a07d0f847043e66073739634891f56eceade0142996bcf7ee36e4b8d0eccd`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.097.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Compassion in the Nest`,text:`At the foot of a remote mountain lay a child whom his father had rejected. Sam had feared the boy’s white hair and let that fear overrule his care. Far above, Simurgh had made her nest in a place human feet could not readily reach.

She saw the child while seeking food and carried him upward. What began as a raptor’s discovery changed into compassion. She would not let her young harm him. Instead, she made room for him among them and fed and tended him. The boy grew through many seasons beneath wings that had become his family’s shelter.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.097.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Father Below the Mountain`,text:`News of the remarkable youth eventually traveled down from the mountain. Sam heard it, and remorse turned rumor into a search. He came with his people and looked up at the nest. There was his son, alive and strong, yet the father could not climb high enough to meet him.

Simurgh saw what had brought him. She told Zal that his own people waited below and that another future lay among them. The young man was distressed. He knew the nest as a home and the bird as a parent; he feared that this farewell meant he had ceased to be wanted.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.097.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Farewell Without Abandonment`,text:`She answered with the same care that had sustained him. Sending him down was not a withdrawal of love. She gave him a feather so that he could call upon her in need and remain within her protection even when distance lay between them.

Then she carried him to Sam. The father bowed in gratitude and asked his son’s forgiveness. Simurgh returned to the heights, leaving Zal to enter a new world. Her greatest act in the episode was more than rescuing an abandoned child. She had nurtured him until he could grow beyond the nest, and had offered a bond that would survive his departure.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.102`,candidateId:`AS-102`,definitionId:174,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d77d564228195810717fe0ff55ca5e5eeef4835266b77a791087ec8aa50fd692`,variantSha256:`62bfc12e342b86e4d80fe27e3ca9b5fb68071c7b515882e31ec6488bcefbea3f`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.102.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Journey Nearly Ends`,text:`The desert gave Rostam no place to rest from its heat. He and Rakhsh had traveled onward after the danger among the reeds, but the new day stripped away their strength. Sun burned above them and the ground burned beneath. The rider searched for water and found no sign of it.

At last he could go no farther. He thought of Kai Kavus, still captive and waiting for the help that Rostam had promised to bring. He prayed that the king would not be abandoned even if the rescuer failed. Then he lay down in the sand.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.102.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Ram and the Spring`,text:`A ram passed nearby. It was well nourished, unlike creatures nearly overcome by thirst. Rostam noticed the difference. Somewhere this animal must drink; otherwise it could not flourish here. Its ordinary needs offered a direction when every other direction had proved empty.

He struggled to his feet and led Rakhsh after it. The ram’s path brought them to a clear spring. Rostam drank, watered his horse and washed the desert’s heat from their bodies. Water gave back the strength that courage alone had not preserved.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.102.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Guide Has Gone`,text:`When he could attend to anything beyond immediate thirst, he looked for the ram. It was nowhere to be seen. The creature that had arrived at the edge of despair had gone once its guidance was no longer needed. Rostam recognized the encounter as a wonder worked for his sake and gave thanks to the divine power he had called upon.

Horse and rider could continue toward the captive king. The ram had not fought their enemies or carried their burden. It had done something smaller and indispensable: it had shown a living path through a place that seemed empty of life. Before the mighty warrior could rescue anyone else, he had to read that sign and follow.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.104`,candidateId:`AS-104`,definitionId:175,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f616378a784ac08b42002c2260c48aacbdb698f18eaa4fd055769492bd7b8b83`,variantSha256:`3cffef61c6bd84c08643e73a77bca78b3acae66c48b7a5031ec8d9f3845e586d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.104.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Promise and Seven Stages`,text:`Esfandiar had driven his father’s enemies away and returned expecting the promised throne. Goshtasp answered that the work remained unfinished: the prince’s sisters were still prisoners in Arjasp’s fortress. Rescue them, he said, and the promise would be fulfilled.

Three roads led there. The longest offered safety and pasture; a second crossed a desert; the shortest demanded only seven stages but was filled with danger. Esfandiar chose the shortest and went with his army. Advice against the choice did not persuade him.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.104.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Fortress Requires Cunning`,text:`At the first stage he overcame wolves, at the next lion-shaped foes, then a dragon. A magician threatened him farther along, and a mighty bird barred another part of the way. The dangers changed, but the travelers could not remain behind to recover. Then snow closed around the army. Esfandiar prayed, and the crisis passed. A flood challenged the final stage before the fortress.

After all those victories, the walls nearly defeated his resolve. They were so broad and formidable that force seemed useless. He paused and devised another way. Disguised as a merchant, he entered with a caravan bearing fine goods and chests in which selected warriors were hidden.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.104.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Rescue and the Withheld Crown`,text:`Inside, he found his sisters. They recognized his voice but wisely kept their recognition secret. He won the ruler’s trust, arranged a feast and brought his hidden companions out when the moment came. Fighting overtook the fortress; Esfandiar defeated Arjasp and signaled the army outside. At last he could bring his sisters home.

There were celebrations on his return, but the promised crown remained beyond his reach. His father soon demanded another undertaking. The road had shown Esfandiar’s endurance, courage and resourcefulness. It had also revealed a crueler uncertainty: a son might meet every danger set before him and still find the person who set them unwilling to keep his word.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.106`,candidateId:`AS-106`,definitionId:176,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`09f1d93829c571164a106ff35f39eebd2a8010ecb3c9de7383c5facde93d5474`,variantSha256:`3ab49572d1cb45c8c3f198a494f1132fee68f812bc1850cd14e162936f4354be`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.106.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Terror Seeking Approval`,text:`Zahhak had allowed a deceiver to guide his choices, and the consequences became part of his body. Two serpents grew from his shoulders. No attempt to remove them succeeded. Fear of their demands joined his hunger for power, and suffering spread through the land he ruled.

The throne could compel obedience, but Zahhak wanted obedience to look like approval. He ordered a declaration of his justice and expected the people to endorse it. Those who stood before him knew the danger of refusal. The court seemed ready to produce the praise he required.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.106.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Petitioner Who Names the King`,text:`Then a petitioner’s cry interrupted the assembly. Zahhak had the man brought inside and asked him to name the person who had wronged him. The answer came without the protection of flattery. Kaveh, a blacksmith, named the king himself. His family had suffered under Zahhak’s rule, and the son still left to him had been taken.

Zahhak granted the son’s release. He spoke gently, as though the favor might close the grievance and turn a threatened father into a witness for the throne. Then he asked Kaveh to sign the declaration.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.106.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Consent Refused`,text:`The blacksmith would not do it. He tore the document and scattered its pieces, refusing to let a single act of relief erase the wrongs that had made relief necessary. Before the astonished assembly he left the court. Zahhak did not stop him.

Outside, Kaveh raised his working apron as a banner and gathered people toward Feredun. Inside, the king retained his crown and the serpents at his shoulders, but the declaration had failed in the hands of the person whose consent he sought. Zahhak’s power could frighten an entire court. In that encounter it could not make one man give injustice another name.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.108`,candidateId:`AS-108`,definitionId:177,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`8039861f36a7cd5875c13579311e8cd6f261db641f7e343beeeb4318b44ebfa5`,variantSha256:`8f08e707cb641d633f89ed4df7ae6f7cb6d2a8be6166ecb0f7be921fabdced2d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r12-persian.json`,reportSha256:`511700f6460051d93981af1d4b89f791cbce4aa861360f9235b98f9792248e67`,packetPath:`traditions/persian.json`,packetSha256:`cdebb8522867435aa0f35ddfa6c66c7dfee369b15fdb953afdaaea787f4d4962`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:47:04.089Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research, not coordinator. Coordinator read all current story/brief fields and reopened selected complete relevant Zimmern episodes, named scholarly discussion and institutional descriptions.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.108.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Apron Raised`,text:`When Kaveh left Zahhak’s court, he carried no royal permission to speak. He had refused the declaration of the king’s justice and torn it before the nobles. In the marketplace, he told the people what had happened and called them to seek Feredun rather than endure the tyranny in silence.

He needed a sign that could rise above a gathering crowd. The answer was already on his body: the leather apron he wore at his work. It protected him when he struck metal with a hammer. Now he took it off and lifted it on a lance. A useful object became a visible invitation.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.108.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Leather Becomes a Standard`,text:`People gathered beneath the raised leather. Kaveh led them away from the city, and the apron traveled with them as their banner. Its authority began with the deed they had heard about, not with silk, jewels or a claim inherited from the throne.

Feredun received the procession and accepted the sign. As he prepared to challenge Zahhak, he dressed the working leather in splendid cloth and hung jewels upon it. The improvised banner became a royal standard. Yet the richer surface did not change where it had begun: with a blacksmith who would not sign a lie.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.108.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Splendour Keeps Its Beginning`,text:`Feredun’s followers carried it toward the ruler they opposed. Alongside it went the cow-headed mace the challenger had designed. One object remembered the care that had preserved him in childhood; the other remembered the public refusal that had gathered people around him.

The banner joined those memories to the undertaking ahead. It could be seen from a distance, but its meaning depended upon events close enough to tell: a petition in court, a torn declaration, an apron lifted in the marketplace and an alliance made beneath it. In the tale, splendor follows that history rather than replacing it.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.110`,candidateId:`AS-110`,definitionId:178,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`70ee46e4447cf5109362ff3f3e0f50a4aa90cfb4386cd5cd7c79e65d40817343`,variantSha256:`1e6b007f821794c270926a4ec8322f027be03be85395e1a8ff7f5714d3df9a16`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r21-anatolian.json`,reportSha256:`a052453e50deb8318cd4a5daf1148acbadc9993054c39fcea368100fad7beba3`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-anatolian.json`,packetSha256:`173670a1fbc6eb274364fda5ac2f078ae4c6d1fc393e266c64e75f65c3dcdb56`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:23:40.400Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read assigned actual prose/prompts and primary translations; added two actually read direct witnesses, not a fabricated third.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.110.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Planning and a Complicated Bargain`,text:`Inara answered a defeat with preparation. The serpent had overcome the Storm God, who called upon the other gods for help. Instead of arranging another contest on the same terms, she made a feast.

The abundance mattered. She prepared vessels of drink on a large scale, making the invitation attractive. The surviving account names different beverages, but this retelling gives no recipe or directions for reproducing the gathering. The feast belongs to the myth's plan, not to a modern drinking challenge.

She also needed a human participant. Inara went to Hupasiya and asked for his help. He demanded a sexual bargain before agreeing, and the text reports the exchange. That condition complicates the alliance; it does not need an erotic image to remain part of the story.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.110.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Host and Hidden Helper`,text:`Inara brought the helper to the site and concealed him. She readied herself, then called the serpent from its pit to eat and drink. Her public role was that of host. The hidden human supplied the part of the plan the guests could not see.

The serpent came with its children, according to a partly restored phrase. They consumed the prepared drink and stayed outside their pits. Hupasiya emerged with a rope and bound the serpent. The Storm God returned and killed the bound enemy, with the other gods present.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.110.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Victory and Confinement`,text:`Inara's planning had changed the outcome, but the full account does not close with an uncomplicated reward for everyone. Later she placed Hupasiya in a house on a rock and forbade him to look toward his wife and children. After twenty days, he looked and asked to return home. The next passage is broken.

Her resourcefulness and the human cost remain together in the surviving account. No certain fate is invented for Hupasiya, and the stolen-heart-and-eyes version of the serpent battle is kept outside this plan.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.112`,candidateId:`AS-112`,definitionId:179,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0b01f40e9e4f43fe9e041500c2335c2f84340d716a599c2ac4e82e2d8f258141`,variantSha256:`a539bdcb796c4c8a58c88acc26adabc38b3e4fe87aa84dfc91c1edfb807c5b61`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r21-anatolian.json`,reportSha256:`a052453e50deb8318cd4a5daf1148acbadc9993054c39fcea368100fad7beba3`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-anatolian.json`,packetSha256:`173670a1fbc6eb274364fda5ac2f078ae4c6d1fc393e266c64e75f65c3dcdb56`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:23:40.400Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read assigned actual prose/prompts and primary translations; added two actually read direct witnesses, not a fabricated third.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.112.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Departure and Failed Growth`,text:`Telipinu left, and the world lost more than his visible presence. The beginning of the surviving version is broken. It includes shoes placed on opposite feet, but the lost words do not let us reconstruct a complete quarrel that caused his departure.

Smoke filled the house; the hearth's logs failed. Animals rejected their young. Telipinu carried away the things by which fields and living bodies prospered, then concealed himself in a marsh. A plant grew over him, its precise identity unresolved by the translation.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.112.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Search and Checked Anger`,text:`Grain and emmer no longer flourished. Springs and pastures dried. Hunger reached humans and gods. The Sun God assembled a vast divine feast, yet eating and drinking brought no satisfaction. Abundance placed on a table could not replace what had disappeared from the land.

The Storm God recognized his son's absence. Gods searched, and an eagle returned without finding him. Hannahanna urged the Storm God to search in person. When that effort also failed, she sent a bee. His doubts about its size survive; the continuation of that search is damaged in this selected version.

Later surviving passages return to Telipinu amid anger and thunder. Kamrusepa appears and checks the anger, though the wording of one eagle-wing action is uncertain. The text then moves through ritual speech and analogies concerned with release and restored relations. Their details are not recast here as a practical cure or a modern diagnosis.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.112.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Recognition Restored`,text:`Near the end, a return is preserved. Telipinu comes back to his house and attends to the land. Smoke and haze depart. Mothers recognize children; sheep and cattle recognize their young. He acknowledges the king and queen and supplies them with renewed life and future.

The tablet joins disruption with restoration, while gaps remain between them. The surviving story holds to that shape. His withdrawal threatens ordinary nourishment and attachment; his return restores those relations without requiring the missing pieces to become an invented complete adventure.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.113`,candidateId:`AS-113`,definitionId:180,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ae10f5d4a4b6ab71d8da59c4c7fd6610abc8ae9cf3960c54079da6b65b8883ce`,variantSha256:`37af0667292859cbd927ee83f273cfaa89ffea5e971295ffa44639d9669b5821`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r21-anatolian.json`,reportSha256:`a052453e50deb8318cd4a5daf1148acbadc9993054c39fcea368100fad7beba3`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-anatolian.json`,packetSha256:`173670a1fbc6eb274364fda5ac2f078ae4c6d1fc393e266c64e75f65c3dcdb56`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:23:40.400Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read assigned actual prose/prompts and primary translations; added two actually read direct witnesses, not a fabricated third.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.113.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Gods Remain Hungry`,text:`Hunger had reached the gods. Telipinu was missing, and a feast assembled by the Sun God did not satisfy the gathering. The lack could not be solved by putting more food before those who sat together.

The search had already traveled far. The Sun God's eagle was sent to high mountains, deep valleys, and quiet waters. It returned with no discovery. Greater and lesser gods sought Telipinu, but their collective effort also failed.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.113.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Father Told to Search`,text:`The Storm God came to Hannahanna, worried about the absent son and the starvation surrounding them. Her first response was direct. He should do something himself; he should go and look for Telipinu. She did not immediately relieve him of the task by assigning another servant.

He searched. In the surviving account he reached a city gate and could not open it. His hammer and peg broke. A gap follows, and then he wrapped himself in his garment and sat down. The father who had feared the consequences now had a failed effort of his own to add to those already reported.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.113.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Hannahanna Sends the Bee`,text:`Hannahanna changed the search. She sent a bee to find the missing god. The Storm God answered with doubt: so many gods had tried, and this creature's wings and body were small. His objection treated physical scale as evidence against usefulness.

The selected passage preserves her choice without recording a long explanation of it. Hannahanna's action sets the little messenger beside the powerful searchers. She does not turn the bee into a giant or claim that failure elsewhere is proof that another attempt must fail.

The next part of this first version is damaged. The surviving exchange ends at the dispatch and the objection; what the bee does next is lost at this point. Hannahanna's role here is specific: she urges the father to act, then appoints a different searcher when the effort stalls. Her identity need not absorb every mother goddess of Anatolia to make that decision matter.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.133`,candidateId:`AS-133`,definitionId:193,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`3016e07ea5e0f297db012fb151414d88a08d97b4e51891d796ef083cff4ec00a`,variantSha256:`0347d7d7732d6b423f28844492501c78514f972fa490c811c5ee36dbd51018d0`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r08-haida-mapuche-ramayana.json`,reportSha256:`ee5c1737ea7d4680d2b163b345803b85e9afac4c94a13deae19e11775b3728c2`,packetPath:`traditions/south-asian-sanskrit-ramayana.json`,packetSha256:`ecd664c5b2b4a8b624a992f7f5c76a7121659045a35e0066cf0ae64acb7b3b08`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent reviewer /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator authored all three packets; this reviewer authored none. Only the two assigned review reports may be changed.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.133.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Changed Morning`,text:`The celebration expected in Ayodhya has become a demand for departure. Kaikeyi tells Rama that another brother is to receive the throne and that Rama must live in the forest. His father does not give him the news in the familiar way.

Rama hears it without answering with an attempt to seize the kingdom. He accepts the forest life as the fulfillment of the king's promise. The change will reach beyond his title: he speaks of leaving royal comfort for braided hair and rough clothing, and of remaining away for fourteen years.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.133.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Duty and Hurt`,text:`Yet composure does not remove every hurt. One question troubles him. Why has his father not spoken to him himself? The silence between them weighs on the conversation more heavily than the loss of the ceremony that had been prepared.

Rama says that wealth is not the purpose by which he intends to live. He understands this departure through duty, and treats the promise as something that his own action must now carry. What others had arranged as a royal succession becomes a decision about how he will answer a difficult command.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.133.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Before the Departure`,text:`There are people he must tell before he goes. His mother and Sita are part of the life that this news changes, and leaving the city cannot be reduced to a single exchange about a throne. The forest lies ahead, but the first steps of exile pass through relationships inside the palace.

The expected crown remains behind as his attention turns toward departure. Rama does not know every trial that the coming years will bring. In this moment he knows the length of the exile, the promise he has accepted, and the people whose lives must be addressed before a royal morning becomes the beginning of a forest journey.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.134`,candidateId:`AS-134`,definitionId:194,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`45de37a35b50e377a106d34900a3db4b8f29e68a2ac9d771a6176eb6cfd13746`,variantSha256:`b9511344ba21397c795c1f7ac1be0abb41aea0eb36440ec60478f51ee2695411`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r08-haida-mapuche-ramayana.json`,reportSha256:`ee5c1737ea7d4680d2b163b345803b85e9afac4c94a13deae19e11775b3728c2`,packetPath:`traditions/south-asian-sanskrit-ramayana.json`,packetSha256:`ecd664c5b2b4a8b624a992f7f5c76a7121659045a35e0066cf0ae64acb7b3b08`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent reviewer /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator authored all three packets; this reviewer authored none. Only the two assigned review reports may be changed.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.134.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Her Answer`,text:`Rama speaks of going to the forest, and Sita answers him. The proposal that she should remain behind does not end the matter. She has a voice in the conversation and a clear intention: she will accompany him.

She argues from the marriage they share and the commitments by which she understands it. Then she moves from principle to the imagined ground of the journey. If the path is difficult, she says she can go before him through the sharp grass and thorns. The person being told to stay in safety presents herself as someone able to take a difficult step.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.134.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Forest She Imagines`,text:`Sita recalls that her parents have already taught her how to act. She does not need to be treated as if she has never learned judgment. The forest contains wild animals and places far from the city, but those conditions do not make her withdraw her decision.

She also imagines what the forest can hold besides fear. There are rivers and mountains, small lakes and groves. There are flowering lotus ponds with water birds. She wishes to see these places with Rama rather than to hear later about a landscape that he alone has crossed.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.134.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Ready to Share the Hardship`,text:`Daily life will change as well. She says she can live on roots and fruit and will not demand that palace comfort follow them into exile. Her answer joins affection with practical willingness; it makes a shared journey something she actively chooses.

The discussion does not erase the hardship ahead. Sita has named it and still remains ready. At the threshold between the familiar house and the unfamiliar forest, she refuses to let the plan be settled without her. Before the long search and war, this part of her story begins with speech: a determined answer about where she intends to stand.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.135`,candidateId:`AS-135`,definitionId:195,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`2e2633290c1396d584b0dae51b3a3c139ea01415179b5bd5f3c2d337a024d935`,variantSha256:`19c15cf6d56b8c70f671f606f1066d1616614d4366af14a3c5d73031d1d4abfa`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r08-haida-mapuche-ramayana.json`,reportSha256:`ee5c1737ea7d4680d2b163b345803b85e9afac4c94a13deae19e11775b3728c2`,packetPath:`traditions/south-asian-sanskrit-ramayana.json`,packetSha256:`ecd664c5b2b4a8b624a992f7f5c76a7121659045a35e0066cf0ae64acb7b3b08`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent reviewer /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator authored all three packets; this reviewer authored none. Only the two assigned review reports may be changed.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.135.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Facing the Sea`,text:`Hanuman faces the sea on a task for Rama and the vanaras. Somewhere beyond it is Lanka, and the search for Sita requires a crossing that cannot be made by an ordinary walk along the shore. His purpose gives the immense distance a direction.

Before he leaps, his body gathers itself. His tail curls behind him. He braces his arms against the mountain, bends at the waist and draws his feet into position. The preparation is concentrated rather than casual: he looks along the way he means to take, and the company around him hears his declaration.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.135.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Leap Begins`,text:`He says that he will go to Lanka with the speed of an arrow released by Rama. The comparison places his strength inside the mission entrusted to him. He intends to find Sita and does not describe the sea as a reason to abandon the attempt.

Then the leap begins. The force lifts flowering trees into his passage. For a little while they follow him, as if the mountain itself has sent companions after the departing figure. Their brief motion makes the power of the departure visible before they fall away.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.135.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Blossoms on the Water`,text:`Blossoms scatter across the water. Where the trees can no longer follow, their flowers remain for a moment, bright against the vast sea. Hanuman continues beyond them; the shore that supported his crouch is now behind him, and the search lies ahead.

This is the beginning of the crossing, not the completion of every challenge on the route. The strength that raised him has become forward motion, and the ordinary boundary of water no longer keeps him beside the mountain. He carries the purpose of a messenger through open space, with the falling flowers marking only the first span of the journey.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.137`,candidateId:`AS-137`,definitionId:197,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0a18b0d997cb42159aed5a5f7bd019e3d3a8c36a7bf150d434a99c9c6c6536a1`,variantSha256:`9999ff398490f9e84550552271f9729500ef352beb3bc7eb9936f3b8a4563b5e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r08-haida-mapuche-ramayana.json`,reportSha256:`ee5c1737ea7d4680d2b163b345803b85e9afac4c94a13deae19e11775b3728c2`,packetPath:`traditions/south-asian-sanskrit-ramayana.json`,packetSha256:`ecd664c5b2b4a8b624a992f7f5c76a7121659045a35e0066cf0ae64acb7b3b08`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent reviewer /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator authored all three packets; this reviewer authored none. Only the two assigned review reports may be changed.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.137.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Request to Join`,text:`Lakshmana hears Rama and Sita discussing the journey into exile. His eyes fill with tears, but he does not remain only a listener. He asks to accompany them, saying that he will carry his bow and go ahead on the forest path.

Rama tries to persuade him to stay. The request is not a rejection of his affection or courage. Their mothers will remain in Ayodhya, and someone must attend to them in the grief and uncertainty that the departure has brought. To leave is also to decide who will care for those left behind.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.137.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Work for the Journey`,text:`Lakshmana answers gently. He believes that Bharata will honor Kausalya and Sumitra. He does not accept that care inside the city requires him to abandon the companions he wishes to follow. The argument stays close to particular people rather than becoming a contest over who loves Rama most.

Then he describes what his presence would mean in practice. Along with bow and arrows, he will carry a spade and a basket. He can show the path, gather roots and fruit, and attend to needs that continue whether the others are waking or sleeping. Loyalty takes the form of daily work.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.137.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Permission and Preparation`,text:`Rama is pleased by this answer and gives him permission to take leave of his friends. He also asks him to bring the weapons kept with their teacher, gifts connected in this witness with Janaka's earlier ceremony. Preparations replace the attempt to keep him in the city.

Lakshmana now has a place in the departure that he heard being planned. His insistence has been heard, and the work he offered can travel with him. His chosen companionship includes the basket, the path and the repeated tasks by which three exiles can continue together.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.138`,candidateId:`AS-138`,definitionId:198,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`8807fc61ed4434c3adcf804449095b93354edcd4d6976cdc9757c6148b850691`,variantSha256:`ed429b41af704c5a2d4bf2cafe3e1c1c64fd1f974dc8e8cf0de22055836e78a5`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r08-haida-mapuche-ramayana.json`,reportSha256:`ee5c1737ea7d4680d2b163b345803b85e9afac4c94a13deae19e11775b3728c2`,packetPath:`traditions/south-asian-sanskrit-ramayana.json`,packetSha256:`ecd664c5b2b4a8b624a992f7f5c76a7121659045a35e0066cf0ae64acb7b3b08`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent reviewer /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator authored all three packets; this reviewer authored none. Only the two assigned review reports may be changed.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.138.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Stranger on the Path`,text:`On the way to Panchavati, Rama and Lakshmana meet a bird of extraordinary size and strength. In the forest, such an unfamiliar presence makes them cautious. Before accepting him as a companion, they ask who he is.

The bird answers in a gentle voice. He tells Rama that he is a friend of his father, Dasharatha. What first looked threatening becomes a connection with the home that Rama has left behind. The forest encounter is no longer only a question about danger on the road.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.138.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Name and an Offer`,text:`Rama receives him with respect and asks his name and lineage without haste. The bird tells a long account of ancestry. Within it he names Aruna, identifies Sampati as his elder brother, and introduces himself as Jatayu. He is not an anonymous creature standing at the edge of the path.

Jatayu offers help if Rama wishes to accept it. The forest is difficult, and he can be a companion at their dwelling. When Rama and Lakshmana go out together, he says that he can look after Sita. The offer belongs to a continuing relationship, rather than to a demand for authority over the travelers.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.138.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Friendship Welcomed`,text:`Rama welcomes him gladly and embraces him. The friendship with Dasharatha has been spoken of repeatedly, and Rama's response gives that remembered bond a place in the present. A connection from the city can still matter among trees and unfamiliar paths.

They proceed toward Panchavati together. Nothing in this meeting tells every later event of Jatayu's life, and no future fight is needed to give it its meaning. The travelers have found a named ally who can speak, remember and offer care. At this stage of the journey, strength has announced itself through friendship before any struggle begins.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.139`,candidateId:`AS-139`,definitionId:199,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0fbbcafc8cc32f1842cbed4fb64e2a76f9ae3ed02838a7a6312ff92b81389475`,variantSha256:`e7e38d97bf10fcacf12e20d4c032b7d8319a076458422b7a80411128c69ba711`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r08-haida-mapuche-ramayana.json`,reportSha256:`ee5c1737ea7d4680d2b163b345803b85e9afac4c94a13deae19e11775b3728c2`,packetPath:`traditions/south-asian-sanskrit-ramayana.json`,packetSha256:`ecd664c5b2b4a8b624a992f7f5c76a7121659045a35e0066cf0ae64acb7b3b08`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent reviewer /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator authored all three packets; this reviewer authored none. Only the two assigned review reports may be changed.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.139.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Contest at Height`,text:`Sampati recalls a flight he once made with his younger brother Jatayu. They were eager to test their strength against one another, and the challenge drew them far above the ground. Following the sun promised a measure of achievement greater than the heights they already knew.

As they rose, the earth changed beneath them. Towns became small enough to resemble chariot wheels. Forests looked like patches of pasture, and rivers ran across the landscape like threads. The familiar scale of places fell away while the two brothers continued into the sky.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.139.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Wings for Shelter`,text:`The height did not remain a triumph. Heat, exhaustion and fear overtook them, and they struggled to keep their sense of direction. What had begun as a contest now threatened both of them. The sun could no longer serve only as the distant mark toward which they had chosen to fly.

Jatayu began to fall. Sampati saw the danger and moved after him, spreading both wings to shelter his brother from the scorching heat. For that moment his flight was directed by protection rather than rivalry. He placed his own body between the sun and Jatayu.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.139.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Cost Remembered`,text:`The effort injured Sampati. The brothers fell in different places, and he believed Jatayu had come down in Janasthana while he himself reached the Vindhya mountain. The story carries a cost, even though the shielding had spared his brother the same exposure.

Sampati tells the recollection to a sage. The memory holds the view from immense height and the decision made when height became danger. It does not turn his injury into a measure of his worth. The competitive flight has become a remembered act of care: two brothers in the sky, and one pair of wings spread where the other bird needs shelter.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.140`,candidateId:`AS-140`,definitionId:200,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`a184d0a34e122e555b30a65c5e062d5b34063b3c052b08a63a2e0df955155312`,variantSha256:`71658492323c1e993d50183fb3c7e52d1a1cb33a60656119fcc63d05a5f0e54b`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r08-haida-mapuche-ramayana.json`,reportSha256:`ee5c1737ea7d4680d2b163b345803b85e9afac4c94a13deae19e11775b3728c2`,packetPath:`traditions/south-asian-sanskrit-ramayana.json`,packetSha256:`ecd664c5b2b4a8b624a992f7f5c76a7121659045a35e0066cf0ae64acb7b3b08`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent reviewer /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator authored all three packets; this reviewer authored none. Only the two assigned review reports may be changed.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.140.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Dazzling Disguise`,text:`Maricha travels with Ravana toward Rama's forest dwelling. When they reach its surroundings, Ravana urges him to carry out the purpose of their visit. Maricha changes into a deer and begins to move near the threshold, where Sita can see him.

The new form is dazzling. Its antler tips are compared with sapphires, its face combines pale and dark colors, and its body gathers comparisons with flowers and precious stones. Silver-like spots break across the surface. The appearance belongs to a transformation rather than to a newly discovered ordinary animal.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.140.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Returning Deer`,text:`Maricha does not simply stand still and wait. He retreats, returns and moves through the nearby growth. He passes among banana plants and flowering trees, changing his position while staying within sight. The charm of the form and the rhythm of the movement work together.

Other animals approach. Yet they do not keep him company as if he were only another deer. When they come close and smell him, they withdraw. The disguise that can attract the travelers does not persuade every creature in the forest.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.140.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Purpose of the Lure`,text:`For Maricha, each return toward the dwelling has a purpose. He means to remain visible and draw attention away from safety. The beautiful body is part of a deception arranged with Ravana, not a harmless gift placed outside the exiles' home.

The later pursuit takes Rama farther into the forest as the deer repeatedly appears and disappears. This moment stays before its violent outcome. One transformed being holds the center of the scene: bright antlers, slight legs and a turning head, with a path opening behind him and the intention of a lure concealed inside a remarkable animal shape.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.142`,candidateId:`AS-142`,definitionId:202,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ab630b332ce804b098c21af26841f2707355fec8c7eb4ad77f647cd1f012b6d6`,variantSha256:`37153d83711435c3210f06a94b0a8a3ed7491471fe1f47a696744f0dbeb08f54`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r08-haida-mapuche-ramayana.json`,reportSha256:`ee5c1737ea7d4680d2b163b345803b85e9afac4c94a13deae19e11775b3728c2`,packetPath:`traditions/south-asian-sanskrit-ramayana.json`,packetSha256:`ecd664c5b2b4a8b624a992f7f5c76a7121659045a35e0066cf0ae64acb7b3b08`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent reviewer /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator authored all three packets; this reviewer authored none. Only the two assigned review reports may be changed.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.142.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Offered Signet`,text:`Hanuman speaks to Sita as Rama's messenger. Words alone must cross the distance between a stranger's arrival and trust, and he offers something that has come with him: the ring that Rama gave for this purpose.

The signet bears Rama's name in the selected passage. Sita takes it and looks at what had adorned her husband's hand. The small object connects the messenger before her with a person absent from the place where she is held. Her response changes as recognition gives his account something she can examine.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.142.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Trust and Questions`,text:`She praises Hanuman's strength and intelligence. He has crossed the sea and reached a place ruled by Ravana, and she does not regard him as an ordinary monkey. Yet her recognition is not only admiration for a feat. She considers whether Rama would have sent an untested messenger to her.

Questions about those far away remain urgent. She wants news of Rama and Lakshmana and thinks about the help that their allies might bring. The ring makes a conversation possible; it does not close the distance or complete the rescue at the instant it is handed over.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.142.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Hope Across the Distance`,text:`Her memories reach back to the exile. Rama had left the kingdom and walked into the forest with her. Now she wonders whether he still holds the courage that sustained that departure, and whether separation has made him suffer as she has suffered. A token can carry assurance without ending every question.

Hanuman has brought more than a precious object to be admired. He has brought a sign by which his mission can be recognized. In Sita's hand, a small circle connects two places that the story has kept painfully apart. It begins a renewed exchange of news and hope, while the work required to reunite them still lies ahead.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.as.143`,candidateId:`AS-143`,definitionId:203,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0afb943f97a7c4326cb8d8ccc88afd58153e29514e1b0881639740503388a12d`,variantSha256:`5deff5e5499ed299b26341cede10deebf2c7cf681364bdaa295b855780ef0ab8`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r08-haida-mapuche-ramayana.json`,reportSha256:`ee5c1737ea7d4680d2b163b345803b85e9afac4c94a13deae19e11775b3728c2`,packetPath:`traditions/south-asian-sanskrit-ramayana.json`,packetSha256:`ecd664c5b2b4a8b624a992f7f5c76a7121659045a35e0066cf0ae64acb7b3b08`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent reviewer /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator authored all three packets; this reviewer authored none. Only the two assigned review reports may be changed.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.as.143.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`An Urgent Task`,text:`Rama is distressed by Lakshmana's injury, but Sushena tells him that his brother is still alive. He sends Hanuman to a mountain for herbs that the story credits with restoration. The task is urgent, and returning empty-handed would let valuable time pass.

Hanuman reaches the mountain but cannot identify the plants he was sent to find. Strength has brought him to the place; it has not made him able to distinguish the particular herbs. He stops to consider how the mission can continue without pretending to know what he does not know.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.143.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Whole Peak`,text:`He decides to bring the mountain peak itself. The needed plants should be growing there, according to Sushena's direction. If he carries the whole peak back, the person who can recognize them will have them before him. The uncertainty changes the scale of his answer.

Hanuman grasps the peak with both arms, shakes it and lifts it free. Trees in flower remain upon it. Against the sky the raised mass is compared with a dark rain cloud, a great piece of the landscape moving where mountains ordinarily do not move.`},{id:`vision.definition.as.143.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Knowledge Completes the Help`,text:`He returns quickly, sets the peak down and rests briefly before explaining what he has done. He could not recognize the herbs, so he brought their entire growing place. Sushena praises him and selects what the tale requires; the narrative then gives Lakshmana a recovery without making Hanuman the expert who had identified the plants.

The deed joins extraordinary strength with an acknowledged limit. The peak is not a trophy taken for its own sake, and its flowers are not a lesson in real medicine. It is the scale of Hanuman's response to an urgent need: the landscape carried back so another person's knowledge can complete the help he began.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.002`,candidateId:`EU-002`,definitionId:205,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`39f2c3f1c6d9e03e33474ed4c9dc2a564095583789e466ae6a1d554d91097cf5`,variantSha256:`3afff8dffdbe3e7629b2580358217cae38d2526fdba4204e892395d32f4b81e3`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.002.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Water in the Forbidden Room`,text:`The forbidden room contained a captive rather than a treasure. Marya Moryevna had left Ivan in charge of her kingdom while she went away, warning him to keep that one door closed. He opened it. Hanging inside was Koshchei the Deathless, who said that ten years had passed without food or drink.

Ivan gave him water. One great draught was followed by another and then a third. With the third, strength returned. Koshchei shattered the twelve chains that held him, thanked his unwitting rescuer, and announced that Ivan would lose Marya. He rushed away and abducted her.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.002.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Pursuit, Death and Another Chance`,text:`His horse made the threat seem impossible to undo. When Ivan and Marya escaped together, the animal told its master there was time for whole harvests and meals before setting out: it could still overtake them. Koshchei recaptured Marya twice and spared Ivan because of the water. The third attempt ended in Ivan's death.

That was not the end of the rescuer's story. Ivan's bird-shaped brothers-in-law recovered him and restored him with the waters of death and life. Marya then helped him learn how Koshchei had obtained his exceptional mount. Beyond a river of fire lived Baba Yaga, whose mares could furnish another such horse.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.002.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Horse That Changes the Pursuit`,text:`Ivan undertook her deadly service. Creatures he had spared helped him gather the fleeing mares, and a bee directed him to a neglected foal. Fed and cared for, it became a powerful mount. When Ivan and Marya fled again, Koshchei pursued them, but this time his horse could not guarantee victory.

The stronger steed struck him down, and Ivan finished the fight. Marya rode home on Koshchei's horse beside Ivan on his own. The man called Deathless had been overcome. A rival horse and direct combat ended the power he recovered in the forbidden room; the journey onward belonged to those he had tried to keep apart.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.005`,candidateId:`EU-005`,definitionId:206,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`b01e26a9360da31bcdc3326807b1c12edbd6ce9fa52bc396dbac5d46464b8129`,variantSha256:`d62e5f2528c133ff85299d508551340f09eb94f631268724790879fce2abfb87`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.005.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Care Hidden at Home`,text:`Vasilisa carried a gift through years of trouble before she carried it into the forest. Her mother had died when she was eight, leaving a little doll and a blessing. A new stepmother and two stepsisters made home difficult, piling work upon her and blocking the suitors who came as she grew older. At night she fed the doll and asked for help. Its counsel and labor kept her from being overwhelmed.

One evening the family's last candle was deliberately extinguished. Her stepsisters refused to fetch another light and sent her to Baba Yaga. Vasilisa consulted the doll, put it in her pocket, and entered the woods.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.005.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Journey and the Hostess’s Tests`,text:`Riders in white, red and black passed through the journey like moving changes of time. At the clearing she found a dreadful fence and the forest hostess arriving in a mortar. Fire could be earned, Baba Yaga told her, but the work must be finished or she would be eaten.

Vasilisa survived two days of cleaning, cooking and impossible sorting. The doll completed the hidden labor, yet Vasilisa still had to stand before the hostess and speak for herself. She asked about the riders and left the strange servant hands alone. When she named her mother's blessing as the reason for her success, Baba Yaga sent her away with a skull bearing fire.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.005.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Dangerous Light and Fine Work`,text:`The return was not harmless. Its light destroyed the household that had sent her into danger. Vasilisa buried the skull and left for town, finding shelter with an older woman.

There she began to spin fine flax. With the doll's help she obtained a means of weaving, then sewed a dozen remarkable shirts herself. The work reached the tsar and led to the tale's marriage ending. Her father and the older woman joined her, and the doll remained close. The fine work of her hands had reached beyond the house that once confined her; the small companion that helped her make it still traveled at her side.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.006`,candidateId:`EU-006`,definitionId:207,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6868467d196f095de7bde3a9063a0d3f5ec0f4ae34e8c92050f16f8e7a445867`,variantSha256:`8c7017105b7ebeca9ca414b8f3a8a6cebcc088a66840e2f102ef289a36989925`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.006.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Ruler’s Welcome and Warning`,text:`Ivan encountered Marya through the consequences of her strength. Before he saw her tents, he found a defeated army and learned that Marya Moryevna had conquered it. She met him as a ruler receiving a traveler, asked about his journey, and invited him to stay. Their marriage brought Ivan into her kingdom.

When she went away to war, she entrusted her possessions to him and gave one prohibition: leave the lumber-room closed. He opened it and found Koshchei hanging in twelve chains. By giving the captive three draughts of water, Ivan restored his strength. Koshchei broke free and took Marya from the road.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.006.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Failed Escapes and New Knowledge`,text:`She recognized her husband when he reached her place of captivity. Her first question named the cause of their trouble: why had he ignored her warning? Nevertheless, she went with him. Koshchei's horse overtook them, and he took her back. A second escape failed in the same way. Before the third, Marya warned Ivan that he would be killed, but he insisted; the warning proved true.

After Ivan's brothers-in-law restored him to life, he returned with a better plan. He asked Marya to find out where Koshchei's horse had come from. She questioned her captor and passed the knowledge to Ivan, who took the cloth that could bridge the river of fire. The search led him to Baba Yaga and finally to a neglected foal whose strength could rival the pursuer's horse.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.006.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Two Riders Return`,text:`When he returned, Marya did not pretend the earlier danger had vanished. She feared another fatal chase. This time, however, Ivan's new mount turned the encounter, and Koshchei was defeated.

Marya took the fallen captor's horse; Ivan rode his own. They visited his sisters and their husbands before returning to their kingdom. The danger had begun when her warning was ignored. Her knowledge had finally helped undo it, and now she returned as the ruler of her own kingdom.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.011`,candidateId:`EU-011`,definitionId:208,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ae2e21fd682101f14d32ed4ef372e075596c5ace32125df1d7d87395564101c3`,variantSha256:`b5bb52dc2cf1425a83e663a756c06bcfcfbdc857092948a48278dd97d575eab8`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.011.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Strength Under a Household Warning`,text:`Ilya's journey began with a household blessing and a limit on his strength. After thirty years at home, he could walk with extraordinary power. He prepared a steel lance, obtained a fine horse, and asked his parents to let him travel to Kyiv. They agreed but charged him to avoid needless harm.

On the road, robbers coveted his mount. He did not begin by striking them. Instead, he sent a dart deep into the earth to demonstrate what an attack would cost. They surrendered and offered payment; he refused their goods and rode on. His journey later carried him through besieged cities, where he fought the armies surrounding them.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.011.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Road Beneath Twelve Oaks`,text:`The most unsettling barrier lay on the direct road beyond Chernihiv. The Nightingale Robber had kept travelers away for thirty years, using no ordinary weapon. His whistle could overwhelm anyone approaching the woods. Even before Ilya reached the nest, the sound shook his horse.

Above twelve oaks sat the robber. Ilya steadied himself, raised his bow, and brought the aggressor down. He took him alive to Kyiv. The ruler initially doubted that anyone could have traveled that road and captured its keeper. Other knights went out to look and confirmed the captive's presence.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.011.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Danger at the Court`,text:`Curiosity then created another danger. The ruler wanted to hear the famous whistle. Ilya protected the royal couple with a heavy fur garment and ordered only a restrained demonstration. The robber used his full power instead, and the gathered knights collapsed. Ilya killed the robber for that final defiance.

The route that had defeated so many travelers had yielded to his strength, but the danger had followed him into the palace. He had warned the first robbers and spared them; against this enemy he had needed both force and precautions. Even bound and carried into a court, the terrible whistle had not become a harmless curiosity.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.012`,candidateId:`EU-012`,definitionId:209,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`86c38462bd8fa87ae9e0c3eebc6c642761f63c8101b9e5be751f496255bf148a`,variantSha256:`b5bb52dc2cf1425a83e663a756c06bcfcfbdc857092948a48278dd97d575eab8`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/eastern-slavic.json`,packetSha256:`f91e93467506e37edafff7de71ef1d1e405211eb782d584089b4298166fbc7f2`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.012.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Voice Above the Closed Road`,text:`The road belonged to the Nightingale Robber long before Ilya rode along it. For thirty years, the narrative says, he prevented passage on foot or horseback. His weapon was a whistle strong enough to defeat travelers before they came within reach of any blade.

His nest stretched above twelve oaks. From that height he could turn distance into an advantage: the first warning reached Ilya far away, and another, stronger whistle made the rider's horse stumble. The grove did not shelter a harmless singing bird. It held an antagonist with a household, married daughters, and power that made an ordinary road unusable.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.012.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Capture and the Robber’s Household`,text:`As Ilya approached, the robber used the full force of his voice. It failed to stop the rider. Ilya answered with his bow, brought him down, and bound him as a captive. The height of his perch had not protected him from the rider's arrow.

They passed the robber's own palace. His daughters first mistook the returning pair for their father bringing home a prisoner, then understood that their father was the prisoner. They called their husbands to attack. The robber himself warned them against challenging Ilya and urged a different approach. Captivity had not made him ignorant of danger.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.012.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Whistle at Court`,text:`At Kyiv, the ruler doubted Ilya's account until other knights inspected the captive. Then he wanted to hear the whistle. Ilya took precautions and asked for only part of its force. The robber ignored the limit. Sound struck the assembly and the knights fell.

Ilya killed him after that last attack. The voice that had commanded the woodland road could no longer call down from its nest. For years, travelers had learned to fear a sound before they saw its maker; now the high oaks stood over a route whose keeper had been brought down, and the court had learned how much danger a captive could carry.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.014`,candidateId:`EU-014`,definitionId:211,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`b544edbcc30846308cbd880706cb277b1c354130c4f2a4547961a092d0db4d68`,variantSha256:`d26c6793f42dcf04529e145d3d3461bf1775e82d94a92be7ed545cc7413cd04d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.014.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A New Instrument`,text:`Hermes had scarcely been born before he began making a place for himself among the gods. Outside his mother's cave he found a tortoise. Its shell became the body of a new instrument, fitted with supports and seven strings. When he touched them, the small lyre answered with music.

That evening his inventive mind turned to Apollo's cattle. He separated fifty animals from the herd and drove them away, disguising their tracks. The stolen beasts made their way to Pylos. By dawn Hermes was back in his cradle, wrapped to resemble an ordinary helpless infant.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.014.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Cattle Dispute`,text:`Apollo was not persuaded by that appearance for long. The missing cattle, the strange footprints and the child's extraordinary denials brought the brothers before Zeus. Their father laughed at Hermes's ingenuity, but ordered him to lead Apollo to the animals.

At Pylos the quarrel still had sharp edges. Apollo saw evidence that two cattle had been killed, and tried to bind the thief. Hermes escaped the binding. Then he took up the lyre and played.

The music changed Apollo's attention. He listened with delight as Hermes sang of the gods and their beginnings. Anger gave way to questions: where had this sound come from, and how had so small a child learned such an art?`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.014.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Music Between Brothers`,text:`Hermes offered the lyre as a gift. Apollo could make music with it, while Hermes would tend the wandering cattle. The agreement let each brother keep something he valued. Apollo received the instrument and placed his herding whip in the child's hand.

Together they turned the cattle toward their meadow and returned to Olympus. Zeus welcomed their friendship. Hermes's mischief had caused the dispute; his invention helped resolve it. The hymn leaves the new instrument sounding in Apollo's hands and the youngest god with a recognized place among his kin.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.015`,candidateId:`EU-015`,definitionId:212,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ca49fe5dd7a842fbc71a7112a5d94435dbfdaabccaadb0c6005a840a2e1672b5`,variantSha256:`c31edfbc44a8a740ea9a76e14b3a8f4c9e389818db4f28b9af50db7b19edc186`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.015.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Search for Persephone`,text:`Demeter heard her daughter's cry, but could not see where it led. For nine days she searched the earth with torches, refusing the ordinary sustenance of the gods. On the tenth, Hecate told her what she had heard. Together they asked Helios, whose sight crossed the world, to reveal the truth.

He said Hades had taken Persephone below and that Zeus had allowed it. His attempt to make the arrangement sound acceptable did not quiet Demeter. She withdrew from Olympus and came among human beings, disguising herself as an old woman.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.015.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Empty Fields`,text:`At Eleusis, the daughters of Celeus found her beside a well and brought her into their house. She became nurse to their little brother Demophoon. When the household interrupted her attempt to make the child immortal, she revealed herself and required a temple. There she remained apart, still longing for Persephone.

The seed stayed hidden in the soil. Ploughing and sowing brought no harvest. Human hunger also threatened the offerings on which the gods' honored relationship with mortals depended. Gifts and invitations could not move Demeter: she would not restore the fields until she saw her child.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.015.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Divided Reunion`,text:`Zeus sent Hermes to bring Persephone back. Mother and daughter ran together and embraced. Yet Demeter asked whether her daughter had eaten below. Persephone told her that Hades had forced her to taste a pomegranate seed against her will.

Reunion therefore came with a division. Persephone would spend one third of the year below and two thirds with her mother and the other gods. Rhea brought the settlement, and Demeter accepted it.

The fields could grow again. Leaves and flowers returned across the earth. The hymn then speaks of Demeter teaching mysteries whose contents are not to be disclosed. Her public story reaches renewal without turning that guarded knowledge into a spectacle.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.016`,candidateId:`EU-016`,definitionId:213,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`78e4342418796049507572e042a948942adcca989e3a9a42870c1f55c2e2492e`,variantSha256:`1e89bb216cfc07c31aef496a5122638f514ac47b476465110eb389504e35b303`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.016.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Taken Below`,text:`Persephone was gathering flowers with her companions when a remarkable narcissus drew her attention. Its beauty hid a trap. As she reached toward it, the earth opened and Hades rose in his chariot.

He carried her away against her will. She cried to Zeus and hoped, while the sun and sea were still visible, that she would see her mother again. The landscape vanished above her, but the cry reached Demeter.

Below, Persephone longed for that reunion. The hymn places her beside Hades, reluctant and grieving, while her mother's withdrawal leaves the earth unable to feed its people. Eventually Zeus sent Hermes to bring her into the light.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.016.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Return to Light`,text:`Hades agreed to the departure. He told Persephone of the authority she would hold in his realm, but her immediate joy was at the prospect of seeing Demeter. Before she left, he secretly gave her a pomegranate seed. Later, in her own telling to her mother, she said he had put it in her mouth and forced her to taste it against her will.

Hermes drove the immortal horses through the long distance above. When Persephone saw Demeter, she leapt from the chariot and ran into her mother's arms. Their relief did not erase the question of what had happened below. Demeter asked about food, and Persephone answered plainly.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.016.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Between Two Worlds`,text:`The seed bound the return to a divided year. One third would be spent beneath the earth; the other two thirds would be with Demeter and the gods above. The settlement promised that spring's flowering would bring her upward again.

Mother and daughter comforted one another. Hecate joined them and became Persephone's companion. The hymn gives Persephone both a reunion and a continuing separation. Her movement between worlds is born from coercion and a negotiated settlement, rather than a freely chosen romantic adventure.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.017`,candidateId:`EU-017`,definitionId:214,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`efa638059f90960f39c52467e00d78f0533062a159bdbe87a394e88e628919a3`,variantSha256:`3b7f457d629fc3a7c36be6674764bab5fd8add110c93786808e8ea1147dd2296`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.017.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Dangerous Commission`,text:`The request for Medusa's head began far from the Gorgons. On Seriphus, King Polydectes wanted Perseus removed so that he could pursue Danae, the young man's mother. A demand disguised as a contribution to a marriage celebration became the dangerous commission.

Perseus did not find the Gorgons unaided. Hermes and Athena guided him to the daughters of Phorcus, three sisters who shared an eye and a tooth. By withholding those shared objects, he obtained directions to the nymphs who could supply what his journey required.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.017.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Sleeping Gorgons`,text:`The nymphs gave him winged sandals, a carrying bag and the cap of Hades, which hid its wearer from sight. Hermes supplied a hard-edged sickle. Equipped with these gifts, Perseus flew toward the ocean.

There he found Stheno, Euryale and Medusa asleep. The Bibliotheca gives the Gorgons heads encircled with scaled, dragon-like coils, great tusks, bronze hands and golden wings. Anyone who looked upon them could become stone. Only Medusa was mortal, and that difference made her the target of the commission.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.017.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Reflected Encounter`,text:`Perseus stood above the sleepers without looking directly. In a bronze shield he watched the reflected image while Athena guided his hand. He killed Medusa. The narrative brings Pegasus and Chrysaor from her body; it does not tell the later Roman story of a woman transformed after an assault.

Perseus placed what he had taken inside the bag and fled. The immortal sisters woke and pursued him, but the cap prevented them from seeing their quarry.

Medusa has little opportunity to speak in this version. The story follows the visitor's preparations and escape, while its sleeping encounter exposes the mortal Gorgon's vulnerability. A restrained illustration can pause before the killing without pretending that the killing is absent from the tale.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.023`,candidateId:`EU-023`,definitionId:215,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`003245851daa2be4a52125dc264e1c74d061192bb00cc40633a7bc61c1190591`,variantSha256:`f712be7b7ef7bc34c7dd8c03801efb48e4bf6cbe9445a4d667056c99786812bf`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r00-ancient-greek.json`,reportSha256:`d096d5e2fbf55e8d3e5b9861388cb3673713c82056149ea05b8edeb565a020aa`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-greek.json`,packetSha256:`9aefc2bb840f48ecbcd6f2b3d49d1d53851d17137d0980684334f801cd1bf6bc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:13:45.266Z`,independence:`Original packet researched/authored by /root/ancient_greek_research. Coordinator independently reopened relevant sources and read every story/chapter; coordinator minor corrections are explicitly recorded below. Not a self-review of coordinator-authored tradition.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.023.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Inherited Seat`,text:`Before entering the sanctuary, the Pythian priestess remembered those whom she understood to have held its prophetic seat. Earth came first in her prayer. Themis followed her mother, and Phoebe received the place with Themis's consent. Phoebe then gave it to Apollo as a birthday gift.

In the account she recited, Apollo traveled from Delos to the slopes of Parnassus. Road-builders accompanied him with reverence, and the people welcomed his arrival. Zeus gave him prophetic knowledge and established him as the fourth holder of the seat.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.023.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`An Ordered Welcome`,text:`The priestess's prayer reached beyond that succession. She acknowledged Athena before the temple, the neighboring nymphs, the river, Poseidon and Zeus. The sanctuary stood within a landscape of remembered divine relationships, rather than alone as a mysterious room.

She prepared to take her place and wished for a fortunate entrance. Visitors could come forward in the order determined by lot. Her task was to speak under the god's guidance.

Then she went inside.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.023.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Broken Entrance`,text:`What she found broke the expected pattern. A man occupied the place of a suppliant, holding a sword and an olive branch wrapped with wool. His appearance carried the stain of violence. Before him sat a strange company of sleeping women whose presence she could scarcely describe.

Fright sent the old priestess back outside. She struggled to stand upright and told what she had seen, comparing and then rejecting familiar images in her effort to explain the sleepers. The discovery demanded more than an ordinary consultation.

She left the future of the matter to Apollo, the master of the house, whom she named as healer and reader of signs. Her part in this opening episode ends there.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.025`,candidateId:`EU-025`,definitionId:216,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`92f26cd5f7aa9add614983a5d786383bfcaf2c193d6110a5bca67a5b9a414067`,variantSha256:`ec1faab6a1b31492272ff5a277d1b70d1020ff0517cd29429836b9418ce405fa`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.025.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Guardian Appears`,text:`The poet was preparing to write about the beginning of the year when the house seemed suddenly brighter. Before him stood a god with a face looking each way, a staff in his right hand and a key in his left. Janus answered the poet's astonishment with an invitation to ask.

Why two faces? The reply reached back before the ordered world. Janus described the former confusion of things and their eventual separation. His own appearance retained a double view. Now he guarded openings: the passages of the world, the routes of heaven, the entrances through which people went out and returned.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.025.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Two Directions and the Year’s Beginning`,text:`The poet imagined an ordinary doorway. A porter on one side could miss what happened behind him. Janus needed no turn of the head to watch both directions. His faces did not quarrel or announce competing characters. They belonged to one guardian whose office required attention beyond a single line of sight.

Questions followed. Why did the year begin in winter? Why should work start on the first day? Why offer sweet gifts, and why did people also give money? Janus answered through the customs Ovid wanted to explain. Pleasant beginnings promised pleasant continuations; an opening mattered because what followed took its first direction there.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.025.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Remembered Arrivals and Doors of Peace`,text:`The conversation also entered Roman memory. Janus explained the ship stamped on old money through Saturn's arrival. He recalled a threatened gate during the Sabine attack and told how heated springs had checked the attackers. These were stories of passages defended, arrivals received, and a city remembering its beginnings.

Finally the poet considered the god's doors in war and peace. Open doors allowed those who had gone to battle a way back; closure signaled peace in Ovid's account. The poem then praises imperial peace, bringing its ancient guardian into the politics of the poet's own age. Janus remains at the threshold of that conversation, looking toward what has happened and toward what is about to enter.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.026`,candidateId:`EU-026`,definitionId:217,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`bcff309b4151120585878552c97dc5edc24c333618db0d65cf262b838a9fd2f6`,variantSha256:`a7faecdd5518ed5ef11661671d61eef6ad3301532c892af80351e1d21bb049bd`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.026.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Presence Without a Portrait`,text:`The poet began with a prayer and an expectation. A goddess might appear to explain her festival, as other divinities had appeared in his poem. Instead, Ovid reports a sense of sacred presence without claiming that he saw Vesta. His first correction was therefore a limit on the picture he could offer.

He turned toward the temple and its beginnings. In his account, Numa established a modest building in a city that had not yet covered itself with magnificent materials. The later shrine belonged to another stage of Rome's life. Splendor had changed, but the poet sought the meaning that joined the different structures.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.026.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Round Shrine and Living Fire`,text:`The building's round form suggested the earth. Ovid set it inside an ancient account of the cosmos, where the earth held the middle position. That model is part of his explanation, rather than a map a modern reader must accept. Its purpose in the poem is to connect the shape of the shrine with the goddess's place in the world.

Then his attention moved from architecture to fire. He understood Vesta through living flame: sustained, luminous, and unlike a body that produces children. The goddess's virginity entered this explanation without supplying the human portrait he had first expected.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.026.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Image That Was Never There`,text:`The decisive discovery concerned the interior. Ovid admits that he once supposed an image of Vesta stood there. He learned otherwise. Beneath the round roof there was no sculpted likeness of the goddess, and no separate statue of the fire standing in for it.

This episode ends in a revised act of looking. The poet does not unveil a hidden woman or invent a conversation with a priestess. He removes an imagined figure from his account and directs attention to the flame itself. The absence is meaningful: to describe this Vesta faithfully, he must let the sacred fire occupy the space where he had expected a face.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.028`,candidateId:`EU-028`,definitionId:219,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6fa5b22c87b2d8ef2e23d453eba2875806b2c7d77786caa7a9ed5b3096471892`,variantSha256:`0ee6698cf2a8af62f8dab9bc8bb9a991647645e5ebd627cf94391a3f6e534d24`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.028.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Signs and a Household’s Route`,text:`The house had become a place of decision. Troy was burning, but Anchises had resisted leaving it. Then signs interrupted the family's fear: thunder and a shining track across the sky changed the old man's resolve. He acknowledged the gods' warning and agreed to go.

Aeneas arranged the escape around the people he could carry and guide. His father would ride upon his shoulders. His son Iulus would walk beside him, while Creusa followed behind. The servants were to meet them outside the city at an old shrine of Ceres, near a cypress that made the rendezvous recognizable.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.028.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Father and Sacred Things Carried`,text:`Before lifting Anchises, Aeneas made another distinction. His father must take the sacred things and the Penates. Aeneas had just come from fighting. His hands still bore the pollution of battle, and he would not handle those objects until he had washed in running water. The burden was therefore divided: the son carried his father, and the father carried what the household held sacred.

Aeneas spread a lion's pelt across his shoulders and neck. Anchises climbed onto him. Iulus held his father's right hand and kept pace with shorter, uneven steps. This small procession moved away through the darkness, with Creusa following.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.028.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Fear for Those Beside Him`,text:`The same warrior who had faced weapons without trembling now started at every sound. A breeze, a movement, or a faint noise could suggest danger. Courage had not vanished, but its attention had changed. He feared for the child at his side and the weight entrusted to his back.

The selected passage stops with this movement through the threatened city. It does not reveal the Penates' shapes or tell their whole later journey. What it makes clear is their place in an escape: sacred belongings carried with a living household, requiring care rather than serving as trophies. Father, son, child, and the objects in Anchises' keeping travel together toward the meeting place.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.029`,candidateId:`EU-029`,definitionId:220,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0009ea68f05af7bba16a9fb19f6c53c96dd6aadfd6782b98981e0910fe9bf5af`,variantSha256:`2ebfa1a4f4acdb913e9b7be9ec99c53f50b4202885e55f9a8ad172a9a398411f`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.029.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Marker Between Neighbours`,text:`At the edge between neighboring fields, a stone or an old stump could bear the name Terminus. Ovid addresses that unassuming marker as a god. Its importance did not depend on the beauty of a face or the strength of an arm. It stood where one person's ground met another's.

The poet pictures both sides acknowledging the boundary. Two households brought their garlands, and the families shared a celebration. Adults and children appeared around the marker, each with a place in the gathering. The poem describes offerings and a meal; it does not turn a quarrel over land into a battle.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.029.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Impartial Ground and a Refusal`,text:`In their praise, the neighbors gave Terminus a special kind of firmness. Riches could not buy his favor. He should distinguish the fields without flattering either owner. His stillness represented a judgment that belonged to neither side, even while both depended on it. Ovid's account presents this as a religious ideal, rather than a practical method for settling modern property disputes.

The same refusal to move entered a story about Rome's great temple. When space was being prepared on the Capitol for Jupiter, the other gods withdrew from their former sites. Terminus did not. His old place remained his place.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.029.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Roof Makes Room`,text:`The new building therefore had to accommodate him. The boundary god shared the great temple, and an opening in its roof left the sky visible above his position. In the poet's telling, a small, apparently powerless presence altered the plans for the most imposing divine neighbor.

Ovid closes by drawing the marker's stubbornness into claims about Rome's extent. Those imperial ambitions belong to the poem's historical voice. The quieter image survives beside them: a stone acknowledged by two neighbors, and a roof that cannot completely close over it. Terminus holds his position without becoming a warrior. His story makes room around a thing that will not be pushed aside.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.030`,candidateId:`EU-030`,definitionId:221,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`40c0766ee2828916da1cd6a57d9692e42c26c0c3f8fb2c21816c4ecb048b2c46`,variantSha256:`b112ad5be760d596a4a95019be9aba4ba14c103ed74060355dc2794af69bb41e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.030.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Garden and the Brief Blossom`,text:`When Flora spoke, the poet noticed flowers and their scent before he had finished asking his questions. She connected her Roman name with Chloris and described a garden where spring did not have to yield its place. The Hours gathered blossoms there, and the Graces made wreaths from what the garden supplied.

Her account was not confined to decorative flowers. Blossoming mattered to the harvest. Grain, vines, and fruit trees depended on a stage that people might overlook when they thought only about what they would eventually eat. Flora asked the poet to recognize that brief, vulnerable beginning.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.030.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`What Neglected Care Leaves Exposed`,text:`She also explained why Rome's games in her honor had become recurring celebrations. Earlier honors had not settled into an annual observance. When she was neglected, she withdrew the care by which she had protected growing things. Ovid lets the goddess describe flowers fading and crops suffering from damaging weather.

Flora distinguished withdrawal from a campaign of destruction. In her telling, she stopped preventing the harm. That distinction did not make the consequences small. A world that took blossoms for granted discovered how many later benefits depended on them.

The Senate promised annual games if the year proved fruitful. The arrangement restored a place for the goddess in the calendar. Her explanation moved between a garden imagined in perpetual spring and a city that had to remember its obligations again each year.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.030.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Questions and Lingering Fragrance`,text:`The poet asked about colorful clothing, lamps, and the animals associated with the games. Flora answered through her concern with blossoms, fields, and gardens. The celebrations belonged to the goddess’s account of the city: color, lamps and gentle animals each had a place in the explanation she gave.

Her earlier account of Zephyrus includes violence followed by marriage, an uneasy part of Ovid's story that cannot be made harmless by flowers. The conversation nevertheless gives Flora a voice beyond that episode: she explains her own work, the neglect it suffered, and its consequences. When she vanishes, fragrance remains, and the poet is left to follow the seasonal beginnings he had nearly missed.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.031`,candidateId:`EU-031`,definitionId:222,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`69d418f192c7259b8be826fc8bb6f0d4902bf7b9829c02f83ebd1b34a02dfdb3`,variantSha256:`94995d8d7c120513f598705bf029c80547ce942d8462352ebb8d757efab98dbc`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.031.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Skilled Work Within a Boundary`,text:`Pomona's attention belonged to the orchard. She did not seek the chase or wander the woods for its own sake. A curved pruning knife suited her hand better than a hunting weapon. She removed unhelpful growth, joined a cutting to a new stock, and brought water to thirsty roots.

The trees answered careful work with fruit. Around this cultivated space she kept a boundary, because she feared the violence of the rustic beings who desired her. The orchard was her chosen occupation and her protected ground. Its keeper was not waiting for a visitor to give her purpose.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.031.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Disguises and a Threatening Argument`,text:`Vertumnus wanted to enter. He adopted the appearances of workers and travelers: a reaper, a man carrying hay, a pruner, a fruit gatherer, and others. Each disguise provided another way to come near her. Finally he took the shape of an old woman, with covered white hair, a bent posture, and a supporting stick.

The visitor admired the fruit, then pointed to a vine supported by an elm. Partnership became an argument for marriage. The supposed old woman praised Vertumnus, describing his youth, changeable appearance, and devotion to the same cultivated things Pomona loved.

Persuasion then darkened into a warning. The visitor told of Iphis and Anaxarete: an unsuccessful suitor died, and the woman who watched his funeral became stone. That tale was used to threaten the consequences of refusal. It was an argument made by a disguised pursuer, rather than a judgment this retelling asks the reader to adopt.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.031.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Revealed Pursuer`,text:`The speech failed. Vertumnus abandoned the old woman's form and appeared as himself, bright and youthful. Ovid says he contemplated force, but that it was unnecessary because Pomona responded with desire. Both the stated response and the preceding threat belong to this version.

The scene ends with that sudden turn. It does not erase the orchard work that defined Pomona at its opening, or make deception and contemplated violence into harmless courtship. Her skill in the orchard had been established long before the visitor’s arrival: tending a tree’s future growth required care, and this was the work she had chosen.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.032`,candidateId:`EU-032`,definitionId:223,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`2cb414c4d1f90577ccb2f0aa0e31420a7484f0fb9ab96992176f0d080722d6b5`,variantSha256:`2686704d1cb7b5b6dd580f27b89d83dbbe29d505052373509c1139daefc4b4a9`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.032.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Many Trades, One Desire`,text:`Vertumnus could change the form by which others saw him. In Ovid's orchard story, that ability served a fixed desire: to get close to Pomona. She devoted herself to fruit trees and kept unwanted men outside her cultivated space.

He tried the appearances of ordinary occupations. Barley could make him look like a reaper; hay suggested another kind of field worker. A pruning knife, a ladder, fishing gear, or a soldier's equipment gave him further identities. The disguises were opportunities for access. They did not mean that he became a different god for every trade.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.032.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`An Elder’s Disguise and False Recommendation`,text:`His longest attempt took the shape of an elderly woman. White hair was covered, the body bent, and a stick supported the assumed age. Once inside, he praised the orchard's fruit and used the sight of a vine climbing an elm to propose the benefits of partnership.

Speaking about himself as though he were someone else, he presented Vertumnus as a desirable match. He emphasized youth, faithfulness, the power to change appearance, and shared interest in cultivation. The deception gave his praise the sound of an independent recommendation.

When that did not secure agreement, he introduced a harsher story. Iphis had died after an unsuccessful pursuit of Anaxarete; she had become stone while watching his funeral. Vertumnus used their fate as a warning against refusal. Its grief and threatened punishment exposed the pressure beneath his flattering speech.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.032.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Form Falls Away`,text:`The argument was in vain. He dropped the disguise and showed his own youthful form. Ovid then reports an intention to use force, followed immediately by the claim that Pomona's desire made it unnecessary. This ending requires both facts to remain visible. The narrated response does not turn the earlier deception or contemplated assault into an admirable method.

The orchard had given his changing forms a single aim. Whatever appearance he borrowed, Pomona’s protected ground remained her own chosen work. His power altered the face seen at its edge; it did not turn her refusal into a fault.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.033`,candidateId:`EU-033`,definitionId:224,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f08153fdb995a9b00bbf1485bf6a776ab825050ceb73977b1d917e77e683d38c`,variantSha256:`e84a131b99ef55516942fe096b79e4cd9261c89fe4ba24a85e622d7f27f9bdc4`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.033.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Counsel Beyond Arcadia`,text:`Carmenta knew that exile lay ahead for her son Evander. When the loss came, she did not tell him that a life outside Arcadia would be easy. She urged him to endure it, distinguishing the misfortune of leaving home from the guilt of a crime.

Other people, she reminded him, had found new places after losing old ones. The world offered more ground than the country they were forced to leave. Her prophetic knowledge did not prevent separation, but it gave her a way to speak about a future beyond it.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.033.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Riverbank in a Vision`,text:`Their ship eventually entered the Tiber. The bank before them held humble dwellings, rather than the monumental Rome familiar to Ovid's readers. Carmenta stood aboard the vessel with her hair streaming. She stretched her arms toward the shore and struck the wooden deck three times with her feet.

The new land awakened her vision. She spoke of walls yet to be built and of the importance the place would acquire. Trojan arrivals, warfare, the fate of her grandson Pallas, and the burden Aeneas would carry entered that future. Ovid extends her prophecy toward the ruling family of his own age, making the scene serve Roman and imperial memory.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.033.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Landing and Later Memory`,text:`The bank was still quiet, but her vision reached far beyond the huts. To Evander, it offered a destination after exile; to the later city, her arrival would become part of the story of its beginnings. The place before them held both a possible home and the weight of what she foresaw.

Her urgency was so great that Evander had to restrain her from leaping off the ship. They landed and established new homes, bringing an Arcadian name into the landscape. The passage later associates her prophetic voice with Hercules and the festival that remembers her.

At the center of the arrival stands an adult mother who can foresee more than her son can. She has crossed the water with him, comforted him through displacement, and recognized a destination before it looked like the city it would become.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.036`,candidateId:`EU-036`,definitionId:225,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f122f0e87f8e217c7292806f1328d063fe9458967ac3c9435c35cfc56905fc90`,variantSha256:`673bf956f6645addf82bf23ada79fb8310520a719924135f62820d1636f71361`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r10-slavic-roman.json`,reportSha256:`72bf75f0f3e0663b484474648fd8655b58ce651831ba47cd9fd55cb1dd4386b8`,packetPath:`traditions/ancient-roman.json`,packetSha256:`6793dfbd9733263a3c4fc5cb8d777a4a6815fb74d587449cf7dd06251cafc412`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:37:48.175Z`,independence:`Eastern Slavic and Ancient Roman packets were authored by other workers; this reviewer did not author them. Medieval Irish is excluded because it was authored by this reviewer. Exact review scope is 24 cards.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.036.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Promise After Thunder`,text:`Storms troubled Numa's effort to establish a more peaceful order in Rome. He sought a way to answer Jupiter's thunder without abandoning the laws and restraint he wanted his people to learn. Egeria advised him, and encounters with woodland powers brought him toward the god's response.

Ovid expressly withholds the secret means by which Jupiter was summoned. The poem's reader receives the resulting meeting, rather than a set of instructions. Numa met the dangerous demands of the exchange with resourceful interpretations. Jupiter accepted the king's ingenuity and promised a heavenly pledge on the following day.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.036.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Crowd and the Descending Shield`,text:`Numa announced what would happen. Not everyone believed him. A promise about the sky could expose a king to ridicule if the sky remained ordinary. At dawn the people gathered, and he waited upon a simple wooden seat, his head covered in white.

The air was clear when thunder sounded. Lightning followed, and a shield descended on a gentle movement of air. The crowd's doubt broke into a shout. The promised object had arrived without a battle to win it or a rival to seize it from.

Numa acknowledged the gift before taking it up. Ovid explains its name through the cut-away shape of its edges, describing a shield without angular corners. The passage offers that verbal clue, not a complete plan from which its material, dimensions, and decoration can be reconstructed.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.036.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Copies and a Maker’s Name`,text:`Protecting the gift required a craftsman's work. Mamurius made matching shields so that the heavenly original could no longer be singled out easily. The poet emphasizes the success of the copies: their likeness concealed which one had fallen from above.

For his reward, Mamurius asked for his name to be remembered in the Salii's song. The story therefore ends with craft and memory as well as divine favor. It gives no magical combat ability to the shield. A heavenly pledge becomes safer through painstaking human imitation, and the maker's name travels with the sacred objects rather than being lost behind them.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.040`,candidateId:`EU-040`,definitionId:229,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`b80ea81bc60e054148e9f64b60072e96b3f8cc9a2f69c1f8c2efc78758c2e824`,variantSha256:`fbe384b8760513f995873b0bd3f5da5107887cb80d08533e00226128a558ea98`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r05-norse.json`,reportSha256:`9399338c3425cc079bcfa934e259fe499f8786dc49ea4024b5c1f92f4fdf57b4`,packetPath:`traditions/old-norse-and-icelandic.json`,packetSha256:`a02f4faf3561b8b80955b4a3658737373f0f4ef80afc37cffde3580f4c24e019`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:36:07.885Z`,independence:`Original packet authored by /root/norse_research. Coordinator independently reread the selected Anderson source passages and all card text, claims, adaptation limits and exact count briefs; not self-review.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.040.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Two Sets of Treasures`,text:`After cutting off Sif's hair, Loki had to find a way to repair his mischief. Thor's anger left him little room for excuses. He promised hair of gold that would grow like the hair he had taken.

The sons of Ivaldi made that marvel. They also made Odin's spear and Freyr's folding ship. With three such treasures in hand, Loki could have returned quietly. Instead, he wagered his head that Brok's brother Sindri could not make three gifts as good.

The brothers set to work. Sindri used the furnace while Brok kept the bellows moving. A fly bit Brok's hand, but he did not stop. A boar with golden bristles emerged. The fly attacked his neck; still the bellows worked. The second treasure was a ring that would produce eight equal rings every ninth night.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.040.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Hammer’s Judgment`,text:`When the brothers worked iron, the fly struck Brok's eyelid. Unable to see, he swept it away, interrupting the air for a moment. The hammer came out with a short handle.

Loki and Brok took all six treasures to the gods. Odin, Thor, and Freyr would judge them. Loki presented the spear, golden hair, and ship. Brok answered with the multiplying ring, the shining boar, and the hammer. Its handle was imperfect, but it could endure Thor's blows and return when thrown.

The judges chose the hammer. Brok had won Loki's wager.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.040.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Narrow Escape and Its Cost`,text:`Loki offered a ransom and then tried to escape. Thor caught him. Facing payment, Loki reached for one last distinction: his head had been wagered, but his neck had not.

That argument prevented the taking of his head. It did not leave him unpunished. Brok sewed Loki's mouth shut, silencing the tongue that had made the wager and then argued against its cost.

The gifts remained with their new owners. Loki's cleverness had helped bring them into the world; it had also brought him to the edge of a bargain he could not simply talk away.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.041`,candidateId:`EU-041`,definitionId:230,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ea22c53431b1c08719ec19f7bfe49423c7c80f3f8578711dd91d6354f43ca8b8`,variantSha256:`2cd76496aa82b61e7687778bd27ed46a711d35d0c8eb67907ebb5137c2124e60`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r05-norse.json`,reportSha256:`9399338c3425cc079bcfa934e259fe499f8786dc49ea4024b5c1f92f4fdf57b4`,packetPath:`traditions/old-norse-and-icelandic.json`,packetSha256:`a02f4faf3561b8b80955b4a3658737373f0f4ef80afc37cffde3580f4c24e019`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T01:36:07.885Z`,independence:`Original packet authored by /root/norse_research. Coordinator independently reread the selected Anderson source passages and all card text, claims, adaptation limits and exact count briefs; not self-review.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.041.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Compensation in Asgard`,text:`Skadi came to Asgard wearing a helmet, mail, and her war gear. The gods had killed her father, Thjazi. She intended to demand an answer with more than sorrow.

The gods offered compensation. Among the terms they agreed upon, Skadi would choose a husband from their company, but she could see only their feet. She selected the pair that seemed most beautiful, believing they belonged to Balder. The man she had chosen was Njord.

She also required the gods to make her laugh, something she doubted they could manage. Loki's absurd performance accomplished that condition. Odin added another act of compensation, setting Thjazi's eyes among the stars. The settlement did not give Skadi her father back.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.041.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Two Unsettled Homes`,text:`Then came the question of where she and Njord would live.

Skadi wanted Thrymheim, her father's home in the mountains. Njord preferred Noatun by the sea. They tried dividing their nights between the two: nine in the mountains, three at the shore.

But the places that comforted one troubled the other. After his stay in Thrymheim, Njord complained that the wolves' howling was harsh beside the voices of swans. Skadi found that she could not sleep on the shore. Each morning the seabirds' cries woke her.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.041.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Mountain Return`,text:`A bargain could join their lives without making either landscape a home for both of them. The mountain sounds belonged to Skadi's country; the sea belonged to Njord's. Neither could borrow the other's ease simply by spending the appointed nights there.

Skadi returned to Thrymheim.

In the mountains she traveled on skis, carrying a bow and hunting wild creatures. The snow gave her a way forward. Her father's former dwelling became hers, and the high country no longer had to compete with the shore for her rest.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.049`,candidateId:`EU-049`,definitionId:233,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`118d7e2ed0da61d8279db43ecd3737f04650664cf39a8f439cf446a00da0b936`,variantSha256:`481895883a962eee9f0ae456fcd3fcef7b3026f95e3c6d022038e517e10d2203`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.049.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Skills at the Gate`,text:`A feast was underway at Tara when a strange company approached the gate. At its head stood a young warrior dressed for royal standing. The doorkeeper asked his name and then the question that governed entry: what art did he practice? Tara had room for skilled people, but no need to duplicate every position already filled.

Lug answered as a craftsman. There was already a craftsman inside. He offered the work of a smith; the household already had one. He named prowess, music, poetry, knowledge, healing and other arts. Each answer met another accomplished person’s name. The court was not empty of talent, and the doorkeeper knew it.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.049.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`One Person, Many Arts`,text:`Then Lug changed the question. Did the king have one person who possessed all those arts together? If such a person was already there, he would stay outside.

The doorkeeper carried the question to Nuada. Before opening the court to the newcomer, the king had gaming boards brought out. Lug won the trials. When the report returned, Nuada ordered his admission, recognizing that a person like this had not entered the household before.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.049.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Place for the Joined Arts`,text:`Inside, Lug took the seat of a sage. Ogma challenged his strength by hurling a huge stone through the hall; Lug sent it back and repaired the damage. Asked for music, he played strains that brought sleep, grief and delight. The king considered whether these joined abilities could help free his people from the oppression facing them.

Nuada changed seats with Lug for a set period. Councils followed, bringing together leaders, smiths, healers and other skilled people. The newcomer’s importance had begun with his refusal to be counted as one more separate appointment. Once inside, he did not make the court’s existing craftspeople vanish. The work ahead required their arts to meet, and Lug’s gift was to make that meeting possible.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.050`,candidateId:`EU-050`,definitionId:234,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`8b89124b31d0198809980ff84d6538850e325a6dcaabde8d47ce00a0f3a95112`,variantSha256:`d4f0178d1ae3fa6562b65726177d1c4de7f568ba3b542e5b038cac2f41694621`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.050.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Truce and a Mocking Meal`,text:`Lug needed time before the battle. The men of Ireland were still gathering, so he sent the Dagda to inspect the Fomorian camp and delay the confrontation. It was a mission of speech before it was a test of strength. The Dagda entered the opposing company and asked for a truce. The request was granted.

His hosts knew his love of porridge and decided to use it against him. They filled the king’s vessel with a huge mixture of milk, meal and fat, with the meat of several kinds of livestock added. Then they poured the cooked food into a hollow in the ground. The meal exceeded the scale of ordinary hospitality; it was meant to expose the guest to ridicule.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.050.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Eat or Be Killed`,text:`Indech made the danger plain. The Dagda must eat it all or be killed. The language of giving a visitor his fill became a threat: if he failed, the hosts would treat the failure as proof that their generosity had been refused.

The Dagda took his enormous ladle and began. Mouthful followed mouthful. He did not leave the meal unfinished. At the end he reached along the bottom of the hollow, collecting what remained among the ground’s grit. The effort and the great quantity of food brought sleep upon him, while the Fomorians laughed at his swollen belly.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.050.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`An Envoy Leaves the Camp`,text:`He nevertheless left their company. Making his way to the shore was difficult under the weight of what he had consumed. The same body they had tried to turn into a joke had carried him through their challenge.

The mission had won the requested pause and brought the envoy back out of the camp. Its comedy was never free of menace: the laughter belonged to hosts who had threatened a guest’s life. The Dagda survived by accepting the impossible scale of their demand and exhausting it.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.051`,candidateId:`EU-051`,definitionId:235,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ca475477b8961185b5c46867ad90a53703db473978bcbd5c882fef46199f6907`,variantSha256:`f6c68e625545ed2c1eef65d6d54f404cbac0e53d4daff7167ddc65dcd3188d16`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.051.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Victory Carried Across the Land`,text:`During the battle, the Morrígan came to strengthen the Tuatha Dé. Their struggle had drawn together warriors and people of many arts, but courage could still fail amid its noise and danger. She urged them toward fierce resolve. The fighting turned, and the Fomorian host was driven toward the sea.

When the victory was won and the battlefield had been cleared, her work continued. News needed to travel beyond those who had survived to witness it. The Morrígan announced what had happened to the royal heights of Ireland, to the hidden companies of the land, to its chief waters and to the mouths of its rivers. Places that had not stood inside the fighting were brought within the victory’s hearing.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.051.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Peace and Abundance`,text:`Those who met her asked for news. Her proclamation opened a vision of peace and strength, of earth and heaven joined in the life of a flourishing world. It was more than the count of enemies overcome. The victory could be understood through what it made possible: people sustained, abundance renewed and a land that might live beyond its conflict.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.051.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Warning Beyond Victory`,text:`But the voice of celebration did not speak alone. She also foretold an ending filled with failure. Seasons would lose their richness, the sea its produce and judgments their justice. Trust between people would break. The future she described could not be secured merely by defeating the host that had opposed them that day.

The news therefore held two horizons. One was the great victory already achieved. The other was the danger of a world whose relationships and sustaining life had gone wrong. The Morrígan’s voice moved between them, allowing neither to erase the other. Her task after the battle was not simply to praise force. It was to make the land hear what triumph had accomplished and what remained possible beyond it.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.052`,candidateId:`EU-052`,definitionId:236,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`e88325442b46bc49b40a78a9e5bdda4462bbb27608c2625e091e2f42d94b35e2`,variantSha256:`412d6626906e6a41479c2f2f079c2298ff468e9f8df3a00760e8cb209fdd69d5`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.052.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Work Behind the Battle`,text:`The Fomorians noticed something troubling in the daily fighting. Their weapons broke or lost their edge, and the damage weakened them the next day. The Tuatha Dé returned with equipment restored. Behind that difference stood a workshop rather than a single champion on the field.

Goibniu worked at the forge. His skill finished metal with marvelous speed, but metal alone could not complete the equipment. Luchta supplied wooden work, Credne supplied fittings, and Crón worked the edges. Their tasks met in objects that warriors could carry back into battle. Healing work at the well sustained the people who used them.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.052.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Spy Receives Their Work`,text:`Ruadan was sent to discover the source of this advantage. His parents linked him to both sides, so he could approach the craftspeople and observe their work. He reported what he had seen. Then he was sent back with another purpose: to kill Goibniu.

He asked the workshop for the parts of a spear. Each request was met, and the assembled weapon was given to him. Those whose work had equipped him were his mother’s people. Once he held their gift, he turned it against the smith.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.052.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Smith Returns to the Well`,text:`Goibniu was wounded, but the attack did not achieve Ruadan’s intended result. The smith pulled the weapon free and struck back. Ruadan died after returning to the gathering where his father stood, and his mother Brig mourned him.

Goibniu went to the healing well and became whole. The workshop could continue, yet the attack had shown how exposed the work behind the battle was. The skill that restored equipment depended on trust, shared labor and people who could be approached. Ruadan had entered through those relationships and tried to destroy one of the hands that held them together. The smith’s survival preserved a network, not simply an individual’s reputation.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.053`,candidateId:`EU-053`,definitionId:237,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`44dc0bbdc7dcc4c68c3453284a9af552985250a5790bc06b08574ce416b07c50`,variantSha256:`9d55d4f67934f06e9fe1543c8b24b766d120c5532fb36b4ebbd849767fd29ed0`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.053.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Hand and the Crown`,text:`Nuada’s people had won a battle, but the victory left their king without a hand. Dian Cecht the physician worked with Credne the metalworker to provide a replacement of silver. It could move as another hand moved. The work answered an injury without erasing the person who had survived it.

Yet the custom surrounding kingship made that injury a reason to exclude Nuada from the throne. Bres was chosen in his place, partly because his family connections were expected to strengthen an alliance. The hand and the crown therefore became different problems: one called for care and craft, the other exposed the community’s rule about who could govern.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.053.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Healing and a Return`,text:`Miach, Dian Cecht’s son, later brought another kind of healing. He restored Nuada’s flesh through his own skill. His father reacted with destructive jealousy, and the family paid for that reaction with Miach’s life. Nuada’s treatment stood within a wider story of skill, rivalry and loss; it was not a promise that marvels came without consequences.

Bres’s own government failed the demands of good rule. When he surrendered the kingship, Nuada returned to it. A great feast at Tara brought another decision before him. The young warrior Lug arrived with many arts, and the doorkeeper described what made him unusual.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.053.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A King Makes Room`,text:`Nuada tested the newcomer and then admitted him. He watched Lug demonstrate strength and music, and considered what those combined abilities might do for the people facing oppression. Rather than hold every advantage inside his own office, the king changed seats with him for a time.

The silver hand remained part of Nuada’s remembered name. But his place in the tale was not only an injury or an extraordinary replacement. He had survived exclusion, returned to responsibility and recognized when another person’s gifts could serve the company before him.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.054`,candidateId:`EU-054`,definitionId:238,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`e108e62c7cdd5863eac9c548428cc41b1ff5321adb6ed631b720bc83551c0ee0`,variantSha256:`35aeb17e55241afd470d7b9fbe9728896275dfaf5948cfe2d0e80532f70b03e4`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.054.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Kinship and the Workshop`,text:`Ruadan belonged by family to both companies in the battle. His father was Bres, and his mother was Brig, the Dagda’s daughter. That relationship allowed him to approach the craftspeople of the Tuatha Dé, observe their work and carry what he learned back to the Fomorians.

He discovered why their opponents could return with equipment renewed. Goibniu worked the metal, Luchta the wood and Credne the fittings. Healers sustained the wounded at a well. The battle depended upon people whose work was less visible than the warriors’ charge.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.054.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Attack Turns Back`,text:`Ruadan was sent back to destroy that advantage by killing Goibniu. He requested a spear and received what he asked for from the craftspeople to whom he was related. Their work supplied the weapon he then used against the smith. The attack wounded Goibniu but did not kill him. Goibniu struck back, and Ruadan died before his father in the Fomorian assembly.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.054.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Mother's Lament`,text:`Brig came for her son. The designs of battle had ended in a loss that could not be made smaller by explaining why he had been sent. She first gave voice to it in a shriek; afterwards she wept. That sound marked the beginning of lament in Ireland.

Her appearance changes the scale of the episode. What had been an inspection of the enemy’s useful skills, then an attempt to remove a craftsman, becomes the death of one person whom a mother mourns. His connections to both sides had made the attack possible. They did not protect him when the weapon turned back.

Goibniu would reach the healing well and recover. Brig’s son would not. Her grief remains beside that difference. The story remembers the voice that answered a death, allowing the mother’s loss to be heard amid the larger account of craft, strategy and victory.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.055`,candidateId:`EU-055`,definitionId:239,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`36c8d77bc492bead672a91756d19f423b1016e7fced3a378b1c9b50026184e48`,variantSha256:`767a5d1f3623266dcdde8910c06fa5522df9d63c4d4e9315f5ce3e88e84edd7e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.055.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Two Answers to an Injury`,text:`Nuada had lost a hand in battle. Dian Cecht answered the injury with a marvel of workmanship: a silver hand that could move as a living hand moved. The physician and Credne the metalworker had made a substitute worthy of wonder. It gave the injured king something precious, yet the work was not the end of the story.

Miach, Dian Cecht’s son, saw a different possibility. He sought to join Nuada’s own flesh again. Over a succession of days and nights, he brought the severed hand back into living union with its owner. Where silver had answered loss, flesh now answered it.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.055.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Jealousy Turns Against a Healer`,text:`Dian Cecht could have welcomed the greater healing. Instead, his son’s success troubled him. The accomplishment that should have enlarged their shared craft became a challenge to his pride.

He attacked Miach. Each time his son recovered, the father struck again. At last he dealt a wound beyond Miach’s power, and Miach died. All the father’s skill could not undo the horror of that act.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.055.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Scattered Knowledge`,text:`After the burial, plants grew over Miach’s resting place. They were counted as three hundred and sixty-five, corresponding to the joints and sinews of his body. His sister Airmed spread her mantle and began to distinguish their properties. Another healer’s knowledge was taking shape, patiently, in the cloth before her.

Dian Cecht came upon her work and mixed the plants together. The order was lost. The father who had given a king a new hand had now broken the work of both his children.

When battle came, he stood among the physicians at a healing well. His power remains part of the company’s strength. But the earlier loss remains beside it: a physician may know how to restore others and still fail disastrously to govern himself.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.056`,candidateId:`EU-056`,definitionId:240,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f3886f1850fd4d06479043477fe771bb36f0f0ff85c6c6fb29f25ff06f8fd71e`,variantSha256:`ad32f53552e648d7dd2f29ef238b47c53b49ef61b9f2d2ecf46e7d7796a09074`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.056.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Plants After the Burial`,text:`Miach had restored Nuada’s living hand, but his skill had brought him danger within his own household. His father, Dian Cecht, could not bear the achievement. After repeated attacks, Miach died and was buried.

The burial did not end the marvel surrounding him. Plants grew over his resting place, three hundred and sixty-five of them, corresponding to the joints and sinews of his body. The healer’s lost life had become a new gathering of possibilities.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.056.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Mantle Becomes an Order`,text:`Airmed, Miach’s sister, came to the plants. She spread out her mantle and began separating them according to their properties. The cloth became a place of patient attention. Each distinction mattered: a plant set beside one neighbor carried a different meaning from a plant set beside another.

This was work that needed care rather than force. Airmed was making an order from what had appeared after loss. Her brother could no longer speak, but there was something here to understand, something she might keep from disappearing with him.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.056.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Order Scattered, the Healer Remains`,text:`Dian Cecht came to her. The same father whose jealousy had destroyed Miach now interfered with the work of his daughter. He mixed the plants together.

The arrangement that Airmed had made was broken. Their properties were no longer held in a knowable order. No new ordering took its place. What her father had scattered lay beyond Airmed’s patient arrangement, and the loss remained beside her.

The lost knowledge had passed beyond ordinary human recovery; only the Holy Spirit might teach it afterward. Airmed’s mantle is emptied of certainty, though the care she brought to it remains visible in the story.

Later she is named among the physicians at the battle’s healing well. She remained a healer, carrying her skill into another gathering of physicians. Yet that work did not undo her father’s interference or bring the lost ordering back to her mantle.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.057`,candidateId:`EU-057`,definitionId:241,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`24ac64188bf403cf5f8b3e45bc1f717523796ad0735865185d88ba81919e04dd`,variantSha256:`6099638ce7ed88aa71a35880b9d355c04d4f2173f5b496d7bfcd7ea9cbc45160`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r13-medieval-irish.json`,reportSha256:`f952f054ae341b126e6266073bc7c8ee1a4c372c7c9f90cf822edcfca03528fd`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-irish.json`,packetSha256:`c724fd4951e501a343a5d6d25e6f4fedaa921749484830c32811093d02f9a6d1`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:58:10.946Z`,independence:`Packet authored by /root/persian_research. Coordinator reopened named primary passages and public institutional/community text, then read every current card story, prompt and contextual field.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.057.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`An Eye and a Kinsman’s Challenge`,text:`Balor’s dangerous eye remained closed until battle. Its power had begun long before, when he looked through a window at his father’s druids preparing their magic. The fumes reached his eye, leaving it with a poisonous force.

That force made sight itself a weapon. An army that looked toward the open eye could lose its strength to resist. Balor carried the danger hidden beneath a lid that required four men to lift it. He could wait until his opponents were before him, then order the opening.

At Mag Tuired, the battle had already taken Nuada Silverhand and Macha at Balor’s hands. The noise of the hosts gathered around another meeting: Balor faced Lug.

They were bound by kinship as well as opposition. Balor was the father of Ethne, Lug’s mother. Yet that connection did not turn this encounter into a welcome. The older warrior meant to bring his great weapon to bear.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.057.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Lid and the Cast`,text:`He called for the lid to be lifted so that he could see the challenger speaking before him. The attendants began their work. Everything depended on the brief passage between a closed eye and an open one.

Lug answered with his sling. He sent a stone into that moment.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.057.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Danger Turned Back`,text:`The cast struck Balor, and the destructive gaze was turned toward the Fomorian host instead of the opponents it had been meant to overcome. The danger that Balor had kept for others now fell among his own side. He went down, and twenty-seven of his company died in the reversal.

The meeting ended with his weapon defeated by its own power. The battle continued, and the Fomorian force was driven toward the sea.

Balor had trusted that an opened lid would settle the encounter. Lug’s answer was smaller than an army: one cast, timed before the gaze could master him.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.061`,candidateId:`EU-061`,definitionId:243,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`047624ccb6f87b9a957fb77f80384bf6c6ac6dd07b2c1ceda11e6542f86e43ee`,variantSha256:`f18a208ad175475881ed68fb0b7c7bac02f7a8ab5e8f72479b64f56c73f62210`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.061.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Offence and the Pact`,text:`Pwyll was hunting in Glyn Cuch when the sound of unfamiliar hounds drew him away from his companions. In a clearing he found them beside a fallen stag. Their bodies shone white and their ears were red. Instead of respecting the other pack's quarry, he drove the dogs away and brought his own forward.

A horseman approached in grey hunting clothes. He offered no greeting. Pwyll's conduct, he said, had been deeply discourteous. The hunter identified himself as Arawn, a king of Annwn. Pwyll asked how he could repair the offence and gain his friendship.

Arawn proposed a year-long exchange of appearances. He would govern Dyfed in Pwyll's likeness; Pwyll would take Arawn's place in the other kingdom. At the end of the year, Pwyll must meet Arawn's enemy Hafgan at a ford. One blow would overcome him. A second, however urgently requested, must not follow.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.061.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Year and One Blow`,text:`Pwyll entered the unfamiliar court. People greeted him as their king, clothed him richly, and brought him into a household of feasts, conversation and music. He listened to the people around him and learned how this court conducted its life. Keeping another ruler's appearance required more than wearing his rich clothes: the promised work at the ford still lay ahead.

When the appointed night arrived, he rode to the ford. The rulers' followers stood aside. Pwyll struck Hafgan once, breaking through his defence. Hafgan demanded that he finish the attack, but Pwyll refused. He remembered the condition on which success depended.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.061.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Two Rulers Return`,text:`The two allies met again in Glyn Cuch and resumed their own forms. In Dyfed, Pwyll learned that Arawn had governed his country more wisely than he himself had done. Pwyll told his nobles who deserved their thanks, and promised to continue the good government they had enjoyed. Arawn returned to the otherworld court, where his household had never recognised his absence.

Their first meeting had concerned possession of a stag. Their friendship endured because each had been trusted with far more, and returned it faithfully.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.062`,candidateId:`EU-062`,definitionId:244,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`fad61502d10e687aeed55860535a78c669f58bbcdc4c209fd5aafce744a19c80`,variantSha256:`3c4e2ba60696f50c8bb5aed46d9620f5f9051d2f277ce0ff61358c67d906621c`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.062.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Lady Who Chose`,text:`From the mound above Narberth, Pwyll and his companions saw a woman approaching on a large white horse. Her clothing shone gold. The horse moved evenly, without haste, yet nobody could catch her. A runner failed. Riders on swift horses fell farther behind the harder they tried.

On the third day Pwyll rode after her himself. Speed failed him too. At last he called and asked her to stop. She did so willingly, observing that the request would have spared his horse much effort if he had made it sooner.

She was Rhiannon, daughter of Hefeydd Hen. She had come on her own errand: to seek Pwyll rather than accept the husband others intended for her. They agreed to meet at her father's court in a year.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.062.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Unfillable Bag`,text:`At their wedding feast, an unfamiliar suitor asked Pwyll for a gift. Pwyll promised before learning the request. The man was Gwawl, the unwanted suitor, and he demanded Rhiannon and the banquet.

Rhiannon rebuked Pwyll's careless answer, then devised a way out. Another feast was appointed. Pwyll returned disguised as a poor petitioner with a small bag she had given him. No amount of food could fill it. When Gwawl stepped inside to press the food down, Pwyll closed the bag around him. After Gwawl was mistreated, Rhiannon counselled a settlement and a promise against revenge. She married the man she had chosen.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.062.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Child and the Truth`,text:`Years later their newborn child disappeared while his attendants slept. To save themselves, the women falsely accused Rhiannon of killing him. She endured an imposed penance at the palace gate.

The child was alive. Teyrnon and his wife had found and raised him, and eventually brought him to Narberth. Teyrnon told the court what had happened and named the accusation a lie. Rhiannon's grief ended in recognition; her son's return restored the truth that her own protests had not secured.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.066`,candidateId:`EU-066`,definitionId:245,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`a502e097060d83524014c35b3d8a9e3b099db73fa018de8fe427842dfb71dc00`,variantSha256:`5e42d483a2bc10c877bb4769b3f54b90d910e3f912abbd3408ea8f419d841232`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.066.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Hounds and the Threshold`,text:`Dyfed was empty. A storm and a wall of mist had taken its people and herds, leaving Pryderi, Rhiannon, Manawydan and Cigfa alone. They had tried living by hunting and making their way through craft in other towns. Now Pryderi and Manawydan were back in their silent country with their dogs.

One morning the hounds recoiled from a small bush, their hair raised. A white boar emerged. It did not simply flee: it held its ground until the hunters approached, then withdrew again. The pursuit led toward a great new castle where no building had stood before.

The boar rushed through the gate, followed by the hounds. Then the dogs fell silent.

Pryderi wanted to enter and learn what had happened to them. Manawydan warned him that a castle appearing in an enchanted land could be part of the same danger. Pryderi would not abandon his dogs. He crossed the threshold.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.066.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Golden Bowl`,text:`Inside there was nobody. No boar, no hounds, no household explained the building. At its centre stood a fountain surrounded by marble. On a slab beside it rested a golden bowl of beautiful workmanship. Chains hung from above, disappearing beyond sight.

Pryderi approached and took hold of the vessel. His hands could not leave it. His feet could not leave the slab. Delight vanished, and speech with it.

Manawydan waited outside until evening, then returned to the palace without his friend. Rhiannon heard the account and went to find her son. She entered without shrinking from the open gate. Seeing Pryderi, she took hold of the bowl too and was caught in the same silence.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.066.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Country Restored`,text:`Thunder and mist arrived with night. The castle disappeared, carrying both prisoners away.

Their story did not end at the bowl. Later, Manawydan forced the enchanter Llwyd to restore them and undo the spell over Dyfed. Pryderi had entered out of loyalty to his hounds; another companion's persistence finally brought him home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.067`,candidateId:`EU-067`,definitionId:246,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`b55d3dd7f60bbc36a9c8639109d5ad65d5305570fbad8c5ebe7d0ed0bfe844a8`,variantSha256:`afc5697398739db4f349ac3e4868db2aa114a169b7557d8056f9bc1f57da4b76`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.067.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Courtesy and an Offer`,text:`The stranger in the clearing did not greet Pwyll. He had reason: Pwyll had driven another pack from the stag it brought down and claimed the quarry for his own hounds. The dogs were unmistakable, brilliant white with red ears. Their master approached on a large light-grey horse, wearing grey wool hunting clothes and carrying a horn.

Pwyll first wondered whether rank explained the withheld greeting. The rider answered that the problem was conduct. Pwyll had acted without courtesy. When the mortal lord offered to make amends, the stranger named himself: Arawn, a king of Annwn.

His proposal reached far beyond the price of a stag. Another king, Hafgan, opposed him. Pwyll could settle the conflict if he took Arawn's appearance and place for a year. Arawn would meanwhile govern Dyfed under Pwyll's likeness. Neither household would recognise the exchange.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.067.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Two Kingdoms in Trust`,text:`Before parting, Arawn gave precise instructions. At the appointed ford, a single blow would defeat Hafgan. If the wounded king asked for another, Pwyll must refuse. Arawn had made that mistake himself, and Hafgan had returned to fight again.

Arawn guided his new ally to the sight of the otherworld court and left him to learn its customs. The year's work in Dyfed would later be visible in what Pwyll's people said: gifts had been generous, justice clear and government wiser than before.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.067.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Enduring Friendship`,text:`At the end of the year, the two returned to Glyn Cuch. Pwyll had followed the instructions at the ford and brought both otherworld kingdoms under one rule. Arawn restored their proper appearances.

Arawn went back to the household that had not recognised his absence. In Dyfed, Pwyll told his nobles who had governed them so well and promised to maintain the justice they had enjoyed. Thereafter gifts passed between the two courts: horses, hounds, hawks and jewels. Arawn's anger in the clearing had become a friendship that endured when both men once more wore their own faces.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.068`,candidateId:`EU-068`,definitionId:247,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`adc5b8463b83899db275e7a61f146a9c4ac8d1ff4fcac451a809baad159e9782`,variantSha256:`97c1549d8c01321f09bf50daaeb720751cfc1c9e5131e7c24be6789b216870ef`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.068.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Flower Bride and an Alliance`,text:`Arianrhod had declared that Lleu would never have a wife from the people of the earth. Math and Gwydion found a way around the restriction: they gathered blossoms of oak, broom and meadowsweet, and made a woman through enchantment. They named her Blodeuwedd and gave her to Lleu as his bride.

The pair lived at Mur y Castell. While Lleu was away visiting Math, Blodeuwedd heard a hunting horn and saw a company passing. Their leader was Gronw Pebyr. As evening approached, she invited him into the court rather than let a traveller leave without hospitality.

The two became lovers. Soon they began considering how they could remain together. Their chosen answer was to learn the exceptional circumstances under which Lleu could be killed and bring those circumstances about.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.068.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Prepared Ambush`,text:`When Lleu returned, Blodeuwedd presented her questions as concern for his safety. He explained the difficult requirements: a specially made spear, a roofed bath beside a river, and a posture with one foot on the bath's edge and one on a goat. She sent what she learned to Gronw.

A year later the weapon was ready. Blodeuwedd arranged the bath and persuaded Lleu to demonstrate the posture he had described. Gronw attacked from concealment. Lleu rose into the air as an eagle and vanished, leaving Gronw to take his land.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.068.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Owl Sentence`,text:`Gwydion searched, found the wounded eagle in an oak, and restored his nephew to human shape. Physicians brought Lleu back to health. When they returned, Blodeuwedd fled with her attendants. Gwydion overtook her.

He did not describe what followed as freedom. He sentenced her to become an owl, feared and attacked by other birds and unable to show herself safely by day.

The flower-born bride had become a bird barred from the safety of daylight. Lleu, restored and healed, returned to the land Gronw had taken. The marriage made to defeat one restriction had led through another set of carefully arranged conditions to betrayal, flight and a sentence that changed Blodeuwedd's life again.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.069`,candidateId:`EU-069`,definitionId:248,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6be1bebebcdb1fd8c99567251d29ed5a1d27c2d87a02627bef3e0f7ae1fd62f9`,variantSha256:`bb1d494581e197ef0d978ebee678a03ef60d43b60a99cb8772eed38af4db11ae`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.069.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Conditions for a Wound`,text:`Lleu did not consider himself easy to kill. When Blodeuwedd asked about his safety, he explained the strange conditions surrounding a fatal wound. The weapon must take a year to make, worked only at the appointed Sunday observance. He could not be slain simply indoors or outdoors, on foot or on horseback.

The vulnerable moment lay between ordinary categories. A bath must stand beside a river under a thatched roof. A goat must be brought beside it. With one foot upon the bath's edge and the other upon the animal's back, he could be struck.

Blodeuwedd carried the knowledge to Gronw Pebyr, her lover. Together they prepared what Lleu believed would remain difficult to arrange. A year passed. The spear was ready, the bath built, the animal brought.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.069.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Eagle and Three Songs`,text:`Lleu agreed to show the posture. As he stood there, Gronw attacked from concealment. The blow wounded his side. Instead of falling into the conspirators' hands, he flew away as an eagle, crying out. Nobody there knew where he went.

Gwydion began searching for his nephew. The trail eventually came through an ordinary household and its unusually wandering sow. Following the animal, he reached an oak above a brook and looked upward. A suffering eagle remained in the branches.

He sang to it. At the first verse the bird came partway down. Another brought it to a lower branch. At the third, it settled upon his knee. Gwydion touched it with his wand, restoring Lleu's human form.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.069.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Care Before Retribution`,text:`That return was only the beginning of recovery. Lleu was terribly wasted. He was taken to Caer Dathyl, where skilled physicians cared for him until he was healed before the year's end.

Later he would seek retribution. This moment belongs to the interval before it: a man who had survived by changing shape now depended on another's patience, recognition and care to come back.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.070`,candidateId:`EU-070`,definitionId:249,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`adcba12dfb61d36812389d95c4cf329f8ea1396a7df17db527490f16492bde3b`,variantSha256:`772f2977b1df3e1b4113e36bf1423fee703cb7d4d9f53b11532587716341853f`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r09-welsh.json`,reportSha256:`c4dcf64fbd8242bdb67c6a90ef96b2feac0570dec2b62d9b240b06fd14478a0a`,packetPath:`traditions/medieval-welsh.json`,packetSha256:`004b34ce1ca382c81ed69fa74c9327ada7ee2a784ce64717e2c46b7a8764cf03`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T02:12:16.915Z`,independence:`Packet originally authored by /root/welsh_research; coordinator reopened and read selected full Guest episodes and institutional context, all card stories and actual prompts. Minor wording/attribution refinements recorded below, without changing plot or catalog gates.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.070.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Search Without Rest`,text:`News of Lleu's disappearance reached Math's court with grief. The attack had come through an elaborate betrayal: Blodeuwedd and Gronw had arranged the conditions Lleu described as necessary to kill him. Wounded, he had fled as an eagle. Gronw now held the land he left behind.

Gwydion declared that he would not rest until he learned what had become of his nephew. He travelled through Gwynedd and Powys, searching without finding him. At last he stayed in a household in Arfon and heard a small piece of local puzzlement.

One of the pigs left its sty each morning and returned late. Nobody knew where the sow went. Gwydion asked the keeper to wait for him before opening the sty next day.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.070.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Sow and the Oak`,text:`At first light the animal rushed out. Gwydion followed it against the course of a river to a brook. There it stopped below an oak. He looked at its feeding place, then into the branches, and recognised what the dreadful evidence meant: the wounded eagle above was Lleu.

Gwydion answered with song. His first verse called the eagle down from the top of the tree. A second brought it to the lowest branch. With the third, the bird descended to his knee.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.070.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Back Within Human Reach`,text:`He touched Lleu with a wand. Feathers gave way to human form, but the person beneath them was severely wasted. Gwydion brought him to Caer Dathyl, where physicians undertook the care needed to complete his recovery.

The journeys had brought him no answer until he listened to a swineherd's ordinary report. Then the animal's path led him where his own search had failed. The three calls carried Lleu from the highest branches back to the reach of another person.

Gwydion had helped make the flower-born bride whose plot sent his nephew into the tree. Later he would pursue her and change her into an owl. For the moment, the work before him was neither pursuit nor punishment. He had found Lleu alive, and brought him home to people who could heal him.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.073`,candidateId:`EU-073`,definitionId:250,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`75beedf924a1f834040fd3d58f002bd8c5c4ea190c11499324a93ac6e8cc2ac4`,variantSha256:`6b3c9a699d8468f14f336dec4cb62da288127acfef060f92ab21221f877aeb81`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.073.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A World Before the Singer`,text:`Before Väinämöinen saw the world, Ilmatar had travelled through the boundless sea. A duck nested upon her raised knee and laid six golden eggs and one iron egg. When she moved under their heat, the nest fell and the broken eggs became the earth, sky and lights above.

Ilmatar continued her work. Her movements shaped banks and bays, deep waters, rocks and islands. Land and heavens existed, but her son had not yet looked upon either.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.073.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Release Into the Sea`,text:`He remained unseen for thirty summers and winters, yearning to leave the narrow darkness around him. He appealed to the moon, sun and Great Bear for release. He wanted to walk in the open air, see the lights and consider the stars. None of them brought him out.

At last he made his own way into the world. He entered the deep ocean and turned through the water with his hands. North, south, east and west became directions he could explore rather than names for something beyond his reach.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.073.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`First Footing on Earth`,text:`The sea did not immediately deliver him to a homeland. Five years passed, then six and seven. In the autumn of the eighth year, he finally reached a promontory that held no green growth.

He came out on his knees, placed both feet upon the ground and stood. Above him were the sun, the moon and the Great Bear he had long desired to see. The surrounding land was still bare, but it was a place where he could breathe and look outward.

The figure who would become a singer and traveller began with that change: from unseen waiting to movement, and from movement through the sea to his first footing on the earth.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.074`,candidateId:`EU-074`,definitionId:251,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`171e3dddffee3cca9028e70c4073c23c990c80ee69dd6b053f299c4d4fa66620`,variantSha256:`7872c2986debfb95f8872280bca97cc5d96b757d441fad92e659ab8d35141f1f`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.074.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Tree and a Storm`,text:`Väinämöinen returned home with a promise he had made elsewhere: Ilmarinen would go to Pohjola and forge the Sampo. The smith would not agree to the journey. So the singer offered him a different wonder—a tree bearing moonlight and the Great Bear in its branches.

Ilmarinen went to see it and climbed. Väinämöinen called a storm wind, which carried him away to the distant land he had refused to visit.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.074.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Judgment at the Furnace`,text:`Louhi met the unexpected arrival, recognized the smith and welcomed him at her table. She asked for the Sampo and promised her daughter for the work. Ilmarinen accepted the challenge, but there was no workshop waiting. He searched the countryside, chose a rock and built his own furnace and bellows.

His first beautiful creations were unsuitable. A bow demanded victims; a boat sought conflict; a cow wasted its milk; a plough damaged the wrong fields. He returned each to the fire. The work required judgement as well as skill: an impressive thing could still be a bad result.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.074.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Work Finished, Choice Unchanged`,text:`Finally the winds strengthened the furnace and the Sampo appeared. He hammered it into completion beneath its coloured lid. It made flour, salt and money, and Louhi secured it in the mountain.

Ilmarinen then asked the daughter to come with him. She refused. Her life, freedom and work belonged to her home, and she would not leave them. The fulfilled task did not turn the promise made about her into her own agreement.

Disappointed, the smith prepared to return. Louhi supplied provisions and a copper-banded boat, calling the wind to guide him. He arrived home able to say that he had forged the Sampo, but had brought no bride.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.075`,candidateId:`EU-075`,definitionId:252,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`280d57c51dc659605634aaf3f877a5a43417067e109c46ed6b1092c4c61ef968`,variantSha256:`d6fa1885b0fa4459511cb07cd9f007f163719edb3ce301f9c3ac509e22c1f921`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.075.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Brush’s Warning`,text:`Lemminkäinen had not returned from Pohjola. At home, his wife and aged mother found a dreadful sign in the brush he had left behind. His mother immediately set out to learn what had happened.

Louhi first gave uncertain answers, then admitted that she had sent him to hunt the swan in Tuonela’s river. His mother searched through forests and paths, asking what they knew. Trees and roads answered with their own troubles. The moon could not help her. At last the sun told her that her son had perished in the river.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.075.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Search Beneath the River`,text:`She went to Ilmarinen and asked for a great rake. With it she returned to the waters, seeking her son while the sun helped quiet the dangers around her. Her search was long and difficult, but she would not abandon him. She recovered him and worked to restore his broken form.

Life alone did not bring back his senses and speech. She called a bee to fetch healing sweetness. Its first journey brought honey from forest flowers, but that was not enough. A second crossed the seas to an enchanted island. Still Lemminkäinen could not answer.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.075.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Bee’s Last Journey`,text:`For the third journey, the bee travelled upward toward Ukko’s dwelling. It returned with the balm that finally reached him. His mother tended him again, and he woke, believing he had been in a long sleep.

She explained the peril and the labour that had brought him back. Yet his thoughts still went to the daughter in Pohjola and the task involving the swan. His mother urged him to leave that bird alive and come home.

Together they returned to Kalevala. The journey he had imagined as a heroic pursuit ended in a homecoming made possible by someone else’s persistence.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.076`,candidateId:`EU-076`,definitionId:253,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`06bb637f8082441926860bf61493bfd797a2ba1a8d9f0e6c1c8ecec8968b7ef3`,variantSha256:`9853b16ee9fc9f099d70c0eaa67d19ccb7cbb447a73bb5655c5ffbc61f15324d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.076.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Unexpected Smith`,text:`A traveller arrived in Pohjola on a storm wind, without alerting the hunters or dogs. Louhi stood in the courtyard and questioned the stranger. When he named himself Ilmarinen, the smith she had been waiting for, she called her daughter and prepared a welcome.

She seated him at a well-provisioned table and gave him the comforts of her house. Then she asked whether he could forge the Sampo from the extraordinary materials she named. She offered her daughter for the work.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.076.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Wonder in Her Keeping`,text:`Ilmarinen built his workshop and began. Beautiful things rose from the furnace, but each had an unsuitable disposition. A weapon wanted victims; a boat sought quarrels; other creations wasted or damaged what they should have sustained. The smith rejected them until the Sampo appeared.

Its coloured lid covered a production of flour, salt and money. Louhi took possession of the wonder and brought it into a copper-bearing mountain. Nine locks protected it, and three strong roots extended beneath the surrounding world. The valuable thing would remain firmly in her keeping.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.076.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Provisions for the Return`,text:`The smith asked her daughter to depart with him. The young woman refused. She would not leave her home, seasonal work, birds and freedom. Her mother’s promise did not speak for her own choice.

Ilmarinen became heavy-hearted and wished to go home. Louhi asked why he was troubled and heard his answer. She gave him food and supplies, placed him in a copper-banded boat, and called on the north wind to guide his return.

She remained the keeper of the Sampo. Yet the departing smith did not leave her house without care: the same hostess who had made a difficult bargain furnished the means for his journey back.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.077`,candidateId:`EU-077`,definitionId:254,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`8e0eb740b9261e9cf4b38b01ca3f7b09b000af503f89d69db3086dcdda7838f6`,variantSha256:`3840c1a8764c09b875cff91c666806be3727ff289c2ec29558cd66d899cffd58`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.077.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Search for Shelter`,text:`Ilmatar had lived for ages in the open air, alone above the sea. Wearied by that solitude, she descended into the waters. The wind and waves surrounded her, and the sea became the place of her long wandering.

Time passed without a shore to reach. She called for help in her distress. Soon a duck appeared, flying from side to side in search of somewhere safe to nest. Wind could destroy a nest hung in the air; waves could wash away one made upon the water. No patch of land offered the bird a refuge.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.077.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Seven Eggs Break`,text:`Ilmatar raised her knee above the surface. The duck took it for a grassy resting place and built there. It laid six golden eggs and a seventh of iron, then settled to warm them.

For three days the duck brooded. The heat grew so intense that Ilmatar moved. The nest slipped, and the eggs fell into the sea and broke. Their pieces did not simply vanish. They changed into the foundations and lights of the world: earth below, sky above, sun, moon, stars and clouds.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.077.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Boundaries in the Water`,text:`Ilmatar remained in the waters, but the space around her had changed. She began shaping it further. A turn of her hand raised a hill; a movement of her body made banks and bays. Where she rested or dived, depths, rocks and reefs took form. Islands and firm ground appeared among the waters.

Her work gave the sea boundaries and the land places where life could later stand. Yet Väinämöinen, her son, still had not emerged to see the new lights. The world’s shaping preceded his first journey through it, beginning with the small shelter she had offered a searching bird.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.080`,candidateId:`EU-080`,definitionId:257,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`05cf4b6c9b8371a3b9117409804f193949f6f86a6efa0727b458a59739e850f5`,variantSha256:`37758aaf9d77753dfc87e4ec8161f766c4fba7e1b146bd9acba1e52f83b726de`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.080.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Materials from the Sky`,text:`During the telling of Otso’s origin, the bear is given a beginning beyond an ordinary forest den. His first materials come from the regions above, near the moon and Great Bear. A maiden travelling through the sky carries hair and wool, which she casts into the waters below.

Wind and current rock the floating materials and bear them toward a forested island. On its shore, Mielikki finds them. She gathers the wool and hair, joins them and places the bundle in a basket made of birch bark.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.080.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Mielikki’s Cradle`,text:`She secures the cradle among the high branches with golden chains. There she rocks and nurtures the small being until it lives and grows. Pine and fir surround the cradle, making the forest part of the bear’s earliest shelter.

Otso develops his broad forehead and wide mouth, short nose and soft coat. He can move, but his teeth and claws are not yet ready. Mielikki considers giving him these powers only if he will use them without harming the worthy.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.080.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Promise and Forest Life`,text:`The bear makes that promise. She searches for materials in several trees, finding that the first choices will not serve. At last she takes what she needs from the splendid branches and cones of a tree on the mountain, fashions teeth and claws, and sets them in place.

She lets Otso leave the cradle and teaches him how to move through marshes and woodland, rest in comfort and find shelter in winter. The young bear’s life opens into the forest around him.

The account gives him strength, but it also gives that strength a history of gathering, shelter and care. Before he becomes the bear encountered by hunters, he is the creature Mielikki has patiently helped to grow.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.082`,candidateId:`EU-082`,definitionId:258,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`e32151374c82eea620e95578b161059ddea362dd2885cf4309fa2c3f37c5314e`,variantSha256:`147ab0b60310f883fd3ab812b9be5122f8a09424e7bbf4091d8666d346d188a8`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.082.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Obstruction and Possibility`,text:`Väinämöinen steered the travellers’ vessel through difficult waters. When it reached the open lake, it stopped so firmly that Ilmarinen and Lemminkäinen could not free it. Looking below, they discovered that the boat rested on a giant pike.

The first attempts to overcome the obstruction failed. Lemminkäinen fell into the water and needed help; Ilmarinen broke his sword. Väinämöinen finally dealt with the fish, and the vessel could move again. They went ashore and shared a meal.

Afterward, Väinämöinen studied what remained. Could the bones become something wonderful? Ilmarinen did not believe his smith’s craft could make a fine object from fish bones and teeth. No other artisan offered the work.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.082.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`An Instrument No One Can Play`,text:`Väinämöinen therefore undertook it himself. He fashioned the instrument’s arches from the jawbones, used teeth for its pins, and made strings from the tail of Lempo’s stallion. People of different ages gathered to look at the unusual result.

The first players could not make it sound pleasing. Young and old tried, then Lemminkäinen adjusted it and tested the strings, but discord still answered. They took it to Pohjola in search of someone who could play it. Louhi and the people there also failed.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.082.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Maker’s Hands`,text:`A resting old man, disturbed by the harsh sound, demanded that it be discarded or sent back to its creator. The instrument itself answered. It would not sink into the water; it would sing in the hands of the one who had made it.

They carried it back to Väinämöinen. Making the object had been only the first part of its beginning. The next would depend on a meeting between those strange materials and a player who knew how to reach their sound.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.083`,candidateId:`EU-083`,definitionId:259,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f1112b778b77426467403e0d5602329256d8b4fb165300153729d598a233241e`,variantSha256:`b95b857d7cb09dd17b2265bee0ea97edddb4cf7ed518f75ef7bcc3375ed1de17`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/finnish-and-karelian-kalevala.json`,packetSha256:`334795c5a8898a7b0fca3d2e0721359ad02c62babd8cf368da866e7562b21ef1`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.083.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Three Missing Words`,text:`Väinämöinen had nearly completed a boat when three necessary words failed him. Searching ordinary sources did not supply them, so he decided to seek wisdom in Tuonela, the realm reached across a dark river.

After travelling through difficult country, he called for a ferry. Tuoni’s daughter answered, but she wanted to know why he had come while his body was still hale and active. He first claimed that death had brought him. She saw that the signs did not fit.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.083.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The River’s Questions`,text:`He tried other explanations: iron, water and fire. Each time, the keeper pointed to the evidence missing from his clothing and appearance. At last he admitted that he had come because his work was incomplete. She warned him that few visitors returned, yet eventually ferried him across.

Inside, the hostess offered drink. Väinämöinen inspected it and refused. He explained the words he sought, but she told him they would not be given and that he would not leave. Sleep overcame him.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.083.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Escape Without the Answer`,text:`Meanwhile, metal-thread nets were prepared and laid through the river to prevent his escape. When he woke, he sensed danger. He changed his form and slipped through the water in a small, serpent-like shape, passing the meshes that could have trapped his ordinary body.

The keeper of the nets found other creatures in them, but no Väinämöinen. He had escaped to the world of his people.

The missing words had not been recovered there. What he brought back instead was knowledge of a boundary he had treated too lightly. He warned against crossing the river while alive: reaching the far shore was possible, but finding a way home could not be assumed.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.085`,candidateId:`EU-085`,definitionId:260,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`274c481ec2d99e440a31485a133fd82409e9cc7127028e58a6bad59feeac9e3e`,variantSha256:`2c0ecec26c0e189714a868eed1bd90e1512b8cd4932610a48870de926ba03cf7`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/lithuanian-and-latvian-baltic.json`,packetSha256:`78481592db3b2917bd7f0c752eaaf9ca0b09332f74ee570ee9d5d7fad057e283`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.085.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Hunter and the Old Man`,text:`A hunter waited for rabbits when a cloud passed overhead. Thunder sounded, and a strange small creature emerged from beneath a rock. It retreated whenever the sky boomed, then came out again. The hunter fired at it. What lay there was no ordinary animal: it was a velnias.

An old man approached and asked what had happened. After looking at the hunter’s weapon, he remarked that his own shots from above had repeatedly missed. The old man was Perkūnas, and he proposed an exchange.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.085.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Loan with One Limit`,text:`His gift was a small firearm unlike the hunter’s familiar musket. It could bring down game, but it came with a warning: the hunter must never use its trigger. The man accepted the exchange and returned home.

For three years he lived comfortably. He had a farm, a wife and children. The strange gift had become part of a prosperous life, while the warning remained the one limit attached to it. At last he decided to test that forbidden part of the weapon.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.085.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Thunderous Reckoning`,text:`An immense thunderclap followed. The hunter fell, and Perkūnas appeared beside him. The old man demanded to know what he had done. The damage had reached far beyond a single shot; his words describe a third of the world struck down. He recovered his weapon and returned the ordinary musket.

At home, the hunter found his buildings flattened. For three years there was no thunder there. The teller says that Perkūnas never again gives that weapon to people.

The gift had brought abundance, but it had never become something the hunter fully understood. The warning belonged to its power, and his decision to ignore it changed both his household and the sky above it.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.086`,candidateId:`EU-086`,definitionId:261,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ec747f27be2987419f488582cebc5b1f0ff5497dc79430435dd37d3801452cf5`,variantSha256:`217545e74e33d3c85ebe3785b0e888984521811503e09d2b9958779bba0f1c43`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/lithuanian-and-latvian-baltic.json`,packetSha256:`78481592db3b2917bd7f0c752eaaf9ca0b09332f74ee570ee9d5d7fad057e283`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.086.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Night Request for Help`,text:`A woman knew something of healing. One night a man came to her home and asked for help. His wife was sick, and he wanted someone to come with him. The woman agreed and followed him to a great palace.

He brought her into the room where his wife lay. She attended to the patient and gave her medicine. The sick woman began to recover. Only after that change did the husband ask what payment he owed.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.086.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Charcoal Reward`,text:`The healer said she wanted nothing. She had come because somebody needed care; she did not name a price for the recovery. The man left the room carrying a sieve and returned with pieces of charcoal inside it.

He thanked her, handed over the charcoal, and accompanied her out. Away from the palace, she had an awkward gift to carry home. Charcoal could dirty her hands, and a sieve was an odd container for a reward. On the way she let some pieces fall, keeping only part of what she had been given.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.086.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Gold at Home`,text:`When she reached home, she discovered that the pieces she had kept were no longer charcoal. They had become gold. What had seemed burdensome and almost worthless now supplied her with wealth, and the short tale closes with her happy life.

The visitor’s identity is carried by the title: he is the velnias, and the recovering patient is his wife. Inside the encounter, however, he behaves as a worried husband and grateful host. The healer sees a patient before she sees a treasure. Her success rests on answering the request for help, while the reward becomes extraordinary only after she has returned to her own home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.087`,candidateId:`EU-087`,definitionId:262,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`79567529ce12e870de4dcd61af25d974ac1b566e7cc3bd4eac5b41a1ef63f96d`,variantSha256:`583198fe050b149522c66a33baa1198f2d6ac93c2190869d01aa7e2e46a72e28`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/lithuanian-and-latvian-baltic.json`,packetSha256:`78481592db3b2917bd7f0c752eaaf9ca0b09332f74ee570ee9d5d7fad057e283`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.087.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Wedding Guest’s Answers`,text:`A laumė walked toward a wedding with a white bag full of cakes. Along the road she met a young man, and they continued together beside the cultivated fields. He began asking where she could go.

Rye, wheat, barley: for these grains her answer left him uncertain. When he mentioned flax, however, she gave a definite reply. That was a field she could not enter. He asked about the force of a curse, and her answer suggested that angry words need not reach the heavens.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.087.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Help Becomes Theft`,text:`The young man remembered both answers. As they approached a flax field, he offered to relieve her of the bag. She accepted his help. He took the cakes, crossed into the flax, and settled where she could not follow.

There he began eating. The laumė ran around the field, furious at the trick. She had been carrying food for a celebration and had trusted a companion with it; now the boundary that protected him also prevented her from recovering what was hers.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.087.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Empty Bag`,text:`The young man answered her anger with a taunt, using her own earlier words against her. He kept eating until the bag was empty. The laumė finally demanded only that he return the container.

He threw the bag out of the field. She seized it and hurried away. The wedding cakes were gone, and the walk that had begun with company ended in distrust.

The encounter turns on a precise limit: this laumė cannot cross into flax. It does not make the youth’s offer honest or his theft generous. From her side of that field, an apparently helpful stranger has used an answer she freely gave to take the celebration she was carrying.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.089`,candidateId:`EU-089`,definitionId:264,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`9247f130d5097f03e324ecda56af6d187025081014f21626ac3a71689821cf10`,variantSha256:`692bac7a626aaa663b21a29418bf08ac9aab6e6e02832db9b13dc146119f273c`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/lithuanian-and-latvian-baltic.json`,packetSha256:`78481592db3b2917bd7f0c752eaaf9ca0b09332f74ee570ee9d5d7fad057e283`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.089.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Purchase in Riga`,text:`A farmer travelled to Riga intending to buy an aitvaras. Someone directed him to a merchant who had one for sale. The merchant said the purchase depended on knowing how to handle it, then gave the farmer an unsettling condition about the greeting he must give his wife.

The farmer paid five rubles and received a pair of hobbles. He set off for home with his new purchase, but the required greeting troubled him. He had missed his wife; the demand did not sound like something he wanted to say when he saw her again.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.089.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Road and the Changed Greeting`,text:`On the journey, the wagon shaft broke. He had no rope, so he used the hobbles to hold the damaged part together. The object connected with his strange purchase had become a practical repair for an ordinary problem on the road.

When he reached home, he changed the merchant’s requested words. The tale’s language now calls the fleeing being a velnias. It ran away and returned to the seller, leaving the farmer without the companion or benefit he had meant to buy.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.089.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Seller’s Complaint`,text:`The farmer travelled back to Riga and found the same merchant. The seller showed him the returned hobbles, now damaged by the misuse, and complained that he had not treated the purchase properly. According to the merchant, the being might have grown accustomed to him if he had obeyed the condition.

There is no successful bargain at the end. The farmer’s payment has not secured a reliable household helper, and the merchant’s instructions have collided with both a broken wagon and the buyer’s hesitation. What sounded like a commodity has proved difficult to define, let alone control.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.090`,candidateId:`EU-090`,definitionId:265,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`bccb19b3ea5de1e1e398b7113eb729fcd91e1c7e21537d748e6cb66af52f5590`,variantSha256:`6f52f2ab5859a689873e57f4ce5fd082f48ec1763912c9affa2671636c1ca93d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/lithuanian-and-latvian-baltic.json`,packetSha256:`78481592db3b2917bd7f0c752eaaf9ca0b09332f74ee570ee9d5d7fad057e283`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.090.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Night Delivery`,text:`A traveller stopped at a farm and asked for somewhere to sleep. The farmer took him in. The place was ordinary enough: a house offering shelter, the quiet outside a window, and the work of feeding animals continuing beyond the visitor’s arrival.

During the night the guest heard a voice announce that three wagon-loads of hay had been brought back. Another voice asked where they had been left. The answer was surprising: on the beehive.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.090.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Three Blades on the Hive`,text:`The traveller lay listening. A wagon-load was large; three would be larger still. He could imagine fodder stacked high enough to require space and labour. A beehive seemed an unlikely place for such a delivery. The announcement and its chosen location would not fit together in his mind.

In the morning he went outside to see. He did not find wagons or a towering heap. On the beehive lay three blades of grass. Against the remembered promise, they seemed almost too little to notice.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.090.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Another Measure of Abundance`,text:`But the short tale does not expose a cheat. Its closing explanation changes the measure of the gift: those three blades were as filling as three wagon-loads of hay. Quantity seen by the eye and nourishment supplied by the gift were different things.

The telling introduces kaukases as beings who bring feed, yet none is described standing in daylight beside the hive. The encounter comes through voices in the night and a small trace left behind. The visitor has travelled from a familiar estimate of abundance to something he cannot judge merely by its size. A useful gift can be slight enough to miss, while its effect exceeds the space it occupies.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.093`,candidateId:`EU-093`,definitionId:268,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ae7cbb183e45a60d6461199f119f0afd08a0a285b240163c23ff53156b9c650d`,variantSha256:`4a582a9af26a1aea68dff72eaad8354e7d7f77ba7a87522d5d1ef9188b936bcd`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/lithuanian-and-latvian-baltic.json`,packetSha256:`78481592db3b2917bd7f0c752eaaf9ca0b09332f74ee570ee9d5d7fad057e283`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.093.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Boast at the Flax Barn`,text:`People were processing flax in a drying barn when someone announced that a žiburinis was approaching. One worker answered with a boast: he was not afraid and wanted to see it. He went outside and fired. The shot passed through the visitor, and he returned claiming nothing had happened.

The workers continued their task, then went home, ate and lay down on straw spread across a dirt floor. The mistress of the house had heard nothing about the encounter. While her husband stayed with the drying flax, she prepared an early meal and let the men sleep.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.093.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Visitor Returns`,text:`She noticed a žiburinis passing the windows. She considered waking the sleepers but did not. The visitor returned, entered through a window, and changed into the form of a beautiful maiden.

The young-looking figure walked among the men, looking closely at each. When she reached the one who had fired, she touched him from head to foot. Then she moved back toward the window and vanished. The mistress was overwhelmed and lost consciousness; the men continued sleeping.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.093.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Morning’s Discovery`,text:`In the morning the master came home with a lantern, wondering why no one had returned to work. He found the sleepers still there and his wife unconscious. The workers woke, helped her recover, and discovered that the man who had fired was dead.

The teller closes with questions about that first decision to shoot. The night’s visitor has crossed from a sight outside the barn to a figure inside the household. What the worker dismissed because his weapon passed through it has returned in a form his companions can neither measure nor command.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.098`,candidateId:`EU-098`,definitionId:272,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7c873222b34407f206a0cf04710138f895122140940435ab818bdf174f98311a`,variantSha256:`987af1a429b440fd055f5ff8358df2cf5abad5a2a7a53655a0b1e04d4bc34348`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.098.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Strength and Broken Promises`,text:`Amirani's strength had carried him farther than the companions who grew up beside him. He had fought giants, escaped a monstrous captor, recovered an eye stolen from his adoptive father, and reached a tower suspended by a chain. When the world seemed full of obstacles, his answer was usually to break through them.

But strength had not made his promises reliable. In this telling he pledged protection in the name of Christ and then betrayed those who had helped him. The tale counts three broken promises. It also remembers his anger at a younger companion whose skill might one day exceed his own.

Eventually Amirani travelled alone. Few opponents remained. His victories had thinned the living world around him, and the account makes those deeds part of the reason for his punishment.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.098.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Two Captives Pull`,text:`He was chained to an iron post driven into rock. Qursha, a dog punished for killing deer belonging to God, was bound to the same post. The great human and the powerful animal now faced a task that could not be settled with one sudden stroke.

Throughout the year they pulled. The iron shifted by degrees, and the post began to rise. Freedom seemed to approach through patient effort: a little more exposed iron, a little less buried in stone.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.098.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Bird and the Renewed Prison`,text:`Then a small bird arrived and settled on the post. It had been sent by God. Amirani's anger returned, and he swung an iron beam at it. The bird flew away before the blow landed. The beam struck the post instead, forcing it back into the rock.

The cycle began again. The telling leaves him there with Qursha, close to freedom yet repeatedly undoing their labour. A hero able to overcome enormous enemies is defeated by the timing of his own response. The story offers no final escape in this version, and no promise that another display of force will solve the prison he has helped rebuild.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.099`,candidateId:`EU-099`,definitionId:273,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`327a1517bbd0fd5a2200c00e6ed13f131f0579b2776bdbd69f9e3f5d9dd219b7`,variantSha256:`1ad7d0f9a533d21e5ddbed604f0352d279481ab811cca039349cf78893eef603`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.099.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Beside the Same Post`,text:`Qursha shares an iron post with Amirani at the end of the tale. The account does not bring him into that scene as a harmless pet. It says that he had killed many deer belonging to God, and that he too was punished.

The dog and the hero are chained to the same anchor driven into rock. Whatever distances they once crossed, their world has narrowed to stone, iron, and the length of a chain. Their effort becomes a repeated pull against that fixed centre.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.099.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Year of Pulling`,text:`A year passes in the work. Together they draw the post farther out. The tale measures progress in what the chains can move, not in the number of enemies overcome. No sudden transformation gives him another way out.

At last the post is nearly free. A small bird arrives and perches on it. To the dog, it is another living creature at the centre of their enclosure; the account explains that God sent it. Its arrival is timed to the moment when the year's labour might succeed.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.099.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Mistimed Blow`,text:`Amirani becomes angry. He lifts an iron beam and strikes at the bird. The bird leaves the perch before the blow can reach it. The beam falls upon the iron post and drives it down again.

Qursha remains bound beside him. The same rock must be resisted, the same iron drawn upward, the same work begun again. The story does not say that the dog causes the blow or that he deserves the hero's renewed frustration. It keeps their shared captivity in view while giving the decisive action to Amirani.

In this version, Qursha's place is beside a hero whose power cannot finish their release. Its ending is an unfinished motion: two captives straining together, while one mistimed act repeatedly restores the obstacle.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.102`,candidateId:`EU-102`,definitionId:274,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`05449a510bb7ba947b15aa1b11eb1aeb4810836903f82fc251dc5ad2cfaab23f`,variantSha256:`a0d19ffd2127b02599f3308217a70b1f5ff36c47a5b2105b36b9a73c71446dec`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.102.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Master’s Bargain`,text:`A peasant wanted his son to learn a trade. On their journey he drank from a stream and exclaimed at the goodness of the water. Out came Vakhraca, a supernatural master in human form, offering exactly the opportunity the father had been seeking.

The offer had a condition. After one year the father must recognise his son. If he failed, the boy would belong to the master. The peasant accepted without understanding how the test was arranged.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.102.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Dove That Returns Last`,text:`A year later the courtyard was filled with boys, and the father could not pick out his own. But his son recognised him and quietly explained what would happen next. Vakhraca would turn the pupils into doves. On the outward flight the boy would lead; on their return he would be last.

The birds rose. The father watched the movement instead of looking for an unchanged face. When the doves came back he pointed to his son, and the bargain required Vakhraca to release him.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.102.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`What the Pupil Has Learned`,text:`The pupil had learned transformations well. On the road he became a hound and then a falcon, arranging sales from which he could escape and rejoin his father. Becoming a horse was more dangerous. He warned his father against a particular buyer and, especially, against surrendering the bridle. The father ignored the warning for a higher price. The buyer was Vakhraca.

Locked in a dark room, the pupil noticed a sunbeam entering through a hole. He became a mouse and slipped out. The master followed as a cat. Each new form answered the previous danger: fish and net, pheasant and falcon, apple and knife, millet and hen.

At the end the pupil became a needle and the master a thread passing through it. The needle reached the fire; the thread burned, and the boy escaped.

Vakhraca's teaching was real, but his claim over the pupil failed. What the son had learned became the means of leaving the bargain that had trapped him.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.104`,candidateId:`EU-104`,definitionId:275,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`bd5b987befee9a700f2080d6ef8737a87e9158f19fc5648d792bd74fd0ff23f2`,variantSha256:`e100531d93b4fa4267a9d7fa230b76d18efd162fa525e74e3eee00c2dd9e5cdb`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.104.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Guest Who Chooses`,text:`Asphurtzela reached the house across the stream with questions about the family he had never met. His sister had been drawn there by a false trail, and three brothers had vanished after following her. Now the youngest brother arrived without accepting the household's demand that a human guest should hide.

He recognised his sister and explained how he had been born after her disappearance. When the hundred-headed devi returned carrying game and tree roots, Asphurtzela announced himself as the host's wife's brother. He asked to be welcomed, though everything about the place warned against trusting that welcome.

The evening became a contest. Asphurtzela prepared the supper with astonishing speed. Where his brothers had accepted bones, he chose meat. Where they had settled for a drinking horn, he asked for the larger vessel. When offered a bed or a place in the stable, he took the bed.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.104.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Sound in the Night`,text:`The devi went to the stable instead, unable to sleep. In the darkness he began sharpening a huge sword. Its sound reached the room where Asphurtzela lay.

The boy understood enough. He rose, put a log under the coverlet, and concealed himself nearby. The room still seemed occupied when the devi entered. A great blow fell on the bed, splitting the hidden log and throwing up dust. Satisfied, the attacker left.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.104.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Alive in the Morning`,text:`Asphurtzela returned to the bed and slept. In the morning he appeared alive before the astonished host. Asked whether he had felt any pain, he answered calmly. Even the suggestion of a flea bite brought no admission.

Then came wrestling. The devi could not move him; Asphurtzela drove his opponent into the ground and demanded the truth about his brothers. The disclosed coffer and its restoring handkerchief allowed him to bring them back.

He had heard danger being prepared and moved before it arrived. A piece of wood had received the force intended for him; now he stood with the brothers he had come to find.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.105`,candidateId:`EU-105`,definitionId:276,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7eccd92a40effd96f3b09361cd463d359f60928ead4a3c5505eaa1b1d144a78d`,variantSha256:`e43348df62e2223620780940b763bdb5c7d0dd8b6337b93e86dedc23415d269a`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.105.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Abduction and the First Pursuit`,text:`The princess was travelling from her wedding on a flying carpet when Kazha-Ndii swept down and carried her away. Her husband left the wedding company to find her. The journey eventually brought him to the village where his sisters lived with three demi brothers.

Those brothers had seen the abduction. They had pursued Kazha-Ndii-Kerkun through the air, but managed only to pull away a lock of the woman's hair. When the prince recognised it, he knew their story was his own.

The next morning he borrowed a horse and a little dog. Kazha-Ndii was away when the prince reached the house. Inside, the princess warned that her captor could not easily be overcome. The prince nevertheless placed her on the horse and rode toward the gate.

The gate was an accomplice. It sounded an alarm so great that a star fell, then called to Kazha-Ndii that the woman had been carried away. The pursuer came swiftly. His horse neighed, arresting the escape. Kazha-Ndii killed the prince and returned with the princess.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.105.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Restored Prince Returns`,text:`The little dog carried the fallen prince back to his helpers. The youngest demi restored him and lent a three-legged horse for another attempt.

Once again the princess left the house with her husband. Once again the gate cried out. Kazha-Ndii pursued, confident in the horse whose voice had stopped them before. For a moment the new horse slowed too.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.105.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Pursuer’s Horse Stops`,text:`But this time the pursuing horse heard an answering neigh. The three-legged helper's voice stopped it, and the prince had an opening to confront Kazha-Ndii. He defeated the captor and escaped with the princess, returning by way of the demis' home.

Kazha-Ndii is the adversary in this telling, not the youth attempting the rescue. His swiftness, his house's speaking gate, and his horse's power make captivity difficult to leave. Their defeat belongs to the particular sequence of assistance gathered against him, rather than to an assumption that every demi has the same loyalties.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.107`,candidateId:`EU-107`,definitionId:277,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`b2e6be2b26d4be610ee39c1e43de944d0125a3d66fb612c15f3479cf8d37751c`,variantSha256:`3e12af0817ee118de6c3263e9f4d34eecebaf4a3386ce01bc6498c39781affa2`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r14-georgian.json`,reportSha256:`e6757d7cc5bb32c5608e5a188c6abc68695a89c7013b3ea003afe0e4a702be7f`,packetPath:`traditions/georgian-and-kartvelian-caucasus.json`,packetSha256:`9dcd733021f0bde7e82d66e336843cfdf71339dbdf5c15af30e73d12a04ab0cc`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:07:38.905Z`,independence:`Packet first-authored by /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read complete assigned episodes and public academic selected text.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.107.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Two Impossible Tests`,text:`A strong man arrived from far away, asking who in the kingdom would wrestle him. The king gathered a crowd, but nobody stepped forward until a much smaller man offered to face him.

The challenger dismissed what he saw. His opponent proposed a test before the wrestling began. The strong man took a stone and squeezed it so hard that moisture emerged. The smaller man substituted a sponge resembling the stone and pressed out still more.

A second test followed. The strong man threw a stone down and broke it into dust. His opponent hid a stone in the ground and scattered flour instead. To the watching giant, the result looked like another impossible feat.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.107.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Brotherhood and the River`,text:`The giant decided against wrestling. He offered friendship and brotherhood, then asked why the smaller man would not return his handshake. The answer was another claim of dangerous strength: he could not safely control his grip.

Together they set out for the giant's house. At a stream the smaller man asked to be carried, explaining that cold water would worsen a stomach ache. When the giant remarked how little weight he felt, his passenger claimed to be supporting himself by holding the sky.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.107.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Improvisation at Dinner`,text:`At the house, preparing dinner produced new difficulties. The smaller man could not lift the weights on the wine jars, so he called out as if ready to bring up the entire buried stock. Alarmed, the giant rushed to protect it.

Then a huge loaf trapped the visitor beneath its weight. He explained that he was using its warmth to treat his stomach, and asked his host to remove it. Even a sneeze that sent him toward the rafters became an occasion for a boast and a demand for a ladder.

The tale gives the smaller wrestler ingenuity rather than actual giant strength. His tricks keep a confrontation from becoming a contest his body cannot win. Its humour rests on improvised explanations and a repeatedly mistaken impression, not on treating short stature as a failure.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.118`,candidateId:`EU-118`,definitionId:285,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`db0d65edb863189b21000ed3dd5fad7146bd387198d0ea834589bbecee1e9a0a`,variantSha256:`caf729811cdcbab2dc889f440a9b09e1f88a9735692ba87a27d54bf5f215dd75`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r20-armenian.json`,reportSha256:`1f94ec3b88574c170face0ecbc8905626cd25c44c6a1552a2cab49856605436c`,packetPath:`traditions/armenian.json`,packetSha256:`b546842bb40d23bb25872cb0c571f75de78174cf72a0d70dc1d1dce375fd4f07`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:17:08.056Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently compared actual stories/prompts to selected relevant witnesses.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.118.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Hunters Who Do Not Return`,text:`Hunters did not return from the strip of country beyond the steep hills. A king went there despite the warnings of his companions, and he too disappeared. After his elder son inherited the kingdom, the younger set out to learn what had happened.

An antelope drew him and his companions over broken ground toward a thick forest. When their horses could go no farther, they dismounted. Among the trees they found a tent beside a fountain, with a meal laid out inside.

The prince held back. His companions ate and drank while he searched the ground around them. Signs of earlier deaths made him hurry back, but he came too late. The food and water had been poisoned. He could not save the men who had followed him.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.118.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The White-Armored Leader`,text:`Hoofbeats approached. He tied his horse to a sycamore and hid in the bushes.

Riders entered the clearing and began taking the dead men’s belongings. Their leader wore a complete suit of white armor. Long hair fell around the rider’s face. The prince raised his bow, then lowered it when he recognized a woman.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.118.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Name and a Challenge`,text:`He stepped out to confront her. He demanded to know who she was and challenged her over the deaths in this country. His questions offered several possible answers—human, fairy or demon—but none was an answer she gave him.

Anger crossed her face. Then she spoke, and the sound of her voice held his attention as firmly as the white armor had.

Her name was Zoolvisia. If his courage matched his challenge, she told him, he must come to the place where she lived.

She spurred her horse away. Trees and rocks closed behind the riders, leaving the prince in the clearing where his search had ended in another loss. He now had a name to follow. Whether that name led to an explanation, another danger, or both, he had yet to discover.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.119`,candidateId:`EU-119`,definitionId:286,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`08b3a151b0b12e543da63d45c9bcbf3381d03e6d777d7686b1c2ceb8c1391820`,variantSha256:`a273ab94d2352f249b5018e7be4ecd16bb2011911fda68e78c6cb46a6fd4e06d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r20-armenian.json`,reportSha256:`1f94ec3b88574c170face0ecbc8905626cd25c44c6a1552a2cab49856605436c`,packetPath:`traditions/armenian.json`,packetSha256:`b546842bb40d23bb25872cb0c571f75de78174cf72a0d70dc1d1dce375fd4f07`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:17:08.056Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently compared actual stories/prompts to selected relevant witnesses.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.119.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Four Rescued Companions`,text:`His mother had given him one rule to carry into the world: do no harm to an animal.

The young woodcutter earned little. One day, after selling his load, he found boys mistreating a kitten and spent his coins to set it free. He went home without the bread he had meant to buy. On another day he rescued a mouse. Then he freed a small dog, and finally rescued a little snake.

All four stayed with him.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.119.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Bread and the Snake-Prince`,text:`In the forest, when he stopped cutting wood to eat, his companions gathered near a fountain. He tried to send the snake away, thinking it should return to its own life, but it would not leave. He offered it bread from his hand.

The snake changed into a boy.

He was the son of a king, transformed by magicians and unable to escape their spell until a human hand gave him bread. The woodcutter had freed him without knowing whom he helped. Now the restored prince wanted to take his rescuer to his father.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.119.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Ring Requested, a Gift Received`,text:`Before they reached the court, he explained what reward to ask for. His father wore a ring with a jewel. Turned in a particular way, it could call two supernatural helpers. Other riches might look more generous, but the prince knew the ring’s value.

The king welcomed his lost son and promised the woodcutter a reward of his choosing. When the young man asked for the ring, the king hesitated. It was dearer to him than the treasures he had expected to give. Yet he had made his promise, and he kept it.

The woodcutter returned to his old mother with the ring and a gift of gold. He had left home carrying wood to sell and advice to remember. Kindness to creatures that could offer him nothing had brought him back with something he could never have bought.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.121`,candidateId:`EU-121`,definitionId:287,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`e70b1b4c6e8c6c586d268c3d3e4c89ff00ec954d3aefacb97c7444d3002ef09b`,variantSha256:`3e1877909e98919e2f985d9b758cdcc13030c434eb6df6453ec421f80c50ea08`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r22-basque.json`,reportSha256:`59c49d8e023c50ba78dc1b1789a62fc255e8ae981f14c3576591a489dc1b5661`,packetPath:`traditions/basque.json`,packetSha256:`f18c2e140da525792c23ba62405368e3402e3b663dbe872528cceafa2076324b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:31:12.705Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read actual current card prose/prompts and specifically scoped fresh source passages.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.121.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Wheel That Will Not Turn`,text:`At Olarte, below Urigoiti, a great wooden wheel had been fitted for the works. It should have turned, but it remained stubbornly still. The owner had invested in movement and received a motionless circle instead. No ordinary explanation settled his fear.

He sent a tenant to Supelegor to ask the Lady of Anboto for help. The visitor found her spinning. A calf lay beside her, and the work continued with the steadiness the wheel at Olarte lacked. The cave was not presented as an empty wilderness: someone lived and worked there.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.121.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Spinning, Advice and a Cup`,text:`The tenant described the problem. The Lady answered that the wheel had not been blessed and that a demon held it fast. He should go to the superior of a convent. The remedy she named belonged to Christian practice, even though the person explaining it was the extraordinary woman of the cave.

Before he left, she offered him cider. He accepted the cup and, following his customary habit, invoked Jesus as he drank. The words changed the hospitality between them. The Lady told him to keep the vessel, because that name made taking it back painful to her. The collection’s cross-reference identifies the cup as gold.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.121.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Movement Returns at Olarte`,text:`The tenant carried both advice and cup away. Back at Olarte, the convent superior came in clerical dress to bless the wheel. The tale describes a great noise, the departure of the obstruction and the wheel’s first movement. What had been a still construction became a working one.

The account leaves the Lady at her own spinning and the household at its turning wheel. It does not erase the strange overlap that made the resolution possible: an otherworldly adviser sends a visitor toward a Christian rite, then parts with a cup because of the visitor’s Christian words. Two kinds of movement answer each other across the story, a thread drawn in a cave and a wooden wheel released below the mountain.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.122`,candidateId:`EU-122`,definitionId:288,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`1e5ebcb43f487cced0e174ebfd51c44d6e760f3308eab26eb88c4865d4d3576f`,variantSha256:`e8084f2803d90860f984c6b3d91088041636d28f9c061f81e8600483dee07438`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r22-basque.json`,reportSha256:`59c49d8e023c50ba78dc1b1789a62fc255e8ae981f14c3576591a489dc1b5661`,packetPath:`traditions/basque.json`,packetSha256:`f18c2e140da525792c23ba62405368e3402e3b663dbe872528cceafa2076324b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:31:12.705Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read actual current card prose/prompts and specifically scoped fresh source passages.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.122.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Two Brothers and a Remembered Attack`,text:`Two brothers entered Baltzola and saw Sugaar there. The younger brother attacked the serpent with stones and damaged his tail. The elder disapproved. They left the cave with the same journey behind them, but they had not treated its inhabitant in the same way.

Years passed. The elder brother was far from home and missed the place he had left. When a mysterious man met him, the stranger offered more than sympathy. He brought him back to Baltzola with astonishing speed, collapsing the long distance between longing and return.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.122.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Two Different Gifts`,text:`There were gifts as well. The elder received a box filled with gold. For his younger brother, the stranger gave him a red belt. The brothers’ different conduct had been answered with different objects, though the elder could not yet know what that difference meant.

At home, he delivered the belt. His brother refused to wear it. Instead, he fastened it around a chestnut tree. The tree burned, and a hole remained where it had stood. An object that looked suitable for a human waist had revealed another purpose when placed around wood.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.122.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Stranger Recognized`,text:`Only then did the brothers understand the visitor’s identity. The man who had crossed the distance so easily was Sugaar, the same being they had encountered in the cave. The elder’s objection had not been forgotten, and neither had the younger’s attack.

The short account closes with that recognition rather than another fight. Its objects hold the contrast: gold carried home by the brother who protested, red cloth taken to a tree by the brother who distrusted his gift. The story does not need to make every stranger a serpent or every belt a threat. It concerns this cave encounter, these brothers and a remembered choice that reaches forward through years.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.124`,candidateId:`EU-124`,definitionId:290,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`2c8731f23761a4123e263522610a2b8fd8fb574b682190dec5ac464345624124`,variantSha256:`e1cc2fa5691553989aa5c5105848b3e5774c34cfcf1110c852701a3e4839128d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r22-basque.json`,reportSha256:`59c49d8e023c50ba78dc1b1789a62fc255e8ae981f14c3576591a489dc1b5661`,packetPath:`traditions/basque.json`,packetSha256:`f18c2e140da525792c23ba62405368e3402e3b663dbe872528cceafa2076324b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:31:12.705Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read actual current card prose/prompts and specifically scoped fresh source passages.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.124.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Linen Taken from the Willow`,text:`Near the house of Munaguren stood a willow above a pool associated with lamias. Its branches held more than leaves. The lamias climbed there to comb their hair, and when their washing was finished, they spread cloth through the boughs to dry.

People could glimpse this work from nearby, but an approach changed everything. The lamias slipped back into the water. The willow remained, with its hanging linen and the quiet pool beneath it. A passerby might mistake that sudden absence for an invitation.

A woman of Munaguren took one of the sheets. It was an ordinary household need met by an extraordinary theft: cloth carried away from someone who had washed it and set it out. She brought it home, leaving its owner nothing to gather from the branch.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.124.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`An Owner Comes Asking`,text:`That night a lamia came to the house. She did not arrive to tempt a traveler or to perform a marvel for an audience. She knocked because something belonging to her was inside. Her demand was simple: return the sheet.

The woman kept it. The visitor came again, and the request returned with her. What had seemed a small gain became a sound at the door, recurring after dark. The household could shut its entrance, but it could not make the borrowed silence last.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.124.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Return and Promised Abundance`,text:`At last the housemistress threw the sheet back to the lamia. The cloth had crossed the threshold twice, first as stolen property and now as something restored. The waiting owner received it.

The ending offered more than the woman had expected. Instead of announcing another demand, the lamia declared that Munaguren would never be short of linen. In this telling, abundance followed the return of what had been taken. The willow’s cloth was not a treasure without an owner; it was work, and the one who had done it could come asking for it.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.125`,candidateId:`EU-125`,definitionId:291,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`674580ada6a1731b75e168f6e9e637da6f38c463f06298e50169a28b78976268`,variantSha256:`88595da9c9faca9b54da7b5d1e445e06b188b5e8cfce4fa9d7a39586ffedb7fa`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r22-basque.json`,reportSha256:`59c49d8e023c50ba78dc1b1789a62fc255e8ae981f14c3576591a489dc1b5661`,packetPath:`traditions/basque.json`,packetSha256:`f18c2e140da525792c23ba62405368e3402e3b663dbe872528cceafa2076324b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:31:12.705Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read actual current card prose/prompts and specifically scoped fresh source passages.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.125.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Dangerous Host Falls Asleep`,text:`A shepherd searching the mountains for his flock came upon Tartalo. The giant had one eye in the middle of his forehead and a strength that made ordinary resistance seem useless. When he invited the shepherd into his cave, the young man followed. Only inside did he discover what kind of host had welcomed him.

Tartalo meant to keep him there. The shepherd watched for an opening and found none: the giant was too strong, the cave too easily guarded. The shepherd acted while Tartalo slept and left him unable to see. This telling leaves the violence behind that change offstage. Blindness did not make the doorway safe.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.125.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Fleece beneath the Giant’s Hand`,text:`Tartalo stood astride the entrance and began letting the sheep out. He ran a hand over each woolly back. If the shepherd tried to pass, the hand would find a man instead of an animal.

The shepherd took a sheepskin and crouched beneath it. He waited until he could join the moving flock, then crept forward. A hand descended. It felt fleece. He kept his fear from breaking his posture and passed under the giant’s legs. Beyond the entrance, he cast the hide away and ran.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.125.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Ring That Gives Away Its Place`,text:`But Tartalo’s possessions were as dangerous as their owner. A speaking ring warned the giant of the escape. He threw it after the shepherd; it caught the little finger and began betraying the direction of every step. The giant could follow a voice where his eye could no longer guide him.

The shepherd tried to free himself. The source makes the separation costly, and its violent moment remains outside this account. Eventually the ring was away from him in the water, still calling as though its new position were all that mattered.

Tartalo rushed toward the sound and entered the water after it. He did not return. The shepherd’s escape had depended first on understanding the giant’s hand, then on turning the ring’s certainty into uncertainty for its owner. Strength had guarded the cave; a voice had undone its guardian.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.126`,candidateId:`EU-126`,definitionId:292,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`340d6417d4337f1939566062efbf8d08657c43d262993e8b1c2c1deafdcda1fe`,variantSha256:`055e4c713dd2c07dc7b2c090b79cf560da1bac1dc07666df5aa8623ca5b49819`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r22-basque.json`,reportSha256:`59c49d8e023c50ba78dc1b1789a62fc255e8ae981f14c3576591a489dc1b5661`,packetPath:`traditions/basque.json`,packetSha256:`f18c2e140da525792c23ba62405368e3402e3b663dbe872528cceafa2076324b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:31:12.705Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read actual current card prose/prompts and specifically scoped fresh source passages.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.126.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Charred Gift Turns Gold`,text:`A shepherd from Urigoiti went into Supelegor. Inside he found a jentil tending a heap of burning charcoal. It was a place of work, warmth and another person’s rules, though the visitor had entered it without understanding any of those rules.

The jentil told him to leave. The master was asleep, and waking him would not be wise. Yet the keeper did not send the shepherd away empty-handed. He offered a partly burned piece of wood from the fire.

The shepherd was unimpressed. His father made things like that, he replied; a charred stick seemed no gift worth seeking in a cave. Still, he took it and went outside. Away from the firelight, he discovered that what he had carried was gold.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.126.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Visitor Returns for More`,text:`The discovery changed his understanding of the encounter. The same hand that had warned him away had given him something valuable, and he had barely recognized the generosity while standing before it. He could have continued home with the first gift.

Instead, he returned to ask for another. The keeper repeated the warning about the sleeping master. The cave had not become safer simply because one visitor had become richer.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.126.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Second Gift and Uneasy Words`,text:`Nevertheless, the jentil gave him a second piece. This time the gift was accompanied by a grim prediction: the shepherd’s house at Arkotxa would never lack people judged mad. The narrator’s old judgment is left as a troubling saying, rather than treated as a medical explanation or an accusation against a real household today.

The shepherd had learned to recognize gold, but recognition did not tell him when to stop asking. The account closes with abundance made uneasy by the words attached to it. The first firebrand crossed the threshold as something easily dismissed; the second crossed it with a consequence the visitor could not dismiss.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.129`,candidateId:`EU-129`,definitionId:295,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c70f5ed18ba2c70a94a2df69d40fdf0c5af78639eb2584c96a1ea9923252bbc9`,variantSha256:`e7c9ee8948cc776ba51ce5fc915db7e88e4fa1b229b2f7b55b7289237430121d`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r22-basque.json`,reportSha256:`59c49d8e023c50ba78dc1b1789a62fc255e8ae981f14c3576591a489dc1b5661`,packetPath:`traditions/basque.json`,packetSha256:`f18c2e140da525792c23ba62405368e3402e3b663dbe872528cceafa2076324b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:31:12.705Z`,independence:`First author /root/georgian_research. Coordinator independently read actual current card prose/prompts and specifically scoped fresh source passages.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.129.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Dog at the Rescue`,text:`A serpent lived in a cave near a king’s town. It had seven heads, and the people could not keep themselves safe from its hunger. They made a dreadful bargain: each year a young woman would be surrendered. When the choice fell upon the king’s daughter, the king promised his inheritance and her hand to whoever could save her.

The princess waited beside a tree while onlookers kept their distance. A shepherd came by with his dog, César. He asked why she was there and heard the bargain that had brought her to that place. Instead of joining the distant watchers, he stayed.

The serpent approached with a terrible commotion. The shepherd entrusted the struggle to César, and the dog overcame it. This account leaves the battle and its grisly aftermath outside the scene. The princess was free to return home.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.129.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`False Credit and Three Visits`,text:`But rescue and recognition did not travel together. Another man reached the court claiming he had defeated the monster. He carried trophies from it and seemed to have the evidence the king needed. The shepherd had kept the missing proof, separately wrapped in seven pieces of cloth from the princess’s clothing. He did not arrive in time to stop the celebration.

At the feast, César went in for food and returned to his master. Three times the dog crossed between the banquet and the shepherd. The princess recognized him. A command to restrain the animal came too late; he was already back with the person who had actually helped her.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.129.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Missing Proof`,text:`The shepherd finally entered and spoke. The claimant displayed his trophies. The shepherd showed the proof they lacked, and the pieces of cloth connected his account to the princess and the rescue. The false story could no longer stand alone.

The tale ends with the king honoring his promise to the shepherd. Its reward follows the marriage conventions of this old account. The moment that makes the ending possible, however, belongs to a witness who cannot speak at the feast: a dog returning again and again to the right master.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.135`,candidateId:`EU-135`,definitionId:298,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6d5bc4ba62dad0d4ca36b50a1efc71a1261d78b2c4230ee20c0f2b651ee6955a`,variantSha256:`5299a774e4b4d75654c8f533cb092abed8bd004a608b801739f95aebcb712861`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.135.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Pool and the Open Cage`,text:`Hunters vanished in a king’s forest until one visitor dared to investigate its stillness. At a deep pool he saw an arm rise from the water. With help, he emptied the pool and found a wild man whose body was brown like rust and whose long hair covered his face. The king confined him in an iron cage.

The king’s eight-year-old son lost a golden ball inside that cage. The captive would return it only if the boy opened the door. On the third day the child took the hidden key and let him out. Afraid of punishment, he called after the departing man, who carried him into the forest.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.135.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Golden Well and a Hidden Helper`,text:`Iron Hans gave him a moss bed and set him to guard a clear golden well. Nothing must fall into its water. First the boy dipped a sore finger; then a fallen hair touched the surface. On the third day, while he studied his reflection, his long hair entered the water and became entirely gold.

His service ended, but Iron Hans promised help if he called from the forest. The youth found work in another court’s kitchen and then its garden, hiding his golden hair under a cap. As he grew older, war threatened that kingdom. He called the wild man, received a strong horse and armed companions, and helped turn the battle before disappearing again.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.135.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Three Days and a Freed King`,text:`At a later festival lasting three days, Iron Hans lent him different armour and horses. Each day he caught the princess’s golden apple. His hidden identity eventually came to light, and his parents arrived for the wedding, relieved to find their lost son.

Then a stately king entered the feast and embraced him. This was Iron Hans, released at last from the enchantment that had made him a wild man. The helper’s earlier form had never been his whole identity.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.140`,candidateId:`EU-140`,definitionId:299,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`02f4dd404411842c8ae13665ed15718fa88bc08ba9490ae42b3f11e6960f8a17`,variantSha256:`7368a9736204c19a859b896992db21b24b469a9c5041f5833ad986f8f509ffd4`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.140.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Horse Sees the Exchange`,text:`Falada carried the queen’s daughter toward the court where she was to marry. He was a horse who could speak, and he travelled beside the attendant’s mount while the princess carried her mother’s protective cloth. At first the journey still belonged to the life prepared for her at home.

That changed beside a stream. The attendant had refused to bring her water, and while she bent to drink, the cloth drifted away. The attendant forced her to surrender her fine clothes and exchange horses. She mounted Falada herself and threatened the princess until she swore to keep silent at the royal court. Falada saw the exchange and knew which rider was the true princess.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.140.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Voice at the Gate`,text:`The false bride was welcomed at the palace; the true one became a goose tender. The impostor feared the speaking horse would reveal her secret, so she arranged his death. But Falada’s testimony did not end. At the dark gateway where the geese passed morning and evening, the princess addressed him, and a voice answered that recognized her sorrow and remembered her mother.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.140.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Somebody Listens`,text:`Conrad, the boy who tended the flock beside her, heard this exchange. He also saw the wind carry his hat away whenever he tried to take the princess’s hair. He complained to the old king, who then hid at the gateway and listened for himself. What seemed strange to the boy became a reason for the king to inquire further.

The princess finally told her account to an iron stove, where the king overheard it through the pipe. Her identity was restored. Falada had not travelled the whole road safely, but the witness the impostor tried to destroy had kept answering until somebody listened.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.eu.142`,candidateId:`EU-142`,definitionId:300,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`e246c993c5833bfbd7b35228f69fb9102e89b7a29528c8b1e37fbd8ca8dc5695`,variantSha256:`b0593e707d7c57b5cd4de4e4a917dd9f0ab03c380f34204bd1185b04040341af`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r07-kalevala-baltic-grimm.json`,reportSha256:`c84cecaf997128c7672a1c8a07047f28908308f722c4ecbb1a67ec8819786c66`,packetPath:`traditions/german-speaking-grimm-folktales.json`,packetSha256:`fd8e4c26039f3ade9055c30d20921756a90d484d254215a6a356ca43223d9178`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":true,"packetAuthors":"All three packets were authored by coordinator; this reviewer did not author them.","writeScope":"Only this report; packets and integration remain unchanged."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.eu.142.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Hunger and an Exchange`,text:`Three hungry brothers left home to seek their fortunes. The eldest stopped at a mountain of silver; the second chose a mountain of gold. The youngest kept walking until he was exhausted in a great forest. After climbing a tree to search for a way out, he found a meal spread below him. The cloth beneath it could produce food whenever he asked.

He took the cloth onward. A charcoal burner offered to share a simple supper, but the traveller spread his own feast for both of them. Impressed, the burner proposed an exchange: the cloth for an old soldier’s knapsack. A tap on the bag would summon a corporal and six armed men, obedient to its holder.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.142.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Trades Turned into Seizures`,text:`The traveller agreed. Once away, he tested the bag and commanded the seven soldiers to demand his cloth back. They obeyed. He had converted an agreed trade into a seizure and kept both gifts.

At two more fires he repeated the pattern. He gained a hat that called destructive guns and a horn that could bring buildings down, then sent the soldiers to reclaim the cloth again. Each acquisition left another person without the promised exchange.`},{id:`vision.definition.eu.142.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Power Without Opposition`,text:`Home brought no welcome. His brothers mocked his ragged appearance and refused to recognize him. He answered with soldiers, escalating the quarrel until the king’s forces intervened. Still greater force let him demand the princess in marriage and the government of the kingdom.

The princess tried to take away the objects that held that power. Each attempt brought another violent response. In the end the horn destroyed the old royal household, and the traveller made himself king. What had begun with hunger and a miraculous meal ended in a country where nobody could oppose him.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.001`,candidateId:`LW-001`,definitionId:302,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`2ce6f93fe974a3ccf9fe7d52063b917f95486e406043883069c5281512cb904d`,variantSha256:`2ffffdca270fb3f10e26ae0386cd9ee5aa02a8f8ae5e85d0066601db29838afd`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.001.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`After defeating a giant and several powerful animals, Akiti no longer waited for anyone to offer him a title. He called himself king of the forest. He travelled with a triumphant wrestling song, and those who knew his strength feared and respected him.

But a claim repeated confidently is still a claim. The elephant challenged it. This wise rival knew charms and regarded itself as the forest’s monarch. Akiti had defeated other opponents; he had not defeated this one.

The hunter threw his spear. It struck the elephant’s protected hide and glanced away. He tried arrows and then his knife, without success. The means by which a hunter ordinarily overcame an animal failed against the elephant’s charm.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.001.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Akiti had a charm too. He changed from a man into a lion and attacked, replacing the reach of his weapons with the force of another body. The elephant threw him off. Akiti next became a serpent. Even that form could not crush his rival. Each change made him dangerous in a different way, but none settled the title.

Then he abandoned the large forms. As a fly, he approached the elephant’s flapping ear. The contest that had filled the forest with great bodies and weapons narrowed to an opening a tiny insect could enter.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.001.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The ending is severe: Akiti went inside, became human again and killed the elephant from within. His rival fell. Akiti emerged and claimed the forest’s kingship without further opposition.

The story grants him an unquestioned title at the close. Yet the sequence makes the listener follow what the title required: failed weapons, failed strength, a final small transformation and a fatal attack the elephant could not meet outside its body. Akiti’s ingenuity wins the contest. The fallen rival remains part of that victory, rather than becoming an evil creature whose death needs no thought.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.005`,candidateId:`LW-005`,definitionId:304,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`11e050c41e57b4f6450aaa0a0a50b2365eccee0fa1621b2732f86ac151577fe4`,variantSha256:`1d33d6ca9f5d69ce2901160d06e026b9d8f6c06d0c7afad45b60022a8c126ebf`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r16-vietnamese-yoruba.json`,reportSha256:`86737b49ac6c61b9390aaaf9f135a21220347765303c6b099b1a199d36ec28e8`,packetPath:`traditions/yoruba.json`,packetSha256:`29e4bd641070e652bed5cbc148b1358d89de64acfbc89cec5299c65218fdd19d`,reviewer:`gpt-6.1-sol independent /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`These exact packets were authored by the coordinator, not this reviewer. Prior Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts were disclosed and excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.005.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Raids had filled Ile-Ife with fear. The attackers looked so strange that people thought they were more than human, and attempts to appease the gods had not stopped them. Moremi decided to learn what fear had hidden. She would allow herself to be captured and discover who the raiders really were.

Before leaving her husband and their son Ela, she went to a stream. She promised its god the richest sacrifice she could afford if she succeeded. Then she entered the danger she had chosen.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.005.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Taken prisoner, Moremi was brought into the enemy king’s household as a slave. Her intelligence and character gained respect there, and she learned to observe what her own people had only seen from a distance. The attackers were ordinary men. Their frightening appearance came from coverings of grass and bamboo fibre, not supernatural bodies. Their disguises also made them fear fire.

Moremi escaped and returned to Ile-Ife carrying this knowledge. Her people received her news with joy. Armed with the understanding she had gained, they defeated the raiders. The force that had seemed beyond human resistance had become something people could recognize and oppose.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.005.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`But the stream promise remained. Moremi brought valuable animal offerings, and the god demanded more: her son’s life. The tale turns here from liberation to grief. Ela was sacrificed, and the people mourned with his mother. They promised to be her sons and daughters always, acknowledging a loss that victory had not prevented.

The chapter does not end with Ela lying there. After the mourners departed, he recovered consciousness. A rope of grass carried him upward to heaven, and the telling holds out his future return.

Moremi had brought her people out of terror by looking carefully at what they feared. Their gratitude could not erase the cost the story placed beside her courage. Both remain in the account: a woman returning with knowledge, and a community remembering what followed.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.033`,candidateId:`LW-033`,definitionId:320,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`aec96bdb49c9de43c0b3396dd65f00a29ba063cf6cf73481226eeb862350005d`,variantSha256:`71f0abe8b6fe5284593b5216a21e070823f4cbd8c5879d1269fd37b4b70ff941`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r23-maori.json`,reportSha256:`2e4708add0a8b57d7fa0bd4ad69ff698b31a28c11098e93d134ce7fca7541516`,packetPath:`traditions/maori.json`,packetSha256:`86725b7f5a5aae9b4f48ecfad63701acafc09b2504eaa4c7798e34732fb3c09b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:51:05.704Z`,independence:`First author /root/welsh_research. Coordinator independently inspected actual21 card records and fresh selected source passages, including community-published full narratives. Exact unavailable primary detail remains held.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.033.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A World within an Embrace`,text:`Ranginui and Papatūānuku held one another so closely that darkness lay between sky and earth. Their children lived within that embrace. They had come into existence, but had no open world in which to grow.

The parents’ bond surrounded them. To the children, it was also the closeness that kept light away.

They began to consider separation. If sky could be lifted from earth, there would be room between the two. Their lives would no longer be confined to the darkness where they had begun.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.033.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Tāne Opens the Interval`,text:`Tāwhirimātea did not join the attempt. The others tried to force their parents apart, but the embrace held. Effort alone had not yet changed the world around them.

Then Tāne used his legs.

He pushed against the sky while braced upon the earth. Ranginui rose away from Papatūānuku, and the space that had been closed began to open. What the children had sought became possible: light could enter between their parents.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.033.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Sky and Earth Remain Parents`,text:`The father was now above them, the mother below. They remained the two parents of the family whose act had separated them. The children’s new world had emerged from a change within that relationship.

Tāne’s association was with the forest; Tangaroa’s with the sea. Rongo belonged with cultivated foods, and Haumia with foods growing without cultivation. Tāwhirimātea was associated with wind. The natural world held these relationships as well as the space between sky and earth.

For the children, separation brought room and light. For Ranginui, it meant that the embrace in which they had lived no longer enclosed them. The sky that rose above their new world was still their father, and the earth beneath them was still Papatūānuku.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.034`,candidateId:`LW-034`,definitionId:321,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ea9d374649b11c6fefa27a6e8d6300dd8ffe92ce57df92486c5acf651073460c`,variantSha256:`6c16f19534f3e334ca1ea02b53a45200e20ea50e42e8a85739de2f20eaf2fb77`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r23-maori.json`,reportSha256:`2e4708add0a8b57d7fa0bd4ad69ff698b31a28c11098e93d134ce7fca7541516`,packetPath:`traditions/maori.json`,packetSha256:`86725b7f5a5aae9b4f48ecfad63701acafc09b2504eaa4c7798e34732fb3c09b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:51:05.704Z`,independence:`First author /root/welsh_research. Coordinator independently inspected actual21 card records and fresh selected source passages, including community-published full narratives. Exact unavailable primary detail remains held.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.034.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Children in Darkness`,text:`Papatūānuku lay with Ranginui in an embrace that left no opening for light. Between earth mother and sky father, their children lived in darkness.

The children wanted space in which their lives could unfold. To gain it, they decided that the two parents must be separated. Their hope for daylight began inside the relationship that had brought them into being.

Tāwhirimātea stood apart from the attempt. The other children tried, and the parents remained together. The earth and sky did not yield to those first efforts.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.034.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Earth and Sky Part`,text:`Tāne found another way. He used his legs to push the sky upward from the earth.

As Ranginui rose, the closed space between the parents opened. The darkness in which their children had lived was no longer all around them. Light could reach the new interval, and earth and sky became the two great presences around it.

Papatūānuku remained below.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.034.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Mother beneath the New Light`,text:`The children had not gained their world by finding another mother or another father. Their own parents now stood in a changed relation to one another. The place that had confined them became the place in which there was room to live.

The family’s associations extended through that world. Forest, sea, wind, cultivated foods and foods growing wild were linked with different children of the two parents. Their lives were not detached from the earth and sky that surrounded them.

For Papatūānuku, the movement carried Ranginui away from the embrace. For the children, it brought the opening they had sought. Both belonged to the same act.

The earth mother stayed beneath them as light entered. Above her, the sky father had been raised. Between the two, the children could begin to inhabit the world their separation had made possible.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.035`,candidateId:`LW-035`,definitionId:322,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`95754e226e8779134878146369c5696e79c1079a284d2cf8620675e75ed93773`,variantSha256:`19d7df78b5034b08dac3c93e12ac55f10eedfbf4febb991d3b3b96f8dc9d53ed`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r23-maori.json`,reportSha256:`2e4708add0a8b57d7fa0bd4ad69ff698b31a28c11098e93d134ce7fca7541516`,packetPath:`traditions/maori.json`,packetSha256:`86725b7f5a5aae9b4f48ecfad63701acafc09b2504eaa4c7798e34732fb3c09b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:51:05.704Z`,independence:`First author /root/welsh_research. Coordinator independently inspected actual21 card records and fresh selected source passages, including community-published full narratives. Exact unavailable primary detail remains held.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.035.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Attempts That Fail`,text:`Tāne lived with his siblings in the darkness between Ranginui and Papatūānuku. Sky father and earth mother were held together in an embrace, and their children had no open space around them.

The children decided to try to separate their parents. Their aim was to bring light into the place where they lived, so that existence would no longer remain enclosed.

Tāwhirimātea did not take part in that effort.

The others tried, but the parents remained together. What they wanted was clear; achieving it was another matter. Their efforts had not yet opened the darkness or lifted the sky from the earth.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.035.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Effort of Two Legs`,text:`Then Tāne used his legs to press the two apart.

He braced against the earth and pushed the sky upward. His action changed the relationship that had held their world closed. Ranginui rose above Papatūānuku, and an interval opened between them.

Light could now reach the children.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.035.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Room for the Family’s World`,text:`The parents remained sky father and earth mother, but they no longer surrounded their offspring in an unbroken embrace. What had been a confined place had become an open world. Tāne’s effort had given the children room between the two presences from which they had come.

In that world, Tāne was associated with the forest. Tangaroa was associated with the sea, Tāwhirimātea with wind, Rongo with cultivated foods, and Haumia with uncultivated foods. Their relationships extended through the natural world rather than ending when the sky rose.

For Tāne, the decisive act had been bodily effort: legs pushing where other attempts had failed. Its consequence was larger than his own place within the family.

Earth stayed below and sky stood above. Between them lay the light the children had sought, made possible by the separation Tāne had brought about.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.042`,candidateId:`LW-042`,definitionId:329,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c11a2687c950878096742a92cfa1210991d6aded4a3b7e770122b3b362c5edbc`,variantSha256:`c1f0f085813e7ca3ae5d5d8dc5fca5a8966219e7b7309d949932fcbfb270429e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r23-maori.json`,reportSha256:`2e4708add0a8b57d7fa0bd4ad69ff698b31a28c11098e93d134ce7fca7541516`,packetPath:`traditions/maori.json`,packetSha256:`86725b7f5a5aae9b4f48ecfad63701acafc09b2504eaa4c7798e34732fb3c09b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:51:05.704Z`,independence:`First author /root/welsh_research. Coordinator independently inspected actual21 card records and fresh selected source passages, including community-published full narratives. Exact unavailable primary detail remains held.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.042.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Hidden Passenger`,text:`Māui wanted to fish with his brothers, but they would not take him. They feared that his extraordinary powers would bring trouble into their canoe.

After they returned, he went down to the shore in darkness and hid beneath its bottom boards. The next day they launched without seeing him. Only when they were well out at sea did he climb from his hiding place.

His brothers wanted to turn back. But the shore was suddenly far away, and Māui offered to help bail the canoe. They continued.

At their usual fishing place, he urged them onward. At the next, he urged them farther still. At last they were out of sight of land. There the brothers lowered their lines, and the canoe soon filled with fish.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.042.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Catch Larger than the Canoe`,text:`They were ready to return. Māui had not yet made his own catch.

He lowered his line into the deep. Something took hold below, and he began to pull. The weight strained against him. Foam and bubbles rose as his brothers feared that the canoe would overturn in the open sea.

Māui kept drawing the line upward.

His great fish emerged: a portion of the earth itself. The canoe, which had floated over water, now rested upon the catch.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.042.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Brothers Do Not Wait`,text:`Before leaving, Māui told his brothers to be patient and keep the fish whole until his return. He intended to make the proper acknowledgments before it was divided.

They did not wait.

They began cutting while he was away, and the fish struggled. Its movement left the great catch uneven, with heights and hollows where it might have lain smooth.

Māui had brought land from beneath the sea. The brothers had gained something far beyond their ordinary day’s fishing, but their impatience had changed the shape of what he raised.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.047`,candidateId:`LW-047`,definitionId:334,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0d0121b500d9c1df1ff42f6dae882fd05c6ac6dc4760b812b38fc1ab5b268da2`,variantSha256:`13f1dba4642738a83bbd56aed16e6f650c441ecc549f17b8383d8f2d788ee4eb`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r23-maori.json`,reportSha256:`2e4708add0a8b57d7fa0bd4ad69ff698b31a28c11098e93d134ce7fca7541516`,packetPath:`traditions/maori.json`,packetSha256:`86725b7f5a5aae9b4f48ecfad63701acafc09b2504eaa4c7798e34732fb3c09b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:51:05.704Z`,independence:`First author /root/welsh_research. Coordinator independently inspected actual21 card records and fresh selected source passages, including community-published full narratives. Exact unavailable primary detail remains held.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.047.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Promise and the Parting`,text:`Before Taurenga was born, Hine-kōrako asked Tāne Kino for a promise. He must care for their child until the child could care for himself. Keeping that promise would allow her to remain free of the pull of her watery ancestry.

Tāne Kino agreed.

When Taurenga was old enough to crawl, the family attended a gathering. In the middle of it, the baby needed cleaning. Embarrassed, his father called Hine-kōrako to do the work he had promised to undertake.

She took Taurenga to the stream, washed him and fed him.

Then Tāne Kino remembered his promise. He went after them and asked forgiveness. But the broken commitment could not be undone. Hine-kōrako wept over their son, handed him to his father and told him she must return to her home beneath Te Rēinga Falls.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.047.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Flood and a Call for Help`,text:`Her departure did not end her relationship with the people descended from her.

One night, a heavy flood came down the Hangaroa River. The community was driven from its homes, and canoes swept downstream toward the falls. What was ordinarily a waterfall had become a raging cascade.

The people aboard the canoes were carried toward that danger.

One elder kept his presence of mind. He sought Hine-kōrako’s help.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.047.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Canoes Are Saved`,text:`The rush toward the falls stopped.

The canoes no longer swept into the cascade, and everyone aboard was saved. Hine-kōrako, who had returned beneath the water after a promise was broken, still answered the need of her descendants.

The earlier parting had placed her beyond the home she shared with Tāne Kino. Yet in the flood, that distance did not erase the bond. The people who were being carried away found protection through the relation that remained.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.048`,candidateId:`LW-048`,definitionId:335,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`152187897388ddfb6ea258c5aa555a9b88d001c3e94e8722058ecfae5d0220fa`,variantSha256:`4c97a377ba3d045aded222115ee70739c0dba3d4cc65549f44614d661c258145`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r23-maori.json`,reportSha256:`2e4708add0a8b57d7fa0bd4ad69ff698b31a28c11098e93d134ce7fca7541516`,packetPath:`traditions/maori.json`,packetSha256:`86725b7f5a5aae9b4f48ecfad63701acafc09b2504eaa4c7798e34732fb3c09b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:51:05.704Z`,independence:`First author /root/welsh_research. Coordinator independently inspected actual21 card records and fresh selected source passages, including community-published full narratives. Exact unavailable primary detail remains held.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.048.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Scales and a Journey toward Parikawhiti`,text:`Ngārara Huarau lay in a cave while people on the Heretaunga plains lived without thinking of him. Water moving over the rocks muffled the sound of the great sleeper.

Then he emerged, breaking the quiet.

As his scales scraped against stone, some fell away and became tuatara. His enormous body cut a channel through the land, and people fled. He reached the ocean and caught the familiar scent of his sister, Parikawhiti.

He followed it south along the coast, searching at river mouths. His journey led up the Pahaoa River, through the Wainuioru and along the Marumaru Stream. Near the Maungarake hills, he knew she was close.

Climbing left great impressions in the ground. At the heights, he rested and found another scent: people. He settled in the Kourarau valley near his sister.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.048.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Tupurupuru Answers the Danger`,text:`Travellers began to disappear.

The people sought Tupurupuru. They needed an answer to a being too large to meet safely in an ordinary fight. He listened and prepared a trap among the trees near the cave.

His dog’s barking drew Ngārara Huarau from the lair. As the taniwha chased it, his sweeping tail struck the weakened trees. They fell upon him, and the threat was brought to an end.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.048.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Traces of the Journey`,text:`The great journey had left its traces in the telling: channels, river routes, hills and the small tuatara born from fallen scales. It had also left people who needed help before they could travel safely again.

Ngārara Huarau had followed a familiar scent toward his sister, but his presence brought terror to the valley. Tupurupuru’s response changed the ending for the people whose lives had become bound to that danger.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.050`,candidateId:`LW-050`,definitionId:337,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`cd28734ab56353bba1c78d12f62d5403e77e240e0083395abf58f7b7c8c3868d`,variantSha256:`98c4a152865e91e460fce6dc5d03285b404d0b613df933eea9aa6ca09d6ea14a`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r23-maori.json`,reportSha256:`2e4708add0a8b57d7fa0bd4ad69ff698b31a28c11098e93d134ce7fca7541516`,packetPath:`traditions/maori.json`,packetSha256:`86725b7f5a5aae9b4f48ecfad63701acafc09b2504eaa4c7798e34732fb3c09b`,reviewer:`/root coordinator secondary review`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:51:05.704Z`,independence:`First author /root/welsh_research. Coordinator independently inspected actual21 card records and fresh selected source passages, including community-published full narratives. Exact unavailable primary detail remains held.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.050.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Visits between Shore and Sea`,text:`At sunset, Pānia came from the sea to the shore below Hukarere cliff. She sheltered in a clump of flax beside a freshwater spring. Before dawn, she returned to the water.

One day Karitoki, a chief of the area, came to the spring because he was thirsty. He saw Pānia and took her home to become his wife.

Her days on land did not end her relationship with the sea. Each morning, she went back before daylight. The chief’s home and the waters beyond it remained different parts of her life.

After some time, she had a son, Moremore. The child was without hair.

Karitoki began to fear that the sea people might take both mother and son from him. The daily return that had always belonged to Pānia’s life now seemed to him a threat to the family he wanted to keep ashore.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.050.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`An Attempt to Keep the Family Ashore`,text:`He sought advice about preventing them from going back.

Then he acted while they slept, attempting to bind them to life on land. But his attempt did not succeed in keeping the family as he wished. It brought a different and permanent change.

Pānia became a rock, forever in the ocean. Moremore became a taniwha in shark form.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.050.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Reef and the Guardian`,text:`The sea had not become a place they could simply leave behind. The chief’s fear and his effort to prevent their return had changed their lives rather than securing them within his home.

Moremore remained connected with his people. He lived in a cave in the sea and guarded the coastal waters and inner harbors where they fished and gathered food.

At the beginning, Pānia could move between shore and sea. At the end, her place was in the water, and her son’s continuing relationship with the people took the form of guardianship.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.055`,candidateId:`LW-055`,definitionId:342,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`dc9efacdad9cf9c244fa9a7462d61ae16dd9ff413c8bec5afc7076bb79ded5c2`,variantSha256:`c3e6ec8f3a02cfc48188e5c33ad28a5e38e8eaad5dab1e95c37388dddd52b4df`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.055.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Before fire filled the darkness, there was time to listen.

Lonomakua brought Pele into a lava tube when she came of age. The stone enclosed a quiet space, but the lessons reached beyond its walls. He directed her attention toward the sun and moon, ocean tides, and the many clouds, winds and rains of the world outside.

For months, the teaching continued. The island was not a simple background. Freshwater descending from volcanic heights followed rock and soil toward the sea. The sky changed above those paths. Ocean movement belonged beside the movement of rain. Pele received knowledge whose parts were connected even when they could not all be seen from the same place.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.055.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`The cave remained the place where that knowledge was handed on. It held a teacher and a learner before it held a blaze. The preparation gave weight to what would follow: fire would not arrive as an isolated display, detached from the world she had learned to notice.

At last Lonomakua turned to the lesson of fire. In the darkness, the earth began to tremble. A spark appeared. Pele encouraged it, and the fire rose until its light reached the cave’s roof. What had been enclosed in shadow became visible around them.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.055.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The growing flame excited her, but the decisive moment was more than excitement. Pele understood that keeping this fire burning was her kuleana, her responsibility.

The months of instruction had led to that recognition. Light filled the place where she had listened, and the world beyond the cave remained part of what the fire meant. Lonomakua’s teaching had brought Pele to the work she knew was hers.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.056`,candidateId:`LW-056`,definitionId:343,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`010b04407ae5d4f038827bd27895e8b9e32d6219c6f816422a62f44bb627ad3b`,variantSha256:`7ee9de303422fb147e709a6841043d28258c3798f91cc07298a5a3f57c2772ca`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.056.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Hiʻiaka agreed to travel to Kauaʻi to bring Lohiʻau back to Pele. Before leaving, she asked for the protection of her groves and of Hōpoe. What she loved would remain behind while the journey carried her across the islands.

On Kauaʻi, the task was not simply to find a traveler ready to leave. Lohiʻau had died. Hiʻiaka restored him to life, and together they began the return. What had seemed an impossible task became a return journey with Pele’s beloved alive.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.056.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Oʻahu brought another obstacle. An unnamed chiefess who had been Lohiʻau’s lover wished to keep him for a night. A contest stood between that wish and the travelers’ onward course. Hiʻiaka won, and the return continued.

Meanwhile Pele’s patience ended. She destroyed the groves Hiʻiaka had asked her to preserve, and Hōpoe was overtaken by lava. From afar, Hiʻiaka perceived what had happened. Her journey now carried the knowledge that the trust left behind had been broken.

At Kīlauea’s rim, Hiʻiaka embraced Lohiʻau. Pele directed her sisters to kill him, but they hesitated. Pele herself brought about his death.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.056.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Hiʻiaka’s anger reached into the ground. She struck through successive layers beneath the volcano. Below lay water that, if released into the fires, could end Pele’s volcanic power. A return from a distant island had become a threat to the foundations of the home itself.

At the last moment, Hiʻiaka was dissuaded. Reconciliation prevented that final breaking open. She was not an interchangeable sister or a decorative companion: her promises, losses and decisions shaped the journey, from the grove she left to the depths she nearly opened.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.057`,candidateId:`LW-057`,definitionId:344,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d129c87c682e5243834abff9a588224e1fc1ae83b4868a5a3838e6e9e615a753`,variantSha256:`b75071b027a4b6b34308e453c20bcf2db031f76ee6cc9aba7578c1727e640af2`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.057.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`ʻŌhiʻa already loved Lehua when Pele wanted him for herself. That existing love stands at the center of the account: before either transformation, there was already a bond between two people.

In anger and jealousy, Pele changed him into a gnarled tree. The man Lehua loved remained before her in another form, rooted where he could no longer stand beside her as a human companion. Their separation now had a visible, living shape.

Lehua grieved for him. The rough trunk and branching shape held the person she had lost, but offered no simple way to bring him back. Pele would not undo what she had done.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.057.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`There was no simple return to the earlier moment. The tree remained, and Lehua’s appeal had to turn elsewhere. She sought help from her ʻaumākua, her family ancestors. The telling leaves them unnamed; they are not an unrelated company of fairies.

They answered by changing Lehua into the bright red blossom of the ʻōhiʻa tree. Reunion came through a second transformation rather than the cancellation of the first. Tree and blossom could now remain together. What had seemed a fixed separation became the shape of renewed connection.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.057.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The story relates the rain said to follow the picking of a lehua blossom to the pain of separating the lovers again. That association gives the flower a place in the emotional ending; it is not a promise that a reader can command weather.

ʻŌhiʻa’s story ends in the living tree, with Lehua flowering upon it. The red bloom makes visible the bond that neither the first change nor Pele’s refusal to reverse it could finally remove.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.058`,candidateId:`LW-058`,definitionId:345,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`af87c9f5bd0ee03cee3c457a7f11c399caf5f6844c75337b5d7331c11516b1f9`,variantSha256:`46b113d283273f9ec0b78707e653648192dcd3778fb7d1983f12d78e5e31fa93`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.058.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Lehua’s beloved was no longer a man standing beside her. Pele had wanted ʻŌhiʻa, who was already in love with Lehua. In jealousy, Pele changed him into a gnarled tree, leaving the lovers separated by a transformation neither had shared.

She had not chosen the separation. The tree made it visible, but it did not tell her how to accept it. A return to his human form would have been the simplest repair: the man restored and the lovers together once more.

Pele would not undo the change.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.058.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Lehua then appealed to her ʻaumākua. In this account they are her family ancestors, unnamed by the public telling. Her plea moved the story away from Pele’s refusal toward another possibility for the bond she wished to preserve.

The answer did not bring back ʻŌhiʻa’s former body. Instead, Lehua became the bright red flower borne by the ʻōhiʻa tree. Both lovers now existed in changed forms, and the blossom belonged with the tree. Their reunion differed from the restoration Lehua first sought, yet it answered the separation that had driven her appeal.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.058.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The tree held its red flowers where a companion’s human presence had once been desired. The telling associates the rain said to follow a picked blossom with the lovers’ renewed separation. The rain association brings the story back to the lovers’ bond, and to the pain of breaking it again.

Lehua is not simply an ornament added to ʻŌhiʻa’s ending. Her grief, request and appeal to family ancestors carry the account toward reunion. At the end, the blossom marks that action: the connection she sought remains visible, bright against the changed shape of the one she loves.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.059`,candidateId:`LW-059`,definitionId:346,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`6074c310f72fadeec24da5ec70194bc790b747f3006465fbc81baeb07eb59b53`,variantSha256:`38d42bfc413c846f990b0e69db1a9e1967b5e60a65d16af51a4366ab85c9a4e7`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.059.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Kamapuaʻa’s beginning did not promise an easy place in his family. Born in hog form on Oʻahu, he was rejected, recovered by his mother and raised with his grandmother’s care. The account then describes a strong grown man whose life carried both human and hog contexts.

His travels were as extraordinary as his beginnings. On a large leho shell, he crossed the sea toward the southeast Kīlauea coast. The shell was a cowry, not a pointed conch, and its carrying scale belongs to the story rather than ordinary natural history.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.059.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`At Akanikōlea he encountered Pele. Their exchange sharpened into hostility, and Pele’s fire rose against him. A coastal arrival became a confrontation in which the landscape carried the opposing forces.

Kamapuaʻa’s sister Keliʻiomakahanaloa brought a cloud from the south. Heavy rain filled the crater and put out the fire. The rain changed the struggle’s balance, but did not bring peace.

In hog form, Kamapuaʻa swallowed the crater. Pele’s family faced danger from the very place that had held their power. Under his ʻaumākua’s direction, he released them rather than completing their destruction. Mercy interrupted the danger.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.059.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The conflict resumed when he returned toward Akanikōlea. Pele’s lava advanced again. Kamapuaʻa took other forms, including ʻamaʻu, to oppose it. Neither side yielded the complete victory a straightforward battle ending might promise.

The account closes in stalemate. The traveler who crossed on a shell remains more than a pig-headed warrior. Human and hog, rain and fire, vegetation and lava belong to the changing contest. His voyage brought him into that meeting; it did not settle it forever.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.060`,candidateId:`LW-060`,definitionId:347,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`b61ba85808f3a9420a47c2c4577747b784525b8edb554481ad230fec3faf1f65`,variantSha256:`7f085d3cfd54c7eb17df02254eccea1f749e2cd1e88f97458f7e3c557252901b`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.060.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Punaʻaikoaʻe was surfing at Waimānalo when he met the beautiful Kalamainuʻu. He followed her from Oʻahu to Molokaʻi, where the place she brought him became captivity rather than companionship.

Kalamainuʻu’s brother, Hīnale, told him that she was a moʻo. The knowledge changed the meaning of the cave and the person who kept him there. Punaʻaikoaʻe sought a way to escape. He asked her for cold water associated with Poliʻahu on Maunakea, and a leaking vessel delayed her long enough for him to flee.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.060.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`He reached Kīlauea. An earlier relationship connected him to Pele’s family, and he found protection. The flight across islands brought him out of a confined place into the shelter of a powerful household.

Kalamainuʻu pursued him. She summoned moʻo from the islands and threatened the volcano. Their force met Kīlauea’s own heat: the confrontation was no longer enclosed within the cave where he had been held.

Volcanic eruption overcame the pursuers. Kalamainuʻu escaped to Lokoaka, but did not carry Punaʻaikoaʻe back into captivity. Their deaths ended the threat gathered at the volcano. What matters for his story is that the pursuit could not reclaim him.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.060.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`At the end, the account identifies koaʻe kea as Punaʻaikoaʻe’s kinolau. The white-tailed tropicbird continues to fly at Kīlauea under protection. Its passage is not a decorative mascot appended to a rescue tale. It is the named relationship through which the telling carries his continued presence.

The bird’s long tail follows its turn above dark rock. The ending holds movement where the beginning held confinement: a protected life remains in flight, rather than returning to the place that closed around him.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.061`,candidateId:`LW-061`,definitionId:348,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`045ae8413e9b77878747e7ded4a8b83e218211a171a9424c15c71595cae64999`,variantSha256:`794f510df86eb0a6bc897c0f622978ef71673cd61d68ba18c77a2765190e4f90`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.061.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`At Pānau on Hawaiʻi Island, the shark Kaʻehuikimanōopuʻuloa belonged to a family and a coastal home. His name recalled Kaʻahupāhau of Oʻahu, but the Puʻuloa of his own account was not Pearl Harbor.

The selected journey begins when his father dreams of the shark’s wish to travel. He wanted to meet the sharks of Hawaiʻi’s districts and seek adoption by Kamohoaliʻi. The sea beyond home was not empty territory: it held other named beings and relationships.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.061.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`He traveled along the island’s coast, meeting district sharks in Hilo, Kaʻū, Kona, Kohala and Hāmākua. Companions joined the journey. Each stretch of coast brought him into a wider company than the one with which he had begun.

Then the voyage left the sheltering outline of Hawaiʻi. The ʻAlenuihāhā Channel lay between the travelers and Maui, and its rough crossing made the move beyond the island a difficult one. On Maui, the shark Kauhuhu attacked him. Kaʻehuikimanōopuʻuloa survived and overcame the attacker, continuing the journey he had begun beyond his own coast.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.061.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`At Kahoʻolawe he found Kamohoaliʻi. The great shark figure received him, and the adoption he had sought became part of his life. His new kinship brought many forms and long life, widening the relationships that had carried him across the sea.

His travels extended onward toward Kahiki, but the ending retains the coast from which he came. The account remembers his continuing ʻaumakua relationship at Pānau and his connection with Pele’s group. Distance had enlarged his relationships without erasing his home.

The small reddish-brown shark’s journey therefore closes with more than a successful crossing. It links a named family, district companions and a sought elder while keeping the two Puʻuloa places distinct.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.062`,candidateId:`LW-062`,definitionId:349,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`78fb17461cecb57916ce7b5414f5f9db3e57377f80c209ae1a413aa7b4493aa5`,variantSha256:`0326659a04cee22d76272ab4e1e72e6cd102902a29d4789f04160cc4024e12f1`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.062.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`A young couple left their disapproving families and settled at Kamāʻoa in Kaʻū. People gathered around them and came to regard them as chief and chiefess. When the chiefess expected a child, the community shared their joy.

She died before giving birth. The chief mourned her, and the story links a remarkable new vine with the place where she rested. The vine moved westward across the districts, carrying the account beyond the household’s loss.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.062.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Near the boundary between Kaʻū and Kona, a gourd grew beside a fisherman’s home. He wanted it as a vessel for fishing gear. He tested the ripening gourd without knowing the relationship the long vine carried.

The chiefess appeared to her husband in a dream, distressed by what was happening. He followed the vine through the land until he reached the fisherman’s home and the gourd growing there.

The men disputed its ownership. To the fisherman, the gourd was on his land. To the chief, its origin connected it with the wife he had lost. Once the fisherman understood that connection, he allowed the chief to take it home.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.062.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The chief carried the gourd back to Kamāʻoa wrapped in fine white kapa. The next morning it cracked open. Two seeds fell into his hands and quickly grew into twin girls.

They remained unnamed in the public telling, but their place in it was anything but anonymous. They became ancestresses of Kamāʻoa and the districts crossed by the vine. A story that began with a couple seeking a home and a community mourning a death ended with a continuing family line.

The vine’s route held those places together. The twins’ emergence belonged to that particular ancestry, rather than to an invented genealogy for all Hawaiʻi.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.063`,candidateId:`LW-063`,definitionId:350,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`5ac99084673ac554bd1b398b408164d1f7a193f0a4193947d638378811a88f1f`,variantSha256:`3495dad4820617b2bb649829cb5ed11b1384fdf905144fd6a6e4f6b0781d1e23`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.063.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Pīkoi had traveled from Kauaʻi when he looked across the country near Puʻumanawaleʻa. On Hōlei he saw a rat unlike the small creatures others knew around the sweet potatoes: a great grey-black form, sleeping on the hillside, with a crooked tooth.

The people did not accept what he described. Their disagreement was not merely over whether a shot could be made. They disputed the animal itself. The distance let its size and shape remain doubtful to those who had not recognized it.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.063.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Pīkoi said he could strike its nose with his bow. Hōʻiu challenged him, and their contest became a wager far more severe than a test of skill. Pīkoi and ten men stood on one side; Hōʻiu and ten on the other. Their lives were part of the stakes, alongside food.

The bow-shot broke the stillness. The rat startled and ran toward ʻĀpua, where it died. What had seemed a motionless dark shape had become an event that could be followed across the land.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.063.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Eight witnesses checked the result. The proof settled the claim, but success did not turn the gathering into a harmless celebration. Hōʻiu and the ten men on his side were killed, fulfilling the deadly stakes they had accepted.

Pīkoi’s perception was vindicated, yet the account’s ending carries the consequences of the wager with it. A large rat had first been mistaken for something ordinary or impossible; a boast about seeing and shooting had exposed people to irreversible harm.

The named hills and coast keep the story local. They hold both the remarkable sighting and the cost of the proof that followed it.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.064`,candidateId:`LW-064`,definitionId:351,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`046c710c049446b76be85991abe890eaa8ffb8f4fefbfa08cb2c8d27191f35e8`,variantSha256:`3959685083e93357a654faf86f6a33d26b52a89443766c6c82fd2c37e244098e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.064.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`On Hōlei, the giant rat lay asleep. Its grey-black body could be read from a distance as a dark part of the hillside, but a crooked tooth and the scale of its form made Pīkoi recognize something living.

He had come from Kauaʻi. When he described the animal, others dismissed the claim. They knew small rats around the sweet potatoes; the creature he saw did not fit that familiar measure. Their disbelief left the sleeping rat suspended between an ordinary landscape shape and an extraordinary animal.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.064.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Pīkoi’s answer was a promise that he could hit its nose with his bow. Hōʻiu challenged him. The dispute grew into a wager in which eleven people on each side were exposed to death. Food was also staked, but those lives made the contest much more than a contest of skill.

The rat had no part in making that bargain. It remained the object through which the people proposed to prove or disprove a claim.

Pīkoi shot. The animal woke and fled toward ʻĀpua, where the account says it died. The quiet form on the ledge had become movement, and the movement ended beyond the original hillside.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.064.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Eight witnesses established that Pīkoi’s claim had been fulfilled. Hōʻiu and his ten companions were then killed, a severe consequence of the wager they had made.

The rat’s extraordinary size had disturbed what the people thought they knew. The stillness on the hillside was broken by a dispute the animal never joined. Its grey-black shape and twisted teeth had first shown Pīkoi a living creature asleep within the land; the proof of that sighting brought consequences beyond the rat itself.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.065`,candidateId:`LW-065`,definitionId:352,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ea51e898db79b6873e4fd745768b95f9b3bea0961371c6f8bc1341746bd0c26f`,variantSha256:`e78d166e4b66c8c4afba2f71dd23117ed3c5fe2a747e98bcc7f2b60260da23b6`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.065.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The sun hurried across the sky, and Hina’s kapa remained damp. Its passage was too brief for the work she needed to finish. The difficulty belonged to daily life below the bright movement: there was effort, cloth and too little time.

Māui saw the problem faced by his mother. He went to Haleakalā to meet the sun. The mountain brought him toward the place where its passing could be confronted, rather than leaving him to watch another short day disappear.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.065.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`In the selected telling, a rope held the sun. Māui restrained its movement and kept it from simply racing onward. Restraint created a pause the hurrying light could not otherwise give him, making room for the request that had brought him up the mountain.

The effort mattered because holding the sun opened time for a conversation. Māui’s purpose was not to claim a throne in the sky or destroy the source of light. He sought a slower passage, enough warmth and daylight for the work waiting below.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.065.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The sun was persuaded. Its movement changed, and the day gained the time people needed. Hina could dry her kapa. The resolution returned to the material problem with which the story had begun.

The mountain and rope make the encounter striking, but the finished cloth gives it its human scale. A vast light had been checked for the sake of careful work that would otherwise remain unfinished.

At the end, Māui’s action is measured by the longer day, not by an injury shown in the sky. The telling leaves the sun shining and Hina’s work able to reach completion. Power has made room for time, warmth and the ordinary needs that called it into action.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.066`,candidateId:`LW-066`,definitionId:353,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`265b39741b1c7de794c27a71b9af098d046cea41eae45ef147e5c0455aef56f5`,variantSha256:`c44a47f055d75349fe20e1d8676011ae673992ec00b6408bb963557eb7aea5b4`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.066.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Hina had kapa to dry, but the day would not give her enough time. The sun moved so swiftly that the warmth passed before her work could be completed. Damp cloth made the difficulty visible: effort alone could not finish what depended on lasting light.

Her son Māui responded to that problem. The account does not begin with a search for battle or a wish to remake the sky for its own sake. It begins with his mother’s work and the sun’s unsuitable pace.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.066.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Māui went to Haleakalā. There he met the sun and restrained it with rope. Held from its swift passage, the brightness could no longer hurry beyond the encounter before he pressed the need for more time.

Held from racing onward, the sun could be urged to slow. The request concerned time for the people below: work required more than the brief warmth they had been receiving. Hina’s kapa belonged within that larger need without disappearing into it.

The sun agreed to a slower passage. Daylight remained long enough for the cloth to dry. The resolution was not only something that happened on the mountain; it reached the work that had prompted the journey.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.066.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`Hina’s place in the account is therefore active even though Māui makes the ascent. Her unfinished task defines the problem, and its completion shows whether the change has answered it. The cloth gives the great encounter a scale that can be understood in hands, warmth and time.

The story ends with daylight sufficient for careful work. Enough warmth remained for work that had previously been interrupted by the sun’s swift passing. Hina remains the mother whose damp kapa made those consequences plain, and whose completed work carries the answer home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.067`,candidateId:`LW-067`,definitionId:354,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`2761ebe6ae25e1e445b6351aed583e29dd3943aa53693f545b527c47abfb3cec`,variantSha256:`e65b960c525b1359a270c356db92d23799c286a4059d701524c37ec553a14180`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.067.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`The sun’s passage was swift. Across the world below, work had to fit within a warmth that did not last. Hina’s kapa stayed damp, making the sun’s speed more than a distant movement in the sky.

Māui answered the difficulty faced by his mother. He went to Haleakalā, bringing the account from a place of unfinished work to the mountain where the sun could be met.

In the selected telling, he restrained the sun with rope. Its movement could no longer carry it immediately beyond the encounter. The great bright presence that had allowed so little time was itself made to remain.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.067.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`The rope held what had been too swift for the work below. The sun’s brightness remained, but its onward movement was checked. Māui could now confront the haste that had left his mother’s cloth unfinished.

The restraint made room for Māui to press the need for a slower day. People required time for their work. Hina required warmth long enough to dry her cloth. The sun’s pace touched those tasks whether or not it had attended to them before.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.067.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`The sun was persuaded and slowed its passage. The light did not vanish; it remained longer. Hina’s kapa could dry, and other work gained time as well.

The change returns the account to its beginning. The same source of light that had moved too quickly now supplies the duration the people need. Its brilliance is not the problem by itself; the problem had been how briefly that brilliance stayed.

At the end, the sun is still moving and shining. The altered pace carries the agreement across the day, giving the small drying cloth below an answer to the great brightness above.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.069`,candidateId:`LW-069`,definitionId:356,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ca7f5a49a15c1791b6bd13145698c2873c979310b59fbe6bf1c611c5122c7b82`,variantSha256:`9e7d371a3ae7c02bc72a66456925559c50bda6ba23ed3507b07a540cf35e7a10`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.069.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Poliʻahu and her companions came down Maunakea’s slopes to enjoy sledding. A beautiful stranger joined them, and they welcomed her into the races. At first, the encounter belonged to the movement and pleasure of the hillside.

The stranger was Pele. Rivalry sharpened during the racing, and Pele’s displeasure grew. What had begun as a welcome among the sledders was becoming a contest with a dangerous visitor.

Then the ground grew warm. Poliʻahu recognized the presence she faced. Fire spread on Maunakea, and she fled toward the summit. Her snow mantle caught in the heat as she struggled to draw it away.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.069.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`The retreat was not the ending. At the height, Poliʻahu regained strength and cast her mantle over the mountain. Clouds gathered and snow fell deeply. The slopes that fire had threatened became the paths along which her cold power moved.

Snow spread downward against the lava. Red flow cooled and darkened; advancing fire was checked. Cold reached the running fire through the snow’s spread down the mountain; its advance met a force it could not simply burn away.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.069.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`In the story, cooled lava plugs the openings and runs toward the sea as rock. The struggle leaves a changed boundary between snow, hardened flow and the ocean below.

Pele did not erase Poliʻahu’s place on the mountain. The encounter ended with snow holding its ground against the flow that had pursued it.

A pale mantle folded down a dark shoulder, meeting a lava margin already beginning to blacken. The welcome, rivalry and retreat had led to a response whose force was visible in the boundary where the fire stopped. Snow remained on the mountain above the cooled flow.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.074`,candidateId:`LW-074`,definitionId:361,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`74d7719b879fbcd27f6baf9ca490f444e118ea07aac14a79a2ba60d80410a4b4`,variantSha256:`ee8072c6231937f58b56f7aec100d5f81837a5eb329205157f6a8c2d4dd0d284`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r19-hawaiian.json`,reportSha256:`82e3f707d548237e46f3271c683b3e3e5a737770c633cc44cbe5219f82e0169a`,packetPath:`traditions/hawaiian.json`,packetSha256:`89c53d302634415da5fdff2bf08fb0a4af63012be8e94159a0b1c56598e0a151`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol; /root/welsh_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`Coordinator-authored packet, not authored by this reviewer. Earlier Chinese/Mongolian/Kiche/Maori authorship conflicts excluded.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.074.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Encounter`,text:`Pele’s family left Kahiki under the pressure of conflict. Fire had damaged their home, and her sister Nāmakaokahaʻi pursued her. Their parents urged departure. A new place had to be found beyond the shores they knew.

Kamohoaliʻi, Pele’s elder brother, assisted the voyage in the canoe Honuaiakea. His navigation belonged to the family’s passage across the sea: the crossing carried people whose search for a home was far from finished.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.074.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Turning point`,text:`Across the islands, Pele sought a place for her fire. On Kauaʻi, a pit glowed, but the pursuing ocean force overwhelmed it. On Oʻahu, another attempt at Leʻahi was drowned. The journey continued through Molokaʻi toward Maui. Each halt brought the possibility of settlement, then renewed pressure to move.

Nāmakaokahaʻi’s pursuit ended in a fatal confrontation on Maui. Pele’s bodily death did not end the account’s movement. Her spirit reached Hawaiʻi Island, where the scale of Mauna Loa’s lava exceeded what her rival could overcome.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.074.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Aftermath`,text:`At Kīlauea, Pele found a favorable place for her digging. Halemaʻumaʻu became her enduring home. The voyage that began with a threatened household had reached a place where her fire could remain.

Kamohoaliʻi’s part belonged to the crossing that carried the household onward. The family had been driven from its old shores and moved between places that could not yet hold Pele’s fire. His assistance helped carry that search beyond the threatened home where it began.

Departure had not promised an easy arrival. Fire, ocean and family rivalry followed the voyagers, and their stops did not settle the conflict at once. Yet the journey continued until Pele found the home the telling still names, a place where her presence could endure.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.075`,candidateId:`LW-075`,definitionId:362,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`0c1f39fd86abc74a046e3e65b9ae2e3537df544d64a3c0a973d0ef40d08ee69c`,variantSha256:`56db31e047dc6d5c0f34877615c232a059020062f19d08508dc304439cf575ab`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r01-akan-fon-samoan.json`,reportSha256:`d34690e8bd312de04bbefb368f545910ad0b67862407018b3e552c4d70d22d67`,packetPath:`traditions/samoan.json`,packetSha256:`db40508a0f1f4e1818bf3b028b239b9fde0b88b6a2cbdd86dc59a4683fc62f7e`,reviewer:`/root/persian_research — gpt-6.1-sol independent content reviewer`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":["akan","fon","samoan"],"author":"Coordinator /root","limits":"Reviewer authored other tradition packets, including Hawaiian; none is within this batch. No packet edits or release authority claimed."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.075.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Growing Pet`,text:`Sina found the small eel while gathering seawater for her mother’s cooking. She took it home in a calabash and cared for it in a bowl. It seemed a creature that needed room and attention, and she gave it both.

The eel grew. The bowl became too small, so Sina moved it to the spring beside the house. When the spring could no longer hold it comfortably, her mother suggested the village pool. Sina carried it there, where a ledge offered a place to hide.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.075.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Pursuit`,text:`But the relationship changed as the creature grew larger. When Sina entered the water, it came close around her. What had begun as care now frightened her. She stopped using the village pool and returned to the little spring, only to find the eel there too.

Sina left her village to escape it. She travelled while others slept and continued from place to place. At springs where she stopped to drink, the eel appeared again. Distance did not give her the safety she sought.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.075.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Hidden Identity and a Palm`,text:`At last she entered an assembly and sat between two speechmakers. Before everyone, the great eel approached and explained that it was Tui Fiti, who had changed form to seek Sina as a wife. It could no longer become a man and believed death was near.

The eel asked Sina to bury its head after its death, promising that a useful tree would grow. The account says she did so and that the first coconut palm appeared. Its leaves could become cooling fans, and its fruit supplied drink. The tree closed the story of transformation, but the eel’s desire never made Sina’s fear or her repeated attempts to escape into consent.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.076`,candidateId:`LW-076`,definitionId:363,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`f47e7aaba7061e57c1e88957ee7c1efb6a4f10909108d4ad74a734899ef2f2e6`,variantSha256:`56db31e047dc6d5c0f34877615c232a059020062f19d08508dc304439cf575ab`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r01-akan-fon-samoan.json`,reportSha256:`d34690e8bd312de04bbefb368f545910ad0b67862407018b3e552c4d70d22d67`,packetPath:`traditions/samoan.json`,packetSha256:`db40508a0f1f4e1818bf3b028b239b9fde0b88b6a2cbdd86dc59a4683fc62f7e`,reviewer:`/root/persian_research — gpt-6.1-sol independent content reviewer`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":["akan","fon","samoan"],"author":"Coordinator /root","limits":"Reviewer authored other tradition packets, including Hawaiian; none is within this batch. No packet edits or release authority claimed."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.076.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Hidden Visitor`,text:`Tui Fiti heard of Sina in Samoa and wanted to make her his wife. In this telling he transformed himself into a young eel and crossed the sea. His royal identity disappeared beneath a small creature’s body, and Sina encountered him without knowing who he was.

She found the eel while gathering seawater, brought it home and cared for it. First a bowl held him; then his growth required a spring, and eventually the larger village pool. To Sina these moves answered a pet’s changing needs. They were not an agreement to marry the hidden visitor.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.076.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Following Sina`,text:`The eel continued to grow. He approached Sina in the water and followed when she moved away. She became afraid and stopped bathing in the village pool. Even the small spring beside her home could not keep him at a distance.

Sina then left altogether. The eel followed her journey from village to village and island to island, appearing at the springs where she paused. Her efforts to escape are essential to the account: his continued pursuit increased her distress.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.076.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Disclosure Before the Assembly`,text:`Eventually she sought the company of an assembly. The eel came over the open ground and addressed her there. He disclosed that he was Tui Fiti, that he had lost the power to regain human form and that his long journey was ending in death.

His final request concerned a tree. After his death, Sina was to bury his head beside her home; leaves and fruit would become useful gifts. The account follows that request with the appearance of a coconut palm. Tui Fiti’s transformation thus ends in another transformation, without a wedding and without turning his pursuit into a shared romance.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.077`,candidateId:`LW-077`,definitionId:364,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`a3c14e5a182dd767576c409a9a9dea46fb63deef10a4a4dcc01397f05e73cc51`,variantSha256:`56db31e047dc6d5c0f34877615c232a059020062f19d08508dc304439cf575ab`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r01-akan-fon-samoan.json`,reportSha256:`d34690e8bd312de04bbefb368f545910ad0b67862407018b3e552c4d70d22d67`,packetPath:`traditions/samoan.json`,packetSha256:`db40508a0f1f4e1818bf3b028b239b9fde0b88b6a2cbdd86dc59a4683fc62f7e`,reviewer:`/root/persian_research — gpt-6.1-sol independent content reviewer`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":["akan","fon","samoan"],"author":"Coordinator /root","limits":"Reviewer authored other tradition packets, including Hawaiian; none is within this batch. No packet edits or release authority claimed."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.077.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Before the Tree`,text:`Before the coconut palm appeared, Sina had carried an eel between several homes of water. A bowl had become too small, then a spring, and then the village pool. Each move had seemed to answer the growth of a creature she was tending.

As the eel grew larger, Sina’s care became fear. It approached her in the water and followed when she sought a different place. She finally left her village, but wherever she stopped at a spring the eel appeared again. The story’s new tree begins after that long pursuit, not after a wedding.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.077.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Final Request`,text:`Sina reached an assembly and sat between two speechmakers. There the eel revealed its hidden identity as Tui Fiti. He could no longer return to human form and expected to die. Before that ending he asked Sina to bury his head near her home.

His request promised a useful tree. Its leaves could be made into a fan when cooling wind was absent; its fruit would provide something to drink. The narrative says Sina carried out the burial after his death, and a plant unlike those previously seen began to grow.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.077.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Useful Growth`,text:`That plant became a coconut palm. The tale links the features and uses of its fruit with the eel’s last words. Where the living eel had repeatedly crossed boundaries that Sina sought to maintain, the tree remained in one place and provided leaves and fruit she could use.

This is the etiological conclusion of the selected telling: it explains a familiar plant through a remembered relationship and transformation. It does not describe a botanical process by which coconut palms actually evolved, and the new growth supplies no forgotten marriage or permission to disregard Sina’s earlier fear.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.078`,candidateId:`LW-078`,definitionId:365,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4cd8bc8d5c4dc51ad2c30f0446c4a3f4ba8eb2e5aa526f739c8ac16c86210ceb`,variantSha256:`c5750bb8e492caecc332f77e54655e595d72be0a82312c74b6b25202c6bd272e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r01-akan-fon-samoan.json`,reportSha256:`d34690e8bd312de04bbefb368f545910ad0b67862407018b3e552c4d70d22d67`,packetPath:`traditions/samoan.json`,packetSha256:`db40508a0f1f4e1818bf3b028b239b9fde0b88b6a2cbdd86dc59a4683fc62f7e`,reviewer:`/root/persian_research — gpt-6.1-sol independent content reviewer`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":["akan","fon","samoan"],"author":"Coordinator /root","limits":"Reviewer authored other tradition packets, including Hawaiian; none is within this batch. No packet edits or release authority claimed."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.078.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Family Under Threat`,text:`Nafanua’s family lived on the western side of Savaiʻi, while a struggle for land and titles placed them under threat from the east. Lilomaiʻava’s campaign carried more than the danger of battle. Captured people were subjected to a humiliating punishment, and among them was Nafanua’s uncle Taʻii.

His distress reached Saveasiʻuleo and Nafanua. The suffering was close to them through family relationship, not an anonymous distant conflict. Saveasiʻuleo directed his daughter to obtain wood from the Toa tree so that weapons could be made to defend their people.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.078.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Changed Trunk`,text:`Nafanua cut the tree and left the timber to dry. This part of the task required waiting rather than immediate combat. The fallen trunk lay where it could be collected when ready, an ordinary-looking material chosen for an extraordinary responsibility.

Days later she returned. The surface had changed: pule shells covered the wood. Nafanua had come to recover a trunk and found living things across what had been cut down. The account connects that sight with a remembered saying about the tree’s death and the shells’ survival.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.078.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Strength and Judgment`,text:`The contrast interrupts a simple equation between strength and success. A tree capable of supplying weapons lies fallen, while small shells remain alive across it. The source interprets the saying as a lesson in wisdom and judgment: power alone cannot settle everything.

Nafanua would go on to make the weapons and defend her people in the larger telling. This selected episode pauses before that fighting, at her return to the changed trunk. Her attention to the shells belongs beside her determination to help her family. The scene holds both together, leaving combat outside the image while preserving the reason she came for the wood.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.080`,candidateId:`LW-080`,definitionId:367,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c1c76333e17ef4ad2799d5ef1f4d1fedb5deb95aad80eb4946144d86f987784e`,variantSha256:`a84752cdb2530da744cda33cd399df2845bf4357a542400f2b32cf2bd83636e6`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r01-akan-fon-samoan.json`,reportSha256:`d34690e8bd312de04bbefb368f545910ad0b67862407018b3e552c4d70d22d67`,packetPath:`traditions/samoan.json`,packetSha256:`db40508a0f1f4e1818bf3b028b239b9fde0b88b6a2cbdd86dc59a4683fc62f7e`,reviewer:`/root/persian_research — gpt-6.1-sol independent content reviewer`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"notAuthoredByReviewer":["akan","fon","samoan"],"author":"Coordinator /root","limits":"Reviewer authored other tradition packets, including Hawaiian; none is within this batch. No packet edits or release authority claimed."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.080.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Below the Earth`,text:`Talanga knew a place below the earth where fire burned in the domain of Mafuie. His son Tiʻitiʻi watched him enter and later found his own way inside. Talanga was surprised to see him and warned him to keep quiet, fearing the power whose land they had entered.

Tiʻitiʻi noticed smoke and asked what it was. When he learned that it came from fire, he wanted some for himself. His father warned him again, but Tiʻitiʻi went to Mafuie and asked for it. This first encounter ended with permission: Mafuie allowed him to take fire away.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.080.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The First Oven Broken`,text:`He returned with cinders. Father and son kindled a small fire and prepared taro for the heated stones. Food that had always been eaten raw could now be cooked. For a short time the great discovery was close and practical, held between two people and the meal they were preparing.

Then Mafuie scattered the oven stones and extinguished the fire. Talanga’s earlier fear seemed justified. Tiʻitiʻi went back and challenged the destruction, and the argument became a wrestling struggle. Mafuie suffered a grave injury; the violent detail remains outside this account’s view.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.080.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Bargain for Fire`,text:`Facing defeat, Mafuie offered Tiʻitiʻi wives as a reward, but the son refused. Mafuie then agreed that fire could be obtained in wood, and Tiʻitiʻi accepted that bargain.

The telling closes by linking his struggle with the ability to cook food. It also connects Mafuie’s injured arm with an explanation of earthquakes. Those are narrative conclusions, not geological findings or fire-making instructions. The first attempt to cook taro remains the quiet image chosen from a much more dangerous exchange.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.099`,candidateId:`LW-099`,definitionId:386,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`5e0c5f0ba4fbb53c9c3da90c1642eab64994c8b30096c1b35bdf76204b2295cd`,variantSha256:`7e77ca4468916cf88c825588fff647390dc1dd4fc5bbdfe63c54eab2ee5417d3`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.099.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`False Light`,text:`The world had not yet received its true daylight, but Seven Macaw behaved as though brightness already belonged to him. His gleaming eyes and teeth were his argument. The bird proclaimed himself the light by which others should live.

Hunahpu and Xbalanque could not leave that claim unchallenged. Where wealth alone passed for greatness, the coming people would have no proper place. The brothers took their blowguns and waited beneath the nance tree where Seven Macaw fed.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.099.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Shot and a New Plan`,text:`When the bird settled among the fruit, Hunahpu fired. Seven Macaw fell, yet the struggle did not end on the ground. Hunahpu rushed toward him and suffered a terrible reversal: the bird took his arm. Seven Macaw returned home in pain, keeping what he had seized and expecting the hunters to come after it.

Hunahpu and Xbalanque needed a different approach. They asked two bent elders, Great White Peccary and Great White Coati, to accompany them. The elders would present the brothers as dependent grandchildren and offer their healing skills. The dangerous hunters would appear harmless behind them.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.099.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Boast Ends`,text:`Seven Macaw’s aching teeth made him willing to listen. He feared losing the jewels that established his importance, but relief mattered too. The elders promised replacement teeth, then exchanged his precious finery for plain white maize. His glittering eyes were treated as well. Without the treasures that had sustained his boast, the great bird’s appearance and power failed.

Seven Macaw died, and Hunahpu recovered his arm. The brothers restored it, leaving him whole again. Their first shot had opened the struggle; their shared planning had finished it. Above the nance tree, the sky still awaited a light that no self-important creature could simply claim.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.100`,candidateId:`LW-100`,definitionId:387,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`56361a7db7d360ff5a17d4782d6f12ed67a15ca90df33152bc15f1ed18f2b31e`,variantSha256:`2705b82bcd4dcb9b72917e9332b21398e882fe713903b309ae8c3d47643eb396`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.100.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Ungenerous Household`,text:`Xbalanque knew the sound of a household that valued its musicians and neglected its hunters. One Batz and One Chouen, the older brothers, were gifted singers, players, writers and carvers. Yet when Xbalanque and Hunahpu returned from hunting, their birds and meals were taken from them. Talent had not made the older brothers generous.

The younger pair endured that treatment before arranging a different hunt. They came home without birds and explained that their quarry remained high in a tree. Their older brothers agreed to help bring it down.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.100.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Yellow Tree and Music`,text:`At dawn, all four reached Yellow Tree. Birds filled its branches, but none fell to the ground. One Batz and One Chouen climbed to retrieve them. Then the tree grew. Its increasing height held the two climbers far above an easy descent.

The younger brothers’ advice brought a stranger escape. The elder pair’s garments became tails, and their bodies changed into spider monkeys. They moved away through the forest instead of returning home as they had left it.

Xbalanque and Hunahpu told their grandmother what had happened. They could still call the brothers back, they said, if she would contain her laughter. They took up music together, bringing flute, song and drum into the patio.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.100.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Fourth Call`,text:`The changed brothers answered. They came dancing toward the household they had lost. Their grandmother recognized them, but their unfamiliar movements and faces made her laugh. At once they fled.

The twins tried again. Another arrival, another burst of laughter, another retreat. Even when the grandmother first controlled herself, the brothers’ antics overcame her resolve. On the fourth invitation, they did not come.

Xbalanque’s music had brought them to the threshold, but could not keep them there. Their names would nevertheless endure among artists. The family’s injustice did not erase what One Batz and One Chouen had once accomplished.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.101`,candidateId:`LW-101`,definitionId:388,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`41688835e0041d67274f3b21e8484a9fe76d1d4604363e67efc53e9129b625e7`,variantSha256:`bee94d9393972fd7c07dbacf54f82ef30e531026ad59299daba14797ce02e3fc`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.101.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Bird’s Claimed Dawn`,text:`Seven Macaw looked out upon a world whose true sun and moon had not yet appeared clearly. He did not see far beyond his own seat, but that limitation did not restrain his claims. The shine of his eyes and teeth seemed to him a sufficient reason for the earth to acknowledge his greatness.

He declared that he was its sun and moon. His precious metals, jewels and magnificent plumage were to be the world’s light. Yet the horizon did not become daylight merely because he glittered.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.101.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Nance-Tree Attack`,text:`Hunahpu and Xbalanque saw the danger in that pride. People could not properly begin their lives in a world where such wealth alone established glory. The brothers shouldered their blowguns and sought the nance tree where the bird took his food.

Seven Macaw climbed to the fruit as usual. Hidden beneath the leaves, the twins waited. Hunahpu’s shot struck his jaw and brought him down. But Seven Macaw was strong enough to make their victory uncertain. In the struggle he took Hunahpu’s arm and carried it home, where pain soon kept him from eating or sleeping.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.101.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Maize in Place of Jewels`,text:`When two elderly healers passed with young companions behind them, Seven Macaw called them closer. He wanted relief. The elders told him that his teeth required treatment. He hesitated, because the brilliant things in his mouth and eyes were the foundation of his lordly appearance.

Still, he accepted their help. Ordinary maize replaced the jeweled teeth. The shine of his eyes was removed as well. His proud face changed, and the riches upon which he depended passed away from him.

Seven Macaw died. The companions behind the healers were the twins, who regained Hunahpu’s arm and restored him. The bird had filled his own speech with light, but the world had never mistaken his brightness for the true dawn.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.102`,candidateId:`LW-102`,definitionId:389,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ddae0b3e7f3dbf39cc40a4807af5b3ad0c4e1cef4be070045fc2c86c51880609`,variantSha256:`baee785833e3d34eb379ec5fa2f76ba2bf3abb753ac9ee85c758b627d1eb5b15`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.102.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Hungry Mountain-Maker`,text:`Zipacna could carry mountains at night, yet hunger drove him down to the rivers by day. Fish and crabs were his food. After two days without eating, even the mountain-maker needed someone to show him where a meal might be found.

Hunahpu and Xbalanque approached him with exactly that possibility. They had not forgotten the four hundred boys whom Zipacna had destroyed. Now they had prepared a great red crab beneath the mountain Meauan, shaping a deceptive creature from a forest flower and a hollowed stone.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.102.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Crab Beneath Meauan`,text:`They asked what Zipacna sought. When he told them of his hunger, they spoke of the enormous crab in the canyon. They claimed that they had tried to catch it but feared its claws. Perhaps his strength would succeed where theirs had failed.

Zipacna asked them to guide him. At first they gave directions instead. He pleaded, promising to show them places full of birds for their blowguns. The maker of mountains had become a hungry supplicant.

The twins eventually went with him. They warned that entering on his stomach had frightened the crab away, and suggested trying on his back. Zipacna saw the red shape within the narrow opening and wanted it immediately.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.102.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Mountain Settles`,text:`He went in headfirst nevertheless. The crab moved higher and escaped his reach. Zipacna backed out, admitting that he had failed. Perhaps the other position would work after all.

On his second attempt he turned onto his back and pushed deep beneath the mountain. Then Meauan settled over him. The great weight left no way to turn and no way to rise. Zipacna became stone.

The twins had not competed with him in carrying mountains. They had allowed his hunger to lead him into the one mountain from which his strength could not bring him out.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.103`,candidateId:`LW-103`,definitionId:390,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7cbba9b77603cafb2bbbdc2859604bc15929f4629329aba65ffbec91b2e632af`,variantSha256:`b68c1fce5ee1bff77eea6bdff91628243d991c8081bf76cbe46b96ffd7ad774a`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.103.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Wrecker’s Boast`,text:`Cabracan thought of mountains as things that would fall before him. A small movement of his foot could send their slopes tumbling. He intended to keep breaking them for as long as there was light upon the earth.

Heart of Sky directed Hunahpu and Xbalanque to end that boast. They were to draw Seven Macaw’s second son eastward, toward the place of the rising sun. The twins accepted the task and met Cabracan as hunters travelling through the mountains.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.103.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Road and the Meal`,text:`He asked who they were. They presented themselves as wandering orphans with their blowguns, people who possessed little and knew many slopes. Among those slopes, they said, stood one enormous mountain that kept growing above all the others. Could he truly bring that one down too?

Cabracan wanted to see it. They arranged their journey with him between them, one hunter on either side. Along the road they brought down birds without ordinary pellets, surprising their companion with their skill.

Then the hunters stopped to cook. The fragrance of roasted birds reached Cabracan, and his attention narrowed from mountains to food. He asked for a portion. The twins gave him a bird they had specially prepared, concealing the purpose of their offering.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.103.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`An Unbroken Mountain`,text:`After the meal, the three continued toward the great eastern mountain. Cabracan now found that his arms and legs would not answer with their former power. He could no longer make the earth respond to his touch. The mountain remained standing.

The twins bound him and buried him beneath the earth. His declared task of wrecking every peak had ended.

Cabracan had expected a trial of strength against a mountain. What overcame him was the road to it, the companions beside him, and the small meal he had been eager to accept.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.104`,candidateId:`LW-104`,definitionId:391,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d82fc0ffac818f17a5bdd02cf06408438511b6080edf3e0d0f859aa1815deaa7`,variantSha256:`dbef2483404a616d79eec6bbc552b78e00406b9ac2bca0582df2a6f1b7260c20`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.104.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Food Mountain`,text:`The makers had formed a world, but the people who could remember and sustain them had not yet appeared. Their earlier attempts had not fulfilled that hope. With dawn approaching, they returned to the question of what human flesh should be.

The answer came from a place of food. Fox, coyote, parakeet and raven showed the way from Paxil to the yellow and white ears of maize. The path they revealed led toward a mountain crowded with nourishment: maize, cacao, fruit and honey. What humanity required had been found among things that could feed life.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.104.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Flesh from Maize`,text:`Xmucane took the yellow and white maize and ground it fine. Her work changed the ears into the food from which bodies could be formed. Water joined that food, giving the new people their blood and strength. Their arms and legs were made from the sustenance that would also maintain them.

Four people came into being: Balam Quitze, Balam Acab, Mahucutah and Iqui Balam. They could speak, listen, walk and hold things. Their sight reached astonishingly far. Trees and rocks did not stop their knowledge; mountains and seas did not confine it.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.104.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`People Who Give Thanks`,text:`When the makers asked what they could see, the four acknowledged the world before them and gave thanks for their existence. They had mouths with which to answer, hands with which to act, and understanding with which to recognize those who had formed them.

Their extraordinary knowledge would later be limited, and companions would come into their lives. But the work at this beginning had succeeded where earlier materials had failed.

Before the first daylight spread across the earth, Xmucane had prepared its human flesh from maize. The food was no longer merely waiting in a mountain. It had become people able to meet the world.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.105`,candidateId:`LW-105`,definitionId:392,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4daf49e8d494c80f544320e50b5efa667fef569b530bb0a34d7ed6a4d77d2641`,variantSha256:`92e0150d2db1ea876d81011a2d92eb42968aa297b137ae188cc7fd64530a597a`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.105.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Failed Mud Person`,text:`The first attempt to shape a person from mud had not held together. Its body softened and fell apart; its speech did not carry understanding. The makers undid that attempt, then faced the same question again: how could they form people who would remember them?

They called upon Xpiyacoc and Xmucane. The grandfather and grandmother were seers, elders whose counsel belonged to the work of creation. This time the makers asked them to consider a human likeness shaped from wood.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.105.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Elders’ Counsel`,text:`Xpiyacoc did not stand apart as a solitary maker. He and Xmucane answered within a gathering of divine deliberation. The desired people were to have mouths and faces, to speak upon the earth, to become those who could acknowledge the ones who had framed their lives.

The elders considered the proposed material and spoke in favour of the wooden forms. Their response was followed by the appearance of figures that looked human. Unlike the mud person, these could persist and multiply upon the earth. They talked. They multiplied, producing sons and daughters until the earth was populated.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.105.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Forms Without Remembrance`,text:`Yet their apparent success concealed another failure. The figures had no inward understanding. They did not remember their makers. Their movements and speech had the appearance of human life without its necessary heart and mind.

Their bodies were dry and rigid. They lacked the living fluids and fullness that would belong to later humanity. What had seemed a stronger material had made only another incomplete attempt.

Xpiyacoc’s consultation therefore stands inside a creation that had still to be finished. The elders’ words had helped bring a new form into existence, but form alone could not answer the makers’ hope. People would need more than the ability to resemble themselves and fill the earth.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.106`,candidateId:`LW-106`,definitionId:393,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4bc85b3c1512086b2dab3e5d85d3d053502ca52c14c43a45cab15e8998016cab`,variantSha256:`6f45f8e1c65eaa0ecb3d14942d9fe881bb9c2f2158f74be92cf9927ddcec4a60`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.106.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`A Claim to Kinship`,text:`Lady Blood arrived at Xmucane’s home carrying children not yet born. She presented herself as the grandmother’s daughter-in-law, bound to One Hunahpu and to the household he had left behind. Xmucane would not readily accept that claim. Her sons had died below, and the two elder grandsons were the family she knew.

Lady Blood insisted that the children she carried would reveal the continuing life of that kinship. Xmucane answered with a task. If the newcomer truly belonged to the household, she should bring food for it: a great netful of maize.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.106.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`One Ear Fills the Net`,text:`Lady Blood followed the path into the maizefield of One Batz and One Chouen. There she found only one ripe ear. No second or third ear waited behind it. The demand that had sounded like work now appeared impossible.

Her heart sank, but she did not turn back with an empty net. She called upon the guardians of the food, seeking help without taking away the field’s only ear. Then she grasped the silk above it and lifted.

Maize multiplied within the net. The single ear remained on its plant while the harvest grew until the carrying net overflowed. Animals helped bring the burden back. At the house, Lady Blood took up her pack frame again and appeared to have carried the heavy load herself.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.106.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Grandmother’s Recognition`,text:`Xmucane met the harvest with suspicion. Had the newcomer stolen it? Had she stripped the plant of everything? The grandmother went to see the field.

The ear still stood there. Beneath it remained the sign of where the net had rested. Xmucane returned with a different answer: this was a sign that Lady Blood truly belonged to the family. The test had not emptied the field. It had opened a place for the woman who brought its abundance home.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.107`,candidateId:`LW-107`,definitionId:394,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7f0b226055c67b8dfbbbc592211be06d6fcb1a2be702eb2dc366793bcac04e86`,variantSha256:`54aa156d2ff5b1d3da81bfa340a055eca9dde52607a7962ff75318bf62697446`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.107.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Summons Below`,text:`One Hunahpu and his brother Seven Hunahpu filled their days with games. Their skill and knowledge also passed to One Hunahpu’s sons, One Batz and One Chouen, who became musicians, writers, carvers and accomplished artisans. The household held both play and the arts.

But the sound of the ballcourt travelled downward. In Xibalba, One Death and Seven Death heard the players overhead and treated their noise as an affront. They decided to summon the brothers below, presenting the demand as an invitation to enliven their own game.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.107.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Farewell to Xmucane`,text:`Four owl messengers arrived above the court. They repeated the lords’ command and named the equipment the brothers were to bring: their protective gear and rubber ball. One Hunahpu and Seven Hunahpu recognized whose words these were. Before setting out, however, they asked the guides to wait while they went home.

There they told Xmucane that they must leave. She wept. Their father had already died, and now the two sons were departing for a place from which she feared they would not return. The brothers tried to reassure her.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.107.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Ball Left at Home`,text:`One Hunahpu and Seven Hunahpu also spoke to the two younger men who would remain behind. One Batz and One Chouen should continue their music, writing and carving. Their work was to keep the house warm and comfort the grandmother who stayed within it.

The rubber ball, too, would remain. Despite the lords’ demand, the brothers tied it above the house, promising to return and use it again. Their departure did not carry that possession away.

Then they joined the owl guides. Below waited the summons and its hidden dangers. Above, the suspended ball held the promise they had spoken before leaving, while the household’s arts were entrusted to those who stayed.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.108`,candidateId:`LW-108`,definitionId:395,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d23e86bad5b81924ec8edeb5ea8eac426cfb6c08917edc854bb5301e42e0c18a`,variantSha256:`a5e3b6ff34a9f5d176a4ec84fbcd45f9fe06fa96c75287e44a6c0fe3d270a071`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.108.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Steep Descent`,text:`Seven Hunahpu left home beside his brother One Hunahpu. Four owl guides carried the summons of Xibalba, and the brothers had agreed to follow them. Behind them remained their mother and the younger artists charged with comforting her; the rubber ball was tied above the house.

The path led down steep steps. Beyond them lay turbulent canyons and rivers. Seven Hunahpu and One Hunahpu passed through the dangerous waters without surrendering to them. Scorpions did not sting them, and the other threatening streams did not stop their progress.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.108.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Speaking Road and False Hosts`,text:`At the crossroads, danger took another form. Four roads offered different directions: red, black, white and yellow. The Black Road spoke as though it possessed the authority the brothers sought. It declared itself the lords’ road.

The brothers followed it.

When they arrived at the council place, the first seated figures appeared worthy of greeting. One Hunahpu and Seven Hunahpu addressed them as One Death and Seven Death. The figures gave no answer. They were dressed effigies carved from wood.

The real lords burst into laughter. Their guests had already mistaken appearance for authority. Next the brothers were invited to sit, but the apparent bench was heated stone. What looked like hospitality became another trap.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.108.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The House of Darkness`,text:`They were sent to the House of Darkness, where a messenger brought burning light with a demand that the supplied objects be returned intact at dawn. The brothers used them up. When morning came, the lords judged that failure against them.

One Hunahpu and Seven Hunahpu did not survive the reception. The many other trial houses were named, but this pair had not passed through them all. Their defeat began earlier, when a road spoke and wooden likenesses looked like hosts.

Seven Hunahpu had travelled faithfully beside his brother. That loyalty could carry him down the steps, but could not make the welcome below truthful.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.109`,candidateId:`LW-109`,definitionId:396,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`ca78905f52d6208dbd7d5af7567e0c6e3405db58fe96ea7380e05df06933c429`,variantSha256:`0f8084dd6a0abb6c4a05ef0612781e14664c4eb2aa4e403c877ad6041e64d0bb`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.109.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Gifts and Jealousy`,text:`One Batz knew how to make beautiful things. With One Chouen he played and sang, wrote and carved, and practised the arts taught within the family. Their achievements were real, but so was their jealousy of the younger brothers who hunted outside the house.

Hunahpu and Xbalanque returned with birds and found their own food taken. Their elders treated them as dependants without granting them a dependant’s share. The younger pair endured it, then arranged a hunt that would change the household.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.109.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`From Tree to Music`,text:`One Batz and One Chouen were asked to fetch birds lodged high in Yellow Tree. They climbed, only to find the tree growing beneath them. When they sought a way down, the advice from below led to transformation. Their garments became tails; they became spider monkeys and moved into the forest.

Yet One Batz would hear the household’s music again.

Hunahpu and Xbalanque told their grandmother that they could call the elder brothers back. She must restrain her laughter when she saw them. The twins played and sang, summoning both names.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.109.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Names That Artists Remember`,text:`One Batz came dancing with One Chouen. The music reached the changed brothers, and for a moment the distance between forest and patio seemed small enough to cross. Then the grandmother saw them and laughed. They departed immediately.

Another invitation brought another return. Again laughter drove them away. On the third attempt she held herself steady at first, but the brothers’ movements overcame her resolve. At the fourth calling, no dancing figures arrived.

One Batz stayed in the forest with his brother. The family did not recover its earlier form. Still, the twins said that both names would be remembered. Singers, players, writers and carvers would continue to acknowledge the gifts of those who had lost their place at home through pride.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.110`,candidateId:`LW-110`,definitionId:397,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`9fcf98c950f148f0aefce8e552590cf5c7010022eb08af35e7a96357663cdadc`,variantSha256:`624353d058133b535e93eb99ae1b85372792405c2dbc2479d773d0959842ab2f`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.110.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Brothers’ Privilege`,text:`One Chouen had grown accomplished beside One Batz. Their music and skilled work comforted the grandmother, and the two older brothers understood much. But their success did not soften their treatment of Hunahpu and Xbalanque. When the hunters returned, the elder pair took their birds and food.

Then the younger brothers came back empty-handed. They said that the birds they had shot remained in the top of a tree, beyond their reach. If their older brothers would come along, the food could be brought down.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.110.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Growing Tree`,text:`At dawn, the four arrived beneath Yellow Tree. Its canopy was full of birds. The younger hunters aimed their blowguns, but no birds fell onto the ground. One Chouen and One Batz climbed to retrieve them.

The tree began to swell and rise. Branches that had seemed a route toward the birds became a height from which the two could not descend. They called down for help, asking their younger brothers to take pity on them.

The response offered another way of moving. The elder brothers adjusted their garments as instructed, and the trailing ends became tails. Their bodies changed into spider monkeys. High in the branches, One Chouen could now move as an animal through the canopy.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.110.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Forest and the Arts`,text:`Both went into the forest. Hunahpu and Xbalanque later called them back with music, attempting to restore their presence in the household. The changed brothers danced into the patio, but the grandmother’s laughter made them withdraw.

Repeated calls did not resolve the loss. On the fourth attempt they stayed away.

One Chouen’s name nevertheless remained beside One Batz’s among the arts. Their transformation had not meant that their old talents were imaginary. It meant that those talents had belonged to brothers whose pride and cruelty had finally overturned their place within the family.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.111`,candidateId:`LW-111`,definitionId:398,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`c98e0fd8e87c07387bde749fe4fdb8858929ab35d45311e4347e8b3aa9333a78`,variantSha256:`24dcb5a26784665e4dd10850419e3d92f6e60c06cc83853069fc27fc167692a8`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.111.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Captive’s Knowledge`,text:`The rat was caught after the twins’ work in the maizefield had been undone by animals returning the land to its former state. Hunahpu and Xbalanque treated their captive harshly. Its chance of survival lay in something they did not yet know.

The rat told them that their task was not simply to farm maize. There were possessions waiting for them, belongings of One Hunahpu and Seven Hunahpu, who had died in Xibalba. Their grandmother had kept the ancestral gaming things hidden above the house.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.111.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`A Bargain and a Distraction`,text:`The news changed the twins’ attention. The rat knew where the rubber ball, yokes and arm protectors hung. It bargained for release and food, and the brothers promised it maize, seeds, beans and other things it could gnaw.

They planned how to obtain the equipment without their grandmother stopping them. One twin brought the rat to a corner of the house while the other went inside. At their meal, the brothers watched the roof above through its reflection in a plate of chili sauce.

They drained the water jug and asked for a drink. Their grandmother went to the river. A mosquito pierced the jug so that its water kept escaping. When she failed to return, the twins sent their mother after her.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.111.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Gear Comes Down`,text:`With both women occupied, the rat worked at the ropes overhead. The ball came down, along with the other gaming things. Hunahpu and Xbalanque carried the equipment away and hid it beside the road to the ballcourt.

Then they went to the river, where their mother and grandmother were still struggling with the leaking jug. They sealed it and returned home together.

The rat had gained a bargain from a dangerous encounter. The twins had gained the objects that would bring the old ballcourt to life again, and with it another summons from below.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.112`,candidateId:`LW-112`,definitionId:399,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`7c7412f59ef00f6ccd560c78e75ac9527014ddba8b0d75b8f47604581002af14`,variantSha256:`da89d3aec5bb5634cc4963b87a8324b67a756ccb9f1c4c1129e9709386372160`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.112.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Silent Effigies`,text:`Before Hunahpu and Xbalanque entered the reception court of Xibalba, they sent something small ahead of them. It took the form of a mosquito. Its task was to discover what ordinary greetings might conceal.

The scout approached the first seated figure and bit it. There was no answer. It tried the second, and again nothing spoke. These apparent hosts had no living response to give.

At the third seat, the result changed. One Death cried out, and the neighbouring lord asked what troubled him. That question supplied a name. The next bite startled Seven Death, whose own name was spoken in the answering exchange.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.112.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Lords Give Their Names`,text:`The scout continued down the line. Each reaction made another lord address his neighbour. What might have stayed hidden beneath rank and dressed appearance became audible. The little biting shape did not need to ask for formal introductions; discomfort made the court introduce itself.

There were twelve real lords beyond the two wooden figures. The chain of names reached all of them, including Bloody Teeth and Bloody Claws at its end. Hunahpu and Xbalanque heard what they needed.

The scout was no independent visitor seeking favour at the court. It was a hair that Hunahpu had plucked from his knee, working in mosquito form.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.112.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Reception Unmasked`,text:`When the twins arrived, a tempter urged them to greet the first seated figures. They recognized those figures as dressed wooden effigies. Instead, they addressed the actual lords by their newly learned names, leaving none unnamed.

A further invitation offered them a seat. They identified the heated stone beneath that appearance of welcome and declined it. Then they went onward into the House of Darkness.

The smallest agent had changed the reception. Silence had exposed the false figures; startled speech had revealed the living ones. The twins entered with knowledge their elders had lacked.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.113`,candidateId:`LW-113`,definitionId:400,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`9c6f50aeda5184568ec8472d2b1d5b95fe877ec6461a64de1333a955839f5397`,variantSha256:`11aa11cc3dbc3d43c73c4fbc9019708c9b39e1face01d4e6b201cddacf2f994a`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.113.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Nested Journey`,text:`Tamazul met a louse on the road. The tiny creature carried a message from Xmucane to Hunahpu and Xbalanque: the messengers of Xibalba had come, and the twins were required below in seven days.

The youthful toad noticed how slowly the louse travelled. He offered a quicker journey. If the louse let itself be swallowed, Tamazul could hop with the message safely within him. The louse agreed, and the toad took it into his mouth.

But Tamazul’s hopping was not quick. White Life, a great snake, met him and made the same observation. Perhaps the snake could carry the message faster. Tamazul agreed in turn and was swallowed.

Then a falcon encountered the snake. It swallowed that carrier and flew to the court where the twins were playing ball. The message had gained another transport, though none of the larger animals could yet speak its words.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.113.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Message Unpacked`,text:`The falcon called from the edge of the court. The twins shot at it with a blowgun, injuring its eye. When they questioned it, the bird insisted on being healed before delivering what it carried. They restored its sight, and it released the snake.

The snake released Tamazul. Now the toad was asked to speak, but the message still belonged to the louse. Tamazul tried to bring his passenger out and failed. Under the twins’ rough questioning, they finally searched his mouth.

The louse was there, lodged before his teeth. It had never reached the belly where Tamazul claimed to carry it.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.113.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Louse Speaks`,text:`At last the smallest messenger spoke. It repeated the summons and the grandmother’s distress. The twins immediately returned to advise her.

Tamazul had promised speed and become part of a much larger journey. Yet the words arrived only when everyone found the tiny creature that had known them from the beginning.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.114`,candidateId:`LW-114`,definitionId:401,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`855c61149be00c4f5278aa9000696d473e5c362518b940e039a09f34e1bf5231`,variantSha256:`4845db2896c8737dcc22bc0b922dc565bd0665ad97d6f8a37ead384fc1fe5a87`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r18-kiche.json`,reportSha256:`576aaca4ed701e6a38f57e043e83bb60e09f3e70bbf1c443f260800e729b8f33`,packetPath:`traditions/k-iche-maya.json`,packetSha256:`5c9ce795da71486483f0a642e36c458bad791d3ae4b215b50b35e65e86b56fb7`,reviewer:`Independent gpt-6.1-sol reviewer /root/persian_research`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05T03:53:53.118Z`,independence:`This packet was authored by another worker, not by this reviewer. Exact16-card scope.`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.114.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`Four Messengers and an Order`,text:`Four messengers served the lords of Xibalba. Arrow Owl was likened to a piercing arrow. One Leg Owl possessed wings and a single leg. Macaw Owl showed a red back. Skull Owl was a winged skull with no legs. Together they carried demands between the world below and the earth above.

Lady Blood faced one of those demands after her father discovered her pregnancy. Gathered Blood brought his accusation before One Death and Seven Death. The lords ordered the messengers to take her away and return with proof that she had been killed.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.114.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Listening to Lady Blood`,text:`The owls carried the command, but the woman did not accept its judgement. She told them that the children within her had been created through the marvel of One Hunahpu, not through the conduct her father accused her of. She asked the guides to spare her.

They did not want her to die. Yet they had to answer the lords, who expected proof and would examine what was brought before them.

Lady Blood proposed a substitute from a red tree. Its sap could take the place of what the lords demanded, leaving her alive. The messengers agreed. The deception would require them to do more than repeat their masters’ words: they would have to help the woman escape them.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.114.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`The Passage Upward`,text:`When the substitute reached the court, One Death accepted it. The lords gathered around the fire, absorbed in its fragrance. While their attention remained there, the four guides took Lady Blood toward the opening leading above the earth.

Through that passage she reached the upper world. The messengers then returned below.

The company’s strange forms had first marked them as agents of a threatening summons. In this journey, the same four figures carried a different outcome. They had listened to the person they were ordered to destroy, and helped her live.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.132`,candidateId:`LW-132`,definitionId:419,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`fcd25722baec4964feaecfd7c39909f454f2952294f24bdbbfed2def320571e3`,variantSha256:`f35a6d397d873b4c143413549999e08a54a5bffc46fbfe26d460da37e8a6429e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r04-quechua-nahua-ugaritic.json`,reportSha256:`007ad680739adf1ecb17a0bc283bb92c7511de94ce39ada8e3d847911f0a6e2c`,packetPath:`traditions/quechua-inka.json`,packetSha256:`b7580d8865086c180a10b2024821a6f22963c0ad6059c46853ded503e263b691`,reviewer:`Independent reviewer /root/welsh_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"statement":"These three packets were authored by the coordinator, not by this reviewer. Only the assigned review report is written.","packetsNotAuthoredByReviewer":["quechua-inka","nahua-mexica","ancient-levantine-ugaritic"],"scope":"Local original text, evidence and chapter meaning. No artwork pixels inspected and no change to current artwork eligibility or historical gates."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.132.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Sun’s Charge`,text:`Inti sent a son and daughter to begin a journey from Lake Titicaca. In the remembered account recorded by Garcilaso, their task joined founding a place to responsibilities toward the people who would live there. Manco Capac and Mama Ocllo were to become rulers, but their father’s instructions did not concern power alone.

The sun’s example supplied a measure for that responsibility. Light allowed people to see and work; warmth sustained them in cold conditions. Fields, pastures and fruit benefited from the changes through which a day and its seasons passed. The instructions compared good rule with this continuing attention to the needs of life.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.132.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Sceptre’s Sign`,text:`Inti also gave the pair a gold sceptre. At each place where they rested, they were to test the ground. Where the sceptre sank and disappeared, they should remain. The sign would end their search without making every stop along the way their final home.

The pair travelled north. Several stopping places failed the test. After leaving a resting place near dawn, they entered the valley that would become Cusco. On a hill there, the sceptre sank out of sight.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.132.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Founding and Sustaining`,text:`Manco Capac and Mama Ocllo then went in different directions to gather people. Their common journey opened into separate work, before those who followed them came together in a growing settlement. Food, houses and practical skill became part of the founding account.

Garcilaso preserves this as a royal family’s account of its beginnings, rather than a neutral history of every Andean community. Within that telling, Inti’s presence remains both the origin of the mission and its demanding example: authority is accompanied by the continuing obligation to sustain the people it claims to lead.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.134`,candidateId:`LW-134`,definitionId:421,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`4074dfa770bd44af1bfeb8c69fdcbd8698f789c7a887c26b205dd998ecc0f287`,variantSha256:`f35a6d397d873b4c143413549999e08a54a5bffc46fbfe26d460da37e8a6429e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r04-quechua-nahua-ugaritic.json`,reportSha256:`007ad680739adf1ecb17a0bc283bb92c7511de94ce39ada8e3d847911f0a6e2c`,packetPath:`traditions/quechua-inka.json`,packetSha256:`b7580d8865086c180a10b2024821a6f22963c0ad6059c46853ded503e263b691`,reviewer:`Independent reviewer /root/welsh_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"statement":"These three packets were authored by the coordinator, not by this reviewer. Only the assigned review report is written.","packetsNotAuthoredByReviewer":["quechua-inka","nahua-mexica","ancient-levantine-ugaritic"],"scope":"Local original text, evidence and chapter meaning. No artwork pixels inspected and no change to current artwork eligibility or historical gates."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.134.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Northward Search`,text:`Manco Capac and Mama Ocllo began together at Lake Titicaca, sent by Inti to find a place where they would settle. The sun gave them a gold sceptre and a sign to watch for: at the right place, it would enter the ground and disappear.

They travelled north and tried the sign wherever they stopped. The earth did not receive the sceptre. Resting places became stages in the journey rather than its end, and the pair continued toward the valley of Cusco.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.134.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`The Sign at Cusco`,text:`After leaving a stopping place at dawn, they reached the valley. On the hill named Huanacauti in this translation, the test changed. The sceptre sank easily and was seen no more. Manco Capac told Mama Ocllo that this was where their shared instruction should be fulfilled.

The next task required two directions. He went north and she went south, each inviting people to gather. Those who followed them came together near the chosen place. A foundation was becoming a community, and a community required more than a name and a sign.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.134.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`Feeding and Building`,text:`Some people obtained food so the gathering could remain together; others worked on houses. The account remembers an upper and a lower part of the city, associated with the groups gathered by the two founders. Manco Capac also taught agricultural and practical work within the historical telling’s division of duties.

Garcilaso records these events as a royal account of origins and legitimacy. Its claim that the founders first brought civilization is not a judgment we apply to the people already living there. The selected story ends with settlement taking shape: the journey’s sign has been answered by the continuing work of feeding, building and living together.`}]},{stableId:`vision.definition.lw.135`,candidateId:`LW-135`,definitionId:422,contentVersion:1,locale:`en`,retellingSha256:`d50e41c499e95de88dab24131ac0cb1e4800a1feb70ca69b3cd7e4111beed23f`,variantSha256:`f35a6d397d873b4c143413549999e08a54a5bffc46fbfe26d460da37e8a6429e`,reviewProvenance:{contract:`independent-evidence-and-semantic-review-v1`,reportPath:`reviews/r04-quechua-nahua-ugaritic.json`,reportSha256:`007ad680739adf1ecb17a0bc283bb92c7511de94ce39ada8e3d847911f0a6e2c`,packetPath:`traditions/quechua-inka.json`,packetSha256:`b7580d8865086c180a10b2024821a6f22963c0ad6059c46853ded503e263b691`,reviewer:`Independent reviewer /root/welsh_research (gpt-6.1-sol)`,reviewedAt:`2026-10-05`,independence:`{"statement":"These three packets were authored by the coordinator, not by this reviewer. Only the assigned review report is written.","packetsNotAuthoredByReviewer":["quechua-inka","nahua-mexica","ancient-levantine-ugaritic"],"scope":"Local original text, evidence and chapter meaning. No artwork pixels inspected and no change to current artwork eligibility or historical gates."}`,disposition:`accepted-original-retelling`,chapterStatus:`accepted`},chapters:[{id:`vision.definition.lw.135.lore.encounter.v1`,ordinal:1,title:`The Shared Journey`,text:`Mama Ocllo set out from Lake Titicaca beside Manco Capac. Inti had sent both of them, and their shared journey carried the gold sceptre that would identify a place to settle. At each stopping place they tested the ground and continued when the sign did not appear.

The journey led north toward Cusco. After leaving a resting place in the early morning, they entered the valley and reached a hill. There the sceptre sank into the earth and disappeared. The long search had found its chosen place.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.135.lore.turning-point.v1`,ordinal:2,title:`Gathering from the South`,text:`Mama Ocllo’s work did not end with arriving beside her companion. The founders separated to gather people: Manco Capac travelled north, while she went south. Each spoke of their mission and invited people toward the new settlement. The community remembered those gathered by the queen in the lower part of the growing city.

Food and houses became the immediate requirements. The people assembled by both founders supplied meals and worked on dwellings, allowing others to remain rather than scatter again. The account’s practical work continued as the city took form.`},{id:`vision.definition.lw.135.lore.aftermath.v1`,ordinal:3,title:`A Community Begins`,text:`Within Garcilaso’s historically gendered version, Mama Ocllo taught textile and household skills to women. That division belongs to this particular royal telling; it does not limit the skills or responsibilities of women today. Her presence in the story includes travel, gathering people and teaching, rather than only standing beside a ruler.

The remembered founding thus gives Mama Ocllo an active direction of her own within a common task. The gold sign found the place, but the founders and those who joined them made settlement possible. Her journey ends here with a community beginning.`}]}]};export{e as t};